/* Seviye sınavları ve çalışma bölümü (beta). tools/sinav/build.py ile üretilir; elle düzenleme. */
window.SINAV={"exams":[{"id":"a2-1","t":"Sınav 1","sec":{"R":{"min":40,"parts":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","ty":"mc","q":[{"q":"What does the notice say?","o":["You must pay if you bring books back late.","The library is closed every Friday afternoon.","You have to pay 20p a day to borrow a book."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Geç getirilen kitaplar için günde 20 peni ödenir.","x":"LIBRARY\nPlease return books by Friday.\nLate books cost 20p a day."},{"q":"What is Ella telling her mum?","o":["She wants her mum to collect her at 8.","She will eat dinner at Sara's house.","She is going to cook pizza tonight."],"a":1,"tg":["purpose","will-going-to"],"e":"\"Don't cook for me – we're having pizza\" = akşam yemeğini Sara'larda yiyecek. Onu Sara'nın babası getirecek.","x":"Mum,\nI'm at Sara's house. Her dad will drive me home at 8. Don't cook for me – we're having pizza!\nElla"},{"q":"What does the sign say?","o":["The café closes every Monday in August.","You can go to the café on 15th August.","The café is closed for the whole of August."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Kafe 1–14 Ağustos kapalı; 15 Ağustos pazartesi yeniden açık.","x":"CAFÉ\nClosed for holidays 1st – 14th August.\nOpen again on Monday 15th."},{"q":"What has changed?","o":["the time of the match","the day of the match","the place of the match"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Maç cumartesiden pazara alındı; saat aynı.","x":"Hi Tom,\nThe football match is now on Sunday, not Saturday, because the pitch is too wet. Same time – 10 o'clock.\nCoach Ben"},{"q":"What does the notice say?","o":["Students don't need to take a drink.","Students will be given a free lunch at the zoo.","Students can buy food at the zoo."],"a":0,"tg":["inference","must-have-to-should"],"e":"\"Drinks will be provided\" = içecek verilecek, getirmeye gerek yok. Öğle yemeğini öğrenciler kendisi getirmeli.","x":"SCHOOL TRIP TO THE ZOO\nStudents must bring a packed lunch.\nDrinks will be provided."},{"q":"Why is the person selling the bike?","o":["It has some problems and needs repairs.","It is too small for their child.","Their son has already got a new one."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Too small for my son now\" = oğluna küçük geliyor.","x":"FOR SALE\nBoy's bike, 2 years old, good condition. Too small for my son now. £40.\nCall 07700 900123 after 6 p.m."}]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Kişi eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Üç metni oku. Her soru için doğru kişiyi (A, B ya da C) seç. Bir kişi birden fazla kez seçilebilir.","ty":"match","pl":"Tatil yazıları","opts":[["A","Lucy","Every summer my family goes camping by a lake in the mountains. We sleep in a tent, and I share it with my older sister. In the mornings we go swimming, even though the water is really cold! There's no internet there, which I didn't like at first, but now I enjoy reading books instead. My favourite part is cooking dinner on a fire in the evening."],["B","Mark","Last year we went to Rome for a week. We stayed in a hotel in the city centre, so we walked everywhere. I loved the old buildings and the food – I ate ice cream every day! It was very hot, so in the afternoons we went back to the hotel and slept. I'd like to go back one day, but first I want to learn some Italian."],["C","Ana","My grandparents live on a farm, and I spend every August with them. I help my grandfather with the animals – we've got cows, chickens and two horses. I learned to ride a horse this year. My friends think it's boring, but I love it because it's so quiet. The only thing I don't like is getting up at six o'clock every morning!"]],"q":[{"q":"Who shares a tent with a family member?","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Lucy çadırı ablasıyla paylaşıyor."},{"q":"Who learned to do something new this year?","a":"C","tg":["detail","past-simple"],"e":"Ana bu yıl ata binmeyi öğrendi."},{"q":"Who couldn't use the internet on holiday?","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Lucy: \"There's no internet there\"."},{"q":"Who wants to study a language?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Mark önce biraz İtalyanca öğrenmek istiyor."},{"q":"Who has to get up very early?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Ana her sabah 6'da kalkıyor."},{"q":"Who went everywhere on foot?","a":"B","tg":["inference"],"e":"Mark otel şehir merkezinde olduğu için her yere yürüdü."},{"q":"Who says that other people don't like this kind of holiday?","a":"C","tg":["attitude"],"e":"Ana: \"My friends think it's boring\"."}]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"My brother the chef","p":["My brother Daniel is twenty-two and he works as a chef in a busy restaurant in Manchester. When he was at school, he wasn't very interested in cooking. He wanted to be a footballer, and he played for the school team every weekend.","Everything changed when he was sixteen. He broke his leg in a match and had to stay at home for two months. He was bored, so he started watching cooking programmes on TV. Then he began making dinner for the family. At first his food wasn't very good, but he got better quickly.","After school, Daniel went to college and studied cooking for two years. It was hard work, and he often had to start at seven in the morning. But he loved it, and his teachers said he was one of the best students in his class.","Now Daniel works five days a week, usually in the evenings. He doesn't have much free time, but he says he's never been happier. His dream is to open his own small restaurant by the sea. I hope he does – then I can eat there for free!"],"q":[{"q":"What did Daniel want to be when he was at school?","o":["a famous chef","a footballer","a PE teacher"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"He wanted to be a footballer\"."},{"q":"Why did Daniel start cooking?","o":["He had nothing to do at home.","His mother asked him to help.","He wanted to be on TV."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Bacağı kırıldı, evde canı sıkıldı; yemek programları izleyip yemek yapmaya başladı."},{"q":"What does the writer say about Daniel's first meals?","o":["They were excellent.","Nobody wanted to eat them.","They weren't very good."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"At first his food wasn't very good\"."},{"q":"What was Daniel's college course like?","o":["It was difficult, but he enjoyed it.","It finished at seven every evening.","It was easy for him because he could already cook."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"hard work ... But he loved it\". Saat 7 başlama saati, bitiş değil."},{"q":"What would Daniel like to do in the future?","o":["work fewer days","open a restaurant","cook for his brother"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"His dream is to open his own small restaurant by the sea\"."}]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için doğru kelimeyi seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"The giant panda","p":"Giant pandas live in the mountains of China. They (1) ______ most of the day eating bamboo – up to 14 hours! A baby panda is very small when it is born, about the (2) ______ size as a mouse. Pandas usually live (3) ______ their own, but mothers stay with their babies for about two years. There (4) ______ only about 1,800 pandas in the wild today, but the number is growing. Scientists (5) ______ hard to protect them, and many people visit zoos (6) ______ see these beautiful animals.","o":[[["use","spend","take"],1,["collocation"]],[["like","equal","same"],2,["vocab"]],[["on","by","in"],0,["vocab"]],[["is","are","has"],1,["there-is-are"]],[["are working","works","is work"],0,["present-continuous"]],[["for","so","to"],2,["gerund-infinitive"]]],"e":["spend time doing: zaman geçirmek.","the same size as: ile aynı boyda.","live on their own: kendi başlarına yaşamak.","There are + çoğul (pandas).","Şu sıralar süren eylem: are working.","Amaç: to + fiil (to see)."]},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","one":true,"title":"Hi Sam","p":"Hi Sam,\nThanks (1) ______ your message. I'm sorry I didn't reply yesterday – I was very busy. My cousins came to stay with us (2) ______ the weekend. We went to the beach on Saturday and it (3) ______ really sunny. On Sunday we visited a museum. It was (4) ______ interesting than I thought! Are you free next week? (5) ______ you like to come to the cinema with me? There's a new film that I really want (6) ______ see.\nLove,\nMia","a":[["for"],["for","at","over"],["was"],["more"],["would"],["to"]],"tg":[["vocab"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["past-simple"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["question-words"],["gerund-infinitive"]],"e":["Thanks for: ... için teşekkürler.","for / at / over the weekend: hafta sonu için / boyunca.","Geçmiş zaman: it was sunny.","Uzun sıfat karşılaştırma: more interesting than.","Kibar davet: Would you like to ...?","want to + fiil."]}]},"L":{"min":30,"parts":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Beş kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1a","sc":[["F","Shall we go to the cinema tonight? The film starts at seven fifteen."],["M","Seven fifteen? I don't finish work until seven. Is there a later one?"],["F","Yes, there's one at eight forty-five."],["M","OK, let's go to that one."]],"q":"What time will they see the film?","o":["7.15","7.00","8.45"],"a":2,"tg":["spelling","detail"],"e":"7.15 erken; 8.45'teki seansa gidecekler."},{"id":"l1b","sc":[["F","I need a present for my brother. He likes music, but he's got lots of CDs."],["M","What about a T-shirt?"],["F","He's got lots of those too. Oh, I know – headphones! His old ones are broken."]],"q":"What will the girl buy for her brother?","o":["headphones","some new CDs","a T-shirt"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"CD ve tişörtü çok var; kulaklık alacak."},{"id":"l1c","sc":[["M","Sorry I'm late. My car wouldn't start this morning."],["F","Oh no. Did you take the bus?"],["M","No, it was too crowded, so I walked. It took forty minutes!"]],"q":"How did the man get to work today?","o":["on foot","in his car","by bus"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Araba çalışmadı, otobüs kalabalıktı; yürüdü."},{"id":"l1d","sc":[["M","Mum, have you seen my phone? It's not in my bag."],["F","Did you look on the kitchen table?"],["M","Yes, it's not there."],["F","Oh, here it is. It's on the sofa, under your jacket."]],"q":"Where is the boy's phone?","o":["in his bag","on the sofa","on the kitchen table"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Telefon kanepede, ceketin altında."},{"id":"l1e","sc":[["F","It's raining again today. What about tomorrow?"],["M","The forecast says it'll be cloudy in the morning, but sunny in the afternoon."],["F","Good, we can play tennis after lunch then."]],"q":"What will the weather be like tomorrow afternoon?","o":["rainy","sunny","cloudy"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Sabah bulutlu, öğleden sonra güneşli."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","ty":"open","id":"l2","title":"Notlar","sc":[["F","Hello, this is Riverside Sports Centre. Thank you for asking about our children's swimming lessons. The lessons are on Wednesdays. We used to have them on Mondays, but not any more. They start at half past four and finish at a quarter past five. The teacher is Mrs Barker – that's B, A, R, K, E, R. She's very friendly and she has taught swimming for twenty years. Ten lessons cost forty-five pounds. Please remember that children must bring a towel and a swimming hat. Thank you for calling."]],"p":"Riverside Sports Centre – children's swimming lessons\nDay: (1) ______\nTime: 4.30 to (2) ______ p.m.\nTeacher's name: Mrs (3) ______\nPrice for 10 lessons: £(4) ______\nBring: a towel and a swimming (5) ______","a":[["Wednesday","Wednesdays"],["5.15","5:15","quarter past five","a quarter past five"],["Barker"],["45","forty-five","forty five"],["hat"]],"tg":[["detail"],["spelling"],["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"]],"e":["Dersler çarşamba; pazartesi eskiden yapılıyordu.","\"finish at a quarter past five\" = 5.15.","Harf harf söylendi: B-A-R-K-E-R.","\"Ten lessons cost forty-five pounds\".","Havlu ve yüzme bonesi (swimming hat)."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Uzun konuşma","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"İki kişinin konuşmasını dinle ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","id":"l3","sc":[["F","Tom, it's Dad's fiftieth birthday next Saturday. Shall we plan a surprise party for him?"],["M","Great idea, Kate. Where shall we have it? At home?"],["F","Our house is too small for everyone. What about the Italian restaurant in town? Dad loves it."],["M","Hmm, it's very expensive. Grandma's garden is big, and it's free."],["F","You're right. Let's ask Grandma. How many people shall we invite?"],["M","Well, there's the family – that's about fifteen people – and some of Dad's friends from work. Maybe thirty altogether."],["F","OK. And what about food? I can make a cake."],["M","You always make chocolate cake. Dad prefers lemon."],["F","Fine, I'll make a lemon cake. You can buy the drinks."],["M","OK. And Mum can bring some sandwiches. What present shall we get him?"],["F","He wants a new watch, but they cost too much. How about a book about fishing? He's just started going fishing."],["M","Perfect. I'll buy it tomorrow."]],"q":[{"q":"Where will the party be?","o":["at their house","at the Italian restaurant in town","in their grandmother's garden"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Ev küçük, restoran pahalı; büyükannenin bahçesi."},{"q":"How many people will come to the party?","o":["about fifteen","about thirty","about fifty"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Aile yaklaşık 15 kişi, iş arkadaşlarıyla toplam 30. Elli, babanın yaşı."},{"q":"What kind of cake will Kate make?","o":["lemon","chocolate","strawberry"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Babaları limonlu pastayı tercih ediyor."},{"q":"What will Tom bring to the party?","o":["sandwiches","the drinks","the cake"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"\"You can buy the drinks\" – içecekleri Tom alacak. Sandviçleri anne getirecek."},{"q":"What present will they buy?","o":["a book about fishing","an expensive new watch","a fishing rod"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Saat çok pahalı; balıkçılık kitabı alacaklar."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Beş kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l4a","sc":[["F","Hello, this is Anna Cole. I bought a lamp from your shop on Saturday, but when I got home, I found it didn't work. Can I bring it back and change it for a new one?"]],"q":"Why is the woman calling?","o":["to buy a lamp","to change something she bought","to ask when the shop opens on Saturday"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Aldığı lamba çalışmıyor; yenisiyle değiştirmek istiyor."},{"id":"l4b","sc":[["M","Everyone said the trip to the castle would be boring, but I thought it was brilliant. The guide told us lots of funny stories, and we dressed up in old clothes."]],"q":"How did the boy feel about the school trip?","o":["He thought it was boring.","He enjoyed it a lot.","He thought it was too long."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I thought it was brilliant\" – çok beğendi. Sıkıcı diyenler başkaları."},{"id":"l4c","sc":[["F","OK, everyone, before you go home, please remember that the history homework is due on Thursday, not Friday. And don't forget to bring your sports clothes tomorrow."]],"q":"What is the teacher mainly talking about?","o":["when to give in homework","a history test on Friday","a new sports club"],"a":0,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Tarih ödevinin perşembe teslim edileceğini hatırlatıyor."},{"id":"l4d","sc":[["M","My new flat isn't very big, and the kitchen is quite old. But from the living room window, I can see the whole city and the river. That's the best thing about it."]],"q":"What does the man like most about his new flat?","o":["the size","the kitchen","the view"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"That's the best thing about it\" = manzara."},{"id":"l4e","sc":[["M","Can you see the screen OK?"],["F","Yes, but the people in front are talking. And this popcorn is too salty!"],["M","Shh, it's starting."]],"q":"Where are the speakers?","o":["at a cinema","in a classroom","at a restaurant"],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Perde (screen), patlamış mısır, \"it's starting\": sinema."}]},{"k":"L5","nm":"Dinleme 5 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Listening Part 5","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her kişiyi doğru seçenekle (A–H) eşleştir. Üç seçenek fazladır.","ty":"match","id":"l5","sc":[["F","So, Ben, what are your friends doing in the holidays?"],["M","Well, Sam's going to work in his uncle's shop. He wants to save money for a new bike."],["F","Good for him. What about Lily? Is she going camping again?"],["M","No, not this year. She's going to stay with her grandparents in Scotland."],["F","That's nice. And Josh?"],["M","Josh is doing a cooking course. He wants to be a chef one day."],["F","Really? And Emma? She loves the beach, doesn't she?"],["M","Yes, but she's broken her arm, so she can't swim. She's going to paint her bedroom instead. Her mum said she can choose the colours!"],["F","With one arm? And Ryan?"],["M","Ryan's having tennis lessons every morning. He's really good already."]],"opts":[["A","going camping"],["B","doing a cooking course"],["C","visiting family"],["D","working in a shop"],["E","learning to swim"],["F","painting"],["G","playing tennis"],["H","going to the beach"]],"q":[{"q":"Sam","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"Amcasının dükkânında çalışacak."},{"q":"Lily","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Bu yıl kamp yok; İskoçya'da büyükanne ve büyükbabasında kalacak."},{"q":"Josh","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Aşçılık kursuna gidiyor."},{"q":"Emma","a":"F","tg":["detail","inference"],"e":"Kolu kırık, yüzemiyor; odasını boyayacak."},{"q":"Ryan","a":"G","tg":["detail"],"e":"Her sabah tenis dersi alıyor."}]}]},"W":{"min":20,"tasks":[{"id":"a2x-w1","part":6,"t":"Part 6 · E-posta: Parka gitmek","in":"You want to go to the park with your English friend Alex on Saturday. Write an email to Alex. In your email:\n• suggest going to the park\n• say what time to meet\n• say what you can do there.\nWrite 25 words or more.","mail":"To: Alex\nSubject: Saturday","pts":[["Parka gitmeyi öner",["park","shall we","let's","would you like","do you want"]],["Buluşma saati söyle",["o'clock","at 1","at 2","at 3","at 4","at 9","at 10","at 11","half past","meet at","a.m","p.m"]],["Orada ne yapabileceğinizi söyle",["play","football","picnic","ride","walk","frisbee","swim","tennis","basketball"]]],"min":25,"max":60,"model":"Hi Alex,\nWould you like to go to the park with me on Saturday? Let's meet at the park gate at 11 o'clock. We can play football and then have a picnic by the lake.\nSee you,\nEmre","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Alex,\nThe weather is going to be sunny on Saturday, so shall we go to Central Park? We can meet at the café near the big gate at 2 o'clock. I'll bring my bike, so we can ride around the lake. After that we can have an ice cream.\nBye,\nZeynep"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","Hi Alex,\nAre you free on Saturday? I've got a great idea – let's go to the park by the river! I think we should meet at half past ten at the bus stop opposite my school, because it's easy for both of us. There's a new skate park there, so we can try it, and if we get hungry, we can have a picnic under the trees.\nSee you,\nBerk"]]},{"id":"a2x-w2","part":7,"kind":"story","t":"Part 7 · Resimli hikâye: Kırık cam","in":"Look at the three pictures. Write the story shown in the pictures.\nWrite 35 words or more.","mail":"Picture 1: 👦⚽🌳  (Bir çocuk parkta futbol oynuyor.)\nPicture 2: ⚽💥🪟  (Top bir evin camına çarpıp kırıyor.)\nPicture 3: 👦🙇👵🍪  (Çocuk özür diliyor; yaşlı kadın gülümseyip ona kurabiye veriyor.)","pts":[["Çocuğun futbol oynadığını anlat",["football","ball","played","playing","kicked"]],["Camın kırıldığını anlat",["window","broke","broken","smashed"]],["Özrü ve sonucu anlat",["sorry","apologised","cookie","biscuit","smiled","gave"]],["Geçmiş zaman kullan",["was","went","played","kicked","broke","said","gave"]]],"min":35,"max":80,"model":"Last Saturday, Leo was playing football in the park near his house. He kicked the ball very hard and it flew over the fence. It broke the window of an old woman's house! Leo felt terrible, so he knocked on her door and said sorry. The woman smiled and gave him a cookie.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","On Sunday afternoon, Tom was playing football with his friends in the park. He kicked the ball very hard, but it went over the trees and hit a window. The glass broke! Tom was very scared. He went to the house and said sorry to the old woman. She wasn't angry. She smiled and gave him a cookie."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","Last summer, Emir was practising football in the park next to his grandmother's street. He wanted to score a great goal, so he kicked the ball as hard as he could. Unfortunately, it flew into a garden and smashed a window! Emir felt terrible. He knocked on the door and apologised to the old lady who lived there. To his surprise, she laughed and said, \"Don't worry!\" Then she gave him a cookie and a glass of lemonade."]]}]},"S":{"parts":[{"id":"a2x-s1","p":1,"t":"Part 1 · Tanışma","ins":"Sınav görevlisi seninle ilgili sorular soruyor. Kısa ama tam cümlelerle cevap ver; son sorularda biraz daha uzun konuş.","q":[["What's your name? How old are you?",15,["my name is","I'm","years old"],["My name is Elif and I'm fifteen years old. My birthday is in May.","Hello, my name's Burak. I'm sixteen, and I'm in the tenth grade at school."]],["Where do you live?",15,["I live in","it's"],["I live in Bursa. It's a big city in the north-west of Turkey, near the mountains.","I live in a small town near Izmir. It's quiet, but it's close to the sea."]],["Tell me about your family.",30,["there are","I have","I've got","my mother","my father","brother","sister"],["There are four people in my family: my mother, my father, my little brother and me. My mother is a nurse and she works in a big hospital. My father works in a bank. My brother is eight and he loves football. We also have a cat called Pamuk.","I have a big family. I live with my parents, my two sisters and my grandmother. My older sister is at university in Ankara, so she only comes home in the holidays. My younger sister is ten. My grandmother cooks for us every day, and her food is delicious."]],["What do you like doing at the weekend?",30,["I like","I usually","with my friends","because"],["At the weekend I usually meet my friends. We go to the shopping centre or watch films at the cinema. I also like playing basketball in the park, because it's fun and good for my health. On Sunday evenings I do my homework.","I like sleeping late on Saturday mornings! After that, I often help my dad in the garden. In the afternoon I play computer games or read comics. On Sundays we sometimes visit my grandparents in their village."]],["Please tell me about your favourite place in your town.",30,["my favourite","because","there is","you can"],["My favourite place is the big park near my house. There is a lake with ducks and lots of trees. I go there with my dog every evening, because it's quiet and beautiful. In summer there are concerts there too.","My favourite place in my town is a small café near the sea. They make the best hot chocolate! I go there with my friends after school, and we sit by the window and watch the boats."]]]},{"id":"a2x-s2","p":2,"t":"Part 2 · Resimlerle konuşma","ins":"Gerçek sınavda bu bölüm bir partnerle yapılır. Burada resimlerdeki etkinlikler hakkında kendi görüşünü söyle.","opts":["🏊 swimming","🎮 video games","📚 reading","🚲 cycling","🎸 playing music"],"q":[["Here are some pictures of different things to do in your free time. Do you like these different things? Say why or why not.",60,["I like","I don't like","because","I think","it's"],["Let me look at the pictures. I really like swimming because it's good exercise and I feel relaxed after it. I go to the pool every Saturday. Video games are fun, but I don't play them very often because my eyes get tired. I don't like reading very much – it's a bit boring for me. Cycling is great! I go cycling with my dad on Sundays. Playing music is interesting, but I can't play any instruments. I'd like to learn the guitar one day.","I don't like swimming very much, because the water is often cold. But I love video games – I play with my friends online every evening. Reading is OK. I like comics and short stories. I think cycling is dangerous in my city because there are too many cars. Playing music is my favourite! I play the piano, and I practise every day for thirty minutes."]],["Which of these things do you like best? Why?",30,["I like ... best","my favourite","because"],["I like cycling best, because I can do it outside with my friends and see lots of different places. It's also cheap and good for the environment. Last summer we rode to a beautiful lake near my town and had a picnic there.","I like playing music best. When I play the piano, I feel happy and calm, and I can forget about school. My dream is to play in a concert one day."]],["Do you prefer doing things alone or with friends? Why?",30,["I prefer","because","with my friends","alone"],["I prefer doing things with my friends, because it's more fun and we can talk and laugh together. Alone, I sometimes get bored. For example, going to the cinema is much better with friends, because we can talk about the film afterwards.","I prefer doing things alone, because I can decide everything myself. For example, I like reading in my room in the evening. With friends, we always have to agree about what to do, and that can be difficult."]],["Do you think it's important to do sport? Why?",30,["I think","yes","because","healthy"],["Yes, I think it's very important, because sport keeps you healthy and you feel happier. It's also a good way to make friends. I play basketball at school, and my best friends are in my team.","Yes, I think so. Many young people sit at their computers all day. Doing sport helps your body and your mind, and you sleep better. I go swimming twice a week, and I always feel great afterwards."]]]}]}},"lv":"A2","name":"A2 Key"},{"id":"b1-1","t":"Sınav 1","sec":{"R":{"min":45,"parts":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","ty":"mc","q":[{"x":"NOTICE\nSwimming pool closed for cleaning on Monday morning. Lessons will start at 2 p.m. instead of 10 a.m.","q":"What does the notice say?","o":["Swimming lessons have been cancelled for the whole of Monday.","Monday's swimming lessons will take place later than usual.","The pool will be cleaned every Monday morning."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Dersler iptal edilmiyor, sabah 10 yerine öğleden sonra 2'de başlıyor: yani her zamankinden geç."},{"x":"Hi Tom,\nI've left the keys with Mrs Hill next door because I'll be back late tonight. Please don't forget to feed the cat!\nAnna","q":"Why has Anna written this message?","o":["to tell Tom where he can find the keys","to ask Tom to collect the cat from Mrs Hill","to explain why she forgot the keys"],"a":0,"tg":["purpose","present-perfect"],"e":"\"I've left the keys with Mrs Hill\" anahtarların nerede olduğunu söylüyor. Kedi Mrs Hill'de değil; Tom'dan kediyi beslemesi isteniyor."},{"x":"SALE\nAll winter coats half price this week only. Cards accepted, but not for items under £5.","q":"What does this sign say?","o":["You can only pay in cash for sale items this week.","Winter coats are cheaper for a short time.","Coats under £5 are not in the sale."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","vocab"],"e":"\"half price this week only\" = kısa bir süre için yarı fiyat. Kart yalnızca 5 sterlinin altındaki ürünlerde geçmiyor."},{"x":"To: All students\nThe trip to the science museum has been moved from Friday to next Tuesday because the bus company had a problem. Please bring your forms by Monday.","q":"What should students do?","o":["give in their forms before the trip","contact the bus company about the problem","go to the museum on Monday"],"a":0,"tg":["detail","passive-advanced"],"e":"Gezi salıya alındı; formlar pazartesiye kadar, yani geziden önce teslim edilmeli."},{"x":"Lucy, Ben called. He can't meet you at the cinema at 7, but he'll be there by 7.30 if you can wait. Text him to let him know. Mum","q":"What does Ben want Lucy to do?","o":["change the cinema","tell him if she can wait","meet him outside the cinema at 7 o'clock"],"a":1,"tg":["inference","reported-speech"],"e":"\"Text him to let him know\" = bekleyip bekleyemeyeceğini ona haber vermesi isteniyor."}]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Aşağıdaki beş kişi bir yaz kursu arıyor. Her kişi için en uygun kursu (A–H) seç.","ty":"match","pl":"Metinler (A–H)","opts":[["A","Weekend Kitchen","Two-day course on Saturday and Sunday. Learn to bake bread and cakes. Everything you make goes home with you in a box."],["B","Sea Kayaking","A five-day course for teenagers who have never kayaked. All equipment is provided. Daily trips along the coast."],["C","Spanish Conversation","Evening classes, Monday to Thursday, maximum six students per group. Speaking only – no grammar homework!"],["D","City Walks","Discover the old town with an expert guide. Each day we visit a castle, a museum or an old market."],["E","Photo Club","For people who already know their camera well. Weekly competitions and discussions about famous photographers."],["F","Camera Basics","Learn every button on your camera in four lessons. The best photos from the group will appear in the library exhibition."],["G","Spanish Grammar","Morning classes for groups of up to twenty. Lots of written exercises to prepare for exams."],["H","Climbing Day","A one-day climbing taster for adults with some experience. Lunch included."]],"q":[{"q":"Maria: Maria wants to improve her cooking. She works during the week, so she can only go at weekends. She would like to take home what she makes.","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Hafta sonu, yaptığını eve götürme: A."},{"q":"Daniel: Daniel is 16 and loves being outdoors. He wants to try a sport he has never done before, and he would prefer a course that lasts several days.","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Daha önce yapmadığı bir spor, birkaç gün, gençler: B (H tek gün ve deneyimli yetişkinler için)."},{"q":"Sophie: Sophie is a beginner photographer. She wants to learn to use her new camera and she hopes to have her photos shown somewhere at the end.","a":"F","tg":["detail"],"e":"Yeni başlayan, fotoğraflar sergilenecek: F (E kamerasını iyi bilenler için)."},{"q":"Ahmed: Ahmed wants to practise speaking another language before a trip to Spain. He prefers small groups and evening classes.","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Konuşma, küçük grup, akşam: C (G sabah ve kalabalık)."},{"q":"Chen: Chen is interested in history. He would like a course with visits to real places rather than just lessons in a classroom.","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"Gerçek yerlere ziyaret: D."}]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"My first job","p":["When I was seventeen, I got a summer job at a small hotel by the sea. I had never worked before, and to be honest, I was more interested in the beach than in the money. My parents thought it would be good for me to learn what work was really like.","On my first day, the manager, Mrs Grant, gave me a uniform that was far too big and asked me to clean twenty rooms before lunch. I thought it was impossible. By twelve o'clock I had finished only eight, and I was sure she would be angry. Instead, she sat down with me and showed me a better way to organise my work. \"Everyone is slow at the beginning,\" she said.","After a few weeks, I was much faster, and I started to enjoy talking to the guests. Some of them came back every year and told me stories about the hotel in the past. I also made friends with the other young workers, and we often went swimming together after work.","Looking back, I realise that job taught me more than any school lesson. I learned to be patient, to ask for help when I needed it, and to be proud of doing something well. I still use those lessons today in my job as a teacher."],"q":[{"q":"Why did the writer take the job?","o":["He needed money for university.","His parents encouraged him to work.","He wanted to live near the beach.","A friend already worked at the hotel."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"My parents thought it would be good for me\" – ailesi teşvik etti."},{"q":"How did the writer feel at twelve o'clock on his first day?","o":["pleased that he had finished so many rooms","angry with the manager","worried about the manager's reaction","bored with the work"],"a":2,"tg":["inference","past-perfect"],"e":"\"I was sure she would be angry\" – yöneticinin tepkisinden endişe ediyordu."},{"q":"What did Mrs Grant do?","o":["She gave him an easier job.","She explained how to work more efficiently.","She told him to finish the rooms after lunch.","She gave him a smaller uniform."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"showed me a better way to organise my work\" = daha verimli çalışmayı gösterdi."},{"q":"What did the writer enjoy most after a few weeks?","o":["swimming alone","the guests' stories","cleaning the rooms quickly","working extra hours"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Misafirlerle konuşmayı ve onların otelin geçmişiyle ilgili hikâyelerini sevdi."},{"q":"What is the writer's main point in the text?","o":["Hotel work is too difficult for young people.","Teachers should have experience of working in other jobs first.","His first job taught him important lessons for life.","Managers should be kind to new workers."],"a":2,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Son paragraf: o iş ona okul derslerinden daha çok şey öğretti."}]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metinden beş cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–H) seç. Üç cümle fazladır.","ty":"gap","title":"The town that gave up cars","p":"Ten years ago, the small town of Lindhaven decided to close its centre to cars. Many shop owners were against the idea. (1) ______ They were worried that customers would stop coming.\nIn the first few months, things were difficult. Some shops did lose business. (2) ______ People began to walk and cycle into town, and they stayed longer because the streets were quieter and cleaner.\nThe town also built a large car park just outside the centre. (3) ______ The bus is free, so people do not mind leaving their cars there.\nToday, Lindhaven has more shops than before. (4) ______ Visitors from other countries come to see how the town changed.\nNot everyone is happy, of course. Older people sometimes find it hard to carry heavy shopping a long way. (5) ______ It seems that most problems can be solved if people are willing to talk.","opts":[["A","But after a year, the situation started to change."],["B","From there, a small bus takes visitors to the main square every ten minutes."],["C","They believed that people would not want to walk."],["D","The town plans to open the centre to cars again next year."],["E","To help them, the town now offers a free delivery service."],["F","Many of them are cafés with tables outside in the street."],["G","The bus stopped running because nobody used it."],["H","Cars are much cheaper to use in the countryside."]],"a":["C","A","B","F","E"],"tg":[["coherence"],["coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"],["reference"]],"e":["\"They were worried...\" öncesinde dükkân sahiplerinin düşüncesi gelmeli: C.","\"did lose business\" sonrası \"But after a year\" ile değişim başlıyor: A.","\"From there\" = otoparktan; sonraki cümlede \"The bus is free\": B.","Daha çok dükkân → \"Many of them are cafés\": F.","Yaşlıların zorluğuna çözüm: \"To help them\": E."]},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için doğru kelimeyi seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"A weekend in the mountains","p":"Last weekend my family and I (1) ______ on a trip to the mountains. We had booked a small cabin (2) ______ was next to a lake. On Saturday we got up early and (3) ______ a long walk through the forest. It was quite (4) ______, but the views from the top were amazing. In the evening it started to rain, so we stayed inside and played cards. We enjoyed it so much (5) ______ we have already decided to go back next year. I would (6) ______ it to anyone who loves nature.","o":[[["went","made","did","took"],0,["vocab"]],[["who","which","where","what"],1,["relative-clauses"]],[["went","made","took","did"],2,["vocab"]],[["tiring","tired","tire","tiredness"],0,["vocab"]],[["because","that","so","than"],1,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],[["advise","recommend","tell","say"],1,["reporting-verbs"]]],"e":["go on a trip: geziye çıkmak.","Şey (cabin) için which; arkasından fiil geliyor.","take a walk: yürüyüşe çıkmak.","Yürüyüş yorucuydu: tiring (yorucu). tired = yorgun (kişi).","so ... that: o kadar ... ki.","recommend something to someone: birine bir şeyi tavsiye etmek. advise ve tell bu kalıpla kullanılmaz."]},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 6","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","title":"Hi Jess!","p":"Hi Jess!\nI'm writing (1) ______ tell you about my new school. I started here two weeks ago and I really like it. The teachers are very friendly and there are lots of clubs. I've already joined the drama club, (2) ______ meets every Wednesday. My best friend here is called Mia. She lives (3) ______ the same street as me, so we walk to school together. The only problem is that the school is (4) ______ bigger than my old one, and I sometimes get lost! Have you ever (5) ______ to a new school? Write back soon and tell me (6) ______ you are doing.\nLove, Ella","a":[["to"],["which"],["on","in"],["much","far","a lot"],["been","gone","moved","changed"],["how","what"]],"tg":[["gerund-infinitive"],["relative-clauses"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["present-perfect"],["reported-speech"]],"e":["Amaç: to + fiil (I'm writing to tell you).","Virgüllü ek bilgi, şey (club): which.","Sokak adıyla on (İngiltere'de in de kullanılır).","Karşılaştırmayı güçlendirme: much / far / a lot bigger.","Have you ever been to ...? deneyim sorusu (moved / changed da anlamca kabul edilir).","Dolaylı soru: tell me how you are doing (what you are doing da olur)."]}]},"L":{"min":30,"parts":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Yedi kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1a","sc":[["M","Excuse me, how much is a return ticket to Oxford?"],["F","It's twelve pounds fifty. But if you travel after half past nine, it's only nine pounds twenty."],["M","Oh, I'm leaving at ten, so that's fine."]],"q":"How much will the man pay?","o":["£12.50","£9.20","£9.50"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","spelling"],"e":"Saat 10'da gidiyor; 9.30'dan sonra bilet 9,20 sterlin."},{"id":"l1b","sc":[["F","Did you buy the present for Grandma?"],["M","Yes. I was going to get her a scarf, but she has so many. So I got her a book about gardens instead."],["F","Good idea. She loves her garden."]],"q":"What did the boy buy for his grandmother?","o":["a scarf","a book","some flowers"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"I was going to get her a scarf ... I got her a book\" – sonunda kitap aldı."},{"id":"l1c","sc":[["M","Hi, it's Sam. Sorry, I'll be late for the meeting. My train has been delayed. I think I'll be there by quarter to eleven, not half past ten as we planned."]],"q":"What time will Sam arrive?","o":["10.15","10.30","10.45"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","passive-advanced"],"e":"quarter to eleven = 10.45. 10.30 planlanan saatti."},{"id":"l1d","sc":[["F","What was the weather like on your holiday?"],["M","Well, the first two days were sunny, but then it rained for the rest of the week. We spent most of the time in museums."]],"q":"What was the weather like for most of the holiday?","o":["sunny","rainy","windy"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"İlk iki gün güneşli, haftanın geri kalanı yağmurlu."},{"id":"l1e","sc":[["M","Where shall we meet tomorrow? At the café?"],["F","It's closed on Sundays. Let's meet outside the station, and we can walk to the park from there."],["M","OK, see you there."]],"q":"Where will they meet?","o":["outside the café","at the station","in the park"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Kafe pazar kapalı; istasyonun önünde buluşacaklar."},{"id":"l1f","sc":[["F","Have you finished your project yet?"],["M","Almost. I've written the text and I've found the photos. I just need to print it. The printer at home isn't working, so I'll do it at the library tomorrow."]],"q":"What does the boy still need to do?","o":["finish writing the text","find some photos","print the project"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","present-perfect"],"e":"Metni yazmış, fotoğrafları bulmuş; yalnızca yazdırması kaldı."},{"id":"l1g","sc":[["M","I really want to learn a musical instrument. I tried the guitar last year, but it hurt my fingers. My sister plays the piano, but we haven't got space for one. I think I'll try the violin."]],"q":"Which instrument is the man going to learn?","o":["the guitar","the piano","the violin"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Gitarı denedi, piyanoya yer yok; kemanı deneyecek."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Altı kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l2a","sc":[["F","How was the new restaurant?"],["M","The food was lovely, but we waited almost an hour for it. And it was so noisy we could hardly talk."],["F","So you won't go again?"],["M","Probably not."]],"q":"What did the man think of the restaurant?","o":["The food was disappointing.","The service was too slow.","It was too expensive."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Yemek güzeldi ama neredeyse bir saat beklediler: servis yavaştı."},{"id":"l2b","sc":[["M","Are you enjoying your new job?"],["F","The work is interesting, and my colleagues are nice. It's just that it takes me an hour and a half to get there every morning."],["M","That's a long time."]],"q":"What is the woman's problem with her job?","o":["the journey to work","the people she works with","the type of work"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"İş ilginç, arkadaşlar iyi; sorun her sabah bir buçuk saatlik yol."},{"id":"l2c","sc":[["F","Did you see the film last night?"],["M","Yes. Everyone said it was brilliant, but I thought the story was hard to follow."],["F","Really? I loved it."]],"q":"How did the man feel about the film?","o":["He found it confusing.","He thought it was too long.","He agreed it was brilliant."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"the story was hard to follow\" = kafa karıştırıcı buldu."},{"id":"l2d","sc":[["M","I'm thinking of selling my bike."],["F","Why? You use it every day."],["M","I know, but I'm moving to the city centre next month, and I'll be able to walk everywhere."]],"q":"Why does the man want to sell his bike?","o":["It is too old and often breaks down.","He won't need it any more.","He wants a faster one."],"a":1,"tg":["inference","will-going-to"],"e":"Şehir merkezine taşınıp her yere yürüyebilecek: bisiklete ihtiyacı kalmayacak."},{"id":"l2e","sc":[["F","You look tired."],["M","I am. My neighbours had a party last night, and it didn't finish until three. If I had known, I would have stayed at my brother's."]],"q":"Why is the man tired?","o":["He worked late.","He stayed at his brother's.","His neighbours were noisy."],"a":2,"tg":["detail","third-conditional"],"e":"Komşuların partisi sabah üçe kadar sürdü. Kardeşinde kalmadı; \"kalırdım\" diyor (third conditional)."},{"id":"l2f","sc":[["M","Shall we go to the beach on Saturday?"],["F","I'd love to, but I promised to help my mum paint the kitchen. How about Sunday?"],["M","Sunday's fine."]],"q":"What will the woman do on Saturday?","o":["go to the beach","help her mother","paint her own room"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","reporting-verbs"],"e":"Annesine mutfağı boyamada yardım edeceğine söz verdi."}]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bir müze rehberini dinle. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","ty":"open","id":"l3","sc":[["F","Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the City Museum. Before you start your visit, here is some useful information. The museum opens at nine thirty every day and closes at five, except on Thursdays, when we stay open until eight in the evening. On the ground floor you will find our main exhibition, which is about the history of the river. On the first floor there is a special exhibition about toys from the last hundred years. Children love it, and so do many adults! Our café is on the top floor. It has great views of the city, and today the special is vegetable soup. Please note that you can take photos everywhere except in the toy exhibition. If you lose anything, go to the information desk next to the main entrance. Finally, don't forget that our free guided tour starts at eleven o'clock from the shop. Enjoy your visit!"]],"title":"Notlar","p":"City Museum\nOpening time: (1) ______\nOn Thursdays open until: (2) ______ p.m.\nGround floor exhibition: the history of the (3) ______\nFirst floor exhibition: (4) ______ from the last 100 years\nToday's special in the café: vegetable (5) ______\nFree guided tour starts from the (6) ______","a":[["9.30","9:30","half past nine","nine thirty"],["8","eight","8.00"],["river"],["toys","toy"],["soup"],["shop"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["detail"],["detail"]],"e":["\"opens at nine thirty\"","\"until eight in the evening\"","\"the history of the river\"","\"toys from the last hundred years\"","\"today the special is vegetable soup\"","\"starts at eleven o'clock from the shop\""]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Genç bir dağcıyla yapılan röportajı dinle ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","id":"l4","sc":[["M","Today I'm talking to Emma Price, who, at just nineteen, has climbed some of the highest mountains in Europe. Emma, how did you start?"],["F","Well, my parents loved walking, so we spent every holiday in the hills. When I was twelve, my uncle took me to a climbing wall, and I was hooked. I didn't try a real mountain until I was fifteen."],["M","What's the hardest thing about climbing?"],["F","People think it's being afraid of heights, but actually I've never had that problem. For me, the hardest part is the cold. You have to keep going even when you can't feel your hands."],["M","Do you climb alone?"],["F","Never. It's much safer to climb with a team, and anyway, sharing the experience is half the fun. My climbing partner, Lisa, has been my best friend since school."],["M","How do you prepare for a big climb?"],["F","I go running every morning and I go to the gym three times a week. But I think planning is even more important than fitness. We study the weather and the route for weeks before we go."],["M","And what's next?"],["F","Next summer we're going to try a mountain in South America. I'm excited, but I'm also a bit nervous because it's much higher than anything we've done before."]],"q":[{"q":"When did Emma first climb a real mountain?","o":["when she was twelve","when she was fifteen","when she was nineteen"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"12 yaşında tırmanma duvarı; gerçek dağ 15 yaşında."},{"q":"What does Emma find most difficult about climbing?","o":["being high up","the low temperatures","carrying heavy equipment"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yükseklik korkusu yok; en zor kısım soğuk."},{"q":"Why does Emma prefer to climb with other people?","o":["It is more enjoyable and safer.","Her parents won't let her climb alone.","She is not experienced enough."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"much safer ... sharing the experience is half the fun\""},{"q":"Who is Lisa?","o":["Emma's sister","Emma's teacher","Emma's friend"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","present-perfect"],"e":"\"has been my best friend since school\""},{"q":"According to Emma, what is the most important part of preparation?","o":["running","going to the gym three times a week","planning the trip"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","comparatives-superlatives"],"e":"\"planning is even more important than fitness\""},{"q":"How does Emma feel about her next climb?","o":["only excited","both excited and worried","not sure if she will be able to go"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","will-going-to"],"e":"\"I'm excited, but I'm also a bit nervous\""}]}]},"W":{"min":45,"tasks":[{"id":"b1x-w1","part":1,"t":"Part 1 · E-posta: İngilizce kulübü","in":"Read this email from your English teacher, Mr Clark, and the notes you have made. Write your email to Mr Clark using all the notes. Write about 100 words.","mail":"From: Mr Clark\nSubject: English club\n\nDear students,\nWe are starting an English club after school this term. [Great idea!]\nWe can meet on Tuesdays or Fridays. Which day is better for you? [Say which and why]\nWhat activities would you like to do in the club? [Suggest ...]\nCould you also help us to tell other students about the club? [Yes, I can ...]\nBest wishes,\nMr Clark","pts":[["Fikri beğendiğini söyle",["great","good idea","love","excited","fantastic","brilliant","sounds"]],["Gün seç ve nedenini söyle",["tuesday","friday"]],["Etkinlik öner",["film","game","play","song","quiz","drama","speak","talk","music","book","debate"]],["Diğer öğrencilere nasıl haber vereceğini söyle",["poster","tell","social media","message","website","class","friends","announce"]]],"min":80,"max":130,"model":"Dear Mr Clark,\nThank you for your email. I think an English club is a great idea, and I would love to join it.\nFriday would be better for me, because I have basketball training on Tuesdays after school. Also, on Fridays we don't have much homework, so everyone will be more relaxed.\nIn the club, we could watch short English films and talk about them. It would also be fun to play quiz games and sing songs together.\nOf course I can help. I could make some posters for the school corridors and send a message to my classmates.\nBest wishes,\nDeniz","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Dear Mr Clark,\nThanks for letting us know about the new English club – I think it's a fantastic idea and I'd definitely like to be part of it.\nTuesday would suit me better. On Fridays I usually visit my grandparents straight after school, so I wouldn't be able to come.\nAs for activities, I'd love to do some drama, for example acting out short scenes in English. We could also have a book club where we read easy novels and talk about them.\nI'd be happy to help. I could write a short article for the school website and talk to the students in my class about it.\nKind regards,\nEfe"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Dear Mr Clark,\nThank you for your email. I think an English club is a brilliant idea, and I'm sure lots of students will be interested.\nPersonally, Friday would work best for me. Not only do I have football practice on Tuesdays, but by Friday everyone is more relaxed and in the mood for something fun.\nIn terms of activities, it would be great to organise debates on topics that matter to teenagers, as they would help us practise speaking. Watching music videos and discussing the lyrics might also be popular.\nOf course I'll help spread the word. I could design a poster for the noticeboards and share it on our class group chat, which almost everyone reads.\nBest wishes,\nAslı"]]},{"choice":[{"id":"b1x-w2","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"Part 2 · Makale: Unutulmaz bir gün","in":"You see this announcement in an English-language magazine. Write your article in about 100 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED!\nA day I will never forget\nTell us about a special day in your life. What happened? Who were you with? Why will you always remember it?\nThe best articles will be published next month.","pts":[["Ne olduğunu anlat",["was","were","went","had","happened","saw"]],["Kiminle olduğunu söyle",["with","friend","family","mother","father","sister","brother","cousin","grandmother","grandfather"]],["Neden unutamayacağını açıkla",["because","never forget","remember","special","reason","so"]],["Bir başlık kullan",["@title"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"My first concert\nThe day I will never forget is the day I saw my favourite band live. It was my fifteenth birthday, and my older sister had bought tickets as a surprise.\nWe travelled to Istanbul by train and arrived at the stadium two hours early. When the band finally came on stage, everyone started singing and dancing. I knew every word of every song!\nI will always remember that day because it was the first time I had been to a concert, and because my sister had planned everything for me. It made me feel really special.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","The day I met my baby sister\nI will never forget the fourteenth of March, three years ago. That morning, my dad woke me up early and told me that my mum was in hospital. I was so excited that I couldn't eat my breakfast!\nMy dad and I drove to the hospital together, and when we arrived, my mum was holding a tiny baby wrapped in a pink blanket. It was my sister, Ela.\nI'll always remember that day because my life changed completely. I wasn't an only child any more, and now Ela is my best friend – even when she takes my things without asking!"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Lost in the mountains – and found\nThe day I will never forget began like any other school trip. Our class had gone hiking in the mountains, and my best friend and I stopped to take photos of a waterfall. By the time we looked up, the rest of the group had disappeared.\nAt first we laughed, but as the sky grew darker and our phones had no signal, we started to panic. Eventually, we decided to follow the river downhill, and after two long hours we finally saw the lights of a village.\nI will always remember that day, not because it was frightening, but because I discovered I could stay calm in a difficult situation – and because I have never been so happy to see my teacher!"]]},{"id":"b1x-w3","part":2,"kind":"story","t":"Part 2 · Hikâye: Yanlış otobüs","in":"Your English teacher has asked you to write a story. Your story must begin with this sentence. Write your story in about 100 words.","mail":"Your story must begin with this sentence:\nAfter ten minutes on the bus, Maya realised she was going the wrong way.","pts":[["Verilen cümleyle başla",["after ten minutes on the bus"]],["Geçmiş zamanla olayları anlat",["was","were","got","looked","asked","decided","saw"]],["Bir sorun ve çözüm olsun",["but","so","then","luckily","suddenly"]],["Hikâyeyi bitir (sonuç)",["in the end","finally","at last","happy","relieved","laughed","never"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"After ten minutes on the bus, Maya realised she was going the wrong way. She was supposed to be at her friend's birthday party at six, and it was already half past five.\nShe got off at the next stop and looked around. She didn't know the area at all, and her phone battery was almost empty. Luckily, she saw an old man waiting at the bus stop and asked him for help.\n\"Take the number 12,\" he said. \"It goes straight to the city centre.\"\nIn the end, Maya arrived at the party only fifteen minutes late, and she had a funny story to tell everyone.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","After ten minutes on the bus, Maya realised she was going the wrong way. Instead of the city centre, the bus was heading out of town towards the airport.\nShe was on her way to a job interview at ten o'clock, and it was already twenty past nine. She felt her heart beating faster. Then she noticed that the woman sitting next to her was wearing a uniform from the same company.\n\"Excuse me,\" said Maya, \"are you going to Green Office too?\"\nThe woman smiled. \"Yes, our new office is near the airport now.\" It turned out that Maya had been looking at the old address. She arrived early and got the job."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","After ten minutes on the bus, Maya realised she was going the wrong way. The view outside the window, which should have been full of shops and offices, had turned into fields and farms.\nShe was supposed to be meeting her cousin, who was arriving from Germany, at the train station in half an hour. Panicking, she pressed the bell and jumped off at the next stop, only to find that the next bus back would not come for forty minutes.\nJust as she was about to give up, a farmer driving a small truck stopped and offered her a lift. Although she was nervous at first, he turned out to be very friendly and even told her funny stories about his cows.\nIn the end, Maya arrived at the station just as her cousin was walking out, and the two of them laughed about her adventure all the way home."]]}]}]},"S":{"parts":[{"id":"b1x-s1","p":1,"t":"Part 1 · Tanışma","ins":"Sınav görevlisi seni tanımak için sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["What's your name? Where do you live?",20,["my name is","I live in","I come from"],["My name is Deniz Yılmaz, and I live in Izmir, on the west coast of Turkey. I live with my parents in a flat near the sea. I've lived there all my life.","I'm Kaan. I come from Ankara, the capital, but last year my family moved to Antalya because of my father's job. I really like it here because of the weather."]],["Do you work or are you a student?",20,["I'm a student","I study","I work as"],["I'm a student. I'm in my last year at high school, and I'm preparing for the university exam. My favourite subject is biology, because I want to be a doctor.","I work in a bookshop in the city centre. I've been there for about a year. It isn't very well paid, but I love books, and I enjoy talking to the customers."]],["What do you usually do at the weekend?",30,["usually","often","at the weekend","I like"],["I usually meet my friends on Saturday, and we go to a café or play football in the park. On Sundays I often stay at home and relax with my family. Sometimes we have a big breakfast together, which can take two hours!","At the weekend, I like doing something active. In summer I go swimming or cycling along the coast, and in winter I go to the gym. On Saturday evenings I usually watch a film with my brother."]],["Tell us about something you did last week.",30,["last week","I went","it was"],["Last week I went to the cinema with my cousin. We watched a science fiction film about a group of astronauts. It was a bit long, but the special effects were amazing, and afterwards we had dinner at a new burger restaurant.","Last week was my best friend's birthday, so we organised a surprise party for her. We decorated her living room with balloons while she was at work. When she came home, she was so surprised that she started crying!"]],["What would you like to do in the future?",30,["I'd like to","I hope","I want to","because"],["I'd like to study medicine at university, because I want to help people. After that, I hope to work in a big hospital. I'd also love to travel to Canada one day, because my uncle lives there.","In the future, I'd like to open my own café. I love cooking and baking, and I think it would be great to have a place where people can relax. First, though, I need to save some money and learn about business."]]]},{"id":"b1x-s2","p":2,"t":"Part 2 · Fotoğrafı anlat","ins":"Bu bölümde bir fotoğrafı yaklaşık 1 dakika anlatırsın: kimler var, ne yapıyorlar, nerede, neler görüyorsun, nasıl hissediyor olabilirler.","img":[["👨‍🍳👩‍🍳🧑‍🍳🍕🥗🔥","Bir mutfakta üç genç birlikte yemek yapıyor: biri pizza hamuru açıyor, biri salata hazırlıyor, arkada fırın yanıyor. Herkes gülümsüyor.","b1-cooking","Three young people are cooking together in a kitchen: one is making a pizza, one is preparing a salad and one is checking the oven."]],"q":[["Now, here is your photograph. It shows people cooking together. Please tell us what you can see in the photograph.",60,["in this photo I can see","on the left","on the right","in the background","they are","it looks like","they seem"],["In this photo I can see three young people who are cooking together in a kitchen. On the left, a boy is making a pizza – he's putting tomato sauce on the dough, and he's wearing a white apron. In the middle, a girl is preparing a salad. She's cutting some vegetables, like tomatoes and cucumbers. On the right, another girl is looking at the oven, which is in the background. It looks very hot. There are lots of vegetables and bowls on the table. They all seem very happy – they're smiling and laughing. I think they're friends, and maybe they're preparing dinner for a party or a special occasion. It looks like a modern kitchen, and it's quite bright, so it's probably daytime.","This photo shows a kitchen with three young people in it. They're making food together, and it looks like they're having a great time. The boy on the left is working with some pizza dough. He's got flour on his hands and on his face, which is quite funny! The girl next to him is making a salad in a big bowl. The third person is checking the oven at the back of the kitchen. Maybe they're students and they're learning to cook in a cooking class, because they're all wearing aprons. Or perhaps they're flatmates making lunch. I think they feel relaxed and happy, because cooking with friends is fun."]]]},{"id":"b1x-s3","p":3,"t":"Part 3 · Birlikte karar verme","ins":"Gerçek sınavda bu bölüm bir partnerle yapılır. Burada iki tarafı da sen düşün: önce seçenekleri tek tek değerlendir, sonra en iyisine karar ver.","opts":["🎨 sanat atölyesi","⚽ spor turnuvası","🎬 film gecesi","🌳 park temizliği","🎤 müzik yarışması"],"q":[["A school wants to organise an activity to help students make new friends. Here are some ideas they're thinking about. Talk about the different activities and say how they would help students make friends.",90,["what about","I think","because","it would be","on the other hand","that's a good idea","I'm not sure"],["Let's start with the art workshop. I think it would be good for quiet students, because they can be creative without speaking too much. But people mostly work alone, so they might not talk very much. A sports tournament is fun, and students have to work in teams, so they would get to know each other quickly. However, some people don't like sport, and they wouldn't take part. A film night is relaxing and cheap, but you can't talk during the film, so it's not great for making friends. Cleaning the park is a good idea because students work together and do something useful at the same time. They'd have lots of time to chat. A music competition could be exciting, but some students might feel nervous on stage, and only a few people would actually perform.","I'll start with the music competition. It would be exciting and lots of students would come to watch, but the people performing might not have time to meet others. The film night is a nice idea, especially if there's popcorn and time to talk afterwards. The art workshop could work well if students make something together, like a big painting for the school. The sports tournament is probably the most popular idea, because many teenagers love football or basketball, and being in a team is a great way to make friends. Finally, cleaning the park is useful for the environment, but I'm not sure students would want to spend their free time picking up rubbish."]],["Now decide which activity would be best for making new friends.",30,["I think the best","because","I'd choose","in my opinion"],["In my opinion, the best activity would be the park clean-up, because everyone can join, students work together in small groups, and they have plenty of time to talk to each other. They also feel proud because they're doing something good for their town.","I think the sports tournament would be best. Teams have to communicate and help each other, and when you win or lose together, you feel closer to your teammates. Students who don't play can still come and support their friends."]]]},{"id":"b1x-s4","p":4,"t":"Part 4 · Tartışma","ins":"Sınav görevlisi 3. bölümün konusuyla ilgili görüşünü soruyor. Her soruya fikrini ve nedenini söyle.","q":[["Is it easy or difficult to make new friends at a new school? Why?",40,["I think","because","for example","it depends"],["I think it can be quite difficult at first, because everyone already has their own group of friends, and new students often feel shy. But if you join a club or a sports team, it becomes much easier, because you meet people who like the same things as you. Teachers can also help by asking students to work in pairs or groups.","It depends on the person. If you're confident and friendly, it's quite easy, because people are usually curious about new students. But shy people may find it hard to start conversations. When I changed schools, a girl in my class invited me to sit with her at lunch, and that helped me a lot."]],["Do you prefer doing activities in a big group or in a small group?",40,["I prefer","because","on the other hand","in a small group"],["I prefer small groups, because everyone can speak and you get to know people better. In a big group it's more exciting, but quiet people often don't say anything, and it can be difficult to make decisions. For example, when we plan a trip with ten people, it takes hours to agree on anything!","I prefer big groups, because there's more energy and you meet more people. For activities like football or parties, you need lots of people. In a small group, if someone is in a bad mood, it affects everyone. In a big group, there's always someone you can talk to, and you often meet friends of friends."]],["Do you think young people spend too much time online instead of meeting friends?",40,["I agree","I don't think","however","for example","these days"],["I partly agree. These days many young people chat online for hours, and that isn't the same as meeting face to face. You can't really see how someone feels in a message. However, online chats can also help you stay in touch with friends who live far away, so I don't think the internet is always bad.","Yes, I think so. Many teenagers sit in the same room and look at their phones instead of talking to each other. I think they should spend more time outside, doing sport or meeting friends in the park. Parents should also set a good example, because they use their phones a lot too."]]]}]}},"lv":"B1","name":"B1 Preliminary"},{"id":"b2-1","t":"Sınav 1","sec":{"R":{"min":75,"parts":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"The rise of urban gardening","p":"In many cities around the world, people are (1) ______ empty roofs and car parks into vegetable gardens. Urban gardening has become so popular that some cities now (2) ______ residents with free seeds and tools. Supporters argue that growing your own food not only saves money but also (3) ______ people closer to nature. It can also (4) ______ a real difference to local communities, as neighbours who had never spoken before often end up working side by side. However, city gardeners have to (5) ______ with problems that farmers in the countryside rarely face, such as polluted soil and a (6) ______ of space. Some projects have found creative solutions, growing plants in old boxes or even on the walls of buildings. Whether urban gardens can (7) ______ produce enough food to feed a city is still open to (8) ______, but few would deny that they make cities greener.","o":[[["putting","turning","becoming","getting"],1,["collocation"]],[["give","offer","provide","hand"],2,["collocation"]],[["brings","takes","leads","carries"],0,["collocation"]],[["do","have","take","make"],3,["collocation"]],[["cope","handle","manage","face"],0,["vocab"]],[["need","lack","loss","want"],1,["vocab"]],[["ever","once","yet","already"],0,["vocab"]],[["question","discussion","debate","argument"],2,["collocation"]]],"e":["turn something into something: bir şeyi başka bir şeye dönüştürmek. put, become ve get bu kalıba uymaz.","provide someone with something: birine bir şey sağlamak. give/offer/hand \"with\" almaz.","bring someone closer to: birini bir şeye yaklaştırmak.","make a difference: fark yaratmak.","cope with: başa çıkmak. handle/manage \"with\" almaz; face doğrudan nesne alır.","a lack of: eksikliği.","ever: hiç, bir gün (olasılığı vurgular).","open to debate: tartışmaya açık."]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 2","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","one":true,"title":"The man who walked around the world","p":"In 1998, Karl Bushby set (1) ______ from the southern tip of South America with an ambitious plan: to walk all the way back to his home in England. Since (2) ______, he has crossed deserts, jungles and even a frozen sea. Unlike most travellers, Bushby made a rule that he would never use any form of transport (3) ______ than his own feet. This meant that when he reached the Bering Strait, he had (4) ______ walk across the ice to Russia. The journey has taken much longer (5) ______ he expected. At one point he was stuck for years, unable to get the visas he needed. Yet (6) ______ the many difficulties, he has refused to give up. When asked why, he simply says that he has come too far to stop now. Few people would have the patience to do (7) ______ he has done, and his story has inspired thousands (8) ______ people.","a":[["off","out"],["then"],["other"],["to"],["than"],["despite"],["what"],["of"]],"tg":[["vocab"],["present-perfect"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["must-have-to-should"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["relative-clauses"],["vocab"]],"e":["set off / set out: yola çıkmak.","Since then: o zamandan beri.","other than: -dan başka.","had to: zorunda kaldı.","longer than: -den daha uzun.","despite + isim: -e rağmen (although değil, arkasında cümle yok).","what = the thing(s) that.","thousands of people."]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Kelime türetme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Metni oku. Her boşluk için verilen kök kelimeyi uygun biçime getir (isim, sıfat, zarf, olumsuz vb.).","ty":"open","title":"Why we should all take a nap","st":["SCIENCE","EFFECT","SLEEP","IMPROVE","ADVISE","PRODUCE","LIKELY","RESPONSE"],"p":"For many years, sleeping during the day was seen as a sign of laziness. Now, however, (1) ______ research suggests that a short afternoon nap can have a surprisingly positive (2) ______ on our health. People who feel (3) ______ after lunch are not necessarily tired – it is simply the way our bodies work. Studies show that a twenty-minute nap can lead to a significant (4) ______ in memory and concentration. Experts' (5) ______ is to keep naps short, as longer ones can leave you feeling worse. Some companies have even introduced nap rooms, believing that rested employees are more (6) ______. Those who nap regularly are also less (7) ______ to make mistakes. Perhaps it is time we all took more (8) ______ for our own rest.","a":[["scientific"],["effect"],["sleepy"],["improvement"],["advice"],["productive"],["likely"],["responsibility"]],"tg":[["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"]],"e":["İsimden önce sıfat: scientific research.","have an effect on: üzerinde etkisi olmak (isim).","feel + sıfat: sleepy (uykulu).","a significant + isim: improvement (gelişme).","Experts' + isim: advice (tavsiye; sayılamaz).","more + sıfat: productive (verimli).","be likely to: -mesi muhtemel olmak. \"less likely\" anlamı zaten olumsuzlaştırıyor; unlikely olmaz.","take responsibility for: sorumluluk almak."]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 2–5 kelime yaz.","ty":"kwt","q":[{"s1":"\"I'm sorry I broke your phone,\" Tom said to his sister.","key":"APOLOGISED","s2":"Tom ______ his sister's phone.","a":["apologised for breaking","apologised for having broken"],"tg":["reporting-verbs"],"e":"apologise for + -ing."},{"s1":"It isn't necessary for you to bring any food.","key":"HAVE","s2":"You ______ any food.","a":["do not have to bring","don't have to bring"],"tg":["must-have-to-should"],"e":"don't have to: gerek yok."},{"s1":"I'd prefer not to go out tonight.","key":"RATHER","s2":"I ______ out tonight.","a":["would rather not go","'d rather not go"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"would rather + yalın fiil; olumsuz: would rather not go."},{"s1":"They will announce the results tomorrow.","key":"BE","s2":"The results ______ tomorrow.","a":["will be announced"],"tg":["passive-voice"],"e":"Gelecek zaman edilgen: will be + V3."},{"s1":"I didn't study enough, so I failed the exam.","key":"HAD","s2":"If I ______ more, I wouldn't have failed the exam.","a":["had studied"],"tg":["third-conditional"],"e":"Third conditional: If + had + V3."},{"s1":"It's possible that Sarah forgot about the meeting.","key":"MIGHT","s2":"Sarah ______ about the meeting.","a":["might have forgotten"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"Geçmişe dair olasılık: might have + V3."}]},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"Learning to fail","p":["When I was seventeen, I was convinced that I would become a professional tennis player. I trained every morning before school and every evening after it, and my coach told anyone who would listen that I had a real future in the sport. So when I lost in the first round of the national junior championships, I was devastated. I remember sitting in the changing room, unable to move, feeling that years of work had been wasted in a single afternoon.","My father, who had never been particularly interested in sport, found me there. I expected him to tell me that I would do better next time, which is what everyone else had said. Instead, he asked me what I had learned. I was annoyed at the question – it seemed to me that the only lesson was that I wasn't good enough. But he waited, and eventually I admitted that I had been so nervous that I had stopped playing my natural game.","That conversation changed the way I approached not just tennis, but everything. I started to see each defeat as information rather than a judgement. I kept a notebook in which I wrote down, after every match, one thing that had gone wrong and one thing I could do differently. Some of the entries look rather foolish to me now, but the habit of looking honestly at my mistakes has stayed with me.","I never did become a professional. A shoulder injury at nineteen put an end to that dream, and for a while I found it hard to imagine a life without competition. Today I work as a coach myself, and I often tell my students about that afternoon in the changing room. Most of them, I suspect, think I am simply trying to make them feel better about losing. A few, though, start keeping notebooks of their own."],"q":[{"q":"How did the writer feel after losing in the championships?","o":["angry with his coach","determined to train harder","shocked and hopeless","relieved that it was over"],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"devastated ... unable to move ... years of work had been wasted\": yıkılmış ve umutsuz."},{"q":"What surprised the writer about his father's reaction?","o":["He seemed more upset about the result than the writer was.","He did not try to comfort him in the usual way.","He criticised the way the writer had played.","He had watched the whole match."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Herkes \"bir dahakine daha iyi olur\" derken babası ne öğrendiğini sordu."},{"q":"Why was the writer annoyed by his father's question?","o":["He thought the answer was obvious.","He did not want to talk about tennis.","He felt his father did not understand the sport.","He wanted to be left alone."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"the only lesson was that I wasn't good enough\": cevabın belli olduğunu düşündü."},{"q":"What does the writer say about his notebook?","o":["He still writes in it today.","His coach asked him to keep it.","Some of what he wrote now seems silly.","It helped him win the next championship."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Some of the entries look rather foolish to me now\"."},{"q":"Why did the writer stop playing tennis seriously?","o":["He lost interest in competition.","He was injured.","He decided to become a coach.","His father disapproved."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","past-simple"],"e":"19 yaşında omuz sakatlığı."},{"q":"What does the writer suggest about his students in the last paragraph?","o":["Most of them are better players than he was.","They all find his story helpful.","Only some of them take his advice seriously.","They are afraid of losing."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Çoğu onu sadece teselli ediyor sanıyor; birkaçı defter tutmaya başlıyor."}]},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 6","ins":"Metinden altı cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–G) seç. Bir cümle fazladır.","ty":"gap","title":"The secret life of trees","p":"For a long time, scientists believed that trees in a forest competed with each other for light, water and space. Recent research, however, paints a very different picture. (1) ______\nThe key to this network lies underground. Tree roots are connected by fungi which spread through the soil in thin threads. (2) ______ In return, the fungi receive sugars that the trees produce through photosynthesis.\nThis exchange is not just between a tree and its fungi. (3) ______ Researchers have found that older trees, sometimes called \"mother trees\", send food to young trees growing in their shade.\nEven more remarkably, trees appear to warn each other of danger. (4) ______ Nearby trees that receive these signals then start producing chemicals that make their leaves taste unpleasant to insects.\nThese discoveries have important consequences for the way we manage forests. (5) ______ Removing the largest trees, as many logging companies do, may damage the whole network.\nNot all scientists agree on how these findings should be interpreted. (6) ______ Nevertheless, the idea that a forest is a community rather than a collection of individuals is beginning to change the way we think about nature.","opts":[["A","When a tree is attacked by insects, it can send chemical signals through the fungal network."],["B","It suggests that trees actually cooperate, sharing resources through a hidden network."],["C","These threads carry water and nutrients from the soil to the trees."],["D","Some argue that words like \"communication\" make trees sound more human than they are."],["E","Through the same network, trees can pass nutrients to one another."],["F","If trees depend on each other, cutting down the oldest ones could harm the rest of the forest."],["G","Most forests in Europe were planted by humans in the last two hundred years."]],"a":["B","C","E","A","F","D"],"tg":[["coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"],["coherence"]],"e":["\"very different picture\" → işbirliği fikri; sonraki paragraf \"this network\" diyor: B.","\"thin threads\" → \"These threads carry...\". \"In return\" ile mantar karşılık alıyor: C.","\"not just between a tree and its fungi\" → ağaçlar birbirine de besin veriyor: E.","\"warn each other of danger\" → \"these signals\": A.","\"consequences for the way we manage forests\" → en yaşlı ağaçları kesmek: F.","\"Not all scientists agree\" → bazıları \"iletişim\" kelimesine itiraz ediyor: D. G konuyla ilgisiz."]},{"k":"R7","nm":"Okuma 7 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 7","ins":"Dört metni (A–D) oku. Her soru için doğru metni seç. Metinler birden fazla kez seçilebilir.","ty":"match","title":"Four people talk about learning a musical instrument as an adult","opts":[["A","Helen, 45 – cello","I had wanted to play the cello since I was a child, but my parents couldn't afford lessons. When my own children left home, I finally decided it was my turn. The first year was frustrating: my fingers simply wouldn't do what I wanted, and I sounded terrible. What kept me going was my teacher, who never once made me feel stupid. Now I play in a small amateur orchestra, and the feeling of being part of something bigger is wonderful. I only wish I hadn't waited so long."],["B","Raj, 38 – piano","I'm an engineer, and I approached the piano like a technical problem. I bought an app, set myself targets and practised for exactly thirty minutes every day. It worked surprisingly well at first. But after about six months I hit a wall – I could play the notes, but it didn't sound like music. Taking lessons with a real teacher made me realise that I had been so focused on accuracy that I had forgotten to listen. I'm slower now, but I enjoy it far more."],["C","Maria, 52 – guitar","My son plays the guitar, and I started partly so that we would have something to share. It's been a lovely way to spend time together, although he's a rather impatient teacher! I've never been very disciplined about practising, and I don't mind admitting that I'm not particularly good. But I don't play to impress anyone. It helps me relax after work, and that's enough for me."],["D","Tom, 61 – saxophone","After I retired, I needed something to fill my days, and a friend suggested joining a jazz class. I was nervous about playing in front of others – I'd never done anything like that. In fact, the group turned out to be the best thing about it. We're all beginners, so nobody judges anyone. Learning has been harder than I expected, especially reading music, but I've met people I would never otherwise have known."]],"q":[{"q":"Who mentions that their teacher's attitude helped them continue?","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Helen: öğretmeni onu hiç aptal hissettirmedi."},{"q":"Who says that they do not practise regularly?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Maria: \"never been very disciplined about practising\"."},{"q":"Who found that their progress suddenly stopped?","a":"B","tg":["inference"],"e":"Raj: \"after about six months I hit a wall\"."},{"q":"Who regrets not starting earlier?","a":"A","tg":["wish-if-only"],"e":"Helen: \"I only wish I hadn't waited so long\"."},{"q":"Who was worried about performing with other people?","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"Tom: başkalarının önünde çalmaktan çekiniyordu."},{"q":"Who began learning to share an interest with a family member?","a":"C","tg":["purpose"],"e":"Maria: oğluyla ortak bir şeyleri olsun diye."},{"q":"Who used technology to plan their learning?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Raj bir uygulama aldı, hedefler koydu."},{"q":"Who says that making new friends has been the main benefit?","a":"D","tg":["attitude"],"e":"Tom: grup en iyi şeyi oldu; başka türlü tanımayacağı insanlarla tanıştı."},{"q":"Who admits that they are not very skilled?","a":"C","tg":["attitude"],"e":"Maria: \"I'm not particularly good\"."},{"q":"Who realised that they had been concentrating on the wrong thing?","a":"B","tg":["inference"],"e":"Raj: doğruluğa o kadar odaklanmış ki dinlemeyi unutmuş."}]}]},"L":{"min":40,"parts":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Sekiz farklı durumda kısa kayıtlar dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1a","sc":[["F","I've been to that café three times now and I still can't decide. The coffee's fine, nothing special. The cakes are lovely, though a bit pricey. What really gets me is that you can never find a seat – it's always packed with students working on laptops for hours."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking about a café. What is her main complaint?","o":["The coffee is poor.","It is too expensive.","It is too crowded."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"What really gets me ...\" asıl şikâyeti gösterir: hiç yer bulunamıyor."},{"id":"l1b","sc":[["M","So how was the interview?"],["F","Well, they asked all the questions I'd prepared for, which was a relief. But then they asked me to give a presentation I didn't know about. I think I managed, but I could have done it much better with some warning."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking about a job interview. How does she feel about it?","o":["confident that the interview went better than expected","disappointed with part of her performance","annoyed by the questions she was asked"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","modals-deduction"],"e":"\"I could have done it much better\" – sunum kısmından memnun değil."},{"id":"l1c","sc":[["M","The documentary was supposed to be about climate change, but honestly, half of it was the presenter talking about his own travels. There were some amazing shots of the Arctic, I'll give it that. But I learned almost nothing new."]],"q":"You hear a man talking about a documentary. What is his opinion?","o":["It was too focused on the presenter.","The photography was less impressive than expected.","It was too long."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Yarısı sunucunun kendi gezileriydi; görüntüleri beğendi."},{"id":"l1d","sc":[["F","Hi Josh, it's Emma. About Saturday – I know we said we'd meet at the station, but my brother can give us a lift, so we can pick you up from your place at ten instead. Let me know if that works."]],"q":"You hear a woman leaving a message. Why is she calling?","o":["to cancel their plans for Saturday","to change an arrangement","to ask for a lift"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"İstasyon yerine evden alacaklar: plan değişikliği."},{"id":"l1e","sc":[["M","People think being a mountain guide is all about climbing. Actually, most of my job is checking weather reports and making decisions about whether it's safe to go. The hardest part is telling a group who have paid a lot of money that we're turning back."]],"q":"You hear a mountain guide talking about his job. What does he find most difficult?","o":["understanding weather reports","disappointing his clients","climbing in bad conditions"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"The hardest part is telling a group ... we're turning back\"."},{"id":"l1f","sc":[["F","Did you finish that novel?"],["M","I did, eventually. The first half really dragged, to be honest. I nearly gave up. But the ending was so clever that I immediately wanted to read it again."],["F","That's quite a recommendation."]],"q":"You hear two friends talking about a novel. What does the man say about it?","o":["It started slowly but was worth finishing.","It was much better than the film version of it.","The ending was disappointing."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"İlk yarı sıkıcıydı ama sonu çok zekiceydi."},{"id":"l1g","sc":[["M","Right, everyone, before we start. The lab on the second floor is being repaired, so today's experiment will be in room fourteen. Please leave your bags outside and don't touch any equipment until I say so."]],"q":"You hear a teacher talking to students. What is he doing?","o":["explaining a change of room","describing an experiment","apologising for being late"],"a":0,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Laboratuvar tamirde; deney 14 numaralı odada."},{"id":"l1h","sc":[["F","When I started the business, I thought the difficult part would be finding customers. In fact, they came quite quickly. What I hadn't expected was how hard it would be to find reliable staff. I must have interviewed fifty people before I found the right team."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking about starting a business. What surprised her?","o":["how quickly she managed to find customers","the difficulty of hiring good employees","the amount of money she needed"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","past-perfect"],"e":"\"What I hadn't expected was how hard it would be to find reliable staff\"."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Cümle tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Cümleleri bir kelime ya da kısa bir ifadeyle tamamla.","ty":"open","id":"l2","title":"Cümleler","sc":[["F","Hi everyone. I'm Lena, and I spent last summer working as a volunteer at a sea turtle project in Costa Rica. I want to tell you a bit about it in case any of you are thinking of doing something similar. I'd always loved animals, but what made me apply was a documentary I watched about turtles losing their nesting beaches. The project is on a remote beach, and the nearest town is two hours away by boat, so you really are cut off. We lived in simple wooden huts, and there was electricity for only three hours each evening. Our main job was patrolling the beach at night, looking for female turtles coming ashore to lay their eggs. We had to move the eggs to a protected area called a hatchery, because otherwise they'd be taken by people or eaten by dogs. The patrols started at nine p.m. and could last until four in the morning, which was exhausting. People often ask what the most difficult thing was. Surprisingly, it wasn't the long nights – it was the insects! I'd recommend bringing lots of strong repellent. The best moment for me was watching about a hundred baby turtles reach the sea for the first time. If you're interested, the project accepts volunteers for a minimum of four weeks, and you don't need any experience, just a lot of patience."]],"p":"Lena decided to volunteer after seeing a (1) ______ about turtles.\nThe nearest town can only be reached by (2) ______.\nThe volunteers had electricity for (3) ______ hours a day.\nAt night, volunteers looked for turtles on the (4) ______.\nThe eggs were moved to a place called the (5) ______.\nWithout protection, the eggs might be eaten by (6) ______.\nNight patrols sometimes finished at (7) ______ a.m.\nLena found the (8) ______ the hardest thing to deal with.\nHer favourite moment was seeing young turtles reach the (9) ______.\nVolunteers must stay for at least (10) ______ weeks.","a":[["documentary"],["boat"],["3","three"],["beach"],["hatchery"],["dogs"],["4","four","4.00"],["insects"],["sea"],["4","four"]],"tg":[["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"]],"e":["\"a documentary I watched about turtles\".","\"two hours away by boat\".","\"electricity for only three hours each evening\".","\"patrolling the beach at night\".","\"a protected area called a hatchery\".","\"taken by people or eaten by dogs\" – boşluk \"eaten by\"dan sonra: dogs.","\"could last until four in the morning\".","\"it wasn't the long nights – it was the insects\".","\"reach the sea for the first time\".","\"a minimum of four weeks\"."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Beş kişinin konuşmasını dinleyeceksin. Her konuşmacı için doğru seçeneği (A–H) seç. Üç seçenek fazladır.","ty":"match","id":"l3","sc":[["M","Speaker one. I'd always assumed I'd go to university straight after school, like my friends. But I wasn't sure what to study, so I took a year off and worked in a hotel. It was the best decision I ever made. Dealing with difficult guests taught me more about people than any course could have."],["F","#1.2"],["F","Speaker two. For me, the turning point was a teacher who believed in me. I'd always been told I was bad at maths, and I'd started to believe it. She showed me that I'd simply been taught in the wrong way. I'm now in my second year of an engineering degree, which my younger self would never have believed."],["M","#1.2"],["M2","Speaker three. I moved abroad when I was twenty, without speaking a word of the language. The first few months were really lonely. But being forced to manage on my own made me far more confident. These days I'm not afraid of anything new, whether it's a job or a hobby."],["F","#1.2"],["F2","Speaker four. I failed my driving test four times. Each time I was more nervous than the last. Eventually my instructor told me to stop thinking about the test and just enjoy driving. It sounds silly, but it worked. And I learned that worrying about failure can actually make you fail."],["M","#1.2"],["M","Speaker five. When my grandfather got ill, I spent a lot of time looking after him. We talked for hours, and I realised how little I'd known about his life. It made me want to record older people's stories, and that's what I do now – I make podcasts about people's memories."]],"opts":[["A","It made me less frightened of new experiences."],["B","It showed me that someone else's opinion can change my view of myself."],["C","It gave me an idea for my career."],["D","It taught me to deal with people."],["E","It made me realise I should have studied harder."],["F","It helped me become closer to my friends."],["G","It showed me that anxiety can make things worse."],["H","It made me decide to move abroad."]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"D","tg":["gist"],"e":"Otelde zor misafirlerle uğraşmak ona insanları öğretti."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"B","tg":["gist"],"e":"Ona inanan bir öğretmen kendine bakışını değiştirdi."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"A","tg":["gist"],"e":"\"I'm not afraid of anything new\"."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"G","tg":["gist"],"e":"\"worrying about failure can actually make you fail\"."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"C","tg":["gist"],"e":"Büyükbabasıyla konuşmaları onu anı podcast'leri yapmaya yöneltti."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Bir röportaj dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","id":"l4","sc":[["M","My guest today is Sophie Adams, who designs video games. Sophie, how did you get into the industry?"],["F","Almost by accident, really. I studied art at university and I wanted to be an illustrator. But a friend was working on a small game and needed someone to draw the characters. I said yes mainly because I needed the money, and I just fell in love with it."],["M","Many people imagine game designers spend all day playing games. Is that true?"],["F","I wish! Actually, I spend most of my time in meetings, discussing ideas with programmers and writers. The creative part, sketching characters and worlds, is maybe twenty percent of my week. It's the part I love, but games are made by large teams, so communication is everything."],["M","What makes a game successful, in your view?"],["F","People often say graphics, but I disagree. Some of the most popular games have very simple graphics. What matters is whether players care about what happens. If they feel something for the characters, they'll keep playing."],["M","You've spoken publicly about the long working hours in the industry."],["F","Yes. Before a game is released, teams often work late every night for months. Some people see that as part of the job, but I think it's a sign of poor planning. On my current project, we've set clear deadlines from the start, and so far it's working."],["M","What advice would you give to young people who want to do your job?"],["F","Make things. Don't wait for a degree or permission. There are free tools online, and even a very simple game you've finished will teach you more than a perfect one you never complete. And play games critically – ask yourself why something works or doesn't."],["M","And what's next for you?"],["F","I'd love to make a game for younger children that helps them learn to read. It's a completely different challenge, and that's what excites me."]],"q":[{"q":"Why did Sophie first work on a game?","o":["She had always wanted to design games.","She needed to earn some money.","Her university course required it."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"I said yes mainly because I needed the money\"."},{"q":"What does Sophie say about her working week?","o":["She spends most of it sketching characters and worlds.","She would much prefer to spend less time in meetings.","Working with other people takes up most of her time."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Zamanının çoğu toplantıda; yaratıcı kısım %20."},{"q":"According to Sophie, what makes a game successful?","o":["impressive graphics and realistic worlds","an emotional connection with characters","a simple story that is easy to follow"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"If they feel something for the characters, they'll keep playing\"."},{"q":"What is Sophie's view of long working hours?","o":["They are unavoidable in the industry.","They are the result of bad organisation.","They help teams to finish games more quickly."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I think it's a sign of poor planning\"."},{"q":"What does Sophie advise young people to do?","o":["get a degree in game design","finish small projects of their own","wait until they have the right equipment"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Basit ama bitmiş bir oyun, hiç bitmeyen mükemmel bir oyundan çok şey öğretir."},{"q":"Why does Sophie want to make a game for children?","o":["She wants to try something new and difficult.","Her own children have found learning to read hard.","Games for children sell better."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"a completely different challenge, and that's what excites me\"."},{"q":"How does Sophie feel about her career?","o":["She regrets not becoming an illustrator.","She is enthusiastic about it.","She finds it less creative than she hoped."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I just fell in love with it\" ve yeni projeden heyecanla bahsediyor."}]}]},"W":{"min":80,"tasks":[{"id":"b2x-w1","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Part 1 · Deneme: Okul üniforması","in":"In your English class you have been talking about school rules. Now your English teacher has asked you to write an essay. Write your essay using all the notes and giving reasons for your point of view. Write 140–190 words.","mail":"Should all students wear a school uniform?\n\nNotes – write about:\n1. cost for families\n2. students' freedom to express themselves\n3. ............ (your own idea)","pts":[["Aileler için maliyetten bahset",["cost","expensive","money","cheap","afford","price"]],["Kendini ifade özgürlüğünden bahset",["express","freedom","individual","personality","identity","style"]],["Kendi fikrini ekle",["also","another","in addition","moreover","furthermore","what is more"]],["Bir sonuç / görüş belirt",["in conclusion","to sum up","overall","in my opinion","i believe"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"School uniforms are common in many countries, but there is still a lively debate about whether they are a good idea.\n\nOne argument in favour of uniforms is cost. Families do not have to buy a wide range of fashionable clothes for school, and children are less likely to feel pressure to wear expensive brands. On the other hand, uniforms themselves can be surprisingly expensive, especially if they can only be bought from one shop.\n\nCritics also argue that uniforms limit students' freedom to express their personality. Teenagers are developing their identity, and clothes are an important part of this. However, students can still show their individuality through their hairstyles, bags and, above all, their ideas.\n\nIn addition, uniforms may help create a sense of belonging. When everyone looks the same, it is harder to judge others by their appearance, which can reduce bullying.\n\nIn conclusion, although uniforms are not perfect, I believe their advantages outweigh their disadvantages, provided that schools keep them simple and affordable.","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","The question of whether students should wear uniforms is one that divides parents, teachers and students alike. In my opinion, schools should allow students to choose their own clothes.\n\nSupporters of uniforms often argue that they save families money. However, this is not always true. Uniforms frequently have to be bought from specific shops at high prices, and students still need ordinary clothes for evenings and weekends, so families end up paying twice.\n\nMore importantly, uniforms prevent young people from expressing their personality at an age when they are trying to discover who they are. Choosing what to wear helps teenagers develop confidence and make their own decisions.\n\nIt is also worth considering comfort. Many uniforms are made from cheap materials that are too hot in summer and too cold in winter, which can make it harder for students to concentrate.\n\nIn conclusion, although uniforms may appear to create equality, I believe their disadvantages are greater. A simple dress code that bans inappropriate clothing would be a fairer and more practical solution."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","School uniforms are a familiar sight in many countries, yet whether they actually benefit students remains a matter of considerable debate.\n\nFrom a financial point of view, the picture is mixed. Admittedly, uniforms remove the pressure to keep up with expensive fashion trends, which can be a significant burden for less well-off families. On the other hand, compulsory items sold by a single supplier can be surprisingly costly.\n\nThe argument that uniforms restrict self-expression is perhaps overstated. Students have countless other ways of showing their individuality, from their hairstyles and accessories to the music they listen to and the opinions they hold. Arguably, removing clothing as a source of competition allows them to focus on what they say rather than what they wear.\n\nA further point in favour of uniforms is safety. On school trips, for instance, teachers can identify their students instantly in a crowd.\n\nWeighing up these arguments, I am in favour of school uniforms, provided they are affordable and comfortable. Schools should consult students and parents when designing them, so that the uniform becomes a source of pride rather than resentment."]]},{"choice":[{"id":"b2x-w2","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"Part 2 · Makale: Hayatımı değiştiren bir uygulama","in":"You see this announcement in an English-language magazine. Write your article in 140–190 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED\nThe app that changed my life\nIs there an app that has made a real difference to your daily life? What does it do, and why is it so important to you? Would you recommend it to others?\nWe will publish the best articles next month.","pts":[["Uygulamanın ne yaptığını anlat",["app","helps","allows","lets","use"]],["Neden önemli olduğunu açıkla",["because","since","important","difference","changed"]],["Tavsiye edip etmediğini söyle",["recommend","would suggest","should try","advise"]],["Okuyucuya hitap eden bir başlık",["@title"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"The little green owl that taught me Spanish\n\nTwo years ago, I could barely say \"hello\" in Spanish. Today I can chat with my Argentinian neighbours for hours, and I owe it all to a language app with a cheerful green owl.\n\nThe app gives you short lessons that take just five minutes. You match words, listen to sentences and repeat them out loud. It sounds simple, but the secret is that it reminds you every day. If you forget, the owl sends you a slightly sad message – and believe me, you will not want to disappoint it!\n\nWhat really changed my life, though, was not just learning a language. The app taught me that small daily habits can lead to big results. I now use the same approach for exercise and reading.\n\nWould I recommend it? Absolutely, but with one warning. An app alone will not make you fluent. You also need to speak to real people, make mistakes and laugh about them. Think of the app as a friendly start, not the whole journey.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı bir uygulama","How a map app made me braver\n\nI have always had a terrible sense of direction. For years, I was too nervous to explore new places on my own in case I got lost. Then a map app changed everything.\n\nThe app shows you exactly where you are, gives you directions step by step and even tells you which bus to take and when it will arrive. It also suggests interesting places nearby, such as cafés, museums and parks, which I would never have discovered otherwise.\n\nThanks to it, I have become much more independent. Last summer I travelled alone to a city I had never visited, and I found my way around without any problems. My parents were amazed!\n\nWould I recommend it? Definitely, especially to anyone who feels anxious about travelling. However, I would also advise people to look up from the screen sometimes. Getting a little lost can be part of the adventure, and some of my best memories come from the moments when I ignored the app completely."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","The app that gave me back my evenings\n\nI used to be the kind of person who promised to start exercising \"next Monday\" – every Monday. That all changed when a friend persuaded me to download a simple habit-tracking app.\n\nAt first glance, it does very little. You choose the habits you want to build, such as drinking more water or going for a run, and tick them off each day. What makes it surprisingly powerful is the chain it creates: after a week of ticks, you become strangely reluctant to break it.\n\nWithin two months, I was running three times a week and reading before bed instead of scrolling through social media. The app did not make me more disciplined overnight, but it made my progress visible, and that turned out to be enormously motivating.\n\nI would wholeheartedly recommend it to anyone who struggles to stick to good intentions. One word of warning, though: choose one or two habits to start with. Try to change everything at once, and you may well end up changing nothing at all."]]},{"id":"b2x-w3","part":2,"kind":"review","t":"Part 2 · İnceleme: Bir restoran ya da kafe","in":"You see this announcement on an English-language website. Write your review in 140–190 words.","mail":"REVIEWS WANTED\nA place to eat\nHave you been to a restaurant or café recently that you think other people should know about? Write a review describing the place, the food and the service, and say whether you would recommend it.\nThe best reviews will be posted on our website.","pts":[["Yeri tanıt",["located","situated","café","restaurant","atmosphere","decor"]],["Yemekten bahset",["food","dish","menu","delicious","tasty","ordered"]],["Servisten bahset",["service","staff","waiter","waitress","friendly","slow"]],["Tavsiye et ya da etme",["recommend","worth","would suggest","avoid"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Hidden gem: Café Lavanta\n\nTucked away in a narrow street behind the main square, Café Lavanta is easy to miss – but it is definitely worth looking for.\n\nThe café is small, with only eight tables, and the walls are covered with paintings by local artists. Soft jazz plays in the background, which makes it a perfect place to relax or read a book on a rainy afternoon.\n\nThe menu is short but creative. I ordered the lentil soup, which was rich and full of flavour, followed by a lemon cake that was light and not too sweet. Prices are reasonable, especially considering the quality of the ingredients, most of which come from nearby farms.\n\nThe only disappointment was the service. Although the staff were friendly, there were only two of them, and we waited almost twenty minutes for our drinks.\n\nOverall, I would highly recommend Café Lavanta to anyone looking for a quiet place with excellent home-made food. Just make sure you are not in a hurry!","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı bir yer","Burger Box: big flavours, small space\n\nIf you are looking for a quick, tasty meal in the city centre, Burger Box on Station Road is well worth a visit.\n\nThe restaurant is tiny, with just a few high tables and a long counter facing the open kitchen, so you can watch the chefs at work. The atmosphere is lively and the music is loud, which makes it popular with students but perhaps less suitable for a quiet conversation.\n\nThe menu focuses on burgers, and they are excellent. I tried the classic cheeseburger, which was juicy and served in a soft, freshly baked bun. The sweet potato fries were crispy and full of flavour. There are also two vegetarian options, which my friend said were just as good.\n\nThe service was fast and friendly, and our food arrived in less than ten minutes, even though the place was full.\n\nMy only complaint is the price of drinks, which seemed rather high compared with the food. Overall, though, I would definitely recommend Burger Box for a casual lunch with friends."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","A taste of the sea: Balıkçı Ahmet\n\nPerched at the end of a small fishing harbour, Balıkçı Ahmet looks, at first glance, like little more than a wooden hut. Do not be fooled. This modest family-run restaurant serves some of the freshest seafood I have ever eaten.\n\nThere is no printed menu. Instead, the owner, Ahmet, brings a tray of that morning's catch to your table and recommends how each fish should be cooked. We chose grilled sea bass, which arrived simply seasoned with lemon and olive oil, alongside a selection of home-made starters, including a remarkable smoked aubergine salad.\n\nThe service is warm rather than polished. Ahmet's teenage son, who was clearly still learning, forgot our drinks twice, but his enthusiasm more than made up for it. Prices are reasonable, though not cheap, which is hardly surprising given the quality of the ingredients.\n\nIf I had one reservation, it would be the lack of indoor seating, which makes the restaurant far less appealing on cold or windy evenings.\n\nNevertheless, for anyone who appreciates simple food cooked with care, Balıkçı Ahmet is an absolute must. Book ahead in summer, and ask for a table by the water."]]}]}]},"S":{"parts":[{"id":"b2x-s1","p":1,"t":"Part 1 · Tanışma","ins":"Sınav görevlisi seninle ilgili sorular soruyor. Her soruya birkaç cümleyle, nedenler ve örnekler vererek cevap ver.","q":[["Where are you from, and what do you like about living there?",30,["I'm from","what I like","the best thing","because"],["I'm from Izmir, on the Aegean coast. What I like most is that you're never far from the sea – even in the city centre you can walk along the seafront in the evening. People are also generally quite relaxed and friendly, and there's a really good mix of old neighbourhoods and modern areas.","I'm originally from Konya, in central Anatolia, although I've been living in Istanbul for a few years now. What I miss about Konya is how calm it is, and the food, of course. Istanbul is exciting, but it's exhausting – the traffic can be a nightmare."]],["What do you enjoy doing in your free time?",30,["I enjoy","I'm really into","whenever I can"],["I'm really into photography at the moment. Whenever I have a free afternoon, I take my camera and wander around the old parts of the city looking for interesting faces and buildings. I've also started editing my photos, which takes almost as long as taking them!","I spend a lot of my free time playing volleyball. I'm in a local team and we train twice a week, with matches most weekends. Apart from that, I enjoy cooking for friends – I'm trying to learn dishes from different countries."]],["Do you think you will use English in your future job? Why?",30,["I'm likely to","I'll probably","I hope","because"],["I'll probably use it a lot, because I'm hoping to work in tourism, and most of our visitors communicate in English even if it isn't their first language. Even in jobs that don't seem international, English is useful for reading research or using software, so I think it's essential.","Definitely. I want to study engineering, and most of the best articles and online courses are in English. I'd also like to work for an international company one day, perhaps abroad, so being able to communicate confidently would give me a real advantage."]],["Tell us about a memorable celebration you have been to.",40,["I remember","it was","what made it","I'll never forget"],["I'll never forget my cousin's wedding last year. It was held outdoors in an olive grove, and what made it special was that all the food was cooked by the family, not a catering company. There must have been three hundred guests. We danced until three in the morning, and my grandparents, who are in their eighties, danced longer than anyone!","A memorable celebration for me was my graduation from secondary school. After the ceremony, our whole class went to a restaurant by the sea with our teachers. Some of the teachers gave funny speeches about each student, and it was the first time I'd seen them so relaxed. It was emotional too, because we knew we'd all be going in different directions."]]]},{"id":"b2x-s2","p":2,"t":"Part 2 · İki fotoğrafı karşılaştır","ins":"İki fotoğrafı yaklaşık 1 dakika karşılaştır ve sorulan soruyu cevapla. Fotoğrafları tek tek anlatma; benzerlik ve farkları söyle, tahmin yürüt.","img":[["👩‍💻🏢☕","Fotoğraf 1: Kalabalık bir ofiste, bilgisayar başında çalışan genç bir kadın.","b2-office","A young woman is working on a laptop in a busy open-plan office. Two colleagues are working behind her, and there is a coffee machine."],["👨‍🌾🌾🚜","Fotoğraf 2: Tarlada, traktörün yanında çalışan bir çiftçi.","b2-farm","A farmer in a cap is standing in a green field next to a red tractor on a sunny day."]],"q":[["Your photographs show people working in different places. Compare the photographs, and say what might be enjoyable about working in each place.",60,["both photos show","whereas","while","on the other hand","might","I imagine","it looks as if"],["Both photos show people at work, but in very different environments. In the first one, a young woman is working at a computer in what looks like a busy open-plan office, whereas the second shows a farmer standing next to a tractor in a field. I imagine the office worker might enjoy being surrounded by colleagues – she can chat during breaks and probably works as part of a team. Her job is probably quite varied, and she's protected from the weather. On the other hand, the farmer is working outdoors, which must be wonderful on a sunny day. He's probably his own boss, so he can decide how to organise his day, and there's something satisfying about producing food. However, his work is likely to be physically much harder, and he depends on the weather.","These two photographs show contrasting workplaces. The woman in the first one seems to be working in a modern office, surrounded by other people and screens, while the man in the second one is out in the countryside on a farm. What might be enjoyable about the office is the social side – having colleagues to share ideas with and perhaps opportunities for promotion. It also looks comfortable, with a coffee machine nearby. For the farmer, the enjoyable part is probably the freedom and fresh air. He doesn't have to sit at a desk all day, and he can see the direct results of his hard work when he harvests his crops. I suppose it's also peaceful compared with a noisy office."]],["Which of these places would you prefer to work in? Why?",30,["I'd prefer","I'd rather","because"],["Personally, I'd rather work in the office, because I enjoy working with other people, and I'm not sure I'd cope with getting up at five every morning like a farmer. I also like having clear working hours, so I can plan my free time.","I'd choose the farm, to be honest. I can't imagine sitting in front of a screen for eight hours a day. Working outdoors with my hands would make me feel healthier and more connected to nature, even if it's harder physically."]]]},{"id":"b2x-s3","p":3,"t":"Part 3 · Birlikte karar verme","ins":"Gerçek sınavda bu bölüm bir partnerle yapılır. Burada önce seçenekleri tek tek tartış (2 dakika), sonra bir karar ver (1 dakika).","opts":["🌳 more parks","🚲 bike lanes","🎭 a cultural centre","🏊 a sports centre","🚌 cheaper public transport"],"q":[["Imagine that a town wants to improve life for young people. Here are some ideas they are considering. Talk about how these ideas might improve life for young people in the town.",120,["let's start with","what do you think about","that's a good point","I'm not so sure","on the other hand","it would encourage"],["Let's start with more parks. They would give young people somewhere free to meet, which is important because cafés and cinemas are quite expensive. They're also good for mental health. Bike lanes are a good idea too, since they'd make cycling safer and encourage people to be more active, and teenagers could get around without depending on their parents. A cultural centre could offer concerts, theatre and workshops, which might help young people discover new talents, although it would only be useful if the activities were cheap or free. A sports centre is obviously great for health, but many towns already have one, so it might not make much difference. Finally, cheaper public transport would make a huge difference, because it would allow young people to get to school, work and all these other places more easily. In a way, it would make all the other ideas more useful. So for me, the best approach would be to combine a couple of these ideas – for example, cheaper transport together with a cultural centre – because young people have different interests and needs. A sports-loving teenager and an artistic one would want very different things, and the town should try to offer something for both.","I'll begin with the cultural centre, because I think it's the most interesting option. It could host music lessons, art exhibitions and film clubs, and it would give young people a place to express themselves. On the other hand, a sports centre would probably attract more people, since sport is popular with most teenagers. Bike lanes are practical but perhaps less exciting – they'd improve safety, though, which is important. More parks would make the town greener and give young people a place to hang out, especially in summer. As for cheaper public transport, I think it's essential in towns where young people live far from the centre. Without it, they can't really take advantage of any of these facilities. I also think it's worth considering cost. A cultural centre or a sports centre would be very expensive to build and run, whereas bike lanes and parks are relatively cheap and last for years. So the town would need to think carefully about what gives the best value for money, and ideally it should ask young people what they actually want before deciding anything."]],["Now you have about a minute to decide which idea would be the most useful for young people.",60,["I think we should choose","the most useful","because","even though","it would benefit"],["If I had to choose one, I'd go for cheaper public transport. Even though it's less exciting than a cultural centre, it would benefit almost every young person in the town, whether they're students or have part-time jobs. It would also make all the other facilities easier to reach, and it's good for the environment, since fewer people would need to be driven around by their parents. It's also the option that helps young people from poorer families the most, which I think matters.","I think the cultural centre would be the most valuable. Many towns already have parks and sports facilities, but there's often nowhere for young people interested in art, music or drama to meet. It would offer something genuinely new and could become a real centre of community life. It might even encourage young people who usually stay at home to go out and meet others with similar interests, which would be good for their confidence and well-being."]]]},{"id":"b2x-s4","p":4,"t":"Part 4 · Tartışma","ins":"3. bölümün konusuyla ilgili daha genel sorular. Görüşünü nedenler ve örneklerle açıkla.","q":[["Do you think young people today have enough places to spend their free time?",45,["I'd say","it depends","for example","on the whole"],["On the whole, I'd say it depends on where you live. In big cities, there are plenty of options – cinemas, sports clubs, cafés – although many of them are expensive. In smaller towns, young people often end up spending time in shopping centres or online, simply because there's nothing else. I think more free, safe spaces would make a real difference, especially for teenagers who don't have much money.","Honestly, I don't think they do. Most places for young people cost money, and many teenagers don't have much. Youth centres used to be common, but a lot of them have closed. As a result, young people often just hang around in the street, and then adults complain about them! I think towns should invest in youth centres again, with free activities and staff who understand teenagers."]],["Should local governments ask young people for their opinions before making decisions?",45,["definitely","after all","otherwise","I believe"],["Definitely. After all, young people are the ones who will live with the results of decisions for the longest time. Otherwise, money might be spent on projects that nobody actually uses. Some towns have youth councils where teenagers can give their opinions, and I think every town should have one. It would also teach young people how local democracy works, which could make them more likely to vote later.","I think so, but it needs to be done properly. Sometimes governments ask young people's opinions just to look good and then ignore them. If they're going to ask, they should explain afterwards how the opinions were used. Otherwise, young people will stop taking part. Online surveys could be a simple way to reach more of them, since that's where they spend most of their time."]],["Some people say that technology has made young people less active. Do you agree?",45,["to some extent","however","on the other hand","for instance"],["I agree to some extent. Many teenagers spend hours on their phones or playing games instead of being active. However, technology can also motivate people – for instance, fitness apps and online challenges have encouraged lots of my friends to start running, and you can follow exercise videos at home. So it depends how you use it. I'd say the real problem isn't technology itself but the lack of balance.","I'd say yes, mostly. When my parents were young, they played outside until it got dark, but now children spend their free time indoors in front of screens. Schools have also reduced sport in some places. I think it's a serious problem, because it affects both physical and mental health. I think the solution is to make physical activity more attractive, for example with free sports clubs and safe places to cycle."]]]}]}},"lv":"B2","name":"B2 First"},{"id":"c1-1","t":"Sınav 1","sec":{"R":{"min":90,"parts":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"The value of boredom","p":"In an age of constant stimulation, boredom has acquired a bad (1) ______. We reach for our phones the moment we have nothing to do, as if a few minutes of idleness were something to be avoided at all (2) ______. Yet psychologists are increasingly coming round to the view that boredom serves a useful purpose. When the mind is not (3) ______ by external demands, it tends to wander, and it is in this state that many of our most original ideas (4) ______. In one well-known study, participants who had first completed a deliberately tedious task went on to produce more creative answers than those who had not. Boredom, it seems, acts as a (5) ______ that our current situation is unsatisfying, prompting us to seek out new goals. The problem is not boredom itself but our (6) ______ to tolerate it. By filling every spare moment with entertainment, we may be (7) ______ ourselves of the very conditions in which creativity thrives. Perhaps the next time we feel bored, we should resist the (8) ______ to reach for a screen.","o":[[["name","fame","reputation","character"],2,["collocation"]],[["costs","prices","means","rates"],0,["collocation"]],[["engaged","involved","attached","committed"],0,["vocab"]],[["emerge","happen","result","proceed"],0,["vocab"]],[["hint","signal","symbol","notice"],1,["vocab"]],[["inability","incapability","weakness","shortage"],0,["word-formation"]],[["denying","depriving","removing","refusing"],1,["collocation"]],[["urge","wish","will","want"],0,["collocation"]]],"e":["acquire a bad reputation: kötü bir üne kavuşmak.","at all costs: ne pahasına olursa olsun.","engaged by demands: taleplerle meşgul edilmek.","ideas emerge: fikirler ortaya çıkar. happen, result ve proceed bu anlamı vermez.","act as a signal that: -nin işareti olmak.","our inability to: -e katlanamamamız (yapamama). weakness ve shortage \"to + fiil\" almaz.","deprive someone of something: birini bir şeyden yoksun bırakmak.","resist the urge to: -me dürtüsüne direnmek."]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 2","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","one":true,"title":"The longest-running experiment","p":"In 1927, Professor Thomas Parnell set (1) ______ an experiment at the University of Queensland to show that some substances which appear solid are (2) ______ fact extremely slow-moving liquids. He placed a sample of pitch, a material derived (3) ______ tar, in a sealed funnel and waited. It took eight years for the first drop to fall. Since then, only nine drops have fallen, and remarkably, nobody has ever seen one (4) ______ so. On one occasion, the drop fell while the camera that was supposed to be recording it was not working. Parnell himself died long (5) ______ the second drop. The experiment, (6) ______ is still running today, holds a world record as the longest continuously running laboratory experiment. (7) ______ its apparent simplicity, it has fascinated millions of people, many of whom watch a live online video in the hope of finally witnessing the moment when a drop falls. Whether anyone will ever succeed (8) ______ doing so remains to be seen.","a":[["up"],["in"],["from"],["do"],["before"],["which"],["despite","for"],["in"]],"tg":[["vocab"],["vocab"],["vocab"],["vocab"],["vocab"],["relative-clauses"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["gerund-infinitive"]],"e":["set up an experiment: deney kurmak.","in fact: aslında.","derived from: -den elde edilen.","seen one do so: birinin bunu yaptığını görmek (see + nesne + yalın fiil).","long before: -den çok önce.","Virgüllü ek bilgi: which.","Despite: -e rağmen. (\"For all its simplicity\" da mümkün.)","succeed in doing: -mekte başarılı olmak."]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Kelime türetme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Her boşluk için verilen kök kelimeyi uygun biçime getir.","ty":"open","title":"The rise of the four-day week","st":["RECENT","SIGNIFY","ANTICIPATE","SATISFY","DOUBT","FEASIBLE","RESIST","PRESSURE"],"p":"Until (1) ______, the idea of a four-day working week would have been dismissed as unrealistic. However, a number of large-scale trials have produced results of real (2) ______. Contrary to what many had (3) ______, productivity did not fall; in many companies it actually rose. Employees reported far greater job (4) ______ and fewer days off sick. Sceptics remain, of course. Some economists (5) ______ whether the model could work in industries such as healthcare, where constant cover is required. Others question the long-term (6) ______ of the scheme for small businesses with limited staff. Nevertheless, the (7) ______ that the idea once met is beginning to fade, and governments in several countries are coming under growing (8) ______ to support further trials.","a":[["recently"],["significance"],["anticipated"],["satisfaction"],["doubt"],["feasibility"],["resistance"],["pressure"]],"tg":[["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"]],"e":["Until recently: yakın zamana kadar (zarf).","of real significance: gerçek önemi olan (isim).","had anticipated: beklemişti (past perfect, V3).","job satisfaction: iş tatmini.","doubt whether: şüphe etmek (fiil, kök aynen). Sınavda bazen kök değişmez.","the long-term feasibility: uygulanabilirlik.","the resistance that the idea met: karşılaştığı direnç.","under pressure: baskı altında. Kök aynı kalır."]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 3–6 kelime yaz.","ty":"kwt","q":[{"s1":"I didn't realise how difficult the job was until I started it.","key":"DID","s2":"Only when I started the job ______ how difficult it was.","a":["did i realise","did i understand","did i find out","did i discover"],"tg":["inversion","transformation"],"e":"Only when ... ile devrik yapı: Only when I started the job did I realise.","mx":6},{"s1":"People say that the castle was built in the twelfth century.","key":"SAID","s2":"The castle ______ built in the twelfth century.","a":["is said to have been"],"tg":["passive-advanced"],"e":"is said to have been + V3.","mx":6},{"s1":"It was wrong of you to tell her the secret.","key":"OUGHT","s2":"You ______ her the secret.","a":["ought not to have told","oughtn't to have told"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"Geçmiş için eleştiri: ought not to have + V3.","mx":6},{"s1":"The meeting was postponed because the manager was ill.","key":"PUT","s2":"The meeting ______ account of the manager's illness.","a":["was put off on"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"put off = postpone; on account of = because of.","mx":6},{"s1":"I'm sure he didn't mean to upset you.","key":"CAN'T","s2":"He ______ to upset you.","a":["can't have meant"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"Geçmişe dair olumsuz çıkarım: can't have + V3.","mx":6},{"s1":"She was very kind, so we invited her to stay.","key":"SUCH","s2":"She was ______ we invited her to stay.","a":["such a kind person that","such a kind woman that","such a kind woman","such a kind person"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"such a + sıfat + isim + that.","mx":6}]},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"The translator's dilemma","p":["Every translator I know has a story about a sentence that refused to be translated. Mine involves a single word in a Portuguese novel: saudade. Dictionaries offer \"longing\" or \"nostalgia\", but neither comes close. Saudade is longing for something you may never have had, tinged with a strange pleasure in the longing itself. I spent three days on that sentence and eventually settled for a paraphrase that I still find unsatisfactory.","The popular image of translation is of a mechanical process: find the equivalent word, move on. Anyone who has attempted it knows otherwise. Languages do not simply attach different labels to the same reality; they divide up reality in different ways. Russian distinguishes between light blue and dark blue as separate colours. Many languages have no word for \"privacy\". A translator is therefore constantly making choices, and every choice involves a loss.","This is why I am sceptical, though not dismissive, of the claim that machine translation will soon make human translators redundant. For instruction manuals and business emails, machines already perform impressively. But literature depends on tone, rhythm and the associations that words carry – qualities that are notoriously difficult to measure, let alone reproduce. When I read machine translations of poetry, I am struck less by their errors than by their flatness. Everything is correct and nothing is alive.","That said, I have come to use these tools myself, in the way a carpenter might use a power drill. A machine can produce a rough first draft in seconds, freeing me to concentrate on the decisions that matter. The danger, I suspect, is not that machines will replace translators, but that publishers, under pressure to cut costs, will decide that \"good enough\" is good enough. Readers may not even notice what they are missing – and that, perhaps, is the greatest loss of all."],"q":[{"q":"What does the writer suggest about the word \"saudade\"?","o":["It means exactly the same as the English word \"nostalgia\".","Its meaning changes completely depending on the context.","It combines feelings that English words do not capture together.","It is used mainly by older Portuguese speakers in formal writing."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Özlem ve özlemin kendisinden alınan tuhaf zevk; \"longing/nostalgia\" yetmiyor."},{"q":"How does the writer feel about his translation of the sentence?","o":["proud of his creativity","still not entirely happy with it","relieved that it was finished","surprised that it took him so many days"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"a paraphrase that I still find unsatisfactory\"."},{"q":"The examples in the second paragraph are used to show that","o":["some languages are more precise than others.","translators often make careless mistakes.","languages organise experience differently.","certain concepts cannot be expressed at all."],"a":2,"tg":["gist"],"e":"\"they divide up reality in different ways\"."},{"q":"What is the writer's main criticism of machine translations of poetry?","o":["They contain too many errors.","They lack life and character.","They take too long to produce.","They change the meaning of the original."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Everything is correct and nothing is alive\"."},{"q":"Why does the writer mention a carpenter?","o":["to show that translation is a craft that needs training","to explain how he uses technology as a helpful tool","to suggest that machines are more efficient than people","to compare translators with other manual workers"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Makine çeviriyi marangozun matkabı gibi bir araç olarak kullanıyor."},{"q":"What is the writer most concerned about?","o":["Human translators will soon lose most of their jobs to machines.","Readers will prefer machine translations.","Lower standards will be accepted without people realising.","Publishers will stop translating literature."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"good enough\" kabul edilecek ve okurlar neyi kaçırdığını fark etmeyecek."}]},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Metinler arası eşleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 6","ins":"Aynı konuda yazılmış dört görüşü (A–D) oku. Her soru için doğru yazarı seç.","ty":"match","title":"Should children be given homework?","opts":[["A","Educational psychologist","The research on homework is more nuanced than either side admits. For older students, a moderate amount is linked to better results, but for younger children the benefits are close to zero. What matters far more than quantity is purpose: tasks that simply repeat what was done in class achieve little. Homework should prepare students to learn something new, not just keep them busy. Parents, meanwhile, should be supporters, not supervisors."],["B","Secondary school teacher","I set homework because the school day is simply too short to cover the curriculum. Without independent practice, students forget most of what they learn. I accept that some tasks are poorly designed, and I have changed my own approach over the years. However, those who call for homework to be abolished entirely underestimate how much learning depends on repetition. Practice may be dull, but it works."],["C","Parent of three","Every evening in our house is a battle. My children come home exhausted, and then spend two hours on worksheets that seem to teach them nothing new. I end up doing half of it myself. I am not against learning at home, but it should be reading together or a project the children actually care about. The evidence that endless worksheets help young children is, as far as I can tell, non-existent."],["D","Head of a primary school","Five years ago we abolished homework for under-elevens, replacing it with a simple expectation that children read for pleasure each evening. Parents are asked only to show an interest, not to check or correct. I do not claim that homework itself was harmful – we simply wanted to try something different – and the results have been encouraging: reading levels have risen, and families report less stress. Older students preparing for exams may well need more structured independent work."]],"q":[{"q":"Which writer agrees with writer C that research shows little benefit from homework for young children?","a":"A","tg":["inference"],"e":"A: küçük çocuklar için fayda \"close to zero\". C de kanıt olmadığını söylüyor. D ödevin zararlı olduğunu iddia etmiyor."},{"q":"Which writer has a different view from writers A and C on the value of repetitive practice?","a":"B","tg":["attitude"],"e":"B tekrarın işe yaradığını savunuyor; A ve C tekrar eden ödevlerin az işe yaradığını söylüyor."},{"q":"Which writer has a similar view to writer A about the role parents should play?","a":"D","tg":["inference"],"e":"A: veliler denetçi değil destekçi olmalı. D: velilerden yalnızca ilgi göstermeleri isteniyor, kontrol etmeleri değil. C ise ödevin yarısını kendisi yapıyor."},{"q":"Which writer expresses a similar view to writer C about what kind of activity is useful at home?","a":"D","tg":["inference"],"e":"C: birlikte okuma; D: ödev yerine her akşam keyif için okuma."}]},{"k":"R7","nm":"Okuma 7 · Paragraf yerleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 7","ins":"Metinden altı paragraf çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun paragrafı (A–G) seç. Bir paragraf fazladır.","ty":"gap","title":"Rewilding: letting nature take over","p":"When the Knepp estate in southern England stopped farming in 2001, its owners were facing financial ruin. The heavy clay soil had never been ideal for crops, and years of intensive agriculture had left the land exhausted.\n(1) ______\nThe idea was simple, if radical: rather than managing the land, they would let nature manage itself. Fences were removed, and small herds of cattle, pigs and deer were introduced to roam freely, imitating the large wild animals that once lived in Britain.\n(2) ______\nWithin a few years, the fields had begun to transform. Thorny bushes spread across former farmland, providing shelter for young trees. Ponds formed where pigs had dug up the ground.\n(3) ______\nPerhaps the most remarkable change was the return of species that had become rare across the country. Turtle doves, nightingales and purple emperor butterflies all appeared in significant numbers.\n(4) ______\nNot everyone was pleased, however. Neighbouring farmers complained that the estate looked untidy and worried that weeds would spread onto their land.\n(5) ______\nToday, Knepp is held up as a model of what is possible. It attracts thousands of visitors a year, and its income from tourism and nature-friendly meat now exceeds what it once earned from farming.\n(6) ______\nSupporters argue that if similar projects were adopted across the country, they could help reverse the decline in wildlife that has been one of the defining features of the last century.","opts":[["A","These animals turned out to be essential. By grazing, digging and trampling, they created a constantly changing mix of habitats, something no human manager could have planned."],["B","Faced with this situation, the owners took a decision that many of their neighbours considered foolish."],["C","For some, the sight of this \"messy\" landscape was a betrayal of centuries of careful land management."],["D","Critics point out, though, that few landowners could afford to take such a risk, and that Britain still needs to produce its own food."],["E","The estate is now home to one of the largest populations of nightingales in the country, at a time when the species has declined by over 90 per cent elsewhere."],["F","Many of the animals were later sold to farms in other parts of Europe."],["G","This mixture of open ground, scrub and water proved to be exactly what many insects and birds needed."]],"a":["B","A","G","E","C","D"],"tg":[["coherence"],["reference"],["reference"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["advanced-linkers"]],"e":["Mali çöküş ve yorgun toprak → \"Faced with this situation ... decision\": B; sonraki paragraf \"The idea was simple\".","Hayvanlar tanıtıldı → \"These animals turned out to be essential\": A.","Çalılar, ağaçlar, göletler → \"This mixture of open ground, scrub and water\": G.","Nadir türler geri döndü → bülbül nüfusu: E.","Çiftçiler şikâyet etti → bazıları için \"messy\" manzara ihanet: C.","Bugün model örnek → \"Critics point out, though\": D; ardından \"Supporters argue\". F fazladır."]},{"k":"R8","nm":"Okuma 8 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 8","ins":"Metin bölümlerini (A–E) oku. Her soru için doğru bölümü seç. Bölümler birden fazla kez seçilebilir.","ty":"match","title":"Five people describe a decision that changed their career","opts":[["A","Nadia","I spent ten years as a lawyer before admitting to myself that I dreaded going to work. Leaving was terrifying – I had a mortgage and two children – but I retrained as a primary school teacher. My income halved, and some of my former colleagues clearly thought I had lost my mind. I have never regretted it for a second."],["B","Oliver","My move into journalism happened almost by chance. I had written a few blog posts about local politics, and an editor happened to read one. She offered me a trial, and I simply said yes without thinking too hard about it. If I had thought it through, I probably would have turned it down, as I had no training at all."],["C","Priya","After my daughter was born, I wanted to work fewer hours, and my employer refused. So I set up my own consultancy. The first year was extremely tough, and there were months when I earned almost nothing. What got me through was a network of other self-employed parents who shared advice and, when necessary, childcare."],["D","Marcus","I turned down a promotion that would have meant moving to head office in another city. Everyone told me I was making a mistake. But I knew the job would leave me no time for the things I care about outside work. Ironically, three years later, I was offered an even better position without having to move."],["E","Lena","I had always been fascinated by medicine but assumed I was too old to start at thirty-five. A friend who had done exactly that persuaded me otherwise. The training was exhausting, and I often felt out of place among students half my age. But my previous experience in management turned out to be surprisingly useful."]],"q":[{"q":"Who was influenced by someone who had made a similar change?","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Lena: aynısını yapmış bir arkadaşı onu ikna etti."},{"q":"Who says that former colleagues thought their decision was foolish?","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Nadia: eski meslektaşları aklını kaçırdığını düşündü."},{"q":"Who was rewarded later for not accepting an offer?","a":"D","tg":["inference"],"e":"Marcus terfiyi reddetti; üç yıl sonra taşınmadan daha iyi bir teklif aldı."},{"q":"Who received practical support from people in a similar situation?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Priya: serbest çalışan diğer ebeveynlerden destek."},{"q":"Who admits that they made a decision without careful consideration?","a":"B","tg":["third-conditional"],"e":"Oliver: düşünmeden evet dedi; düşünseydi reddederdi."},{"q":"Who found that skills from a previous job were valuable?","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Lena: yöneticilik deneyimi işe yaradı."},{"q":"Who accepted a significant drop in earnings?","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Nadia: geliri yarıya düştü."},{"q":"Who changed career because of a conflict with an employer?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Priya: işvereni daha az çalışmasına izin vermedi."},{"q":"Who mentions feeling different from the people around them?","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Lena: yaşının yarısındaki öğrenciler arasında yabancı hissetti."},{"q":"Who says they have no doubts that they made the right choice?","a":"A","tg":["attitude"],"e":"Nadia: \"I have never regretted it for a second\"."}]}]},"L":{"min":40,"parts":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Üç farklı kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin; her biri için iki soru var. En uygun cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1a","sc":[["M","So you've finally finished the book?"],["F","Yes, after four years. The research was the enjoyable part – I loved spending weeks in archives. The writing itself was agony. I rewrote the first chapter about fifteen times."],["M","And now?"],["F","Now I'm terrified about the reviews. I keep telling myself it doesn't matter, but of course it does."]],"q":"You hear two friends talking about a book. What did the woman enjoy most about the process?","o":["writing the opening","investigating the subject","discussing it with others"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Araştırma kısmı keyifliydi; yazmak işkenceydi."},{"id":"l1b","sc":[["M","So you've finally finished the book?"],["F","Yes, after four years. The research was the enjoyable part – I loved spending weeks in archives. The writing itself was agony. I rewrote the first chapter about fifteen times."],["M","And now?"],["F","Now I'm terrified about the reviews. I keep telling myself it doesn't matter, but of course it does."]],"q":"How does she feel now that the book is finished?","o":["anxious about how it will be received","relieved that she can rest","eager to start work on her next book straight away"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I'm terrified about the reviews\"."},{"id":"l1c","sc":[["F","The new exhibition is a brave choice for a museum that usually plays it safe. Putting contemporary pieces next to centuries-old paintings forces you to look at both differently. Admittedly, a few of the combinations feel forced. But overall I left feeling I'd seen familiar works with fresh eyes."]],"q":"You hear a critic talking about an exhibition. What does she say about it?","o":["It is too traditional.","It gives a new perspective on well-known works.","It contains too many modern pieces next to the old ones."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I'd seen familiar works with fresh eyes\"."},{"id":"l1d","sc":[["F","The new exhibition is a brave choice for a museum that usually plays it safe. Putting contemporary pieces next to centuries-old paintings forces you to look at both differently. Admittedly, a few of the combinations feel forced. But overall I left feeling I'd seen familiar works with fresh eyes."]],"q":"What criticism does she make?","o":["Some pairings of works do not seem natural.","The museum has taken too many risks with its collection.","The older paintings are poorly displayed."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"a few of the combinations feel forced\"."},{"id":"l1e","sc":[["M","I was sceptical when the company introduced flexible hours. I assumed people would take advantage. In fact, the opposite happened – people started working more efficiently because they felt trusted. My only concern now is that it's harder to get everyone together for meetings."]],"q":"You hear a manager talking about flexible working. Why did his opinion change?","o":["Staff became more productive.","Staff asked for more meetings.","It saved the company money."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"İnsanlar güvenildiklerini hissedip daha verimli çalıştı."},{"id":"l1f","sc":[["M","I was sceptical when the company introduced flexible hours. I assumed people would take advantage. In fact, the opposite happened – people started working more efficiently because they felt trusted. My only concern now is that it's harder to get everyone together for meetings."]],"q":"What problem does he mention?","o":["Some staff work too many hours.","Arranging meetings is more difficult.","Customers find it harder to contact staff."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Herkesi toplantıda bir araya getirmek zorlaştı."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Cümle tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Cümleleri bir kelime ya da kısa bir ifadeyle tamamla.","ty":"open","id":"l2","title":"Cümleler","sc":[["F","Hello everyone. I'm a marine biologist, and for the last six years I've been studying octopuses off the coast of Portugal. People often ask why I chose octopuses rather than dolphins or whales. Partly it was practical – octopuses live in shallow water, so they're easier to study. But mainly it's because they're so unlike us, and yet clearly intelligent. An octopus has around five hundred million neurons, and two thirds of them are in its arms rather than its head. This means each arm can, in a sense, make its own decisions. One of the things that surprised me most was how individual they are. Some are bold and curious, others extremely shy. I gave each of the octopuses I studied a name, which some colleagues find unscientific. One of them, whom I called Lola, learned to recognise me and would approach my diving mask. Octopuses are famous for escaping from aquariums. In one case, an octopus repeatedly turned off a light in its tank by squirting water at it. Sadly, octopuses have very short lives – most species live only one to two years. That's one of the great mysteries: why would evolution produce such a clever animal that lives so briefly? My current research focuses on how octopuses sleep. We've found evidence that they may even dream, as their skin changes colour during certain stages of sleep."]],"p":"The speaker chose octopuses partly because they live in (1) ______ water.\nAbout two thirds of an octopus's neurons are located in its (2) ______.\nShe was surprised by the different (3) ______ of individual octopuses.\nSome colleagues think giving octopuses names is (4) ______.\nOne octopus learned to recognise the speaker's (5) ______.\nAn octopus in an aquarium turned off a (6) ______ using water.\nMost octopuses live for between one and (7) ______ years.\nDuring sleep, an octopus's (8) ______ may change colour.","a":[["shallow"],["arms"],["personalities","characters","personality"],["unscientific"],["diving mask","mask"],["light"],["two","2"],["skin"]],"tg":[["detail"],["detail"],["inference"],["detail"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"]],"e":["\"they live in shallow water\".","\"two thirds of them are in its arms\".","\"how individual they are. Some are bold ..., others extremely shy\" → kişilik.","\"which some colleagues find unscientific\".","\"would approach my diving mask\".","\"turned off a light in its tank\".","\"one to two years\".","\"their skin changes colour\"."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bir röportaj dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","id":"l3","sc":[["M","My guest today is Dr Helen Park, an architect who specialises in designing schools. Helen, why schools?"],["F","I started out designing offices, and I found it rather unfulfilling. Then I was asked to work on a school extension, and I was struck by how little thought had gone into the existing building. Children were spending seven hours a day in rooms with poor light and terrible acoustics. I realised design could make a real difference to how they learn."],["M","What do architects most often get wrong?"],["F","We tend to design for how a building looks rather than how it will be used. A glass atrium may win awards, but if it's too hot in summer and too noisy all year, it's a failure. I now spend weeks in schools before I draw anything, watching how students and teachers actually move around."],["M","Is there evidence that design affects learning?"],["F","There is, though people sometimes overstate it. A good building won't turn a poor school into a great one. But studies do show that natural light, fresh air and the ability to control temperature have measurable effects on concentration. Those are relatively cheap to get right, which makes it all the more frustrating when they're ignored."],["M","How involved are students in your designs?"],["F","Very. I used to consult them as a formality, if I'm honest. But a group of eleven-year-olds once pointed out that the toilets I'd planned were too far from the playground. It seemed trivial, but it would have caused chaos at break times. Since then, I take their views far more seriously."],["M","What's the biggest challenge facing school design now?"],["F","Budgets, without doubt. Governments want buildings that are cheap to construct, but cheap buildings are often expensive to run. A bit more spent on insulation at the start saves enormous amounts on heating later. Persuading people to think long-term is the hardest part of my job."]],"q":[{"q":"Why did Helen start designing schools?","o":["She was bored with her previous work.","She had always been more interested in education than offices.","She realised existing schools were poorly designed.","She was offered a large contract."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Mevcut binaya ne kadar az özen gösterildiğini fark etti; tasarımın fark yaratabileceğini anladı."},{"q":"What does Helen say architects often get wrong?","o":["They ignore the cost of buildings.","They focus on appearance rather than use.","They rely too much on glass.","They do not spend enough time consulting teachers."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"design for how a building looks rather than how it will be used\"."},{"q":"What is Helen's view of the research on design and learning?","o":["It shows that design is the main factor in school success.","Its findings are sometimes exaggerated.","It has not produced any clear results.","It focuses too much on expensive features."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"though people sometimes overstate it\"."},{"q":"Why does Helen feel frustrated?","o":["Simple improvements are often not made.","Schools cannot afford fresh air systems.","Teachers do not control the temperature.","Studies are ignored by governments."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Nispeten ucuz iyileştirmeler bile göz ardı ediliyor."},{"q":"What does the story about the toilets illustrate?","o":["Children often complain about small things.","Helen had made a serious error in a design.","Students' opinions can be genuinely useful.","Break times are difficult to manage."],"a":2,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Bu olaydan sonra öğrencilerin görüşlerini çok daha ciddiye alıyor."},{"q":"What does Helen find most difficult about her job?","o":["working with small budgets","convincing people to consider future costs","designing efficient heating systems","meeting strict government regulations on safety"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Persuading people to think long-term is the hardest part\"."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Eşleştirme (görev 1)","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Beş kişinin konuşmasını dinleyeceksin. Görev 1: Her konuşmacının bu işi seçme nedenini (A–H) bul.","ty":"match","id":"l4","sc":[["M","Speaker one. I became a paramedic because, honestly, I needed a job where every day was different. I'd tried office work and I was climbing the walls. What I find hardest isn't the emergencies themselves – you're trained for those – it's the paperwork afterwards. Hours of it."],["F","#1.2"],["F","Speaker two. I'd always been good with numbers, and my careers adviser at school suggested accountancy. I followed the advice without much thought. It's a decent career, but what I struggle with is the isolation. Some weeks I barely speak to anyone except my computer."],["M","#1.2"],["M2","Speaker three. My father was a carpenter, and I grew up in his workshop, so becoming one myself felt natural – it's in the family. The hardest part is the physical toll. At forty, my back already tells me when I've worked too long."],["F","#1.2"],["F2","Speaker four. I went into nursing after my mother was seriously ill. The nurses who looked after her were so kind, and I wanted to give something back. What I didn't anticipate was how hard it would be emotionally – you can't leave patients' problems at the hospital door."],["M","#1.2"],["M","Speaker five. To be honest, I became a pilot for the travel. I wanted to see the world, and this seemed the best way to get paid for it. The irony is that I see almost nothing of the places I fly to. The irregular sleep is the real killer, though – my body never knows what time it is."]],"opts":[["A","wanted variety in their work"],["B","followed a family tradition"],["C","was inspired by a personal experience"],["D","wanted to travel"],["E","was attracted by the salary"],["F","took someone's advice"],["G","wanted to work outdoors"],["H","wanted to be their own boss"]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"A","tg":["gist"],"e":"\"a job where every day was different\"."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"F","tg":["gist"],"e":"Kariyer danışmanının tavsiyesine uydu."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"B","tg":["gist"],"e":"Babası marangozdu: \"it's in the family\"."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"C","tg":["gist"],"e":"Annesinin hastalığı ve hemşirelerin şefkati."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"D","tg":["gist"],"e":"\"I became a pilot for the travel\"."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Eşleştirme (görev 2)","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Aynı kaydı kullan. Görev 2: Her konuşmacının işinde en zor bulduğu şeyi (A–H) bul.","ty":"match","id":"l4","noaud":true,"sc":[["M","Speaker one. I became a paramedic because, honestly, I needed a job where every day was different. I'd tried office work and I was climbing the walls. What I find hardest isn't the emergencies themselves – you're trained for those – it's the paperwork afterwards. Hours of it."],["F","#1.2"],["F","Speaker two. I'd always been good with numbers, and my careers adviser at school suggested accountancy. I followed the advice without much thought. It's a decent career, but what I struggle with is the isolation. Some weeks I barely speak to anyone except my computer."],["M","#1.2"],["M2","Speaker three. My father was a carpenter, and I grew up in his workshop, so becoming one myself felt natural – it's in the family. The hardest part is the physical toll. At forty, my back already tells me when I've worked too long."],["F","#1.2"],["F2","Speaker four. I went into nursing after my mother was seriously ill. The nurses who looked after her were so kind, and I wanted to give something back. What I didn't anticipate was how hard it would be emotionally – you can't leave patients' problems at the hospital door."],["M","#1.2"],["M","Speaker five. To be honest, I became a pilot for the travel. I wanted to see the world, and this seemed the best way to get paid for it. The irony is that I see almost nothing of the places I fly to. The irregular sleep is the real killer, though – my body never knows what time it is."]],"opts":[["A","the administrative work"],["B","the emotional pressure"],["C","the long journeys"],["D","the lack of contact with people"],["E","the physical demands"],["F","the low pay"],["G","the disrupted sleep pattern"],["H","dealing with difficult customers"]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Acil durumlar değil, sonrasındaki evrak işi."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the isolation\" – kimseyle konuşmamak."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the physical toll\" – sırtı ağrıyor."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"\"how hard it would be emotionally\"."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"G","tg":["detail"],"e":"\"The irregular sleep is the real killer\"."}]}]},"W":{"min":90,"tasks":[{"id":"c1x-w1","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Part 1 · Deneme: Şehir merkezlerinde araçlar","in":"Your class has attended a panel discussion on how to make city centres better places to live. You have made the notes below. Write an essay discussing TWO of the methods in your notes. You should explain which method you think is more effective, giving reasons in support of your answer. You may, if you wish, make use of the opinions expressed in the discussion, but you should use your own words as far as possible. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"Methods for improving city centres:\n• banning private cars\n• creating more green spaces\n• encouraging people to live in the centre\n\nSome opinions expressed in the discussion:\n\"Without cars, shops in the centre will lose customers.\"\n\"Parks make people want to spend time in a city.\"\n\"City centres are empty and unsafe at night because nobody lives there.\"","pts":[["İlk yöntemi tartış",["car","traffic","vehicle","ban","pedestrian","green space","park","live in the centre","residents"]],["İkinci yöntemi tartış",["whereas","on the other hand","similarly","second","another method","by contrast","turning to"]],["Hangisinin daha etkili olduğunu söyle ve gerekçelendir",["more effective","most effective","in my view","i would argue","on balance","ultimately"]],["Akademik bağlaçlar kullan",["however","furthermore","moreover","nevertheless","consequently","in conclusion"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Many city centres have become places that people pass through rather than places where they want to spend time. Of the possible solutions, banning private cars and creating more green spaces are two of the most frequently discussed.\n\nRemoving cars from the centre has obvious benefits. Air quality improves, streets become quieter, and pedestrians can move around safely. However, opponents argue that shops will lose customers who are used to driving into town. This concern is not unreasonable, but evidence from cities such as Ghent and Pontevedra suggests that footfall often increases once streets are pedestrianised, provided that good public transport is available.\n\nCreating more green spaces addresses a different problem. Parks and tree-lined squares make a city more attractive, encouraging people to linger, meet friends and support local businesses. They also help to reduce temperatures during increasingly frequent heatwaves. The difficulty is that space in city centres is scarce and expensive, so new parks are often small or located on the edges.\n\nOn balance, I would argue that banning private cars is the more effective measure, largely because it makes the second possible. Once roads and car parks are no longer needed, they can be turned into green spaces, cycle lanes and outdoor cafés. In other words, reducing traffic does not simply remove a problem; it creates opportunities. Nevertheless, such a ban must be introduced gradually and accompanied by investment in public transport, or it risks harming the very businesses it is meant to help.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","City centres in many countries are facing a crisis. As shopping moves online and offices empty, once-busy streets are becoming lifeless, particularly after dark. Two possible remedies are creating more green spaces and encouraging people to live in the centre.\n\nGreen spaces have obvious appeal. Parks, tree-lined streets and small gardens make cities more attractive and healthier places to be, offering shade in summer and somewhere to relax during the working day. As one speaker in the discussion pointed out, parks give people a reason to spend time in a city rather than simply passing through it. However, parks alone cannot solve the problem of empty streets in the evening; once offices close, even the most beautiful square can feel deserted.\n\nEncouraging people to live in the centre addresses this issue more directly. Residents create a constant presence, supporting local shops, cafés and services at all hours, and their presence naturally makes streets feel safer. Many cities have successfully converted unused offices into flats, bringing new life to areas that had been empty for years. The main obstacle is cost, as central housing is often far more expensive than in the suburbs.\n\nIn my view, encouraging residential use is the more effective approach. Green spaces improve the quality of a city centre, but people bring it to life. Ideally, the two should go hand in hand: new housing projects should include parks and gardens, ensuring that city centres become not only busier but also more pleasant places to live."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","The decline of the traditional city centre has been one of the more striking urban trends of recent decades. Among the remedies proposed, banning private cars and repopulating central areas with residents have attracted particular attention, and they merit careful comparison.\n\nA ban on private vehicles promises cleaner air, quieter streets and safer conditions for pedestrians and cyclists. Its critics, notably local retailers, fear that customers will simply take their custom elsewhere. This concern, while understandable, has not generally been borne out where such schemes have been implemented thoughtfully; on the contrary, pedestrianised streets often see an increase in footfall, as people linger rather than merely drive through.\n\nRepopulating city centres, by contrast, tackles the root of the problem: the absence of everyday life outside working hours. As one participant in the discussion observed, centres feel empty and unsafe at night precisely because nobody lives there. Converting redundant office blocks into homes would sustain local businesses, revive evening activity and reduce the need for long commutes. The difficulty lies in ensuring that such housing is affordable, rather than becoming the preserve of the wealthy.\n\nOn balance, I consider repopulation the more effective long-term strategy, since it creates the conditions in which a car-free centre could thrive. A street without cars but also without residents risks remaining just as lifeless as before. Policymakers would therefore be wise to prioritise housing, introducing traffic restrictions progressively as the resident population grows."]]},{"choice":[{"id":"c1x-w2","part":2,"kind":"proposal","t":"Part 2 · Öneri: Öğrenci kafeteryası","in":"The principal of your college has asked students to submit proposals for improving the college canteen. Write a proposal describing the current problems, suggesting improvements and explaining how these would benefit students. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"PROPOSALS INVITED\nHow can we improve our canteen?\nThe college has a small budget available to improve the canteen. Describe the main problems, suggest how the money should be spent, and explain why your suggestions would benefit students.","pts":[["Başlıklar kullan (proposal biçimi)",["introduction","current situation","recommendations","conclusion","purpose"]],["Sorunları anlat",["problem","currently","at present","queue","limited","crowded"]],["Öneriler sun",["recommend","suggest","propose","should","could"]],["Faydaları açıkla",["benefit","would allow","would encourage","as a result","improve"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Proposal for improving the college canteen\n\nIntroduction\nThe purpose of this proposal is to outline the main problems with the current canteen and to recommend how the available budget could be used to address them. It is based on a short survey of sixty students.\n\nCurrent situation\nThe most common complaint concerns the queues at lunchtime, which often take twenty minutes. Since the lunch break lasts only forty-five minutes, many students either skip lunch or eat in a hurry. In addition, the menu offers few healthy or vegetarian options, and the seating area is cramped and noisy, which makes it an unappealing place to relax.\n\nRecommendations\nFirstly, I recommend installing a second serving counter offering pre-prepared items such as salads and sandwiches. This would significantly reduce waiting times. Secondly, the menu could be expanded to include at least two vegetarian dishes each day, ideally chosen with input from students. Finally, a small part of the budget could be spent on replacing some of the large tables with smaller ones and adding plants and sound-absorbing panels.\n\nBenefits\nThese changes would allow students to eat properly during the break, which would in turn improve their concentration in afternoon classes. A more varied menu would encourage healthier eating habits, while a more pleasant environment would make the canteen a place where students choose to spend time and socialise.\n\nConclusion\nWith relatively modest investment, the canteen could become a far more efficient and welcoming space. I strongly recommend that these proposals be considered.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı önerilerle","Proposal: improving the college canteen\n\nIntroduction\nThis proposal identifies the main weaknesses of the college canteen and suggests how the available budget could best be used. It draws on comments from students in three different year groups.\n\nThe current situation\nAlthough the food itself is generally considered acceptable, the canteen suffers from several practical problems. There are no facilities for students who bring their own lunch, such as microwaves, and drinking water is only available if bought in plastic bottles. In addition, the canteen closes at 2 p.m., leaving students with afternoon classes nowhere to buy a snack.\n\nSuggestions\nI would recommend spending part of the budget on two or three microwaves and a free water fountain. These are relatively inexpensive but would make a significant difference to students' daily lives. I would also suggest extending opening hours until 4 p.m., perhaps offering a smaller menu of sandwiches and fruit in the afternoon. Finally, introducing a monthly \"student menu day\", when dishes suggested by students are served, would encourage greater involvement.\n\nBenefits\nThese measures would save students money, reduce plastic waste and ensure that everyone has access to food throughout the day. Involving students in menu choices would also make the canteen feel more like a shared space.\n\nConclusion\nWith modest investment, the canteen could meet the needs of a far wider range of students. I hope the principal will consider these suggestions."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","Proposal for a more sustainable and welcoming canteen\n\nPurpose\nThe aim of this proposal is to recommend improvements to the college canteen that would not only address students' immediate concerns but also reflect the college's stated commitment to sustainability.\n\nKey issues\nFeedback gathered from the student council highlights three recurring complaints. Firstly, the menu relies heavily on processed food, with fresh options often sold out by midday. Secondly, the amount of single-use packaging is considerable, sitting uncomfortably with the college's environmental policy. Thirdly, the canteen is perceived as a place to eat quickly rather than somewhere students would choose to spend time.\n\nRecommendations\nA proportion of the budget could be allocated to forming a partnership with a nearby farm or market, ensuring a regular supply of seasonal produce at reasonable cost. Replacing disposable cups and containers with reusable alternatives, perhaps offered at a small deposit, would substantially reduce waste. Lastly, rearranging the seating to include a quieter area with plants and natural light would encourage students to linger and socialise.\n\nAnticipated benefits\nHealthier meals would support students' concentration and well-being, while a visible reduction in waste would strengthen the college's credibility on environmental issues. A more inviting space, meanwhile, could foster a stronger sense of community across year groups.\n\nConclusion\nThese changes are achievable within the current budget and would bring benefits extending well beyond lunchtime. I would strongly encourage the principal to consider them."]]},{"id":"c1x-w3","part":2,"kind":"review","t":"Part 2 · İnceleme: Bir belgesel","in":"A website is inviting readers to submit reviews of documentaries that have changed the way they think about an issue. Write a review describing the documentary, explaining how it changed your thinking and saying whether you would recommend it. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"REVIEWS WANTED\nA documentary that changed my mind\nTell us about a documentary that made you see an issue differently. What was it about? How did it change your thinking? Would you recommend it to others?","pts":[["Belgeseli tanıt",["documentary","follows","explores","about","director"]],["Düşüncenin nasıl değiştiğini açıkla",["used to think","changed","realised","made me","before watching"]],["Güçlü / zayıf yönleri değerlendir",["however","although","powerful","weakness","strength","criticism"]],["Tavsiye et",["recommend","worth","anyone who","must-see"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"The hidden cost of fast fashion\n\nBefore watching \"Threads\", I considered myself a fairly responsible consumer. I recycled, I avoided plastic bags, and I rarely gave a second thought to the clothes I bought. This quietly devastating documentary changed that completely.\n\nThe film follows a single cotton T-shirt from a field in Central Asia, through a factory in Bangladesh, to a shop window in London and, eventually, to a mountain of discarded clothing in Ghana. Rather than lecturing the audience, the director lets the people involved tell their own stories: a farmer struggling with polluted water, a seamstress working fourteen-hour days, a trader who sorts through bales of unwanted clothes from Europe.\n\nWhat struck me most was not any single shocking fact, but the cumulative effect. By the end, I could no longer see a cheap T-shirt as a bargain; I saw it as a chain of decisions whose consequences fall on people I will never meet. I have since cut down drastically on the clothes I buy and started repairing rather than replacing.\n\nIf the film has a weakness, it is that it offers few practical solutions, leaving viewers feeling somewhat powerless. A short final section on what individuals and governments can do would have made it even more effective.\n\nNevertheless, I would recommend \"Threads\" to anyone who buys clothes – which is to say, everyone. It is beautifully filmed, deeply human and, unlike many documentaries on the subject, never preachy. You may not look at your wardrobe in the same way again.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı bir belgesel","The ocean in a single bottle\n\nI had always assumed that plastic pollution was a problem caused by careless people dropping litter on beaches. \"Blue Silence\", a documentary following a team of marine scientists across the Pacific, showed me how naive that view was.\n\nThe film traces the journey of plastic from our kitchens to the most remote parts of the ocean, where it breaks down into tiny particles that are eaten by fish and, eventually, by us. Its most memorable sequence takes place on an uninhabited island, where the beaches are covered in rubbish that has drifted thousands of kilometres.\n\nWhat changed my thinking was realising that recycling, which I had always considered the solution, is only a small part of the answer. Much of what we put in recycling bins is never actually recycled. The documentary convinced me that reducing the amount of plastic we use in the first place is far more important, and since watching it, I have changed many of my shopping habits.\n\nThe film is not perfect. At times the dramatic music feels manipulative, and some of the statistics are presented too quickly to absorb. Nonetheless, I would recommend \"Blue Silence\" to anyone who cares about the environment, and particularly to those who, like me, believe they are already doing enough. It is uncomfortable viewing, but that is precisely why it matters."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","Rethinking old age: \"The Long Afternoon\"\n\nFew documentaries manage to overturn assumptions so gently, yet so thoroughly, as \"The Long Afternoon\", which follows five residents of a care home in northern Italy over the course of a year.\n\nI went into the film expecting a melancholy portrait of decline. What I found instead was a story of curiosity and reinvention. One resident, a former factory worker in her nineties, learns to paint for the first time; another, once a strict headmaster, forms an unlikely friendship with a teenage volunteer. The director never intrudes, allowing long, unhurried scenes to reveal character rather than imposing a narrative.\n\nThe film challenged my rather lazy assumption that old age is primarily a period of loss. It made me realise how often we speak about older people rather than listening to them, and how much our society loses as a result. I have since started visiting my own grandfather more regularly, not out of duty but out of genuine interest in his stories.\n\nIf the film has a flaw, it is that it largely avoids the harder realities of ageing, such as illness and loneliness, which some viewers may find evasive.\n\nEven so, I would recommend \"The Long Afternoon\" without hesitation, particularly to younger viewers. It is quietly moving, often funny and, above all, a powerful reminder that a life is never simply over."]]}]}]},"S":{"parts":[{"id":"c1x-s1","p":1,"t":"Part 1 · Tanışma","ins":"Sınav görevlisi seninle ilgili sorular soruyor. Doğal, gelişmiş ve örnekli cevaplar ver.","q":[["What do you find most rewarding about your studies or work at the moment?",30,["what I find most rewarding","I'd say","particularly"],["I'd say what I find most rewarding is the feeling of finally understanding something complex. I'm studying economics, and there's a particular satisfaction when a theory that seemed abstract suddenly makes sense of something in the news. It makes all the hours of reading feel worthwhile.","I work as a volunteer teacher at the moment, and the most rewarding thing is seeing students' confidence grow. Some of them barely spoke at the beginning of the year, and now they're happily debating with each other. That kind of progress means far more to me than any grade."]],["If you could live in any period of history, which would you choose and why?",40,["I'd be tempted","I'd have to say","largely because","having said that"],["I'd be tempted to choose the late nineteenth century, largely because so many inventions we take for granted, like electricity, the telephone and photography, were appearing for the first time. It must have been an extraordinary time to be alive. Having said that, I'm not sure I'd cope without modern medicine, and the working conditions for most people were appalling.","I'd choose Istanbul in the sixteenth century, at the height of the Ottoman Empire. The city must have been an incredible meeting point of cultures, languages and trade, and the architecture being built then still amazes people today. Of course, as an ordinary person, life would probably have been far less glamorous than I imagine."]],["How do you usually make important decisions?",30,["I tend to","weigh up","gut feeling","in the end"],["I tend to weigh up the pros and cons quite carefully, sometimes even writing them down. I also like to talk things through with people I trust. But in the end, I often find myself going with my gut feeling anyway – the list mostly helps me understand why I feel the way I do.","I'm quite impulsive, to be honest. If something feels right, I usually go for it without overthinking. That approach has led to a few mistakes, but also to some of my best experiences, like moving abroad for a year on fairly short notice."]]]},{"id":"c1x-s2","p":2,"t":"Part 2 · Fotoğraflar: zor koşullarda çalışmak","ins":"Üç fotoğraftan ikisini seç ve yaklaşık 1 dakika karşılaştır. Soruların ikisini de cevapla.","img":[["🚒🔥🧑‍🚒","Fotoğraf 1: Bir yangına müdahale eden itfaiyeciler.","c1-fire","Two firefighters are using a hose to put out a fire in a building."],["🏔️🧗‍♀️❄️","Fotoğraf 2: Karlı bir dağda kurtarma ekibi.","c1-rescue","Two mountain rescuers are carrying an injured person on a stretcher in the snow."],["🏥🩺🌙","Fotoğraf 3: Gece vardiyasında yoğun bir acil serviste çalışan doktor.","c1-doctor","A doctor is checking on a patient in a hospital at night. A heart monitor is on the wall."]],"q":[["Look at the pictures. They show people working in demanding situations. Compare two of the pictures and say what skills these people need, and how they might be feeling.",60,["whereas","both","they would need","I'd imagine","under pressure","judging by"],["I'll compare the firefighters and the doctor in the emergency department. Both are clearly working under enormous pressure, where mistakes could cost lives. The firefighters need physical strength and courage, and above all the ability to work as a team, since they rely on each other completely in a dangerous situation. The doctor, by contrast, needs to make rapid decisions while staying calm and focused, often after hours without sleep, and she also needs to communicate clearly with patients who may be frightened. I'd imagine the firefighters are feeling a rush of adrenaline, perhaps mixed with fear, whereas the doctor might be experiencing a kind of quiet exhaustion, judging by the fact that it's the middle of the night.","I'd like to compare the mountain rescue team and the firefighters. Both groups are working in hostile, unpredictable environments, and both need to be extremely fit. The rescue team, though, also needs specialist knowledge of climbing and navigation, as well as the patience to search for hours in freezing conditions. The firefighters have to act much faster, assessing risks in seconds. In terms of feelings, I imagine the rescuers might be anxious, especially if they haven't found the missing person yet and the weather is getting worse. The firefighters are probably highly focused, almost on autopilot, relying on their training, although they must be aware of the danger to their own lives."]],["Which of these jobs do you think is the most stressful? Why?",30,["I'd say","arguably","because"],["I'd say the doctor's, arguably, because the pressure never really stops. A fire eventually ends, but an emergency department is busy every single night, and doctors have to deal with the emotional weight of patients who don't survive.","I'd go for the firefighters, because they're putting their own lives at risk every time they go to work. Stress isn't just about workload; knowing you might not come home must be incredibly difficult for them and their families."]]]},{"id":"c1x-s3","p":3,"t":"Part 3 · Birlikte karar verme","ins":"Gerçek sınavda partnerle yapılır. Önce seçenekleri tartış (2 dakika), sonra karar ver (1 dakika).","opts":["📱 social media","📰 traditional newspapers","👨‍👩‍👧 family","🎓 teachers","🎬 celebrities"],"q":[["Here are some things that can influence young people's opinions. Talk together about how much influence these things have on young people's opinions today.",120,["I'd argue","to a certain extent","that's a fair point","it could be argued","whereas","undoubtedly"],["I'd argue social media is undoubtedly the biggest influence today, simply because young people spend so much time on it, and algorithms tend to show them more of what they already believe, which can reinforce their opinions without them realising. Traditional newspapers, by contrast, have much less influence than they once did, although their content is often shared online, so they still shape debates indirectly. Family is still important, especially on values and on issues like religion or politics, but perhaps less so on specific topics like technology or the environment, where young people often know more than their parents. Teachers can have a profound influence on some students, particularly in encouraging them to question things and look at evidence. And celebrities – to a certain extent they influence fashion and lifestyle choices, but I'm not sure young people take their political views very seriously, unless the celebrity has real expertise. One thing all these influences have in common, though, is that they're more powerful when they reinforce each other. If a young person hears the same message from their teachers, their family and the people they follow online, it's likely to shape their views quite profoundly. Whereas when these sources contradict each other, young people are forced to think for themselves, which is arguably healthier.","Let me start with family, because I think its influence is often underestimated. Even when teenagers rebel against their parents' views, they're still reacting to them, so family shapes their thinking either way. Teachers matter too, though it really depends on the individual teacher – a passionate history teacher, for example, can change how students see the world. Social media clearly has enormous reach, but I'd say its influence is quite shallow; it affects what young people talk about, but not necessarily what they deeply believe. Traditional newspapers seem almost irrelevant to most of my friends, sadly. As for celebrities, they can raise awareness of causes, such as climate change or mental health, which can be positive, although sometimes it feels more like publicity than genuine commitment. Overall, I'd say the influence of each depends heavily on the individual. A teenager with a close family and inspiring teachers may barely be affected by what celebrities say online, whereas someone who feels isolated may rely on social media for almost everything. That's why it's difficult to generalise, and why media literacy is so important."]],["Now you have about a minute to decide which of these has the most positive influence on young people.",60,["if we had to choose","on balance","I'd go for","even though"],["If we had to choose, I'd go for teachers. Even though their influence is less constant than social media's, it tends to be more thoughtful, and good teachers encourage young people to form their own opinions rather than simply adopting someone else's. They also teach the skills needed to evaluate information critically, which protects young people from the more negative influences on the list. Unlike social media, teachers also have a professional responsibility to be balanced, which makes their influence more trustworthy.","On balance, I'd say family has the most positive influence, at least in most cases. Parents usually have their children's best interests at heart, and the values they pass on – honesty, respect, hard work – tend to last a lifetime. Social media influences change constantly, but family influence is a foundation. Of course, this assumes a supportive family; where that isn't the case, teachers can play a crucial role in filling the gap, which is why I'd put them a close second."]]]},{"id":"c1x-s4","p":4,"t":"Part 4 · Tartışma","ins":"3. bölümün konusuyla ilgili genel sorular. Görüşünü gerekçelerle ve farklı bakış açılarını da değerlendirerek açıkla.","q":[["Do you think young people today are better or worse at thinking critically than previous generations?",45,["it's debatable","on the one hand","by the same token","I suspect"],["It's debatable. On the one hand, they have access to far more information than any previous generation and are used to questioning sources, at least in theory. By the same token, though, the sheer quantity of information can be overwhelming, and I suspect many people rely on headlines or short videos rather than reading in depth. So I'd say young people have the tools to think critically, but not always the habit. Previous generations had fewer sources, but they were perhaps more likely to read a whole article before forming an opinion.","I'd say better, in some ways. Young people today are much more aware of issues like bias and fake news, partly because they've grown up with them. They're also more willing to challenge authority than previous generations. However, critical thinking takes time and patience, and the fast pace of social media doesn't always encourage that. Schools have a role to play in teaching young people to slow down and check before sharing something."]],["Should schools teach students how to identify false information online?",45,["absolutely","it's essential","not least because","in my view"],["Absolutely. In my view, it's as essential today as learning to read, not least because false information can have real consequences, from health decisions to elections. Students should learn practical techniques, like checking the original source, looking for evidence and recognising manipulated images. Ideally, this should be part of every subject rather than a separate lesson. A science teacher could show how to read a study, for example, and a history teacher could compare different sources.","Yes, although I think it's a responsibility shared with parents and the media companies themselves. Schools can teach the basics, but technology changes so quickly that what students learn at fourteen might be outdated by the time they're twenty. So the focus should be on general habits of scepticism rather than specific tricks. Asking who created something and why is a question that will never become outdated."]],["To what extent should people with influence take responsibility for what they say?",45,["to a large extent","that said","there's a balance","freedom of expression"],["To a large extent, I think they should, because their words reach millions of people, many of them young and impressionable. If a celebrity spreads false health advice, for instance, the consequences can be serious. That said, there's a balance to be struck with freedom of expression – we can't expect everyone to be an expert on every topic, and people have the right to share opinions. Perhaps the key is transparency: influencers should make it clear when they're being paid to promote something.","I'd argue that influence brings responsibility, in the same way that we expect journalists to check their facts. Platforms could do more to label misleading content from high-profile accounts. At the same time, audiences have a responsibility too: we shouldn't automatically believe someone just because they're famous. Being critical of the people we admire is difficult, but it's an essential part of being an informed citizen."]]]}]}},"lv":"C1","name":"C1 Advanced"}],"prac":{"A2":{"R":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","id":"a2r1","t":"Shop and School Notices","ty":"mc","q":[{"q":"What does the sign say?","o":["Only children over eight may use the pool.","Children under eight can't swim without an adult.","Adults can't swim while children are in the pool."],"a":1,"tg":["must-have-to-should"],"e":"8 yaşından küçükler bir yetişkinle yüzmeli: tek başına yüzemez.","x":"SWIMMING POOL\nChildren under 8 must swim with an adult."},{"q":"What does Dad tell Jack?","o":["Mr Green is ill and can't teach today.","Jack should phone Mr Green at 4.","The lesson is an hour later than normal."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Ders 4 yerine 5'te: her zamankinden geç.","x":"Jack,\nYour guitar lesson is at 5 today, not 4. Mr Green phoned. Don't be late!\nDad"},{"q":"What does the shop say?","o":["Three T-shirts cost the same as two.","Shoes are 30% cheaper if you buy two pairs.","Every T-shirt costs 30% less than before."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"buy two, get one free\": iki alana bir bedava.","x":"SALE\nAll shoes 30% off this weekend.\nT-shirts: buy two, get one free!"},{"q":"Why will the library close early on Friday?","o":["The librarian is away on a school trip.","Some teachers are having a meeting.","The students are taking an exam at 3."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"because of a teachers' meeting\".","x":"To all students:\nThe school library will close at 3 p.m. on Friday because of a teachers' meeting."},{"q":"What does Zoe want Mia to do?","o":["take Zoe's broken camera to a shop","bring her camera to the party","buy Zoe a new camera as a present"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"\"Can you bring your camera\" = partiye fotoğraf makinesini getirmesini istiyor.","x":"Hi Mia,\nCan you bring your camera to the party tomorrow? Mine is broken.\nZoe"}]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Kişi eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Üç metni oku. Her soru için doğru kişiyi (A, B ya da C) seç. Bir kişi birden fazla kez seçilebilir.","id":"a2r2","t":"New Schools","ty":"match","pl":"Yazılar","opts":[["A","Can","I moved to a new school in September. It's much bigger than my old one – there are 1,200 students! At first I got lost every day. Now I know my way around, and I've joined the photography club. The lessons are harder here, but my teachers are very helpful."],["B","Sophie","My new school is in a small village, so I take the bus for forty minutes every morning. There are only twelve students in my class, and we all know each other well. I miss my old friends, but we talk online every evening. The best thing here is the school garden – we grow our own vegetables."],["C","Leo","I started at my new school last month. I was nervous on the first day, but a boy called Sam showed me around and now he's my best friend. The school has a great sports hall, and I play basketball there after school. The only problem is lunch – the food isn't very good!"]],"q":[{"q":"Who travels a long way to school?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Sophie her sabah 40 dakika otobüsle gidiyor."},{"q":"Who doesn't like the school food?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Leo: \"the food isn't very good\"."},{"q":"Who finds the lessons more difficult now?","a":"A","tg":["comparatives-superlatives"],"e":"Can: \"The lessons are harder here\"."},{"q":"Who stays in contact with old friends?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Sophie eski arkadaşlarıyla her akşam internetten konuşuyor."},{"q":"Who made a friend on the first day?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Sam, Leo'ya okulu gezdirdi ve en iyi arkadaşı oldu."},{"q":"Who had problems finding places in the school?","a":"A","tg":["inference"],"e":"Can: \"At first I got lost every day\"."}]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","id":"a2r3","t":"A Young Swimmer","ty":"mc","title":"A young swimmer","p":["Maya Lee is fourteen and she is one of the best young swimmers in the country. She started swimming when she was five, because her parents wanted her to be safe in the water. She wasn't very good at first, and she was afraid of putting her face in the water.","Now Maya trains six days a week. She gets up at half past five and swims for two hours before school. After school she goes to the gym. \"It's difficult,\" she says, \"but I love the feeling when I win a race.\"","Maya doesn't have much time to see her friends, but they often come to watch her races. Her best friend Hana makes big signs with Maya's name on them. Last year Maya won a silver medal at the national championships. Next year she hopes to swim for her country."],"q":[{"q":"Why did Maya start swimming?","o":["She wanted to swim with her best friend.","Her doctor said it was good for her.","Her parents wanted her to be safe in water."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"because her parents wanted her to be safe in the water\"."},{"q":"What was Maya's problem when she started?","o":["Putting her face in the water scared her.","She didn't get on with her first teacher.","The other children were much faster."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yüzünü suya sokmaktan korkuyordu."},{"q":"What does Maya do before school?","o":["She does an hour of exercises at the gym.","She trains in the pool for two hours.","She has a long breakfast with her friends."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","present-simple"],"e":"5.30'da kalkıp okuldan önce iki saat yüzüyor. Spor salonu okuldan sonra."},{"q":"How does Hana help Maya?","o":["She drives Maya to all her races.","She trains with Maya before school.","She makes big signs for Maya's races."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Hana üzerinde Maya'nın adı olan büyük pankartlar yapıyor."},{"q":"What does Maya want to do next year?","o":["swim for her country's team","spend more time with her friends","win her first national medal"],"a":0,"tg":["will-going-to"],"e":"\"Next year she hopes to swim for her country\"."}]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için doğru kelimeyi seç.","id":"a2r4","t":"Coffee","ty":"cloze","title":"Coffee","p":"People (1) ______ coffee for hundreds of years. Today it is one of the (2) ______ popular drinks in the world. Coffee comes from a plant that grows in hot countries, (3) ______ as Brazil and Ethiopia. Brazil produces more coffee (4) ______ any other country. Many people drink coffee in the morning because it helps them (5) ______ awake. But doctors say you (6) ______ drink too much of it.","o":[[["have drunk","drink","are drinking"],0,["present-perfect"]],[["more","most","much"],1,["comparatives-superlatives"]],[["like","such","so"],1,["vocab"]],[["then","that","than"],2,["comparatives-superlatives"]],[["stay","be staying","staying"],0,["vocab"]],[["mustn't","shouldn't","don't have to"],1,["must-have-to-should"]]],"e":["for hundreds of years: geçmişten bugüne süren durum → present perfect.","the most popular: en popüler.","such as: örneğin, gibi.","more ... than: -den daha çok.","help someone (to) do: birine bir şeyi yapmada yardım etmek; stay awake.","Tavsiye: shouldn't (doktorlar söylüyor; yasak değil)."]},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","id":"a2r5","t":"A Message from Camp","ty":"open","one":true,"title":"Hi Dan","p":"Hi Dan,\nI'm writing this message (1) ______ the campsite! We arrived (2) ______ Tuesday. The weather is great and the lake is beautiful. Yesterday we went canoeing and I fell (3) ______ the water! It was very cold. Tomorrow we (4) ______ going to climb the mountain. I hope I won't be too tired. What are you doing this week? Write (5) ______ me soon!\nSee you,\n(6) ______ friend Alex","a":[["from","at"],["on","last"],["into","in"],["are"],["to"],["your"]],"tg":[["prepositions-in-on-at"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["vocab"],["will-going-to"],["vocab"],["possessives"]],"e":["from/at the campsite: kamp yerinden / kamp yerinde.","Günlerle on: on Tuesday.","fall into the water: suya düşmek.","we are going to: plan.","Write to me: bana yaz.","your friend: arkadaşın."]},{"k":"R4","id":"a2r4b","t":"My Weekend","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 4 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B ya da C) seç.","title":"My weekend","p":"On Saturday I (1) ______ up late because I was tired. After breakfast, I went to the park (2) ______ my friends. We played football for two hours, and then we (3) ______ ice cream. In the evening, I stayed at home and (4) ______ a film. It was (5) ______ than the last one I saw. On Sunday, I'm going (6) ______ my grandmother's house.","o":[[["got","get","getting"],0,["past-simple"]],[["with","at","for"],0,["prepositions-in-on-at"]],[["had","have","having"],0,["past-simple"]],[["watching","watched","watch"],1,["past-simple"]],[["good","better","best"],1,["comparatives-superlatives"]],[["at","to","in"],1,["prepositions-in-on-at"]]],"e":["Saturday: geçmiş, got up (Past Simple).","with my friends: arkadaşlarımla.","geçmiş olay: we had ice cream.","stayed ... and watched: iki geçmiş fiil.","than ile karşılaştırma: better.","go to + yer: to."]},{"k":"R5","id":"a2r5b","t":"A Message About School","ty":"open","nm":"Okuma 5 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","title":"A message about school","one":true,"p":"Hi Lena, I'm writing (1) ______ tell you about my new school. It is big, and there (2) ______ a lot of students. My favourite lesson (3) ______ English because the teacher is very kind. I usually go home (4) ______ bus, but today my dad picked me up. What (5) ______ you doing on Friday? Do you want (6) ______ come to the cinema with me?","a":[["to"],["are"],["is"],["by"],["are"],["to"]],"tg":[["gerund-infinitive"],["there-is-are"],["to-be"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["to-be"],["gerund-infinitive"]],"e":["amaç: write to tell.","there are + çoğul.","my favourite lesson is: tekil özne.","by bus: otobüsle.","What are you doing: Present Continuous sorusu.","want to + yalın fiil."]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_market2","t":"A Day at the Market","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"A Day at the Market","p":["Every Saturday morning, Elif goes to the village market with her mother. The market opens at seven o'clock, and they like to arrive early because the vegetables are fresh and there are not many people. Elif does not like to wake up early on school days, but on Saturdays she is always ready before her mother.","First, they buy vegetables. Her mother likes to choose every tomato and pepper carefully, and she never buys anything before she checks the price at two or three stands. Today they buy two kilos of tomatoes, some green peppers and a big cabbage. The farmer, Mr. Hasan, always puts an extra lemon in their bag and says, \"This one is a present for the best customers.\"","Then Elif's mother talks to the cheese seller for a long time. They talk about the weather, the prices and their children. She always buys a small piece of white cheese and some olives for breakfast. Elif waits next to her and looks at the colourful scarves at the next stand, but her mother says they do not need a new scarf.","Elif's favourite place is the bread stand. The baker is a kind old man, and he gives her a warm piece of bread every week. She eats it slowly while they walk through the market. It smells so good that other children look at her with hungry eyes!","At ten o'clock they walk home with two heavy bags. Elif carries the lighter one with the bread and the lemons. She is tired but happy. In the afternoon, her mother cooks a big lunch for the whole family, and Elif's grandfather and two uncles come to eat with them. After lunch, everybody sits in the garden and drinks tea."],"q":[{"q":"Why do Elif and her mother arrive early?","o":["The market closes at eight o'clock sharp.","Elif wants to see the baker before shopping.","Vegetables are fresh and the market is quiet.","Her mother must cook lunch before ten."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Metinde: \"the vegetables are fresh and there are not many people.\""},{"q":"What does Elif's mother do before she buys something?","o":["She asks Elif which vegetables look best.","She compares the price at two or three stands.","She phones her husband to ask about prices.","She tastes a little of everything first."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"she never buys anything before she checks the price at two or three stands.\""},{"q":"What does Mr. Hasan give to the customers?","o":["A big cabbage","An extra lemon","A colourful scarf","A piece of warm bread"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"puts an extra lemon in their bag.\""},{"q":"What does Elif's mother always buy from the cheese seller?","o":["White cheese and olives","Yellow cheese and some bread","Olives and a few green peppers","White cheese and fresh milk"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"a small piece of white cheese and some olives for breakfast.\""},{"q":"Why does Elif look at the scarves?","o":["They are colourful, but her mother says no.","Her mother wants to buy one for her today.","The seller offers her a free one as a gift.","She wants to sell her old scarf at the stall."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"her mother says they do not need a new scarf.\""},{"q":"What happens after lunch?","o":["Elif goes to her room to do homework.","The family walks to the market again.","Her uncles take her to visit the baker.","Everyone has tea together in the garden."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"everybody sits in the garden and drinks tea.\""}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_neighbour2","t":"My New Neighbour","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"My New Neighbour","p":["Last month, a new family moved into the flat next to ours. The father is a doctor at the city hospital and the mother teaches music at a primary school. They have a ten-year-old son called Can and a small brown dog named Pamuk. On the first day, I saw many boxes in the corridor and a big piano that four men carried up the stairs.","At first, I was a little shy, so I only said hello when we met in the lift. Can also looked shy and he only smiled. I wanted to talk to him, but I did not know what to say. My mother said, \"Give them some time. Moving to a new place is not easy.\"","Then one rainy evening, somebody knocked on our door. It was Can. His dog had run away through the open door, and he was very worried. He asked if we had seen Pamuk. I took my coat and an umbrella, and we looked for the dog together. We checked the garden, the car park and the small street behind our building.","Finally, we found Pamuk under a bench behind the building. He was wet and afraid of the thunder. Can took him in his arms and said thank you about five times. We walked home together, and my mother made hot chocolate for both of us.","Now Can and I play football in the garden every weekend, and Pamuk always runs after the ball. Can's mother also gives my younger sister piano lessons on Tuesdays. Last week, they invited our family for dinner. I am very happy that we have such nice neighbours, and I am glad that I did not stay shy."],"q":[{"q":"What does Can's mother do?","o":["She plays football for a local team.","She teaches music at a primary school.","She works as a doctor at a hospital.","She looks after the car park."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the mother teaches music at a primary school.\""},{"q":"Why was the writer shy at first?","o":["He did not know what to say to Can.","He did not like the noise from their flat.","He was afraid of the new family's dog.","He was ill and stayed at home."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I wanted to talk to him, but I did not know what to say.\""},{"q":"Why did Can knock on the door?","o":["His dog had run away and he was looking for it.","He wanted to ask the writer to play football.","He needed to borrow a piano for his mother.","He wanted some hot chocolate from the writer."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"His dog had run away... he asked if we had seen Pamuk.\""},{"q":"Where did they find Pamuk?","o":["Inside the lift on the ground floor","Next to the cars in the car park","On the stairs near the entrance","Under a bench behind the building"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"under a bench behind the building.\""},{"q":"Why was Pamuk afraid?","o":["It was hungry and had no home.","It did not like the football.","It was scared of a big cat.","It was afraid of the thunder."],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"He was wet and afraid of the thunder.\""},{"q":"What happened last week?","o":["The writer's family moved to a bigger flat nearby.","Can started going to the writer's school.","The new neighbours invited the family to dinner.","Pamuk ran away again and got lost in the rain."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Last week, they invited our family for dinner.\""}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_phone2","t":"The Lost Phone","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Lost Phone","p":["On Monday, Murat took the bus to work as usual. It was a very crowded bus, and he had to stand all the way. He read the news on his phone for a few minutes, and then he put it in his jacket pocket. When he got off the bus at the city centre, he was thinking about an important meeting at nine o'clock.","When he arrived at his office, he put his hand in his pocket and his phone was not there. He felt cold and a bit sick. All his photos, his work emails and the phone numbers of his friends and family were on it. He looked in his bag and under his desk, but he could not find it.","Then he had an idea. He called his own number from the office phone. Nobody answered at first. He tried again, and then a third and a fourth time. After the fifth call, a woman picked up. \"Hello?\" she said. \"Is this your phone? I found it on the seat of the bus. I was just going to take it to the police station.\"","Murat was so happy that he could not speak for a moment. They agreed to meet at a small café near the bus stop during his lunch break. The woman, who was called Selma, was a nurse and she was going to work in the afternoon.","At the café, Murat thanked her many times and wanted to give her some money, but she said no. \"I know how it feels to lose something important,\" she said with a smile. \"A few years ago, someone found my bag and returned it. Now it is my turn.\" Murat bought her a coffee and a piece of cake instead. That afternoon, he sent a message to all his friends: \"Always put a password on your phone, and sometimes there are still good people in the world.\""],"q":[{"q":"Where did Murat probably lose his phone?","o":["On the bus","In his office","In a café","At home"],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Selma telefonu otobüsün koltuğunda buldu."},{"q":"Why was Murat worried?","o":["He was late for an important meeting at work.","He had lost all of the money in his wallet.","His boss was angry about the missing phone.","All his photos, emails and numbers were on it."],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"All his photos, his work emails and the phone numbers... were on it.\""},{"q":"How did Murat find his phone?","o":["He asked the bus driver to look for it again.","He went to the police station to report it.","A friend found it on the street and called him.","He called his own number and a woman answered."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Kendi numarasını aradı; beşinci aramada Selma açtı."},{"q":"What does Selma do for a living?","o":["She is a teacher.","She is a bus driver.","She is a nurse.","She is a police officer."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"was a nurse.\""},{"q":"Why did Selma not take any money?","o":["She already had plenty of money in her bag.","She was in a hurry to get to her night shift.","Someone had once returned her lost bag.","She did not want to talk to Murat any longer."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"someone found my bag and returned it. Now it is my turn.\""},{"q":"What did Murat buy for Selma?","o":["A big bunch of flowers and a card","A coffee and a piece of cake","A lunch at a nice restaurant","A new case for her own phone"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Murat bought her a coffee and a piece of cake.\""}]}],"L":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Beş kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","id":"a2l1","t":"Everyday Situations","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"a2l1a","sc":[["M","How much are these apples?"],["F","They're two pounds a kilo. But the green ones are cheaper – one pound fifty."],["M","I'll have a kilo of the green ones, please."]],"q":"How much will the man pay?","o":["£3.50","£2.00","£1.50"],"a":2,"tg":["spelling"],"e":"Yeşil elmalar kilosu 1,50."},{"id":"a2l1b","sc":[["F","What did you do on Saturday?"],["M","I wanted to go to the beach, but it was raining. So I stayed at home and cleaned my room."]],"q":"What did the boy do on Saturday?","o":["He went to the beach with a friend.","He tidied his room at home.","He stayed in and watched TV."],"a":1,"tg":["past-simple"],"e":"Yağmur yüzünden evde kalıp odasını temizledi."},{"id":"a2l1c","sc":[["M","Excuse me, where's the post office?"],["F","Go straight on, then turn left at the bank. It's next to the supermarket."]],"q":"Where is the post office?","o":["next to the bank","next to the supermarket","opposite the supermarket"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"It's next to the supermarket\"."},{"id":"a2l1d","sc":[["F","Is your sister older than you?"],["M","No, she's younger. I'm twelve and she's nine. My brother is the oldest – he's sixteen."]],"q":"How old is the boy's sister?","o":["sixteen","nine","twelve"],"a":1,"tg":["comparatives-superlatives"],"e":"Kız kardeşi 9 yaşında."},{"id":"a2l1e","sc":[["M","What would you like to drink?"],["F","I usually have coffee, but it's so hot today. Can I have some orange juice with ice, please?"]],"q":"What will the woman drink?","o":["a cup of hot coffee","iced orange juice","a glass of cold milk"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Sıcak olduğu için buzlu portakal suyu istiyor."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","id":"a2l2","t":"A Bike Tour","ty":"open","title":"Notlar","sc":[["M","Good morning, and welcome to City Bike Tours. Our tour today starts at ten o'clock from the front of the railway station. The tour takes three hours, and we'll stop for lunch at a café by the river. The price is eighteen pounds, and that includes your bike and a helmet. Our guide today is called Jenny – that's J, E, double N, Y. Please wear comfortable shoes and bring a bottle of water. Enjoy your tour!"]],"p":"City Bike Tours\nMeeting place: in front of the (1) ______ station\nLength of tour: (2) ______ hours\nLunch: at a café by the (3) ______\nPrice: £(4) ______\nGuide's name: (5) ______","a":[["railway","train"],["3","three"],["river"],["18","eighteen"],["Jenny"]],"tg":[["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["spelling"]],"e":["\"from the front of the railway station\".","\"The tour takes three hours\".","\"a café by the river\".","\"eighteen pounds\".","J-E-N-N-Y."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Uzun konuşma","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"İki kişinin konuşmasını dinle ve soruları cevapla.","id":"a2l3","t":"The New Sports Club","ty":"mc","sc":[["M","Hi Lucy. I heard you joined the new sports club."],["F","Yes, I joined last week. It's great. It's near the school, so I can walk there."],["M","What sports can you do?"],["F","There's tennis, basketball and swimming. I wanted to try tennis, but the classes were full, so I'm doing basketball."],["M","Is it expensive?"],["F","It's twenty-five pounds a month for students. Adults pay forty."],["M","When do you go?"],["F","On Tuesdays and Thursdays after school. Why don't you come with me on Thursday?"],["M","I can't on Thursday, I have a piano lesson. But I could come on Tuesday."]],"q":[{"q":"How does Lucy get to the sports club?","o":["on foot","by bus","by bike"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Okula yakın, yürüyerek gidiyor."},{"q":"Which sport is Lucy doing?","o":["tennis","basketball","swimming"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Tenis dolu olduğu için basketbol yapıyor."},{"q":"How much does Lucy pay each month?","o":["£15","£25","£40"],"a":1,"tg":["spelling"],"e":"Öğrenciler ayda 25 sterlin."},{"q":"Why can't the boy go on Thursday?","o":["He has basketball practice.","He has to study for a test.","He has a piano lesson."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Perşembe piyano dersi var."},{"q":"When might the boy go to the club?","o":["on Tuesday after school","on Thursday after school","on Friday after school"],"a":0,"tg":["may-might"],"e":"\"I could come on Tuesday\"."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Beş kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","id":"a2l4","t":"Short Messages","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"a2l4a","sc":[["F","Hi Mark, it's Sally. I'm at the shops. Do you want me to buy anything for dinner tonight? Call me back."]],"q":"Why is Sally calling?","o":["to ask Mark to cook dinner","to offer to buy Mark something","to say she will be late home"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Akşam yemeği için bir şey almasını isteyip istemediğini soruyor."},{"id":"a2l4b","sc":[["M","I went to the new Chinese restaurant last night. The food was delicious, but it was very expensive, and we waited forty minutes for a table."]],"q":"What did the man like about the restaurant?","o":["the waiting time","the prices","the food"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Yemek lezzetliydi; fiyat ve bekleme olumsuz."},{"id":"a2l4c","sc":[["F","Attention please. The museum will close in fifteen minutes. Please make your way to the main exit. The gift shop is open until six."]],"q":"Where is the woman speaking?","o":["in a gift shop","at a station","in a museum"],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"The museum will close in fifteen minutes\"."},{"id":"a2l4d","sc":[["M","I usually get up at seven, but today I got up at six because I had to finish my homework before school."]],"q":"Why did the boy get up early today?","o":["He wanted to go running before school.","He had a test at school that morning.","He had to finish his homework."],"a":2,"tg":["detail","past-simple"],"e":"Ödevini bitirmesi gerekiyordu."},{"id":"a2l4e","sc":[["F","You'll love this film. It's about a family who travel around the world in a small boat. It's funny, but it's also quite sad at the end."]],"q":"What is the film about?","o":["a funny story about a school","a family sailing round the world","a sad story about two friends"],"a":1,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Küçük bir teknede dünyayı dolaşan bir aile."}]},{"k":"L5","nm":"Dinleme 5 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Listening Part 5","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her kişiyi doğru seçenekle (A–H) eşleştir. Üç seçenek fazladır.","id":"a2l5","t":"Birthday Presents","ty":"match","sc":[["F","Dad, it's Grandma's birthday on Sunday. What is everyone giving her?"],["M","Well, your uncle Peter is giving her some flowers from his garden."],["F","And Aunt Rose? She usually gives her a book."],["M","Not this year. She's bought her a scarf, a red one."],["F","What about Tom?"],["M","Tom's made a cake. He's been practising for weeks!"],["F","And Mum?"],["M","Mum's giving her some theatre tickets. They're going together next month."],["F","I want to give her something special too. Maybe I'll paint a picture of her garden."],["M","She'll love that."]],"opts":[["A","a book"],["B","a cake"],["C","flowers"],["D","a painting"],["E","a scarf"],["F","theatre tickets"],["G","chocolates"],["H","a photo album"]],"q":[{"q":"Uncle Peter","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Bahçesinden çiçek."},{"q":"Aunt Rose","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Bu yıl kitap değil, kırmızı bir atkı."},{"q":"Tom","a":"B","tg":["detail","present-perfect"],"e":"Tom pasta yaptı."},{"q":"Mum","a":"F","tg":["detail"],"e":"Tiyatro biletleri."},{"q":"The girl","a":"D","tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Büyükannesinin bahçesinin resmini yapacak."}]}],"W":[{"id":"a2w1","part":6,"t":"A Birthday Party","in":"You are having a birthday party next Saturday. Write an email to your English friend Sam. In your email:\n• invite Sam to the party\n• say where the party is\n• say what Sam should bring.\nWrite 25 words or more.","mail":"To: Sam\nSubject: My party","pts":[["Partiye davet et",["party","come","would you like","invite"]],["Partinin yerini söyle",["at my house","at the","in the","park","café","restaurant","home"]],["Ne getirmesi gerektiğini söyle",["bring","please bring","can you bring"]]],"min":25,"max":60,"model":"Hi Sam,\nI'm having a birthday party next Saturday and I'd love you to come! It's at my house at 4 o'clock. Please bring your favourite music, and some snacks if you can.\nSee you,\nAyşe","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Sam,\nGuess what? It's my birthday next Saturday and I'm having a party in the garden of our flat. Would you like to come? It starts at 5 o'clock and finishes at about 9. Can you bring some music? Your speaker is much better than mine!\nSee you soon,\nBurak"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","Dear Sam,\nI'm having a party for my fifteenth birthday next Saturday and I'd really love you to come! We're going to have it at Pizza House, the new restaurant opposite the cinema, from 6 to 9 p.m. My mum is paying for the pizza, so you don't need to bring any money. But could you bring your guitar? Everyone wants to hear you play!\nLove,\nDefne"]]},{"id":"a2w2","part":6,"t":"Borrowing a Book","in":"Your English friend Jo has a book you want to read. Write an email to Jo. In your email:\n• ask to borrow the book\n• say why you want to read it\n• say when you will give it back.\nWrite 25 words or more.","mail":"To: Jo\nSubject: Your book","pts":[["Kitabı ödünç iste",["borrow","can i","could i","lend"]],["Neden okumak istediğini söyle",["because","i want","i love","interested"]],["Ne zaman geri vereceğini söyle",["give it back","return","next week","on monday","on friday","in two weeks","bring it back"]]],"min":25,"max":60,"model":"Hi Jo,\nCould I borrow your book about space, please? I want to read it because we're studying the planets at school and it looks really interesting. I'll give it back next Friday.\nThanks,\nMert","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Jo,\nCan I borrow your cookbook for a few days? My mum's birthday is on Sunday and I want to make a special cake for her. I saw a great chocolate cake recipe in your book. I promise I'll give it back on Monday, and I'll bring you a piece of cake too!\nThanks,\nEce"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","Hi Jo,\nI hope you're well. Could I borrow that book about football you showed me last week? I'm writing a project about famous players for my English class, and your book has lots of interesting information and photos. I'll be very careful with it, and I'll give it back to you at school next Friday, after I finish my project.\nThanks a lot,\nKerem"]]},{"id":"a2w3","part":6,"t":"My New Phone","in":"You have just bought a new phone. Write an email to your English friend Chris. In your email:\n• say where you bought it\n• say what colour it is\n• say what you like about it.\nWrite 25 words or more.","mail":"To: Chris\nSubject: My new phone","pts":[["Nereden aldığını söyle",["bought it","from","at the","shop","online","centre"]],["Rengini söyle",["black","white","blue","red","green","silver","gold","pink","purple","grey"]],["Neyini sevdiğini söyle",["like","love","camera","screen","battery","fast","because"]]],"min":25,"max":60,"model":"Hi Chris,\nGuess what? I've got a new phone! I bought it at the shopping centre last weekend. It's dark blue. I really like it because the camera is excellent and the battery lasts all day.\nBye,\nSelin","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Chris,\nI've got a new phone! My grandparents gave me money for my birthday, so I bought it online. It's white with a silver back. The best thing about it is the screen – it's really big, so I can watch films and play games on it. I'll send you a photo!\nTalk soon,\nMelis"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","Hi Chris,\nI finally bought a new phone yesterday! I got it from the big electronics shop in the city centre because it was on sale. It's green, which is my favourite colour. I really love it because the battery lasts for two days and the camera takes amazing photos, even at night. My old phone was so slow, so this one is much better.\nBye for now,\nArda"]]},{"id":"a2w4","part":7,"kind":"story","t":"The Lost Dog","in":"Look at the three pictures. Write the story shown in the pictures.\nWrite 35 words or more.","mail":"Picture 1: 👧🐕🌳  (Bir kız parkta köpeğini gezdiriyor.)\nPicture 2: 🐕💨🐿️  (Köpek bir sincabın peşinden koşup kayboluyor.)\nPicture 3: 👧👮🐕  (Bir polis memuru köpeği kıza geri getiriyor.)","pts":[["Kızı ve köpeği anlat",["dog","walk","walking","park"]],["Köpeğin kaçtığını anlat",["ran","run","squirrel","lost","away"]],["Sonucu anlat",["police","found","brought","back","happy"]],["Geçmiş zaman kullan",["was","went","ran","found","brought","looked"]]],"min":35,"max":80,"model":"One afternoon, Zeynep was walking her dog, Max, in the park. Suddenly Max saw a squirrel and ran after it. Zeynep looked everywhere, but she couldn't find him. She was very worried. An hour later, a police officer brought Max back to her. Zeynep was really happy.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Last Sunday, Elif took her dog, Pamuk, for a walk in the park. Suddenly, Pamuk saw a squirrel and ran after it into the trees. Elif called him again and again, but he didn't come back. She was very sad and started to cry. Then a police officer arrived with Pamuk. He had found him near the lake. Elif thanked him and hugged her dog."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","It was a sunny afternoon, so Selin decided to take her dog, Max, to the park. While they were walking near the trees, Max saw a squirrel and ran after it as fast as he could. Selin looked everywhere for him for an hour, but she couldn't find him and she was very worried. In the end, a kind police officer found Max and brought him back. Selin was so happy that she gave him a big hug."]]},{"id":"a2w5","part":7,"kind":"story","t":"A Day at the Beach","in":"Look at the three pictures. Write the story shown in the pictures.\nWrite 35 words or more.","mail":"Picture 1: 👨‍👩‍👦🚗🏖️  (Bir aile arabayla plaja gidiyor.)\nPicture 2: 🌧️☔😮  (Plajda birden yağmur başlıyor.)\nPicture 3: 🍕🏠😄  (Aile evde pizza yiyip oyun oynuyor.)","pts":[["Plaja gidişi anlat",["beach","car","drove","went"]],["Yağmuru anlat",["rain","rained","raining","suddenly"]],["Sonucu anlat",["home","pizza","game","played","happy","fun"]],["Geçmiş zaman kullan",["was","went","drove","started","ate","played"]]],"min":35,"max":80,"model":"Last summer, Can and his parents drove to the beach. They wanted to swim and play volleyball. But after ten minutes, it suddenly started to rain! They ran back to the car and went home. In the evening they ate pizza and played board games. It was a fun day after all.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Last Saturday, Mert and his family went to the beach by car. When they arrived, they put up their umbrella and Mert ran into the sea. But after a few minutes, black clouds came and it started to rain very hard. They were all wet! So they went home. In the evening, they ordered pizza and played cards. Mert said it was the best rainy day ever."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","One hot day in July, Ada and her parents drove to the beach. They were planning to swim and have a picnic. However, just after they arrived, the sky went dark and it started to rain heavily. Everyone on the beach ran to their cars. Ada was disappointed, but her dad had a good idea. They went home, made pizza together and played board games all evening. In the end, it was a really fun day."]]}],"S":[{"id":"a2s1","p":1,"t":"School and Home","ins":"Kısa sorulara tam cümlelerle cevap ver.","q":[["What's your favourite subject at school? Why?",25,["my favourite","because","I like"],["My favourite subject at school is science. I like it because we do lots of experiments in the lab, and I like learning how things work. Our teacher is very funny, too.","I really like art. I'm not very good at maths, but I love drawing and painting. In art lessons I can relax and be creative, and the time goes very quickly."]],["How do you get to school?",20,["I go","by bus","on foot","I walk","it takes"],["I usually go to school by bus. The bus stop is near my house, and the journey takes about twenty minutes. Sometimes my dad drives me when it's raining.","I walk to school with my best friend. It's not very far – only ten minutes – and we talk all the way. I think walking is good for me."]],["What do you usually have for breakfast?",20,["I usually have","I eat","I drink"],["I usually have bread with cheese, olives and tomatoes, and a glass of tea. At the weekend my mum makes eggs, and we eat together.","On school days I don't have much time, so I usually just have a bowl of cereal with milk and a banana. Sometimes I have orange juice too."]],["Tell me about your bedroom.",30,["there is","there are","my bedroom","I have"],["My bedroom is quite small, but I love it. There's a bed next to the window, a desk where I do my homework, and a big cupboard for my clothes. On the wall there are posters of my favourite football players. I share it with my little brother, so it's sometimes messy!","My bedroom is my favourite room in the house. It's blue and white, and it has a big window, so it's very bright. I've got a comfortable bed, a desk with a computer and lots of books on a shelf. I often listen to music there in the evenings."]]]},{"id":"a2s2","p":1,"t":"Weekends and Holidays","ins":"Kısa sorulara tam cümlelerle cevap ver.","q":[["What did you do last weekend?",25,["last weekend","I went","I visited","I watched"],["Last weekend I visited my grandparents in their village. We had a big lunch together in the garden, and then I played football with my cousins. In the evening we watched a film.","Last weekend I stayed at home because I had a lot of homework. On Sunday afternoon I went to the cinema with my friends and we watched a funny film. It was great."]],["Where did you go on your last holiday?",25,["I went to","we stayed","it was"],["On my last holiday I went to Antalya with my family. We stayed in a hotel near the sea for a week. It was very hot, so we swam every day and ate lots of ice cream.","Last summer we went to my uncle's house in Trabzon. It's in the mountains and it's very green. We went walking every day and we visited a beautiful lake."]],["What are you going to do next summer?",25,["I'm going to","I want to","I hope"],["Next summer I'm going to work in my uncle's shop for a month, because I want to save some money. After that, I hope to go camping with my friends near the sea.","Next summer I'm going to take swimming lessons, because I can't swim very well. I also want to visit my cousin in Istanbul. We're going to go to a concert."]],["Tell me about your best friend.",30,["my best friend","he is","she is","we","because"],["My best friend is called Arda. We've known each other since primary school, so for about eight years. He's tall and he's got short black hair. He's very funny and kind, and he always helps me with maths. We play computer games together every weekend.","My best friend's name is Zeynep. She lives in the same building as me, so we see each other every day. She's quite quiet, but she's very clever and she gives good advice. We both love music, and we often sing together in her room."]]]},{"id":"a2s3","p":2,"t":"Types of Holiday","ins":"Resimlerdeki tatil türleri hakkında kendi görüşünü söyle.","opts":["⛺ camping","🏙️ city holiday","🏖️ beach holiday","⛷️ skiing","🚢 boat trip"],"q":[["Here are some pictures of different holidays. Do you like these different holidays? Say why or why not.",60,["I like","I don't like","because","I think"],["Let me talk about these holidays. I like camping because you can sleep outside, cook on a fire and see the stars at night. A city holiday is interesting too, because you can visit museums and go shopping, but it can be expensive. I love beach holidays – swimming in the sea is my favourite thing in summer. Skiing looks exciting, but I've never tried it, and it's very cold. A boat trip would be fun, but I sometimes feel sick on boats, so I don't think I'd enjoy it very much.","Well, I don't really like camping, because it's uncomfortable and there are lots of insects. I prefer city holidays, because there are so many things to do and see, like old buildings and restaurants. Beach holidays are nice, but they can be boring after a few days. I'd love to try skiing one day because it looks really exciting. And a boat trip sounds great – you can see lots of places and maybe dolphins too!"]],["Which of these holidays would you like best? Why?",30,["I'd like","best","because"],["I'd like a beach holiday best, because I love swimming and relaxing in the sun. I can also play volleyball on the sand with my friends, and the food near the sea is always delicious.","I think I'd choose skiing, because I've never done it and I want to try something new and exciting. I'd also like to see a lot of snow, because it doesn't snow much where I live."]],["Do you prefer holidays with your family or with friends?",30,["I prefer","because"],["I prefer holidays with my family, because my parents plan everything and we have a lot of fun together. My little brother is very funny on holidays, and my dad always finds good restaurants.","I prefer going with my friends, because we like the same things and we can stay up late. With my family I sometimes have to visit places I don't like, for example museums."]]]},{"id":"a2s4","p":2,"t":"Food and Drink","ins":"Resimlerdeki yiyecekler hakkında kendi görüşünü söyle.","opts":["🍕 pizza","🥗 salad","🍣 sushi","🍲 soup","🍰 cake"],"q":[["Here are some pictures of different food. Do you like these different kinds of food? Say why or why not.",60,["I like","I don't like","because","delicious","healthy"],["OK, I love pizza – it's my favourite food! I eat it every Friday with my family. Salad is healthy, but I don't eat it very often because I don't like some vegetables. I've never tried sushi, but my sister likes it a lot. I think raw fish is a bit strange. Soup is nice in winter when it's cold outside, especially my grandmother's lentil soup. And cake is delicious, but I only eat it on special days like birthdays, because it has a lot of sugar.","I don't eat pizza very often because it's not very healthy, but I like it sometimes. I really like salad, especially with lemon and olive oil. Sushi is my favourite – there's a good sushi restaurant near my school and I go there with my friends. I don't really like soup, it's boring for me. Cake is great, especially chocolate cake. My mum makes a very good one."]],["Which of these do you like best? Why?",30,["I like ... best","because","my favourite"],["I like pizza best, because there are so many different kinds and I can eat it with my friends. My favourite is pizza with cheese and mushrooms. We often order it when we watch football.","I think I like cake best. I know it isn't healthy, but it makes me happy! My favourite is strawberry cake, and I always have one on my birthday."]],["Do you like cooking? Why or why not?",30,["I like","I don't","because","sometimes"],["Yes, I like cooking a little. I sometimes help my mother in the kitchen at the weekend. I can make pasta, eggs and a simple salad. I'd like to learn to make my grandmother's soup.","Not really. I don't have much time because of school, and I'm not very good at it. Once I tried to make a cake, but I burned it! My dad does most of the cooking in our house."]]]},{"id":"a2s5","p":2,"t":"Places in Town","ins":"Resimlerdeki yerler hakkında kendi görüşünü söyle.","opts":["🏬 shopping centre","🏞️ park","🏛️ museum","🎬 cinema","🏟️ stadium"],"q":[["Here are some pictures of places in a town. Do you like going to these places? Say why or why not.",60,["I like","I don't like","because","sometimes"],["I like going to the shopping centre with my friends, because we can look at clothes and eat something, but it's often very crowded at the weekend. Parks are great because they're quiet and green, and you can walk or play sport. Museums can be interesting, but some are boring for me. I love the cinema – I go every month and I always buy popcorn. I've been to the stadium twice to watch football, and it was very exciting because everyone was singing.","I don't really like shopping centres, because they're noisy and I don't like shopping. I prefer parks – I go running in the park near my house every morning. I think museums are interesting, especially history museums. The cinema is OK, but tickets are expensive now. I've never been to a stadium, but I'd like to go to a basketball match one day."]],["Which place do you go to most often? Why?",30,["most often","because","usually"],["I go to the park most often, because it's near my house and I can play basketball there with my friends after school. It's free, too! In summer we also have picnics there.","I go to the shopping centre most often, because my friends and I meet there on Saturdays. There's a café we like, and sometimes we watch a film there."]],["Is it better to live in a city or in the countryside? Why?",30,["I think","better","because"],["I think it's better to live in a city, because there are more things to do, like cinemas, cafés and sports centres. But the countryside is cleaner and quieter, and people are friendlier there.","I think the countryside is better, because there's no traffic and the air is clean. You can grow vegetables and have animals. Cities are exciting, but they're too noisy for me."]]]}]},"B1":{"R":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","ty":"mc","q":[{"x":"LIBRARY\nBooks must be returned within three weeks. You can renew them online if nobody else has asked for them.","q":"What does the notice say?","o":["You must pay a fine if you return a book late.","A book can be kept longer if no one else wants it.","You have to visit the library to renew a book."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","must-have-to-should"],"e":"\"renew them online if nobody else has asked for them\" = kimse beklemiyorsa süreyi uzatabilirsin."},{"x":"Dad,\nThe plumber phoned. He can't come today, but he could come tomorrow at 8 a.m. if someone is at home. Can you call him back?\nKate","q":"What does Kate want her father to do?","o":["be at home when the plumber comes today","phone the plumber to arrange a visit","find a different plumber for tomorrow"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"\"Can you call him back?\" = tesisatçıyı geri araması isteniyor."},{"x":"FOR SALE\nChildren's bike, 2 years old, hardly used. Suitable for ages 6–9. Price can be discussed.","q":"What does the advert say?","o":["The bike is suitable for young teenagers.","The seller may accept a lower price.","The seller won't change the price."],"a":1,"tg":["inference","vocab"],"e":"\"Price can be discussed\" = fiyat konuşulabilir, yani daha düşük bir fiyat kabul edilebilir."},{"x":"Gym members: From 1st June, you will need your membership card to enter the building. Cards can be collected from reception.","q":"What must gym members do?","o":["pay for a membership card at reception","show their card to enter the building","come to the gym before 1st June"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","passive-voice"],"e":"1 Haziran'dan itibaren binaya girmek için üyelik kartı gerekiyor."},{"x":"Hey Paul, I've finished with your camera, thanks! I'd have returned it yesterday but I was ill. I'll drop it round after work. Jack","q":"What is Jack going to do?","o":["bring the camera back after work","keep the camera for another day","replace Paul's camera with a new one"],"a":0,"tg":["inference","third-conditional"],"e":"Dün getirecekti ama hastaydı; bugün iş çıkışı getirecek."}],"id":"b1r1","t":"Notices and Messages"},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Aşağıdaki beş kişi bir kitap arıyor. Her kişi için en uygun kitabı (A–H) seç.","ty":"match","pl":"Metinler (A–H)","opts":[["A","The Last Signal","A thriller about a girl who finds a mysterious radio message. Full of surprises from start to finish, but suitable for all ages."],["B","Midnight House","A horror story that will keep you awake at night. Not for anyone who scares easily!"],["C","Small Space, Big Harvest","How to grow tomatoes, beans and herbs in pots on a balcony or windowsill."],["D","The Perfect Garden","Ideas for designing large gardens, with plans for lawns, trees and ponds."],["E","Mountain Rescue","The true story of a team who saved climbers in a storm. Includes many photographs from the rescue."],["F","Brave Hearts","A novel about two friends who sail around the world. Beautiful writing, but quite long."],["G","My Crazy Dog","A comic story told in very short chapters, perfect for young readers who don't enjoy long books."],["H","Italy on a Budget","Where to sleep, eat and travel in Italy without spending too much. Maps and prices included."]],"q":[{"q":"Leyla: Leyla is going on a long train journey. She wants an exciting story that will keep her reading, but nothing too frightening.","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Heyecanlı ama korkutucu değil: A (B korku)."},{"q":"Oscar: Oscar wants to learn how to grow vegetables in a small space. He has a balcony but no garden.","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Balkon, sebze: C (D büyük bahçe)."},{"q":"Nina: Nina loves true stories about people who did something brave. She prefers books with photographs.","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Gerçek cesaret hikâyesi, fotoğraflı: E (F roman)."},{"q":"Ryan: Ryan is 14 and finds reading difficult. He wants something funny with short chapters.","a":"G","tg":["detail"],"e":"Komik, kısa bölümler: G."},{"q":"Mei: Mei is planning a trip to Italy. She wants practical information about cheap places to stay and eat.","a":"H","tg":["detail"],"e":"İtalya, ucuz kalacak yer ve yemek: H."}],"id":"b1r2","t":"Choosing a Book"},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"Learning to play the drums","p":["I'd always wanted to play the drums, but when I was younger my parents said they were too noisy for our flat. So when I moved into my own house at the age of thirty-two, the first thing I bought was a drum kit.","At first I tried to teach myself using videos on the internet. I learned a few simple rhythms, but I soon realised I was making the same mistakes again and again. A friend suggested that I take lessons, and she gave me the number of a teacher called Marcus.","Marcus was patient but strict. He made me practise slowly with a metronome, which I found boring at first. \"If you can't play it slowly, you can't play it fast,\" he kept saying. After a couple of months, I understood what he meant: my playing had become much more confident.","Six months later, Marcus invited me to join a group of his students who played together every Friday. I was nervous, but it was the best thing I have ever done. Playing with other musicians is completely different from playing alone. Now I can't imagine my life without music."],"q":[{"q":"Why didn't the writer play the drums when he was young?","o":["His parents couldn't afford to buy him a drum kit.","His parents worried about the noise in the flat.","He was too busy with school work at the time.","There was no teacher in his area who taught drums."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Ailesi davulun daire için çok gürültülü olduğunu söyledi."},{"q":"What problem did the writer have when he learned from videos?","o":["He lost interest after a few weeks.","He found even simple rhythms too hard.","He couldn't find videos for beginners.","He kept making the same mistakes."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"making the same mistakes again and again\""},{"q":"What did Marcus believe?","o":["Beginners should start by playing quickly.","Playing in a group is the best way to learn.","A metronome is only useful for experts.","Slow practice is the way to play fast."],"a":3,"tg":["inference","first-conditional"],"e":"\"If you can't play it slowly, you can't play it fast\" – yavaş çalışmak iyi çalmayı sağlar."},{"q":"How did the writer feel about joining the group?","o":["disappointed by the other players","relaxed and confident from the start","anxious but happy in the end","bored with playing on his own"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude","present-perfect"],"e":"\"I was nervous, but it was the best thing I have ever done\""},{"q":"What would be the best title for the text?","o":["How to choose a good drum teacher","The noisiest instrument in the world","Why musicians need to practise slowly","A dream fulfilled in adult life"],"a":3,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Çocukken çalamadığı davulu yetişkinken öğrenip müziği hayatının parçası yaptı."}],"id":"b1r3","t":"Learning the Drums"},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metinden beş cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–H) seç. Üç cümle fazladır.","ty":"gap","title":"A school with no homework","p":"Last year, Westfield School stopped giving homework to students under fourteen. The head teacher, Mr Collins, says the decision was not easy. (1) ______ But after reading many studies, he decided to try it.\nInstead of homework, students now spend the last hour of the school day on projects. (2) ______ Teachers are there to help if anyone gets stuck.\nParents had mixed feelings at the start. (3) ______ Others were happy that family evenings were less stressful.\nAfter one year, the results are interesting. Exam results have stayed the same. (4) ______ Students say they feel less tired and more interested in learning.\nThe school will continue the experiment for another two years. (5) ______ If the results are still positive, other schools in the area may follow.","opts":[["A","Some were worried that their children would fall behind."],["B","Many teachers were afraid that students would learn less."],["C","They can choose what to work on, and they often work in small groups."],["D","However, the number of students who say they enjoy school has gone up."],["E","During this time, researchers will collect more information."],["F","Older students, however, still have to do homework every evening."],["G","Several students said they would rather go home an hour earlier."],["H","Even so, a few parents have asked the school to bring homework back."]],"a":["B","C","A","D","E"],"tg":[["coherence"],["coherence"],["reference"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["reference"]],"e":["Karar kolay değildi çünkü öğretmenler endişeliydi; \"But after reading many studies\" ile devam ediyor: B.","Projelerle ilgili ayrıntı; \"Teachers are there to help\": C.","\"Some ... Others ...\" yapısı: A.","Sınav sonuçları aynı kaldı; \"However\" ile olumlu fark: D (F, G, H boşluklara uymaz).","\"During this time\" = iki yıllık süre: E."],"id":"b1r4","t":"A School Without Homework"},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için doğru kelimeyi seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"The village festival","p":"Every summer, our village (1) ______ a festival to raise money for the local school. It started in 1985 and it has become more popular (2) ______ year. This year more than two thousand people came, (3) ______ the weather was cold and cloudy. There were lots of (4) ______ for children, including face painting and a treasure hunt. My favourite part was the music competition, which was (5) ______ by a local band. We raised over five thousand pounds, which will be (6) ______ on new computers for the school.","o":[[["holds","takes","makes","gives"],0,["vocab"]],[["all","every","each other","whole"],1,["vocab"]],[["despite","however","even though","because"],2,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],[["activities","activity","actions","acts"],0,["countable-uncountable"]],[["won","beaten","earned","gained"],0,["passive-voice"]],[["spent","paid","bought","used"],0,["passive-advanced"]]],"e":["hold a festival: festival düzenlemek.","every year: her yıl.","even though + cümle: -mesine rağmen (despite + isim alır).","lots of + çoğul sayılabilir isim: activities.","win a competition: yarışmayı kazanmak (pasif: was won).","spend money on something: bir şeye para harcamak (pasif: will be spent on)."],"id":"b1r5","t":"The Village Festival"},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 6","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","title":"Dear Mr Lee","p":"Dear Mr Lee,\nThank you for your email. I am very interested (1) ______ the summer job at your café. I have worked in a restaurant before, so I know how busy it can be. I am available from 1st July (2) ______ the end of August. I can work at weekends, but I am not able to work (3) ______ Monday mornings because I have a language class. I would be grateful (4) ______ you could tell me what time the job starts each day. I am sending my CV with this email. I look forward to (5) ______ from you.\nYours sincerely,\nAyşe Kaya\nP.S. Could you also tell me (6) ______ I need to bring a uniform?","a":[["in"],["until","to","till"],["on"],["if"],["hearing"],["if","whether"]],"tg":[["prepositions-in-on-at"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["second-conditional"],["gerund-infinitive"],["reported-speech"]],"e":["interested in: -e ilgili.","from ... until / to: -den ... -e kadar.","Gün adıyla on: on Monday mornings.","I would be grateful if you could ...: nazik rica.","look forward to + -ing: hearing.","Dolaylı evet / hayır sorusu: if / whether."],"id":"b1r6","t":"A Letter to Mr Lee"},{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","ty":"mc","q":[{"x":"SWIMMING POOL\nThe pool will close at 6 p.m. on Thursday for cleaning. Evening classes will take place on Friday instead.","q":"What does the notice say?","o":["Thursday evening classes are moving to another day.","The pool is closed all day on Thursday.","Friday classes are cancelled because of cleaning."],"a":0,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Perşembe akşamı temizlik var; akşam dersleri cuma yapılacak."},{"x":"Hi Sam,\nI've left the keys with Mrs Green at number 12 because I'll be at work when you arrive. She's home all afternoon.\nLucy","q":"Why has Lucy written this message?","o":["to ask Sam to visit Mrs Green","to tell Sam where to collect the keys","to say she will be home in the afternoon"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Anahtarları komşuya bıraktı; Sam onları oradan alacak."},{"x":"CITY MUSEUM\nPhotos are allowed in all rooms except the Egyptian gallery. Please do not use flash anywhere in the building.","q":"What does the notice say?","o":["You cannot take any photos in the museum.","You can take photos without flash in most rooms.","Flash is only allowed in the Egyptian gallery."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","can-cant"],"e":"Mısır galerisi dışında fotoğraf serbest, ama hiçbir yerde flaş yok."},{"x":"Mum,\nDr Patel's receptionist called. Your appointment has been moved from Tuesday to Wednesday, same time. Call her if that's a problem.\nAli","q":"What does Ali tell his mother?","o":["She must phone the doctor on Tuesday.","Her appointment is now on a different day.","The doctor can see her earlier on Wednesday."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","passive-voice"],"e":"Randevu salıdan çarşambaya alındı, saat aynı."},{"x":"SALE\nAll winter jackets half price this week only. Sorry, no refunds on sale items.","q":"What does the notice say?","o":["You can return sale jackets for your money back.","The jackets will be cheaper next week.","You cannot get your money back if you buy a sale jacket."],"a":2,"tg":["inference","vocab"],"e":"\"no refunds on sale items\" = indirimli ürünlerde para iadesi yok."}],"id":"b1r1b","t":"Notices and Notes (2)"},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Aşağıdaki beş kişi bir spor ya da sağlık etkinliği arıyor. Her kişi için en uygun etkinliği (A–H) seç.","ty":"match","pl":"Metinler (A–H)","opts":[["A","Sunrise Walkers","Join our friendly group for a fast walk in the park every weekday at 6.30 a.m. All ages welcome, no equipment needed."],["B","City Gym","Modern machines and personal trainers. Open 24 hours, with a special price for early-morning members."],["C","Aqua Fit","Slow, relaxing exercises in a warm pool, recommended for people with back or knee problems. Saturday and Sunday mornings."],["D","Swim Club","Training for competitive swimmers. Sessions every weekday at 7 a.m. You must be able to swim 1 km."],["E","Friday Volleyball","Never played before? No problem! Our coach teaches beginners every Friday from 7 to 9 p.m."],["F","Fresh Kitchen","A live cooking class in our town centre kitchen. Learn to make tasty, healthy meals in two hours."],["G","Eat Well at Home","Short video lessons showing how to cook healthy food quickly. Watch from your computer or phone whenever you like."],["H","Run for Hope","Training plans and group runs for anyone who wants to run the 10 km charity race in October."]],"q":[{"q":"Can: Can wants to get fit but hates gyms. He would like to exercise outdoors with other people early in the morning.","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Açık havada, grupla, sabah erken: A (B spor salonu)."},{"q":"Elif: Elif has a bad back. Her doctor told her to do gentle exercise in water, and she can only go at weekends.","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Suda hafif egzersiz, hafta sonu: C (D yarışmacı yüzücüler, hafta içi)."},{"q":"Mark: Mark is a beginner who wants to learn a team sport. He works until six, so he needs evening sessions.","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Yeni başlayan, takım sporu, akşam: E."},{"q":"Zeynep: Zeynep wants to learn about healthy cooking because she often eats fast food. She would like to do it online.","a":"G","tg":["detail"],"e":"Sağlıklı yemek, internetten: G (F yüz yüze)."},{"q":"Tom: Tom enjoys running and wants to take part in a race for charity later this year.","a":"H","tg":["detail"],"e":"Hayır amaçlı koşu yarışı: H."}],"id":"b1r2b","t":"Sport and Health Activities"},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"My year as a teaching assistant","p":["When I finished university, I had no idea what job I wanted. My aunt, who runs a small primary school in a village near the mountains, suggested that I spend a year working there as a teaching assistant. I said yes, mainly because I needed time to think.","The first few weeks were harder than I expected. The children were lovely, but they had a lot of energy, and I was exhausted by the end of every day. I also had to get used to living without the shops and cafés I was used to in the city. The nearest town was an hour away by bus.","Things changed when the head teacher asked me to start an after-school music club. I had played the guitar since I was a teenager, but I had never taught anyone. To my surprise, twenty children signed up in the first week. By the end of the year, they gave a concert for their parents, and I was prouder than they were.","I am now training to be a music teacher. People sometimes ask me if I regret not getting a \"proper\" job straight after university. I always tell them that the year in the village was the most useful thing I have ever done, because it showed me what I really enjoy."],"q":[{"q":"Why did the writer accept the job at the school?","o":["He had always wanted to be a teacher.","He needed time to decide about his future.","His aunt offered him a very good salary.","He wanted to live near the mountains."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"mainly because I needed time to think\""},{"q":"What did the writer find difficult at first?","o":["The children were badly behaved.","He did not like his aunt.","He was very tired and missed city life.","The bus to work took an hour."],"a":2,"tg":["detail","used-to"],"e":"\"I was exhausted\" ve şehirdeki dükkân ve kafeleri özledi."},{"q":"How did the writer feel about the music club?","o":["surprised that so many children joined","worried because he had never played the guitar","bored because it took too much time","disappointed with the final concert"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"To my surprise, twenty children signed up\""},{"q":"What does the writer say about his year in the village?","o":["He wishes he had started a proper job sooner.","It helped him discover what he likes doing.","He would not recommend it to other people.","It was the only job he could find."],"a":1,"tg":["inference","past-simple"],"e":"\"it showed me what I really enjoy\""},{"q":"What would the writer probably say to a friend who doesn't know what to do after university?","o":["\"Find a well-paid job in the city as soon as you can.\"","\"Try something different for a while. You might find out what you love.\"","\"Don't work with children – it's too tiring.\"","\"Ask your family to find you a job.\""],"a":1,"tg":["gist","inference"],"e":"Bir yıl farklı bir şey denemek ona ne istediğini gösterdi."}],"id":"b1r3b","t":"A Year in a Village School"},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metinden beş cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–H) seç. Üç cümle fazladır.","ty":"gap","title":"The town that said no to cars","p":"Ten years ago, the centre of Millford was full of traffic every morning. The air was dirty and the streets were noisy. (1) ______ So the town council made a brave decision: no private cars in the centre between 7 a.m. and 7 p.m.\nAt first, many shop owners were angry. (2) ______ In fact, the opposite happened. Because the streets became quieter and safer, more people started walking and cycling into town to do their shopping.\nThe council also improved public transport. (3) ______ It is free for anyone over sixty-five and for school students.\nToday, visitors often say how pleasant the centre is. (4) ______ Cafés have put tables outside, and children play in streets that used to be dangerous.\nOther towns are now studying Millford's example. (5) ______ But Millford shows that, with a bit of courage, a town can change.","opts":[["A","Many parents were afraid to let their children walk to school."],["B","They thought nobody would come to their shops without a car."],["C","A small electric bus now goes round the centre every ten minutes."],["D","There are flowers and trees where there used to be parking spaces."],["E","Not all of them will find it easy to make the same change."],["F","The cars were moved to a new car park outside the town."],["G","Most of them closed in the first year."],["H","The price of petrol went up at the same time."]],"a":["A","B","C","D","E"],"tg":[["coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["reference"]],"e":["Kirli hava ve gürültünün sonucu: aileler korkuyordu, \"So the council made a brave decision\": A.","Dükkân sahipleri kızgındı çünkü kimsenin gelmeyeceğini düşündüler; \"In fact, the opposite happened\": B (G çelişir).","Toplu taşıma geliştirildi; \"It is free for ...\" otobüsü anlatıyor: C.","Merkezin ne kadar hoş olduğu: çiçekler ve ağaçlar: D.","\"Other towns ... Not all of them\": E."],"id":"b1r4b","t":"The Town Without Cars"},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için doğru kelimeyi seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"A storm to remember","p":"Last January, a terrible storm (1) ______ the coast where my grandparents live. The wind was so strong that it (2) ______ down several trees in their street, and the electricity was off for three days. Luckily, my grandparents had (3) ______ of candles and food in the house. Their neighbours, who are much younger, (4) ______ on them every morning to make sure they were all right. When the storm finally ended, everyone in the street worked together to (5) ______ up the mess. My grandfather says the storm was frightening, but it also (6) ______ him realise how kind people can be.","o":[[["hit","arrived","landed","happened"],0,["vocab"]],[["broke","fell","blew","threw"],2,["vocab","past-simple"]],[["much","many","plenty","several"],2,["countable-uncountable"]],[["looked","checked","visited","controlled"],1,["collocation"]],[["wash","make","clean","put"],2,["collocation"]],[["made","let","got","had"],0,["causative"]]],"e":["A storm hit the coast: fırtına kıyıyı vurdu.","blow down: (rüzgâr) devirmek; geçmiş: blew.","plenty of + isim: bolca.","check on someone: birinin iyi olup olmadığını kontrol etmek.","clean up the mess: ortalığı temizlemek.","make someone + yalın fiil: birine bir şey yaptırmak / hissettirmek (let, get, had bu yapıya uymaz)."],"id":"b1r5b","t":"A Storm to Remember"},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 6","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","title":"Hi Deniz","p":"Hi Deniz,\nGuess what? I've just joined a photography club! We meet (1) ______ Wednesday evenings in the library. There are about fifteen members, and most of (2) ______ are older than me, but they are really friendly. Last week we went to the old harbour to take photos of the boats. It was (3) ______ cold that my fingers hurt! Next month there's a competition, and the winner's photo will be printed (4) ______ the local newspaper. I don't think I'll win, but it's a good reason to practise. Why don't you come with me next time? You've always been good (5) ______ taking pictures. Let me know (6) ______ you're free.\nLove,\nMaya","a":[["on"],["them"],["so"],["in"],["at"],["if","whether","when"]],"tg":[["prepositions-in-on-at"],["vocab"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["reported-speech"]],"e":["Gün adı: on Wednesday evenings.","most of them: çoğu (nesne zamiri).","so + sıfat + that: o kadar ... ki.","printed in the newspaper: gazetede basılmak.","good at + -ing.","Let me know if / whether / when ..."],"id":"b1r6b","t":"The Photography Club"},{"k":"R5","id":"b1r5c","t":"A Morning at the Market","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 5 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B ya da C) seç.","title":"A morning at the market","p":"Every Saturday morning, my grandfather takes me to the old market in our town. We always (1) ______ early because the best stalls are busy by nine. He likes buying fresh bread and olives, and he never forgets to ask the sellers about their families. Last week, we saw a man (2) ______ shoes from a small van. My grandfather bought a pair, although he (3) ______ already got three at home! \"You can never have (4) ______ shoes,\" he joked. On the way home, it (5) ______ heavily, so we shared one umbrella. I think it was the best part of the day.","o":[[["arrive","arrived","arrives"],0,["present-simple"]],[["sells","selling","sold"],1,["gerund-infinitive"]],[["has","have","is"],0,["present-perfect"]],[["too many","too much","so much"],0,["countable-uncountable"]],[["was raining","rains","has rained"],0,["past-continuous"]]],"e":["We always + yalın fiil: alışkanlık (Present Simple).","saw a man + -ing: bir adamın ... -diğini gördük.","he has already got: has + V3 (Present Perfect).","shoes sayılabilir çoğul: too many. much sayılamayanlarla kullanılır.","geçmişte süren olay: it was raining."]},{"k":"R6","id":"b1r6c","t":"A New Flat","ty":"open","nm":"Okuma 6 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 6","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","title":"A new flat","one":true,"p":"My sister has just moved (1) ______ a new flat near the city centre. It is much smaller (2) ______ her old one, but she says she loves it. The flat is on the third floor, and there (3) ______ a lift, which is useful when she goes shopping. She has lived there (4) ______ two weeks and has already met most of her neighbours. Next weekend she is going to have a party, so she asked me (5) ______ I could help her cook. I told her I would, as long (6) ______ she let me choose the music.","a":[["into","to"],["than"],["is"],["for"],["if","whether"],["as"]],"tg":[["prepositions-in-on-at"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["there-is-are"],["present-perfect"],["reported-speech"],["first-conditional"]],"e":["move into a flat: daireye taşınmak.","smaller than: -den daha küçük.","there is a lift: asansör var (tekil).","for two weeks: iki haftadır (süre).","asked me if I could help: evet/hayır sorusunun aktarımı.","as long as: -mek koşuluyla."]},{"k":"R1","id":"b1r1c","t":"Library, Bus and Messages","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","q":[{"x":"LIBRARY NOTICE\nBooks can now be returned at the machine by the door, even when the library is closed. Please do not leave books outside.","q":"What does the notice say?","o":["You may return books at any time of the day.","The library is open all night.","Books must be left outside the door."],"a":0,"tg":["detail","can-cant"],"e":"Makine kapıda; kütüphane kapalıyken de iade edilir. Kitapları dışarı bırakmayın."},{"x":"Hi Defne,\nI'm running late – my bus broke down. Start the film without me, but please don't tell me what happens! I'll be there at about 8.30.\nBaran","q":"What does Baran want Defne to do?","o":["wait until he arrives before starting the film","begin watching without him and keep the story secret","buy him a ticket for the 8.30 film"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","purpose"],"e":"\"Start the film without me, but don't tell me what happens.\""},{"x":"BUS SERVICE 14\nDue to road works, buses will not stop at Park Street until the end of May. Passengers should use the stop at the library, two minutes' walk away.","q":"What should passengers do?","o":["walk to the library stop","take a different bus number","wait at Park Street until May"],"a":0,"tg":["detail","prepositions-in-on-at"],"e":"Park Street'te durmayacak; kütüphane durağı iki dakika yürüme mesafesinde."},{"x":"Hello Ms Reed,\nI'm sorry, but Jack can't come to football training this week. He has a bad cold, and the doctor says he should stay at home until Monday.\nJack's mother","q":"Why has Jack's mother written?","o":["to explain why Jack will be absent","to ask the coach about Monday's match","to say that Jack has a new doctor"],"a":0,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Jack hasta olduğu için antrenmana gelemeyecek."},{"x":"SALE – EVERYTHING HALF PRICE\nOur shop is closing after twenty years. Last day: Saturday 30th. No refunds on sale items.","q":"What is true about the shop?","o":["It is moving to a new address.","It will stop trading on the 30th.","It will give your money back if you return things."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Mağaza 20 yıl sonra kapanıyor; son gün 30'u. Satış ürünlerinde iade yok."}]},{"k":"R2","id":"b1r2c","t":"Weekend Courses","ty":"match","nm":"Okuma 2 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Beş kişi bir hafta sonu kursu arıyor. Her kişi için en uygun kursu (A–H) seç.","pl":"Kurslar (A–H)","uniq":true,"opts":[["A","Photo Walk","A relaxed Sunday morning walk through the old town with an expert, who will teach you to take better pictures with your phone."],["B","Italian in a Day","Learn the words you need for a holiday in Italy in one intensive Saturday class. Lunch is included."],["C","Bike Repair","Bring your own bike and learn to fix punctures and brakes. Saturday afternoon, small groups only."],["D","Cooking for Students","Cheap, quick meals you can make in a small kitchen. Evening class on Thursdays."],["E","Drama for Teenagers","Weekly acting workshops for 13–17 year olds, ending in a show for parents."],["F","Garden Volunteers","Help us plant trees and flowers in the town park every Sunday. Tools and tea provided."],["G","First Aid","A one-day course on what to do in an emergency. Certificate at the end."],["H","Web Design Online","Twelve short video lessons on making your own website. Study at any time."]],"q":[{"q":"Selin loves taking pictures but only has a phone. She is free on Sunday mornings.","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Telefonla fotoğraf, pazar sabahı: A."},{"q":"Okan is going to Rome in the summer and wants to learn some basic Italian quickly.","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Tatil için kısa sürede İtalyanca: B."},{"q":"Mina is sixteen and has always wanted to try acting with people her own age.","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"13–17 yaş tiyatro atölyesi: E."},{"q":"Emre lives alone at university and wants to learn to cook cheap meals after classes.","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"Öğrenciler için ucuz yemek, akşam dersi: D."},{"q":"Hülya wants to help the local community outdoors and doesn't mind working on Sundays.","a":"F","tg":["detail"],"e":"Parkta gönüllü çalışma, pazar günü: F."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"b1r3c","t":"Cycling to School","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Cycling to school","p":["When I started secondary school, my parents bought me a bicycle. I wasn't very excited at first, because everyone else in my class took the bus. But the school was only four kilometres from our house, and my father said it would be good for me.","The first week was terrible. I fell off twice, and it rained on Thursday, so I arrived completely wet. Nobody laughed, though. A girl called Ceren, who also cycled, lent me a towel and showed me a quiet road that avoided the traffic. We have been cycling together ever since.","After a few months, something surprising happened. I found that I was concentrating better in lessons. My teacher said it was because exercise wakes up the brain. I don't know if that is true, but I do know that I feel much more awake at nine o'clock than my friends who come by bus.","This year our school has built a new bike shed, and about forty students now cycle every day. We are trying to persuade the head teacher to organise a \"Cycle to School Week\" in the spring. If it goes well, perhaps even the students who live far away will want to try."],"q":[{"q":"How did the writer feel about getting a bicycle?","o":["not particularly pleased","very proud","worried about the traffic","angry with his parents"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I wasn't very excited at first\""},{"q":"What did Ceren do on the writer's first week?","o":["She laughed at him when he fell.","She gave him help and showed him a better route.","She took him to school by bus.","She taught him how to ride a bike."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","past-simple"],"e":"Havlu verdi ve trafiksiz sakin yolu gösterdi."},{"q":"What does the writer say about his lessons?","o":["He feels tired by nine o'clock.","His teacher gave him less homework.","He can concentrate better than before.","He is sure that exercise improves marks."],"a":2,"tg":["detail","inference"],"e":"\"I was concentrating better in lessons\" – ama kanıtlanmış olduğundan emin değil."},{"q":"What are the students hoping to do in the spring?","o":["build another bike shed","take part in a Cycle to School Week","ask parents to buy bicycles","travel to school by train"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"\"persuade the head teacher to organise a Cycle to School Week\""},{"q":"What would be the best title for this text?","o":["A Terrible Week at School","Why Buses Are Bad","Two-Wheeled Changes","My Teacher's Advice"],"a":2,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Bisiklet hayatını değiştirdi: metnin ana fikri."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"b1r3d","t":"Working Nights","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Working nights","p":["My name is Ali, and I work as a baker. Most people are asleep when I start work at ten o'clock in the evening, and by the time they wake up, the bread is already on the shelves.","People often ask me whether I find it difficult to sleep during the day. At the beginning, I did. My flat is on a busy street, and the noise kept me awake. Then my sister gave me thick curtains and a pair of earplugs, and now I sleep from nine until four without any problems.","The best part of the job is the quiet. While the city is silent, I listen to music and enjoy the smell of fresh bread. The worst part is that I rarely see my friends. They work during the day, so we can only meet at weekends, and I am often too tired to go out.","Last month, a customer asked me to teach him how to bake. He now comes to the shop on Sunday mornings, and we make bread together. I was surprised how much I enjoyed explaining things, so I am thinking of starting baking lessons at the weekend."],"q":[{"q":"What does Ali say about his sleep?","o":["He never has any trouble sleeping.","He used to have problems, but not now.","He still can't sleep because of the noise.","He sleeps during the night as well."],"a":1,"tg":["used-to","detail"],"e":"Başta zordu; perde ve kulak tıkacıyla sorun kalmadı."},{"q":"What does he like most about working at night?","o":["seeing the customers","the peaceful atmosphere","meeting his friends","the high pay"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"The best part of the job is the quiet.\""},{"q":"Why does Ali see his friends so little?","o":["They live in another city.","He is often asleep at weekends.","Their working hours are different from his.","He doesn't like going out."],"a":2,"tg":["detail","inference"],"e":"Arkadaşları gündüz çalışıyor, onlar yalnızca hafta sonu görüşebiliyor; çoğu zaman da yorgun."},{"q":"What is Ali thinking of doing?","o":["changing his job","moving to a quieter flat","teaching people to bake","opening a second shop"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","gerund-infinitive"],"e":"\"I am thinking of starting baking lessons\""},{"q":"Which of these might Ali write to a friend?","o":["I'd love to see you, but I'm always asleep when you're free.","My job is boring and I want to leave.","I've moved to a new flat near the bakery.","Please come to the shop at midnight tomorrow."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Metinde hafta sonu bile çok yorgun olduğunu söylüyor."}]},{"k":"R4","id":"b1r4c","t":"A Garden at School","ty":"gap","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metinden beş cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–H) seç. Üç cümle fazladır.","title":"A garden at school","p":"Last year, our school had an empty piece of land behind the sports hall. Nobody used it, and it was covered with weeds. (1) ______ Our science teacher, Mr Aydın, thought it could become a vegetable garden.\nHe asked for volunteers, and thirty students joined. (2) ______ On Saturdays we dug the soil, and parents brought tools from home.\nWe planted tomatoes, beans and herbs. (3) ______ But we learnt quickly: we watered the plants every morning and put up a small fence.\nIn July, we picked our first tomatoes. (4) ______ Some of the vegetables went to the school canteen, and the rest we gave to local families.\nNext year, we want to grow strawberries as well. (5) ______ It has taught us more than any lesson in the classroom.","opts":[["A","It is hard to believe how much we have enjoyed this project."],["B","Most of us had never grown anything before."],["C","They tasted much better than the ones in the supermarket."],["D","The weather forecast for Saturday was very bad."],["E","The head teacher said the school would pay for the seeds."],["F","At first, birds ate many of the seeds."],["G","Mr Aydın was born in a village in the east."],["H","Everyone thought it was a waste of space."]],"a":["H","E","F","C","A"],"tg":[["coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence","reference"],["reference"],["coherence"]],"e":["Boş arazi kimse kullanmıyordu: \"Everyone thought it was a waste of space\" (H) sonraki Mr Aydın'ın fikrine bağlanır.","Gönüllüler katıldı; tohum ve araçlar: E okulun tohum parasını ödeyeceğini söyler, ardından Saturdays gelir.","\"But we learnt quickly\": önceki cümle bir sorun olmalı (F: kuşlar tohumları yedi).","\"They tasted\" – ilk domatesler (C), sonraki cümlede sebzelerin dağıtımı.","Sonuç/duygu cümlesi: A. \"It has taught us\" projeye gönderme yapar."]},{"k":"R5","id":"b1r5d","t":"A Lost Bag","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 5 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B ya da C) seç.","title":"A lost bag","p":"Last Friday, I took the train to visit my cousin in Ankara. I was so busy talking on the phone that I (1) ______ my bag on the seat when I got off. I only noticed it ten minutes later. I felt terrible, because my laptop (2) ______ in the bag!\nI ran back to the station and asked the man in the ticket office. \"What does it look (3) ______?\" he asked. I told him it was a blue backpack with a yellow label. He picked up the phone and called the next station.\nTo my great relief, a cleaner had found the bag and (4) ______ it to the lost property office. I collected it the following morning. Since then, I have always checked my seat (5) ______ I leave a train.","o":[[["forgot","left","lost"],1,["past-simple"]],[["was","were","had"],0,["past-simple"]],[["for","like","as"],1,["question-words"]],[["took","taken","had taken"],0,["past-simple"]],[["after","while","before"],2,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]]],"e":["leave left = bir yerde bırakmak (left my bag). forget bir tümleç almaz bu yapıda.","laptop tekil; geçmişte: was.","What does it look like?: neye benziyor?","had found ... and took: sıralı geçmiş olaylar.","before I leave: ayrılmadan önce."]},{"k":"R5","id":"b1r5e","t":"New Neighbours","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 5 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B ya da C) seç.","title":"New neighbours","p":"Two weeks ago, a family moved into the house next to ours. I had never met them, so on Sunday my mother suggested that I (1) ______ a cake for them. I made a lemon cake, and we knocked on their door in the afternoon.\nA tall woman opened it. She was (2) ______ friendly that we immediately felt welcome. Her name was Marta, and she came from Poland. She (3) ______ in Turkey for only a month, and she was learning Turkish.\n\"If you need any help with shopping or with the language, just ask,\" my mother said. Marta smiled and invited us (4) ______ tea. We stayed for an hour, and by the end I had (5) ______ a new friend: her daughter Zosia, who is the same age as me.","o":[[["bake","baking","to bake"],0,["gerund-infinitive"]],[["so","such","too"],0,["so-such-too-enough"]],[["has been","had been","is"],0,["present-perfect"]],[["to","for","of"],0,["gerund-infinitive"]],[["done","made","had"],1,["present-perfect"]]],"e":["suggest that I + yalın fiil: pişirmemi önerdi.","so friendly (sıfat) ... that: o kadar arkadaş canlısı ki.","for only a month: şimdiye kadar süren durum, has been.","invite someone to tea: çaya davet etmek.","make a friend: arkadaş edinmek (made)."]},{"k":"R6","id":"b1r6d","t":"A Weekend Trip","ty":"open","nm":"Okuma 6 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 6","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","title":"A weekend trip","one":true,"p":"Dear Selim,\nThank you for your letter. I am writing to tell you about our weekend trip to Bursa. We left home (1) ______ seven o'clock on Saturday morning and arrived at ten. The weather was lovely, so we went straight (2) ______ the mountain by cable car.\nThe view was the best I (3) ______ ever seen. Unfortunately, my brother is afraid of heights, so he stayed at the bottom (4) ______ us. In the evening, we ate Iskender kebab, which is the most famous dish in Bursa. I have (5) ______ eaten so much in my life!\nWe will visit you in Istanbul (6) ______ summer, if you are free.\nLove, Yasemin","a":[["at"],["to","up"],["have","'ve"],["without"],["never"],["next","this","in","during"]],"tg":[["prepositions-in-on-at"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["present-perfect"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["present-perfect"],["prepositions-in-on-at"]],"e":["at seven o'clock: saat için at.","go to the mountain: dağa gitmek.","the best I have ever seen: hiç görmediğim en güzel manzara.","stayed at the bottom without us: bizsiz kaldı.","I have never eaten so much: hayatımda hiç bu kadar yemedim.","next summer: gelecek yaz."]},{"k":"R6","id":"b1r6e","t":"Joining a Gym","ty":"open","nm":"Okuma 6 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 6","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","title":"Joining a gym","one":true,"p":"My friend Kerem has just joined a new gym near his office. He says it is much cheaper (1) ______ the one he went to before. It opens at six in the morning, (2) ______ he can train before work.\nThere are many classes, but he likes yoga best. He (3) ______ never done it before, so he was a little nervous at first. The teacher is very patient and always explains (4) ______ to do each movement.\nKerem hopes to lose five kilos (5) ______ the summer. I told him he should eat well too, because exercise alone is not (6) ______ to keep you healthy.","a":[["than"],["so"],["had","has"],["how"],["by","before","in"],["enough"]],"tg":[["comparatives-superlatives"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["present-perfect"],["question-words"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["so-such-too-enough"]],"e":["cheaper than: -den daha ucuz.","so he can ...: sonuç bağlacı.","had never done: o zamana kadar hiç yapmamıştı; has never done da kabul.","explains how to do: nasıl yapılacağını.","by the summer: yaza kadar.","not enough to keep you healthy: sağlıklı tutmaya yetmez."]},{"k":"R1","id":"b1r1d","t":"Signs and Messages","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","q":[{"x":"PARK RULES\nDogs must be kept on a lead at all times. Cycling is allowed only on the main path. Please take your rubbish home.","q":"What does the notice say?","o":["Bicycles may use only one path.","Dogs can run freely in the park.","Rubbish bins are provided."],"a":0,"tg":["detail","can-cant"],"e":"Bisiklet yalnızca ana yolda; köpekler tasmalı."},{"x":"Hi Tom,\nI can't find my charger. Have you seen it? If you have, please leave it in my room. I'll be back from work at six.\nMia","q":"Why has Mia written to Tom?","o":["to ask him to find something for her","to tell him she is working late","to say she has bought a new phone"],"a":0,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Şarj aletini bulamıyor; Tom'dan odasına bırakmasını istiyor."},{"x":"COFFEE SHOP\nBuy any large coffee and get a cake half price. Offer ends Friday. Cannot be used with other offers.","q":"What does the notice say?","o":["A cake costs less if you also buy a large coffee.","Large coffees are half price.","The offer can be used every day."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Büyük kahveyle birlikte pasta yarı fiyat; cuma günü bitiyor."},{"x":"Dear parents,\nBecause of the teachers' meeting, school will finish at 12.30 on Wednesday. Lunch will be served as usual. Buses will leave at 12.45.\nMr Gray, Head Teacher","q":"What is the message about?","o":["an earlier end to the school day","a change in the lunch menu","a new bus route"],"a":0,"tg":["detail","purpose"],"e":"Çarşamba okul 12.30'da bitiyor."},{"x":"Sam – the plumber is coming tomorrow at 9. Please be home! I've left a note on the kitchen table with the problem. Call me if there's a problem.\nDad","q":"What does Dad want Sam to do?","o":["stay at home for the plumber","phone the plumber","mend the kitchen table"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Sam'in tesisatçı için evde olmasını istiyor."}]},{"k":"R2","id":"b1r2d","t":"Summer Activities for Teenagers","ty":"match","nm":"Okuma 2 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Beş genç bir yaz etkinliği arıyor. Her kişi için en uygun etkinliği (A–H) seç.","pl":"Etkinlikler (A–H)","uniq":true,"opts":[["A","Coding Camp","Five days of computer programming for beginners aged 12 to 15. No experience needed. Laptops are provided."],["B","Forest School","Spend a week outdoors, learning to build shelters, make fires and cook in the open air. Ages 10 to 16."],["C","Young Journalists","Write articles, take photographs and interview local people for our summer magazine. Ages 14 to 18."],["D","Sailing Week","Learn to sail in small boats. You must be able to swim 100 metres. Ages 12 and over."],["E","Art in the Park","Free drawing and painting classes in the park every Wednesday afternoon. All ages, bring your own materials."],["F","Language Exchange","Practise English with students from Spain and Italy through games and trips. Ages 13 to 17."],["G","Football Academy","Daily training with professional coaches. Boys and girls from 8 to 14. Matches every Friday."],["H","Volunteer Week","Help older people in your town with shopping and gardening. Ages 16 and over."]],"q":[{"q":"Efe is fourteen and loves taking photographs. He would like to write about his town as well.","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Fotoğraf, röportaj ve makale: C."},{"q":"Nur is twelve. She wants to learn how to make programs for computer games but has never tried it.","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Programlamaya yeni başlayanlar için: A."},{"q":"Kaan is fifteen and wants to spend a whole week camping and learning survival skills.","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Bir hafta açık havada, ateş ve barınak: B."},{"q":"Lara is seventeen. She wants to help the community, and she doesn't mind working with older people.","a":"H","tg":["detail"],"e":"16 yaş üstü, yaşlılara yardım: H."},{"q":"Ahmet is thirteen and wants to improve his English by talking to students from other countries.","a":"F","tg":["detail"],"e":"İspanyol ve İtalyan öğrencilerle İngilizce: F."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"b1r3e","t":"Working with Dogs","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Working with dogs","p":["I have wanted to work with animals since I was a little girl, but I never imagined I would become a dog trainer. I started by chance, when my neighbour asked me to help her with her very naughty puppy, Max. He pulled on the lead, chewed shoes and barked at everyone. After two weeks of patient practice, he was walking quietly beside me.","The secret, I discovered, is not to shout. Dogs respond better to praise than to punishment, so I always give them a small treat when they do something right. It sounds simple, but it requires a lot of patience. Some dogs learn in a few days, while others need months.","Now I run weekend classes in the local park, and about fifteen dogs come every Saturday. Interestingly, it is often the owners who need more training than the dogs! Many people forget that a dog needs exercise and attention every day, not just at the weekend.","The best moments are when a nervous dog finally relaxes. Last month, a rescue dog called Luna arrived at my class, afraid of everything. By the end of the course, she was playing with the other dogs and even let a child stroke her. Moments like that make the job worthwhile."],"q":[{"q":"How did the writer begin working as a dog trainer?","o":["She helped a neighbour with a puppy.","She studied at a special college.","She was offered a job by the council.","She adopted a rescue dog."],"a":0,"tg":["detail","past-simple"],"e":"Komşusunun yaramaz köpeği Max'e yardım etmekle başladı."},{"q":"What does the writer say about training dogs?","o":["Praise works better than punishment.","Every dog learns in a few days.","Shouting is sometimes necessary.","Treats make dogs lazy."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Dogs respond better to praise than to punishment.\""},{"q":"What does she say about the owners?","o":["Some need more training than their dogs.","They always give their dogs enough exercise.","They are too busy to attend classes.","Most of them are children."],"a":0,"tg":["detail","inference"],"e":"\"it is often the owners who need more training than the dogs\""},{"q":"Why does she mention Luna?","o":["To show a moment she finds rewarding","To explain why some dogs are dangerous","To advise people to adopt dogs","To complain about difficult owners"],"a":0,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Korkak köpeğin rahatlaması işin en güzel anı."},{"q":"What is the main idea of the text?","o":["The writer enjoys helping dogs and their owners.","Dogs are easy to train.","Training classes are too expensive.","Puppies are naughty because they are bored."],"a":0,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Metin boyunca işini sevdiği ve köpeklere/sahiplerine yardım ettiği anlatılıyor."}]},{"k":"R4","id":"b1r4d","t":"A Café with a Difference","ty":"gap","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metinden beş cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–H) seç. Üç cümle fazladır.","title":"A café with a difference","p":"When Nilay opened her café two years ago, her friends thought she was crazy. (1) ______ But she had a clear idea: she wanted a place where people could read, talk and relax, without being hurried.\nThe café is small, with a few comfortable sofas and shelves full of second-hand books. (2) ______ If they like a book, they can buy it for a very low price.\nAt first, there were very few customers. (3) ______ Then a local newspaper wrote a short article about it, and everything changed.\nToday, the café is busy all day. (4) ______ Students come to study, and elderly people play chess in the corner.\nNilay is planning to open a second café next year. (5) ______ \"I want it to feel like a home, not a business,\" she says.","opts":[["A","She was nervous about the cost of the rent."],["B","Customers are welcome to read the books while they drink their coffee."],["C","The first month, she sold only a few cups a day."],["D","There are lots of cafés on the same street."],["E","She wants it to have the same friendly atmosphere as the first."],["F","It is always full of different kinds of people."],["G","Nilay was born in a small town on the coast."],["H","Nobody had heard of her before."]],"a":["D","B","C","F","E"],"tg":[["coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["reference"]],"e":["Arkadaşlar çılgın dedi: aynı sokakta çok kafe vardı (D); sonraki \"But she had a clear idea\".","Rafları kitap dolu: B, müşterilere kahvelerini içerken kitap okuma izni.","Başta çok az müşteri: C; \"Then a local newspaper ...\" ile devam.","Her çeşit insan dolduruyor: F; ardından öğrenciler ve yaşlılar sayılıyor.","İkinci kafe: E; sonraki cümle \"I want it to feel like a home\"."]},{"k":"R5","id":"b1r5f","t":"A Music Festival","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 5 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B ya da C) seç.","title":"A music festival","p":"Last summer, I went to a music festival for the first time. My cousin had (1) ______ tickets weeks earlier, but I only decided to go two days before. We travelled by coach, and it (2) ______ about five hours to reach the campsite. When we arrived, we put up our tent in a field, (3) ______ was already full of people.\nThe music started at noon and continued until midnight. My favourite band played on Saturday evening, and the crowd was so excited (4) ______ nobody stopped dancing. On Sunday, it began to rain, but we didn't mind. We just put on our raincoats and carried on. I (5) ______ never forget the weekend.","o":[[["bought","buy","buying"],0,["past-simple"]],[["took","spent","made"],0,["past-simple"]],[["which","who","what"],0,["relative-clauses"]],[["that","than","so"],0,["so-such-too-enough"]],[["will","would","am"],0,["will-going-to"]]],"e":["had bought: önceden alınmış biletler.","it took five hours: yolculuk beş saat sürdü.","a field, which was full of people: tarla (nesne) için which.","so excited that nobody stopped: o kadar heyecanlıydı ki.","I will never forget: asla unutmayacağım."]},{"k":"R5","id":"b1r5g","t":"A New Hobby","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 5 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B ya da C) seç.","title":"A new hobby","p":"A year ago, I (1) ______ playing the guitar, and it has completely changed my free time. I used to spend most evenings watching TV, but now I practise for an hour (2) ______ day. At first, my fingers hurt, and I couldn't play a single song. My teacher told me that I (3) ______ not give up, and she was right.\nNow I can play about ten songs, and I'm even (4) ______ to write one of my own. Last month, I played in front of my family for the first time. I was nervous, but they all clapped (5) ______ the end. I think everyone should learn an instrument.","o":[[["started","have started","start"],0,["past-simple"]],[["every","each of","all"],0,["quantifiers"]],[["should","would","might"],0,["must-have-to-should"]],[["trying","try","tried"],0,["gerund-infinitive"]],[["at","in","on"],0,["prepositions-in-on-at"]]],"e":["A year ago + past simple: started.","every day: her gün.","should not give up: pes etmemeli.","am trying to write: yazmaya çalışıyorum.","at the end: sonunda."]},{"k":"R6","id":"b1r6f","t":"A Letter to a Friend","ty":"open","nm":"Okuma 6 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 6","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","title":"A letter to a friend","one":true,"p":"Dear Ayşe,\nThank you for your letter. I was very happy to hear (1) ______ you. I am writing to tell you about my holiday in Bodrum, which was the best I have ever had. We stayed in a small hotel (2) ______ the beach, so we could swim every morning.\nThe weather was fantastic, and it didn't rain (3) ______ once. One evening, we went to a restaurant (4) ______ you can eat fresh fish. My brother ate so much that he couldn't move! I hope you (5) ______ come with us next year.\nPlease write back soon and tell me (6) ______ your summer.\nLove, Elif","a":[["from"],["near","by","on","next","close"],["even","once","a"],["where"],["can","will","could","might"],["about"]],"tg":[["prepositions-in-on-at"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["present-perfect"],["relative-clauses"],["first-conditional"],["question-words"]],"e":["hear from you: senden haber almak.","near the beach: plaja yakın.","didn't rain even once: bir kez bile yağmadı.","a restaurant where you can eat: ... yenebilen bir restoran.","I hope you can come: gelebilmeni umarım.","tell me about your summer: yazını anlat."]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_sleep2","t":"Why Do We Need Sleep?","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Why Do We Need Sleep?","p":["Most adults need between seven and nine hours of sleep every night, but many people sleep much less than that. Modern life is busy: we work late, we answer messages in bed and we watch \"just one more\" episode of our favourite series. Scientists say that this habit can be a serious problem, because sleep is not a waste of time. It is an active and necessary process.","When we sleep, our body repairs itself. The muscles recover after a hard day, and the immune system, which protects us from illness, becomes stronger. At the same time, the brain is busy too. It sorts the information that we received during the day, keeps the important parts and deletes the rest. This is why students who sleep well usually remember more than students who stay up all night before an exam.","A lack of sleep has clear effects on our behaviour. People who sleep badly find it harder to concentrate, and they make more mistakes at work or at school. They are also more likely to feel angry, nervous or sad. Some studies even show that tired drivers can be as dangerous as drivers who have drunk alcohol. In the long term, regular lack of sleep may increase the risk of serious illnesses such as heart disease.","So how can we sleep better? Experts suggest a few simple habits. First, go to bed and wake up at the same time every day, even at weekends. Second, avoid coffee and other strong drinks in the evening, because the effect of caffeine can last for many hours. Third, turn off your phone and television about an hour before bed, because the bright light of a screen tells the brain that it is still daytime. Finally, make your bedroom dark, quiet and slightly cool.","These small changes do not cost anything, but they can make a big difference. Many people who try them say that after only two weeks they feel more energetic and in a better mood. Perhaps the best advice is the simplest: treat sleep as an important part of your day, not as something you do when everything else is finished."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main idea of the text?","o":["Coffee harms the brain more than lack of sleep.","Students learn best when they study at night.","Adults need to sleep much more than children.","Sleep matters a lot, and good habits improve it."],"a":3,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Metin uykunun önemini ve iyi uyku alışkanlıklarını anlatıyor."},{"q":"According to the text, what does the brain do during sleep?","o":["It switches off and rests until the morning.","It repairs the heart and other body organs.","It sorts information and keeps what matters.","It makes the body feel hungry again soon."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"It sorts the information... keeps the important parts and deletes the rest.\""},{"q":"Which is NOT mentioned as an effect of too little sleep?","o":["Difficulty concentrating","Feeling angry or sad","Growing taller","Dangerous driving"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Boy uzaması metinde geçmiyor."},{"q":"Why should we turn off screens before bed?","o":["They make a loud noise that wakes people up.","Their bright light tells the brain it is daytime.","They make the room too warm to sleep in well.","They use a lot of electricity during the night."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the bright light of a screen tells the brain that it is still daytime.\""},{"q":"What does the writer say about the effect of caffeine?","o":["It disappears within a few minutes.","It can last for many hours.","It helps people to fall asleep.","It only affects young children."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"the effect of caffeine can last for many hours.\""},{"q":"What does \"treat sleep as an important part of your day\" mean?","o":["Give sleep the importance it deserves.","Take a short nap in the middle of the day.","Sleep late at weekends to recover.","Keep sleep for days when you have time."],"a":0,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Uykuyu \"her şey bittikten sonra yapılan bir şey\" değil, günün önemli bir parçası olarak görmek."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_istanbul2","t":"A Weekend in Istanbul","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"A Weekend in Istanbul","p":["Istanbul is one of the few cities in the world that is on two continents. A ferry ride across the Bosphorus takes only twenty minutes, but you go from Europe to Asia. Many visitors say that this is the best way to see the city, and a ticket costs less than a cup of coffee in some countries. If you stand outside on the deck, the seagulls will follow the boat, and you will see old wooden houses, palaces and fortresses on both sides of the water.","Most tourists start their visit in the old part of the city, Sultanahmet. There you can visit the Hagia Sophia and the Blue Mosque, which are only a short walk from each other. Both buildings are more than a thousand years old, but the Blue Mosque is younger than the Hagia Sophia. Early in the morning the queues are shorter, so it is a good idea to go before nine o'clock. Remember to wear comfortable shoes, because you will walk a lot, and women should take a scarf to cover their hair when they enter a mosque.","After sightseeing, most tourists are hungry. A traditional Turkish breakfast is a good idea at any time of the day: white cheese, olives, eggs, honey, tomatoes and endless glasses of tea. For lunch you can try a fish sandwich near the Galata Bridge, where fishermen stand next to each other with their rods all day long. In the evening, many people climb the Galata Tower to watch the sunset over the Golden Horn.","If you have extra time, the Grand Bazaar is worth a visit. It has more than four thousand shops, and it is easy to get lost inside. The sellers usually expect you to bargain, so do not accept the first price you hear. Be polite, smile and offer about half of the price; the seller will probably laugh and give a better offer. Even if you do not buy anything, the colours, the smells and the sounds of the bazaar are an experience that you will remember for a long time.","Finally, a short piece of advice: do not try to see everything. Istanbul is too big for one weekend. Choose a few places, walk slowly, sit in a small café and watch the people. Most visitors who come once want to come back."],"q":[{"q":"Why is Istanbul special, according to the text?","o":["It is located on two continents.","It has more shops than any other city.","It is the oldest city in the world.","It is a city with no traffic problems."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"one of the few cities in the world that is on two continents.\""},{"q":"Which statement about the Blue Mosque and the Hagia Sophia is true?","o":["The Blue Mosque is older than Hagia Sophia.","They are on opposite sides of the Bosphorus.","They are far apart but linked by ferry.","They are near each other and both are old."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"only a short walk from each other\" ve \"Blue Mosque is younger than the Hagia Sophia.\""},{"q":"Why should visitors go to the old part before nine o'clock?","o":["The ferries stop running soon after nine.","Most of the cafés close early in the day.","Entry is free for visitors who arrive early.","The queues are shorter early in the morning."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Early in the morning the queues are shorter.\""},{"q":"What can you do near the Galata Bridge, according to the text?","o":["Buy a hand-made carpet","Take a ferry to Asia","Eat a fish sandwich","Visit a famous palace"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"try a fish sandwich near the Galata Bridge.\""},{"q":"What does the writer advise about prices in the Grand Bazaar?","o":["Always accept the first price offered.","Pay by card to get a discount.","Try politely to get a lower price.","Avoid buying anything at all."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"do not accept the first price... Be polite, smile and offer about half.\""},{"q":"What is the writer's final advice?","o":["Try to see everything in one weekend.","Choose a few places and go slowly.","Spend most of your time in Asia.","Avoid cafés and eat in the street."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"do not try to see everything... Choose a few places, walk slowly.\""}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_walking2","t":"The Power of a Daily Walk","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Power of a Daily Walk","p":["Doctors often say that walking is one of the simplest ways to stay healthy. You do not need special clothes or expensive equipment, you do not have to pay a membership fee, and you can do it almost anywhere. Just thirty minutes of fast walking a day can help your heart, control your weight and make your legs and bones stronger.","Walking is good for the mind as well. Many writers and scientists have said that they get their best ideas while walking. The German philosopher Nietzsche even wrote that \"all truly great thoughts are conceived while walking\". When we move, more blood goes to the brain, and we often feel calmer and more creative. A short walk outside can also reduce stress after a difficult day at work or school, especially if you walk in a park or near trees.","Of course, many people say, \"I don't have time.\" This is a common excuse, but there are many ways to add more steps to a normal day. You can get off the bus one stop earlier, take the stairs instead of the lift, or walk while you talk on the phone. Some people hold \"walking meetings\" with colleagues instead of sitting in a closed room. Others walk to the shops instead of driving.","If thirty minutes sounds like too much, you can start with ten minutes and slowly increase the time. It is better to walk a little every day than to walk for three hours once a month. Wearing comfortable shoes is the only thing that really matters, because painful feet can quickly kill your motivation. A small notebook or a step counter on your phone can also help, since seeing your progress makes you proud.","Over a few weeks, you will probably notice that you sleep better, climb stairs without getting out of breath and have more energy in the afternoon. Many people also discover new streets, shops and views in their own neighbourhood. So tomorrow, why not put on your shoes and take the first step?"],"q":[{"q":"What do you NOT need for walking, according to the text?","o":["Comfortable shoes","Enough time to go out","Two healthy legs","Expensive equipment"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"You do not need special clothes or expensive equipment.\""},{"q":"Why do many people get good ideas while walking?","o":["They walk through beautiful places.","They talk about ideas with others.","More blood goes to the brain.","Night walks make the mind quieter."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"more blood goes to the brain.\""},{"q":"Which of these is suggested as a way to walk more in a normal day?","o":["Use the lift instead of the stairs.","Drive to the shops at lunchtime.","Hold walking meetings with colleagues.","Walk only at weekends for longer."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"Some people hold walking meetings with colleagues.\""},{"q":"According to the text, what is better?","o":["Walking three hours once a month","Walking a little every day","Walking only in perfect weather","Running every other day instead"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"It is better to walk a little every day than to walk for three hours once a month.\""},{"q":"What is the only really important thing to wear?","o":["A good sports watch","Comfortable shoes","A warm hat and gloves","Clothes made for hiking"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Wearing comfortable shoes is the only thing that really matters.\""},{"q":"What does \"excuse\" mean in \"a common excuse\"?","o":["A reason given to avoid doing something","A polite way to ask someone to forgive you","A short rest taken between two long tasks","A medical reason for not exercising"],"a":0,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"excuse = bahane."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_online2","t":"Learning a Language Online","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Learning a Language Online","p":["Ten years ago, people who wanted to learn a language usually had to attend classes at a school. They bought a heavy textbook, travelled to the classroom twice a week and listened to a teacher for two hours. Today, thousands of apps and websites offer lessons that you can follow at home, on the bus or during your lunch break. This makes learning cheaper and more flexible, but it also brings some new problems.","The biggest problem is motivation. When nobody is waiting for you in a classroom, it is very easy to skip a lesson. Many learners start with great energy, buy a yearly subscription and study every day for the first week. After two or three weeks, however, they are tired or busy, and slowly they stop. Studies show that most people give up an online course before they finish it.","How can we avoid this? First, set a small daily goal. Learning five new words every day sounds easy, but after a year it means almost two thousand words. Second, connect your lessons with a fixed time, for example, right after breakfast or just before you go to bed. A habit that is tied to another habit is easier to keep. Third, find a friend or a group who study the same language, because it is more difficult to stop when others are waiting for you.","Another important challenge is speaking practice. Apps are very good for vocabulary and grammar, and many of them use games to make learning fun. However, a game cannot give you a real conversation. In real life, people speak fast, use slang and make jokes that are not in the book. To improve your speaking, you need to talk to real people. Language exchange groups and online conversation partners are a great solution because they are often free, and both sides help each other.","In the end, the best method is a mixture. Use an app for a daily routine, read short texts or stories at your level, listen to podcasts or songs, and speak with other people whenever you can. Do not be afraid of mistakes; they are a normal and necessary part of learning. The only real mistake is to stop."],"q":[{"q":"What was the usual way to learn a language ten years ago?","o":["Using language apps on a phone","Watching English films at home","Living in another country","Going to classes at a school"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"people... usually had to attend classes at a school.\""},{"q":"According to the text, why do many learners give up?","o":["The lessons turn out to be far too hard.","The apps cost much more than normal classes.","They lose their motivation after some weeks.","They do not like their online teacher."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"The biggest problem is motivation.\""},{"q":"What does the writer suggest connecting the lessons with?","o":["A holiday in another country","A prize after each lesson","A fixed time every day","A new phone or tablet"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"connect your lessons with a fixed time.\""},{"q":"Why can apps not replace real conversations?","o":["Apps cannot teach grammar or new vocabulary.","Real people speak fast and use slang and jokes.","App lessons move too slowly for most people.","Apps cost far more than hiring a real teacher."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"people speak fast, use slang and make jokes that are not in the book.\""},{"q":"What does the writer say about mistakes?","o":["They should be avoided whenever it is possible.","They are a normal and necessary part of learning.","They show that the teacher is not very good.","They only happen to complete beginners at first."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"mistakes... are a normal and necessary part of learning.\""},{"q":"What is the best method, according to the writer?","o":["A mixture of different methods","Using one good app every day","Going to a language school only","Reading as many books as possible"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"the best method is a mixture.\""}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_library3","t":"The Library That Changed Its Rules","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Library That Changed Its Rules","p":["For many years, the public library in the small town of Marlow was a very quiet place. People came in, borrowed a few books, and left. The rules were strict: no talking, no food, no phones, and a fine for every book that was returned late. The librarian, Mrs. Hughes, had worked there for thirty years, and she believed that silence was the most important thing a library could offer.","But over time, fewer and fewer people visited. The number of borrowed books fell every year, and the town council began to ask whether the building was worth its cost. Some council members even suggested closing it and selling the land. When Mrs. Hughes heard this, she was shocked. She had always thought that everybody loved the library as much as she did.","Her young assistant, Daniel, had a different idea. He had grown up in Marlow, and he remembered that as a teenager he had stopped going to the library because it felt like a place where he was always doing something wrong. 'People don't hate books,' he told her one afternoon. 'They hate feeling unwelcome.' At first Mrs. Hughes disagreed, but she agreed to try a small experiment for three months.","The first change was simple: the library removed the late fines. Instead of paying money, people who returned a book late only had to wait a few days before borrowing another one. Many families who had stopped visiting because of old debts came back. One woman cried when she was told that she no longer owed anything after six years. She returned with her three children the very next day.","The second change was a quiet room and a 'talking room'. In the quiet room, the old rules stayed the same, so students and people who wanted to read in peace could still enjoy the silence. In the talking room, however, visitors could chat, drink coffee, and even work in groups. Daniel placed a few sofas there and added a small table with free tea. Within a week, the talking room was full every afternoon.","The third change was the most surprising. The staff began to organise free events: a weekly storytelling hour for children, a computer class for older people, and a monthly evening where neighbours could swap clothes and tools. Some events were popular at once, while others took time to attract visitors. A cooking club for beginners, for example, started with only two people, but after two months, more than twenty were coming every Thursday.","Not everyone was happy. A few regular readers complained that the building had become too noisy, and Mrs. Hughes herself felt uncomfortable at first when she heard laughter near the shelves. 'It doesn't feel like a library any more,' she said. Daniel answered calmly, 'Maybe it feels like a library for more people.' She did not reply, but the next day she was seen sitting in the talking room with a cup of tea, listening to a group of teenagers discussing a novel.","After three months, the results were clear. The number of visitors had doubled, and book loans had increased by forty percent. Even better, the library now had a waiting list for its computer classes. The town council, which had planned to close the building, instead decided to give it a small budget increase. One council member said, 'We didn't realise the library was something the whole community could use.'","Today, Mrs. Hughes is retired, and Daniel is the head librarian. The quiet room still exists, and it is as peaceful as ever. But the heart of the building is the talking room, where children, students, and grandparents sit side by side. On the wall hangs a small sign that Mrs. Hughes wrote before she left: 'A library is not a place for books. It is a place for people who love stories, and stories are told in many voices.'","The story of the Marlow library shows that change does not always mean losing what is valuable. By keeping the best of the past and listening to the needs of the present, a place that seemed old-fashioned became important to the town again. Many other small libraries in the region have since visited Marlow to learn how it was done, and several of them have started similar experiments of their own."],"q":[{"q":"Why did the town council think about closing the library?","o":["Fewer people were visiting and borrowing books.","The building was in a dangerous condition.","Mrs. Hughes had decided to retire that year.","The council needed the land for a school."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"The number of borrowed books fell every year, and the council asked whether the building was worth its cost."},{"q":"What did Daniel believe was the real problem?","o":["People no longer enjoyed reading.","The books were too old and dusty.","The library was too small for visitors.","People felt unwelcome there."],"a":3,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"'They hate feeling unwelcome.'"},{"q":"What replaced late fines?","o":["A small donation paid to the library each time","A temporary ban from using the library","Some community work for the town council","A short wait before borrowing another book"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"People only had to wait a few days before borrowing another one."},{"q":"What was the purpose of the \"talking room\"?","o":["To store old books nobody wanted any more","To hold computer exams for local students","To let visitors chat and work in groups","To keep noisy children away from readers"],"a":2,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Visitors could chat, drink coffee, and work in groups."},{"q":"How did the cooking club develop?","o":["It was popular from the very first day.","It was cancelled after only two months.","It grew from two people to over twenty.","It attracted only children and teenagers."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"It started with only two people, but after two months more than twenty were coming."},{"q":"How did Mrs. Hughes's attitude change?","o":["She stayed completely against the changes.","She slowly accepted the new atmosphere.","She asked Daniel to leave the library.","She closed the talking room herself."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"She was seen sitting in the talking room with a cup of tea, listening to teenagers."},{"q":"What is the main message of the passage?","o":["Libraries should always stay silent and calm.","Old places gain value by listening to people.","Free tea is the best way to attract visitors.","Towns should close buildings that few people use."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"By keeping the best of the past and listening to the present, the library became important again."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_sleepy3","t":"Why Teenagers Need More Sleep","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Why Teenagers Need More Sleep","p":["Many parents and teachers complain that teenagers are lazy in the morning. They stay in bed until noon on weekends, they look tired during the first lesson, and they often fall asleep in class. However, scientists who study sleep say that this is not simply a question of bad habits. During the teenage years, the human body goes through real changes that affect when we feel sleepy and when we feel awake.","The main reason is the biological clock, a small system in the brain that tells the body when to sleep. In children, this clock usually makes them tired around eight or nine in the evening. In teenagers, the clock moves later. The body begins to produce the hormone that makes us sleepy about two hours later than before. This means that a fifteen-year-old who is lying in bed at ten o'clock may simply not be able to fall asleep, even if he wants to.","At the same time, teenagers need a lot of sleep, usually between eight and ten hours every night. But many of them get much less. School often starts at eight o'clock or earlier, so a student who falls asleep at midnight and wakes up at six has only six hours of rest. Over a week, this lost sleep adds up, and doctors call it 'sleep debt'. Like a money debt, it has to be paid back at some point, which is why teenagers sleep so long at weekends.","The effects of too little sleep are serious. Tired students find it harder to concentrate, and they remember less of what they study. They are also more likely to feel anxious or sad, and some research suggests that they have more accidents, especially when they begin to drive. Their health suffers too: people who regularly sleep too little are more likely to gain weight and to catch colds.","Modern technology makes the problem worse. Many teenagers use their phones in bed, watching videos or chatting with friends. The screen produces a bright blue light, which tells the brain that it is still daytime. As a result, the body produces less of the sleepy hormone, and it becomes even harder to fall asleep. In addition, messages and notifications can wake young people during the night without them even realising it.","Some schools have tried a simple solution: starting lessons later. In several cities, schools moved the first lesson from eight to nine o'clock. The results were encouraging. Students were more alert in class, their marks improved slightly, and fewer of them were late. Teachers also reported that students were in a better mood and caused fewer problems. However, not everyone liked the change. Some parents said that later lessons meant a later end to the day, which made it difficult to organise sports, jobs, and family time.","What can teenagers do at home? Experts suggest a few practical steps. First, try to go to bed and get up at similar times every day, even at weekends, so that the body clock stays regular. Second, stop using screens at least an hour before bed, and keep the phone outside the bedroom if possible. Third, avoid drinks with caffeine, such as cola or energy drinks, in the afternoon and evening. Finally, make the bedroom dark, cool, and quiet.","Parents can help too. Instead of getting angry about late mornings, they can try to understand that their children are not trying to be difficult. A calm talk about sleep, together with a few agreed rules about phones in the evening, is usually more effective than punishment. Some families even choose a shared 'switch-off hour', when everyone in the house puts their devices away.","In the end, sleep is not a waste of time. It is the period when the brain stores memories, repairs the body, and prepares us for the next day. If we want young people to learn well and stay healthy, we need to take their sleep as seriously as we take their food, their exercise, and their education."],"q":[{"q":"According to the passage, why are teenagers sleepy in the morning?","o":["They are lazier than adults are.","Their biological clock moves later.","They eat too much late at night.","They do not enjoy going to school."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"In teenagers, the clock moves later; the sleepy hormone is produced about two hours later."},{"q":"What is \"sleep debt\"?","o":["Lost sleep that adds up over several days","Money paid back for an expensive bed","A medical test for people with insomnia","A habit of sleeping too many hours"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Lost sleep adds up, and doctors call it sleep debt; it has to be paid back."},{"q":"How does phone light affect sleep?","o":["It tells the brain it is still daytime.","It makes the body produce more sleepy hormone.","It has no effect on how people sleep.","It makes people dream much more."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The blue light tells the brain that it is still daytime."},{"q":"What happened when some schools started lessons later?","o":["Students became lazier and failed more exams.","Many teachers decided to leave their jobs.","Nothing changed in the results of the schools.","Students were more alert and did slightly better."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Students were more alert, marks improved slightly, fewer were late."},{"q":"Why did some parents dislike later lessons?","o":["They believed that exam marks would fall.","They wanted their children to sleep a good deal less.","School fees would have to rise every single year.","Lessons would end later, which affected other activities."],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Later lessons meant a later end to the day, which made sports, jobs, and family time difficult."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT advice given in the passage?","o":["Keep regular times for sleeping and waking","Stop using screens an hour before bed","Drink coffee in the evening to stay awake","Make the bedroom dark and quiet at night"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Experts recommend avoiding caffeine in the afternoon and evening."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_bike3","t":"The City That Fell in Love with Bicycles","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The City That Fell in Love with Bicycles","p":["Twenty years ago, the city of Veldhaven was famous for one thing: traffic. Every morning, thousands of cars moved slowly towards the centre, and the air smelled of exhaust. A journey of five kilometres could take an hour. People complained, the mayor promised improvements, and nothing happened. Then a new transport director, Anneke Jansen, arrived with a plan that many people thought was crazy.","Her idea was to make cycling the easiest way to move around the city. She did not ask people to give up their cars immediately. Instead, she wanted to build a network of safe bicycle lanes, separated from cars by a low wall or a row of plants. 'Most people don't cycle because they are afraid,' she explained. 'If they feel safe, they will try it.'","The first lanes opened on two main roads, and the reaction was bad. Shop owners were angry because they thought customers would not be able to park. Drivers complained that the roads had become narrower. A local newspaper called the project 'a waste of public money', and some residents wrote letters demanding that the lanes be removed. Anneke listened to every complaint, but she did not change her plan.","Within a year, however, the picture began to change. A study counted the people who passed along the main road and found that the number of cyclists had tripled. The shops had not lost customers; in fact, they had gained some, because cyclists stopped more often than drivers and spent more time in the area. Several shop owners who were skeptical at first now asked for lanes outside their own doors.","The city also made cycling more convenient. It built large, guarded parking places near the railway station, where people could leave their bikes for a small fee. Employers were encouraged to offer showers for workers who cycled to the office. Schools organised lessons on road safety, and children who passed a simple test received a small certificate. Over time, riding a bicycle became something normal, even fashionable.","The results were measurable. Traffic jams in the city centre fell by almost a third, and the average commute became shorter, even for the drivers who stayed in their cars. Air pollution dropped, and hospitals reported fewer cases of breathing problems among children. Because people were exercising every day without really thinking about it, the city's health costs also began to fall, and an insurance company even offered lower prices to regular cyclists.","Of course, the system is not perfect. In winter, when it rains or snows, the lanes can be dangerous, and the city has to work hard to keep them clear. Some elderly residents and people with disabilities cannot cycle at all, so buses and special taxis remain very important. Anneke herself admits that the plan would not have worked without good public transport. 'Bicycles are one part of the solution, not the whole of it,' she says.","Visitors from other countries now come to Veldhaven to see how it works. Many are surprised by how calm the streets feel. Parents ride with small children on the back of their bikes, businessmen travel in suits, and old people move slowly along the lanes with shopping baskets. There is no special clothing, no race, and no hurry.","The story shows that big changes in a city often begin with a small, brave decision. Anneke Jansen was not popular at first, but she stayed patient and trusted the evidence. Today, even the shop owner who once called her plan stupid says, 'I was wrong. Veldhaven is a better place to live.'"],"q":[{"q":"What was the main problem in Veldhaven twenty years ago?","o":["Too few cars on the main roads","A lack of roads for new cars","Heavy traffic and polluted air","Very high prices in the shops"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Thousands of cars moved slowly and the air smelled of exhaust."},{"q":"What was Anneke Jansen's central idea?","o":["Ban every private car from the city at once","Build safe bicycle lanes separate from cars","Close all the shops in the city centre","Build more car parks near all the shops"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Build a network of safe lanes separated from cars."},{"q":"Why were shop owners against the lanes at first?","o":["They disliked bicycles in general","They feared customers could not park","They wanted wider pavements outside","Drivers' groups were paying them"],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"They thought customers would not be able to park."},{"q":"What did the study after one year find?","o":["Cyclists tripled and shops lost no business","Shops lost many of their regular customers","Accidents on the roads had increased a lot","Most drivers were happier than before"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Cyclists tripled and shops had even gained some customers."},{"q":"Which is a limitation of the cycling system mentioned in the text?","o":["Winter weather and people who cannot cycle","It is far too expensive for most tourists","There is nowhere safe to park a bicycle","Employers refuse to let their staff cycle"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Lanes can be dangerous in winter; some elderly and disabled people cannot cycle."},{"q":"What does Anneke mean by \"Bicycles are one part of the solution\"?","o":["Bicycles are not very useful","All cars must disappear from the city","Only young people should use bikes","Good public transport is also needed"],"a":3,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"The plan would not have worked without good public transport."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_kitchen6","t":"Cooking Together","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Cooking Together","p":["In many cities, a quiet change is taking place in the way people eat. Instead of cooking alone at home or buying ready meals, more and more people are joining community kitchens. These are shared spaces where neighbours meet once or twice a week to prepare a meal together and then eat it at one big table. Some kitchens are run by charities, while others are organised by the residents themselves.","Nora, a 28-year-old nurse, joined a community kitchen in her neighbourhood last year. She had recently moved to a new city and knew nobody there. \"I worked long shifts and usually ate a sandwich standing up,\" she says. \"At the kitchen, I learned to make soup from scratch and, more importantly, I made friends.\" Now she goes every Thursday evening and often brings a recipe from her grandmother.","The kitchens also help people save money. The food is usually bought in large quantities, so it is cheaper, and the ingredients are shared among everyone. Many kitchens use vegetables that supermarkets cannot sell because they look strange or are close to their expiry date. In this way, the projects reduce food waste as well as the cost of a meal.","Of course, there are challenges. Organisers need a suitable place, and the rules about food safety are strict. Volunteers must wash their hands, label allergies and keep the kitchen spotless. Some people also find it difficult to cook in a large group because everyone has a different idea about how much salt or spice a dish needs. \"We argue about garlic every week,\" laughs one volunteer.","Still, most members agree that the benefits are greater than the problems. Cooking together gives people a reason to leave the house, talk to others and try new foods. As one organiser says, \"A meal is never only about food. It is about sitting together and feeling that you belong.\""],"q":[{"q":"What is the main idea of the text?","o":["Community kitchens bring people together and cut cost.","Ready meals are replacing home cooking in big cities.","Charities ought to run all the kitchens in a city.","Learning to cook matters more than making friends."],"a":0,"tg":["gist"],"e":"The text covers friendship, lower costs and shared meals."},{"q":"Why did Nora join the community kitchen?","o":["She wanted to become a professional cook.","Her hospital asked staff to cook together.","She was bored with her grandmother's recipes.","She had just moved and knew nobody there."],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"She had recently moved and knew nobody."},{"q":"How do the kitchens save money?","o":["They get free meals from restaurants every night.","They grow all their vegetables in their gardens.","They ask members to bring food from home.","They buy in bulk and use food shops cannot sell."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Bulk buying and unsellable vegetables lower the cost."},{"q":"In paragraph 4, \"spotless\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["carefully organised","recently repaired","completely clean","brightly lit"],"a":2,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Spotless means without any dirt or marks."},{"q":"What problem do some volunteers mention about cooking in a group?","o":["There are not enough tools or pans for everyone.","The meals take far too long to prepare each time.","Members disagree about how to season the food.","Many of the members do not know how to cook."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"They argue about garlic, salt and spice."},{"q":"What does the organiser's last comment suggest?","o":["Meals taste better when they cost more.","People value belonging as much as the food.","Most people come mainly to learn skills.","Eating alone is better than eating in a group."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"Feeling that you belong\" is the key idea."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_swim6","t":"Learning to Swim as an Adult","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Learning to Swim as an Adult","p":["Most people learn to swim as children, but a surprising number of adults never do. The reasons vary: some grew up far from water, others had a frightening experience, and many simply felt too embarrassed to ask for lessons later in life. For these people, standing at the edge of a pool can be a source of real anxiety.","Tomas, a 42-year-old accountant, was one of them. When he was seven, he fell into a lake and had to be pulled out by his uncle. After that, he avoided water for thirty-five years. \"I told my family that I didn't like the beach,\" he says. \"The truth was that I was afraid.\" The turning point came when his daughter started swimming lessons and asked him to join her in the pool.","Tomas signed up for an adult beginners' class at his local sports centre. The first lesson was difficult. He held on to the side of the pool and could not even put his face in the water. However, the instructor, a patient woman called Lucia, did not rush him. She explained that fear is a natural reaction and that progress is measured in small steps. In the first month, the class practised only breathing and floating.","Instructors say that adult learners face special challenges. Their bodies are less flexible than children's, and they worry about what other people think. On the other hand, adults are usually more determined, and they understand instructions better. \"Children copy what they see,\" says Lucia. \"Adults ask why, and that helps them to improve their technique.\"","After six months, Tomas could swim twenty-five metres without stopping. Last summer, he swam in the sea for the first time. \"I cried a little,\" he admits, \"but nobody could see because my face was wet anyway.\" He now recommends lessons to anyone who is still afraid. \"It's never too late. The hardest part is walking through the door.\""],"q":[{"q":"Why do some adults never learn to swim?","o":["They think swimming is too dangerous for older people.","Lessons for adults are not available in most cities.","Some lived far from water or felt too embarrassed to ask.","They have no interest in any kind of sport."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"The first paragraph lists three kinds of reasons."},{"q":"What made Tomas afraid of water?","o":["He nearly drowned in a swimming pool.","He fell into a lake when he was seven.","He was laughed at during a school lesson.","His uncle once forced him to swim."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"He fell into a lake at the age of seven."},{"q":"What made him decide to take lessons?","o":["His doctor strongly advised him to exercise more.","His daughter asked him to join her in the pool.","He wanted to swim with his friends on holiday.","His family often complained about his fear."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The turning point was his daughter's request."},{"q":"How did Lucia help Tomas at the beginning?","o":["She let him progress slowly with simple exercises.","She asked him to swim a short distance on day one.","She gave him a private lesson every single week.","She told him to ignore his fear of the water."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"She did not rush him; the class only practised breathing and floating."},{"q":"What does Lucia mean by \"Adults ask why\"?","o":["Adults want to understand how a technique works.","Adults are afraid of making mistakes in front of others.","Adults question the instructor's methods too often.","Adults need much more time to learn than children."],"a":0,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Understanding helps adults improve their technique."},{"q":"What does Tomas suggest at the end?","o":["Swimming in the sea is easier than in a pool.","Only young children should learn to swim.","Swimming lessons should be free for all adults.","Taking the first step is the hardest part."],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"The hardest part is walking through the door.\""}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_tiny6","t":"Living Small","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Living Small","p":["For most of the twentieth century, the idea of a \"dream home\" meant more space: more bedrooms, a bigger garden and a double garage. Recently, however, a growing number of people have chosen the opposite. They live in tiny houses of less than thirty square metres, many of which are built on wheels so that they can be moved.","Ivy, a 31-year-old web designer, built her tiny house in a friend's garden. It has a kitchen, a bathroom, a sleeping area above the stairs and a small desk by the window. \"At first, I worried that I wouldn't have enough room for my things,\" she says. \"So I sold or gave away about three quarters of what I owned. It felt strange, then liberating.\"","There are practical reasons for the trend. Houses in many cities have become so expensive that young people cannot afford to buy one. A tiny house costs a fraction of the price, and its bills are much lower because it needs less energy to heat. Some owners also like the idea of leaving a smaller carbon footprint.","Living small has disadvantages, too. Guests have to sit close together, and storing things is a constant challenge. In many places, it is also difficult to find legal land where a tiny house can be placed, because local rules often require a minimum size for homes. Couples sometimes find that two people in such a small space need a lot of patience.","Nevertheless, supporters say the lifestyle teaches valuable lessons. \"You stop buying things just because they are on sale,\" Ivy explains. \"You ask yourself whether you really need something, and usually the answer is no.\" For her, the biggest benefit is not the money she saves, but the freedom to spend her time on what matters to her."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main idea of the text?","o":["Tiny houses are the answer to the housing crisis.","Large homes are disappearing from modern cities.","People choose tiny homes for practical reasons.","Building a tiny house is easy for everyone."],"a":2,"tg":["gist"],"e":"The text gives reasons, problems and benefits of tiny houses."},{"q":"What did Ivy do before moving into her tiny house?","o":["She asked a friend to design the house.","She got rid of most of her belongings.","She rented out her old family home.","She bought furniture made for small spaces."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"She sold or gave away three quarters of her things."},{"q":"Why are tiny houses attractive from a financial point of view?","o":["They can be sold for more money later.","They are cheaper to buy and to heat.","Owners do not have to pay local taxes.","They are built from cheap recycled materials."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"The price is a fraction of a normal house and bills are lower."},{"q":"In paragraph 2, \"liberating\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["giving a feeling of freedom","causing a feeling of loss","needing a lot of effort","making life more complicated"],"a":0,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Ivy felt free after getting rid of her things."},{"q":"What difficulty is mentioned about the location of tiny houses?","o":["Local rules make it hard to find legal land.","There is very little land in the countryside.","Neighbours often complain about the noise.","Tiny houses cannot be moved at all."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Many places have minimum-size rules for homes."},{"q":"What does Ivy see as the greatest benefit of living small?","o":["Saving enough to buy a bigger house","Owning fewer things than her friends do","Living much closer to her workplace","Being free to spend time on what matters"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"She says freedom matters more than the money saved."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_hotel7","t":"Booking a Hotel Online","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Booking a Hotel Online","p":["Last spring, my cousin Ece and I decided to spend a weekend in Izmir. We had only a week to plan, so we looked for a hotel on the internet. There were hundreds of choices, and at first it was difficult to decide.","Ece wanted the cheapest room, but I preferred to read the reviews first. One hotel cost very little, but many guests wrote that the rooms were noisy and the breakfast was not included. Another one was slightly more expensive, yet people said the staff were friendly and there was a lovely view of the sea. We chose the second one.","Before we paid, I checked the cancellation rules. The booking could be cancelled free of charge until two days before our arrival, and after that we would get only half of our money back. That seemed fair, so we confirmed the reservation and received an email within minutes.","The weekend was perfect. Our room was on the fourth floor, and every morning we had tea on the balcony and watched the boats. At the end, I wrote a short review myself. I think reviews are useful, because the next guests can learn from our experience, just as we learnt from other people's."],"q":[{"q":"Why did the writer choose the second hotel?","o":["It was the cheapest one online.","Ece had stayed there before.","Breakfast was included in the price.","Guests praised the staff and the view."],"a":3,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Yorumlarda personel ve manzara övülüyordu."},{"q":"What happens if they cancel one day before arrival?","o":["They pay nothing at all.","They lose all their money.","They get back half of the money.","They have to book another room."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"İki günden sonra yalnızca yarı iade var."},{"q":"What did the writer do after the weekend?","o":["She sent an email to Ece.","She asked for a refund.","She wrote her own opinion of the hotel.","She booked the same room again."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I wrote a short review myself.\""},{"q":"What is the writer's opinion about reviews?","o":["They are usually not honest.","They help future guests decide.","They are too long to read.","They only matter for cheap hotels."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Sonraki misafirler deneyimlerden öğrenir."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_doctor7","t":"At the Doctor's","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"At the Doctor's","p":["On Monday morning, Burak woke up with a sore throat and a headache. He did not have a fever, so he thought it was only a cold. By the evening, however, he felt much worse, and his mother made an appointment with Dr Aksoy for the next day.","In the waiting room, there were five other patients. Burak had to wait for twenty minutes, which he found boring, so he read an old magazine about gardens. At last, the nurse called his name.","Dr Aksoy asked him about his symptoms and looked at his throat. \"You have an infection,\" she said. \"I'll give you a prescription for some medicine. Take it three times a day after meals, and drink plenty of water.\" She also told him to stay at home for three days and not to go to football training until he felt completely well.","Burak took the prescription to the pharmacy near his house. The pharmacist explained that the medicine could make him a little sleepy, so he should not do any sport. Four days later he had recovered, and he was happy to be back at school. \"Next time,\" he said, \"I'll go to the doctor sooner.\""],"q":[{"q":"Why did Burak's mother call the doctor?","o":["He had a high fever on Monday.","He had missed football training.","He felt worse during the day.","He wanted to leave school early."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Akşama doğru çok daha kötüydü."},{"q":"What did Burak do in the waiting room?","o":["He talked to the nurse.","He read a magazine.","He played on his phone.","He fell asleep."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Bahçe dergisini okudu."},{"q":"What did the doctor tell Burak to do?","o":["Go to hospital for more tests.","Take medicine and avoid training for a while.","Eat nothing for three days.","Go back to school the next day."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"İlaç, bol su ve üç gün evde dinlenme."},{"q":"What does Burak say at the end?","o":["He will see a doctor earlier in future.","He will never go to football again.","He doesn't trust the pharmacist.","He thinks doctors are too slow."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Next time I'll go to the doctor sooner.\""}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_driving7","t":"Learning to Drive","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Learning to Drive","p":["When Selin turned eighteen, she decided to learn to drive. Her parents were pleased, because they were tired of taking her everywhere. She signed up at a driving school near her house and had her first lesson the following Monday.","Her instructor, Mr Polat, was a calm man who never raised his voice. In the first lesson, they practised in an empty car park. Selin found it hard to start the car smoothly, and the engine stopped three times. She laughed about it, but inside she was nervous.","After ten lessons, she felt more confident and began driving on real roads. The roundabouts were the most difficult part, because she never knew when to enter. \"Look to the right and wait for a gap,\" Mr Polat said every time. Slowly, it became easier.","In March, she took her theory test and passed it at the first attempt. The driving test was harder: she made a small mistake and had to try again a month later. This time she was calm, and the examiner smiled at the end. \"You have passed,\" he said. Selin could not stop smiling all the way home."],"q":[{"q":"How did Selin feel in her first lesson?","o":["She was bored with the car park.","She was angry with Mr Polat.","She was anxious but tried to laugh.","She was completely relaxed."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"She laughed about it, but inside she was nervous.\""},{"q":"What was most difficult for Selin on the road?","o":["starting the engine","deciding when to enter a roundabout","parking in small spaces","driving at night"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Döner kavşakta ne zaman girileceğini bilmiyordu."},{"q":"What happened in the first driving test?","o":["She passed it easily.","She made a mistake and failed.","She cancelled it.","She forgot her documents."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Küçük bir hata yaptı ve bir ay sonra yeniden girdi."},{"q":"How did Selin feel at the end?","o":["delighted","disappointed","worried","embarrassed"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"Selin could not stop smiling.\""}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_festival7","t":"The Spring Festival","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Spring Festival","p":["Every April, our town holds a spring festival that lasts three days. People come from nearby villages, and the main street is closed to traffic. For us children, it is the best time of the year.","The festival begins on Friday with a parade. Students from every school walk down the street in colourful costumes, and a band plays traditional music. My class dressed as flowers last year, and my little brother, who was a bee, refused to take off his wings for a week!","On Saturday, there are stalls with local food, handmade toys and flowers. My grandmother always sells her homemade jam, and she usually runs out of it by noon. In the evening, there is a concert in the square, and the whole crowd sings together, even if they don't know the words.","The festival ends on Sunday with a picnic by the river. Families bring blankets and share their food with their neighbours. I like this tradition because it makes the town feel like one big family. If you are ever in the area in April, you should come and see it."],"q":[{"q":"What happens on the first day of the festival?","o":["People sell jam in the square.","Students walk in a parade.","Families have a picnic.","A concert is held by the river."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Cuma: geçit töreni."},{"q":"What does the writer say about his little brother?","o":["He loved his costume very much.","He was frightened of the crowd.","He did not want to be in the parade.","He sold flowers at a stall."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Kanatlarını bir hafta çıkarmak istemedi."},{"q":"Why does the writer like the picnic?","o":["It makes people feel close to each other.","The food is better than at the stalls.","It is the shortest part of the festival.","Nobody has to bring anything."],"a":0,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Kasabayı büyük bir aile gibi hissettiriyor."},{"q":"Who is the text written for?","o":["students in the writer's school","the town council","shop owners","people who might visit the town"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Son cümle: \"if you are ever in the area... come and see it\"."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_jobs7","t":"A Part-Time Job","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"A Part-Time Job","p":["Many students want to earn some money while they study, and I am no exception. Last autumn, I started working in a bookshop on Saturdays and two evenings a week. The wage is not high, but I enjoy the job more than I expected.","My day begins at nine, when I open the shop with my manager, Mrs Yılmaz. First, we put the new books on the shelves. Then I help customers find what they need. Some of them are very polite, but others get impatient when I cannot find a title immediately.","The job has taught me a lot. I have learnt to stay calm, to listen carefully and to organise my time. It is not always easy to balance work and study, though. Before exams, I asked Mrs Yılmaz for fewer shifts, and she kindly agreed.","My advice to other students is to look for a job that fits their timetable. A part-time job gives you experience, which is useful when you apply for a full-time job later. But never forget that your studies come first."],"q":[{"q":"What does the writer say about the pay?","o":["It is much higher than expected.","It is low, but the job is enjoyable.","It is the reason she took the job.","It depends on the number of customers."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"The wage is not high, but I enjoy the job more.\""},{"q":"What is difficult about the job sometimes?","o":["Customers who are impatient","Opening the shop alone","Carrying heavy boxes","Working on Sundays"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Bazı müşteriler kitabı hemen bulamayınca sabırsızlanıyor."},{"q":"What did the writer do before her exams?","o":["She asked to work fewer shifts.","She left the job for a month.","She studied in the shop.","She changed her timetable at school."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Daha az vardiya istedi."},{"q":"What is her advice to students?","o":["Never work during the school year.","Look for the highest salary.","Work as many hours as possible.","Choose a job that suits their lessons."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Programa uyan bir iş bulun; dersler önce gelir."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_flight7","t":"My First Flight","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"My First Flight","p":["I had never travelled by plane before, so I was both excited and afraid when my family went to Antalya last summer. We arrived at the airport three hours early, because my father hates being late.","First, we left our luggage at the check-in desk and received our boarding passes. Then we went through security, where I had to take off my belt and put my bag on a belt. It was easier than I imagined, but I was glad my mother was there to tell me what to do.","At the gate, we waited for almost an hour. When they finally called our flight, I felt my heart beating faster. Inside the plane, I sat next to the window, and my sister sat beside me. During take-off, I held the armrest tightly. After a few minutes, however, the plane was in the clouds, and I started to relax.","The view was incredible: the sea looked like a blue carpet, and the cars were tiny. The landing was smooth, and everyone clapped. \"Was it that terrible?\" my father asked. I answered that I wanted to fly again as soon as possible."],"q":[{"q":"Why did the family arrive early at the airport?","o":["The flight had been cancelled.","The writer's father doesn't like to be late.","They wanted to go shopping.","Their luggage was very heavy."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"my father hates being late\""},{"q":"How did the writer feel about security?","o":["It was simpler than he had thought.","It took a very long time.","It was frightening.","It was boring."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"It was easier than I imagined.\""},{"q":"What did the writer do during take-off?","o":["He held the armrest tightly.","He closed his eyes and slept.","He talked to his sister.","He looked out of the window."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Kol dayanağını sıkıca tuttu."},{"q":"How did he feel at the end of the flight?","o":["He was relieved it was over.","He felt sick.","He was sorry he had come.","He wanted to fly again soon."],"a":3,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"En kısa sürede yeniden uçmak istedi."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_flat7","t":"Looking for a Flat","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Looking for a Flat","p":["When Deniz got a place at university in Ankara, he had to find a flat. His parents advised him to start looking early, so he spent two weekends visiting different apartments with his uncle.","The first flat was big and cheap, but it was a long way from the university, and the neighbourhood was very noisy. The second one was furnished and close to the metro, yet the rent was higher than Deniz could afford. He was beginning to lose hope when his uncle noticed a small advertisement in a shop window.","The third flat was perfect. It had two bedrooms, so Deniz could share it with a friend and divide the rent. The landlord, an elderly teacher, asked for a deposit of one month's rent and showed them a contract that explained everything clearly. \"Read it carefully before you sign,\" he said.","Deniz moved in the following Saturday. On his first evening, he cooked pasta for his new flatmate and his uncle, and they celebrated with tea on the balcony. \"It's not a palace,\" Deniz said, \"but it's ours.\""],"q":[{"q":"Why did Deniz reject the first flat?","o":["It was too small.","The landlord was unfriendly.","It was far away and in a noisy area.","It had no furniture."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Uzak ve gürültülü bir semtteydi."},{"q":"What was wrong with the second flat?","o":["It was far from the metro.","The rent was too high for him.","It was unfurnished.","It was on the ground floor."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Kira karşılayabileceğinden yüksekti."},{"q":"How did Deniz find the third flat?","o":["A friend recommended it.","His uncle saw an advertisement.","He found it online.","The university suggested it."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Amcası vitrindeki ilanı gördü."},{"q":"What does \"It's not a palace, but it's ours\" suggest?","o":["He is happy with a simple home.","He is disappointed.","He wants a bigger flat.","He is angry with his uncle."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Sade ama kendisinin olan evden memnun."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_rain7","t":"A Rainy Holiday","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"A Rainy Holiday","p":["We had been looking forward to our holiday in the mountains for months. The forecast said sunshine all week, so we packed shorts, sun cream and hats. When we arrived at the little cabin on Saturday, however, it was pouring with rain.","It rained on Sunday, and it rained on Monday. By Tuesday, my brother and I were bored. We had finished all our books, and the only things in the cabin were an old radio and a box of board games. Dad, who hates losing, suggested a family tournament.","To my surprise, it turned out to be fun. We played until midnight, and Mum, who nobody expected to win, beat us all. On Wednesday, we put on raincoats and went for a walk anyway. We jumped in puddles, found mushrooms and returned home soaked but laughing.","On Thursday, the sun finally came out. We climbed to the top of the hill and had a picnic. People often ask if the holiday was spoiled by the weather. I tell them it was one of the best we have ever had, because we learnt how to make the best of every situation."],"q":[{"q":"What did the family pack for the holiday?","o":["warm coats","clothes for hot weather","raincoats","skiing equipment"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Şort, güneş kremi ve şapka."},{"q":"Why did the writer and his brother get bored?","o":["They had no more books to read.","They did not like the cabin.","Their parents were angry.","There was no radio."],"a":0,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Kitaplar bitmişti."},{"q":"Who won the family tournament?","o":["the writer's mother","the writer's father","the writer","his brother"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Kimsenin beklemediği anne kazandı."},{"q":"How does the writer feel about the holiday?","o":["It was completely spoiled.","He regrets going to the mountains.","He wants to go in winter next time.","It was one of their best holidays."],"a":3,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"one of the best we have ever had\"."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_reunion7","t":"The Family Reunion","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Family Reunion","p":["Every summer, my father's family gathers at my grandparents' house in the countryside. It is not a small event: more than forty relatives come, from babies to my great-aunt, who is ninety-one years old.","The morning is always busy. The women cook huge pots of food in the kitchen, while the men put tables and chairs under the apple trees. Children run around, and my little cousins usually fall into the stream at least once. My job is to take photographs of everybody, which is not easy when nobody stays still.","After lunch, my grandfather brings out an old photo album. We look at pictures of my parents when they were children, and everyone laughs at their funny clothes. My great-aunt tells stories about the village sixty years ago, when there was no electricity, and the young ones listen carefully.","In the evening, my grandfather makes a short speech and lifts his glass. \"To the family,\" he says, \"and to the next generation.\" I look around the table at all the faces and feel lucky. I know that, wherever I live in the future, this is the place I will always come back to."],"q":[{"q":"How many people usually come to the reunion?","o":["about ten","about twenty","nearly a hundred","more than forty"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Kırktan fazla akraba."},{"q":"What is the writer's job during the morning?","o":["cooking","arranging the tables","taking photographs","looking after the children"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Herkesin fotoğrafını çekmek."},{"q":"What does the great-aunt talk about?","o":["her own children","her favourite recipes","life in the village long ago","the weather"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Altmış yıl önceki köy hayatı."},{"q":"How does the writer feel at the end?","o":["tired and bored","grateful and attached to the place","sad about leaving","worried about the future"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Şanslı hissediyor ve her zaman geri dönecek."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_school7","t":"The New Student","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The New Student","p":["On Monday morning, Zeynep walked through the gates of her new school feeling very nervous. Her family had moved to a different city, and she did not know anybody. Her heart was beating so fast that she could hardly breathe.","In the office, the secretary gave her a timetable, a map of the building and the key to a locker. \"Your form teacher, Mr Kaya, will meet you in room 12,\" she said with a smile. Zeynep got lost twice before she finally found the room.","Mr Kaya introduced her to the class and asked a girl called Lale to look after her. At break, Lale showed her the canteen and told her which teachers were strict. Zeynep was shy at first, but when Lale said that she also loved painting, they started talking about their favourite artists.","By Friday, Zeynep had stopped feeling like a stranger. She had joined the art club, learnt where everything was and been invited to Lale's birthday party. \"I was so worried on Monday,\" she told her mother at dinner. \"Now I can't believe how quickly it happened.\""],"q":[{"q":"How did Zeynep feel on Monday morning?","o":["excited and happy","tired","angry","very nervous"],"a":3,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Çok gergindi."},{"q":"What did the secretary give Zeynep?","o":["a uniform and some books","a list of teachers","a timetable, a map and a locker key","an invitation"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Ders programı, harita ve dolap anahtarı."},{"q":"What did Zeynep and Lale have in common?","o":["They lived in the same street.","They had the same teacher.","They both loved painting.","They were in the same club."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"İkisi de resim yapmayı seviyor."},{"q":"How did Zeynep feel by Friday?","o":["still lost","more comfortable at school","worried about the party","unwilling to return"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Artık yabancı hissetmiyordu."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_wallet7","t":"The Lost Wallet","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Lost Wallet","p":["One cold evening in November, Kerem was walking home from work when he noticed something dark on the pavement. It was a brown leather wallet. He looked around, but the street was empty.","Inside, there was a lot of money, some cards and a student ID with a name: Aylin Demir. There was no phone number, but Kerem found a picture of a young woman with a little dog. He thought for a moment. The money would have been very useful, because he was saving for a new bike, but he knew it was not his.","The next morning, he took the wallet to the nearest police station. The officer wrote down his details and thanked him. \"Honest people are not as common as you might think,\" she said. Kerem shrugged and went to work.","Two days later, he received a call. It was Aylin, who had been crying for hours before the police contacted her. She wanted to give him a reward, but Kerem refused. \"Just be more careful next time,\" he said with a smile. A week later, a small package arrived at his door: a box of chocolates and a card that said, \"Thank you for being kind.\""],"q":[{"q":"What did Kerem find inside the wallet?","o":["money, cards and a student ID","only a phone number","a bus ticket","a key"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Para, kartlar ve öğrenci kimliği."},{"q":"Why did Kerem not keep the money?","o":["He was afraid of the police.","He did not need money.","The wallet was empty.","He knew it belonged to someone else."],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Onun olmadığını biliyordu."},{"q":"What did Kerem say when Aylin offered a reward?","o":["He accepted it happily.","He asked for more.","He did not answer.","He refused it."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Reddetti."},{"q":"How did Aylin thank him in the end?","o":["She invited him to dinner.","She gave him some money.","She sent chocolates and a card.","She called the police again."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Çikolata ve teşekkür kartı gönderdi."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_bike7","t":"Learning to Ride a Bike","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Learning to Ride a Bike","p":["My grandfather decided to learn to ride a bicycle at the age of sixty-eight. When he told us, we thought he was joking. He had lived all his life in a village in the mountains, where people travelled by donkey or on foot, and he had never even sat on a bike.","My brother and I offered to teach him in the empty car park near our house. Grandfather put on a helmet that was far too small, and we held the back of the seat. At first, he wobbled so much that he fell off after three metres. \"It's harder than it looks,\" he said, laughing.","We practised every evening for two weeks. Slowly, he learnt that the secret is not to look at the ground, but straight ahead, and to keep pedalling. On the twelfth day, I let go of the seat without telling him. He rode twenty metres alone before he noticed!","Today, Grandfather cycles to the market every Saturday. Our neighbours wave when they see him, and he waves back with a big smile. \"You are never too old to learn,\" he tells everybody. We are very proud of him."],"q":[{"q":"Why did the family think he was joking?","o":["He was too ill.","He already had a bike.","He had never ridden a bike before.","He lived in a city."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Hayatında hiç bisiklete binmemişti."},{"q":"What was the problem with the helmet?","o":["It was too heavy.","It was too small.","It was broken.","It was not his."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"far too small\""},{"q":"What happened on the twelfth day?","o":["He fell off again.","He rode alone without knowing it.","He bought a bike.","He cycled to the market."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Selenin tutulmadığını fark etmeden 20 metre sürdü."},{"q":"What is the grandfather's message to people?","o":["People can learn at any age.","Cycling is only for the young.","Donkeys are better than bikes.","Markets are best visited on foot."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"You are never too old to learn.\""}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_bank7","t":"Opening a Bank Account","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Opening a Bank Account","p":["When Cem started his first job, his manager told him he needed a bank account so the company could pay his salary. He had never been inside a bank before, so he asked his sister Gül for advice.","On Saturday morning, they went to a branch near the station. Cem took his passport and a proof of address, as Gül had told him. A friendly assistant gave him a form to fill in and explained the different types of account. \"A student account has no fees,\" she said, \"but you can only use it until you are twenty-five.\"","Cem chose a normal current account with a card. The assistant explained that he could use the cash machine at any time, but he should never tell anyone his PIN. She also showed him how to check his balance on the phone.","Within a week, his card arrived in the post, and at the end of the month, his first salary was in the account. Cem felt very grown-up. \"Now I just have to learn not to spend it all in the first week,\" he joked."],"q":[{"q":"Why did Cem need a bank account?","o":["To save for a holiday","To get a student card","Because his sister asked him to","So his company could pay his salary"],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Maaşın yatırılması için."},{"q":"What did Cem take to the bank?","o":["his old bank card","his employer","his passport and proof of address","a letter from his manager"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Pasaport ve adres belgesi."},{"q":"What did the assistant tell him about his PIN?","o":["Write it on the card.","Change it every day.","Never tell it to anybody.","Share it with his sister."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"PIN'ini kimseye söylememeli."},{"q":"What does Cem mean by his final joke?","o":["He will save all his salary.","He may spend his money too quickly.","He wants a second account.","He has lost his card."],"a":1,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"İlk haftada hepsini harcama riski."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_cook7","t":"My First Dinner Party","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"My First Dinner Party","p":["Last Saturday, I invited six friends to dinner. It was the first time I had cooked for so many people, and I wanted everything to be perfect. I chose a simple menu: tomato soup, chicken with rice and an apple cake.","I started cooking at two o'clock. The soup was easy, and the cake smelt wonderful. Then I made my first mistake: I forgot to set the timer for the chicken, and when I opened the oven, one side was nearly black. I felt like crying.","My friend Burcu, who arrived early, saved the day. She cut off the burnt parts and covered the chicken with a delicious sauce made of garlic and lemon. By the time the guests came, nobody could tell that anything had gone wrong.","We ate, laughed and talked until midnight. Everyone said the cake was the best part of the meal. I learnt two things that evening: always use a timer, and never be too proud to ask for help."],"q":[{"q":"What was the writer's mistake?","o":["She invited too many guests.","She chose a difficult menu.","She forgot the cake.","She forgot to set a timer for the chicken."],"a":3,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Tavuk için zamanlayıcıyı kurmadı."},{"q":"How did Burcu help?","o":["She cooked a new chicken.","She served the guests.","She hid the burnt parts with a sauce.","She washed the dishes."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yanık yerleri kesip sosla kapladı."},{"q":"Which part of the meal was the most popular?","o":["the soup","the chicken","the cake","the rice"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Herkes keki en güzel kısım buldu."},{"q":"What did the writer learn?","o":["never to cook for guests","to use a timer and to accept help","to buy ready meals","to start cooking earlier"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Zamanlayıcı kullan, yardım iste."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_volun7","t":"A Day at the Animal Shelter","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"A Day at the Animal Shelter","p":["Every other Saturday, I work as a volunteer at an animal shelter on the edge of our town. The shelter looks after about sixty dogs and cats, most of which were found as strays on the street.","My day begins at nine. First, I help to feed the animals and clean their cages. Then I take the dogs for a walk in the field behind the shelter. They are always very excited, and some of them pull so hard that I almost fall over!","In the afternoon, I sit with the cats. Many of them are shy because they have been treated badly. I talk to them quietly and wait until they come to me. It can take weeks, but it is wonderful when a cat finally climbs onto your lap.","The best day is when an animal is adopted. Last week, a family took home a little brown dog called Pepper. Everyone at the shelter clapped as they left. I was happy for him, but I admit I had tears in my eyes."],"q":[{"q":"Where do most of the animals come from?","o":["They were born at the shelter.","They were bought from shops.","They came from farms.","They were found on the street."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Çoğu sokakta sahipsiz bulunmuş."},{"q":"What does the writer do in the morning?","o":["trains the cats","answers the phone","feeds animals and cleans cages","takes photographs"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Hayvanları besler, kafesleri temizler."},{"q":"Why are many of the cats shy?","o":["They are very old.","They are ill.","They have been treated badly.","They do not like people at all."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Kötü muamele görmüşler."},{"q":"How did the writer feel when Pepper left?","o":["angry","happy but emotional","bored","worried"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Mutlu ama gözleri doldu."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_mobile7","t":"A New Phone","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"A New Phone","p":["My old phone finally died last week. The screen had been cracked for months, and the battery lasted only two hours. I knew I needed a new one, but I had no idea which to choose, because there are so many different models.","First, I asked my friends. Everyone recommended something different, and each of them said that their own phone was the best. Then I read some reviews online and made a list of the features I really needed: a good camera, a long-lasting battery and a reasonable price.","I went to three shops to compare prices. In the first, a salesman tried to persuade me to buy the most expensive model, which I did not need. In the second, the staff were more helpful, and they explained the difference between a contract and paying all at once. In the third, I found exactly what I wanted at a discount.","I paid, took my new phone home and spent the evening copying my photos. It works perfectly, and I am glad I did not rush. My advice: decide what you need before you go shopping, and do not let anybody sell you more than that."],"q":[{"q":"Why did the writer need a new phone?","o":["She lost it on a bus.","Her friends all had new ones.","It was stolen.","The old one was broken and the battery was poor."],"a":3,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Ekran çatlak, pil iki saat."},{"q":"What did she do before going shopping?","o":["She asked her parents for money.","She sold her old phone.","She made a list of features she needed.","She bought a protective case."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"İhtiyaç duyduğu özelliklerin listesini yaptı."},{"q":"What was wrong with the first shop?","o":["The prices were not shown.","The shop was closed.","The salesman pushed an expensive model.","The staff did not speak to her."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Satıcı pahalı modeli satmaya çalıştı."},{"q":"What is the writer's advice?","o":["Always buy the cheapest phone.","Decide what you need before shopping.","Ask friends to choose for you.","Never buy from a shop."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Ne istediğine önceden karar ver."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_landmark7","t":"A Famous Tower","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"A Famous Tower","p":["Every year, millions of tourists travel to Paris to see the Eiffel Tower. It was built between 1887 and 1889 for a world exhibition, and it was supposed to stand for only twenty years. Many Parisians thought it was ugly, and some famous writers even signed a letter against it.","Today, the tower is one of the most visited monuments in the world. It is made of iron, and it is repainted every seven years to protect it from rust. On a clear day, visitors who climb to the top can see for more than sixty kilometres.","If you plan to go, there are a few tips. The queues are shortest early in the morning or late in the evening. You can buy tickets online to save time, and you can walk up the stairs to the second floor if you do not mind climbing about 700 steps. At night, the tower sparkles for five minutes every hour, which is an unforgettable sight.","The tower has survived storms, wars and many arguments about its future. Today, it is loved by locals as well as visitors, and it has become a symbol not only of Paris but of France itself."],"q":[{"q":"Why was the tower built?","o":["To protect Paris from war","For a world exhibition","As a hotel","As a radio station"],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Dünya fuarı için."},{"q":"What did many Parisians think at first?","o":["It was ugly.","It was too small.","It was too old.","It was perfect."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Çirkin buldular."},{"q":"When are the queues shortest?","o":["Early in the morning or late in the evening","At lunchtime","On Sundays","In summer"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Sabah erken ya da akşam geç."},{"q":"What happens at night?","o":["The tower is closed.","The tower changes colour every day.","Fireworks start at midnight.","The tower sparkles for five minutes every hour."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Her saat başı 5 dakika ışıldar."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_repair7","t":"The Broken Washing Machine","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Broken Washing Machine","p":["On Tuesday morning, Hande found a pool of water on her kitchen floor. The washing machine was leaking, and it was only two years old. She turned it off, put towels around it and looked for the warranty papers.","She phoned the repair service of the shop where she had bought it. A polite woman asked for the model number and described the problem. \"A technician can come on Thursday between two and five,\" she said. Hande asked whether somebody could come earlier, but all the other appointments were taken.","On Thursday, the technician arrived at half past three. He checked the machine, found a damaged pipe and replaced it in twenty minutes. Because the machine was still under warranty, Hande did not have to pay for the part or for the visit.","Before he left, the technician gave her some advice: \"Never put too much washing in the machine, and clean the filter once a month.\" Hande thanked him and kept the invoice in a drawer, just in case. That evening, she did three loads of washing and felt very relieved."],"q":[{"q":"What was wrong with the washing machine?","o":["It would not start.","It made a loud noise.","It was leaking water.","It was too old."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Su sızdırıyordu."},{"q":"Why could the technician not come earlier?","o":["He was ill.","All the other appointments were taken.","He had no parts.","The shop was closed."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Diğer randevular doluydu."},{"q":"Why did Hande not pay for the repair?","o":["The technician was her friend.","The machine was under warranty.","It was a free offer.","She paid by card."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Makine garantideydi."},{"q":"What advice did the technician give?","o":["Clean the filter once a month.","Buy a new machine.","Use less soap.","Wash at night."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Filtreyi ayda bir temizle."}]},{"k":"R3","id":"okr_apology7","t":"Saying Sorry","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Saying Sorry","p":["Last month, I forgot my best friend Merve's birthday. I only remembered it two days later, when I saw a photo of her party online. I felt terrible, because we had been friends for ten years and I had never missed it before.","First, I thought about making an excuse: I had too much work, I lost my phone, anything. But I knew she would not believe me, and it would not be honest. So I decided to tell the truth. I called her and said, \"I'm so sorry. I forgot, and I have no good reason. I feel awful.\"","There was a short silence. Then Merve said that she had been upset, but she appreciated my honesty. I offered to take her out for dinner, and she accepted. At the restaurant, I gave her a small present, a book she had wanted for a long time.","That experience taught me something. A good apology has three parts: you say that you are sorry, you accept that it was your fault, and you try to make things right. An excuse can make the other person feel worse, but a sincere apology usually helps to repair the friendship."],"q":[{"q":"What did the writer forget?","o":["A dinner invitation","A promise to call","Her friend's birthday","Her phone"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Arkadaşının doğum gününü."},{"q":"Why did she not make an excuse?","o":["She had no time.","It would not be honest.","She did not know any.","Merve was angry."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Dürüst olmazdı."},{"q":"How did Merve react?","o":["She refused to talk.","She appreciated her honesty.","She cried.","She ended the friendship."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Dürüstlüğü takdir etti."},{"q":"What are the parts of a good apology?","o":["Say sorry, accept fault and make things right","Give a present and leave","Explain and blame others","Wait and say nothing"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Üzgünüm de, hatayı kabul et, düzeltmeye çalış."}]}],"L":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Yedi kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1a","sc":[["F","Hello, I'd like to book a table for Friday evening."],["M","Certainly. For how many people?"],["F","There'll be six of us. Oh, actually, my brother can't come, so five."]],"q":"How many people will be at the table?","o":["six","four","five"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","spelling"],"e":"Önce altı dedi, sonra kardeşi gelemeyeceği için beş."},{"id":"l1b","sc":[["M","What are you doing this weekend?"],["F","I was going to go camping, but the forecast says it's going to rain. So I'll probably visit my cousins in London instead."]],"q":"What will the woman probably do this weekend?","o":["see her cousins in London","go camping near London","stay at home all weekend"],"a":0,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Kampa gidecekti ama yağmur yüzünden kuzenlerini ziyaret edecek."},{"id":"l1c","sc":[["F","Excuse me, where can I find the shampoo?"],["M","It's not with the soap any more. We've moved it next to the toothpaste, at the end of aisle four."]],"q":"Where is the shampoo now?","o":["next to the soap in aisle four","next to the toothpaste in aisle three","next to the toothpaste in aisle four"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","present-perfect"],"e":"Artık sabunların yanında değil, diş macunlarının yanında."},{"id":"l1d","sc":[["M","Hi Jo, it's Dan. The football match on Saturday starts at three, but the coach wants us there forty-five minutes early to warm up. See you then!"]],"q":"What time should Jo arrive?","o":["3.00","2.15","2.45"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","must-have-to-should"],"e":"Maç 3'te, 45 dakika önce orada olmalılar: 2.15."},{"id":"l1e","sc":[["F","How did you get here so quickly?"],["M","Normally I take the bus, but today my neighbour gave me a lift in his car."],["F","Lucky you!"]],"q":"How did the man travel today?","o":["by car","by bus","on foot"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Bugün komşusu arabasıyla bıraktı."},{"id":"l1f","sc":[["M","Did you like the jacket?"],["F","The colour was perfect, but it was too small, and they didn't have a bigger size. So I bought a jumper instead."]],"q":"Why didn't the woman buy the jacket?","o":["There was no bigger size in the shop.","The colour was not what she wanted.","It was more expensive than she expected."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Rengi güzeldi ama küçüktü, büyük bedeni yoktu."},{"id":"l1g","sc":[["F","This is a message for passengers on the twelve fifteen train to Manchester. This train will now leave from platform seven, not platform three. We apologise for any inconvenience."]],"q":"Which platform will the train leave from?","o":["3","12","7"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","spelling"],"e":"\"from platform seven, not platform three\""}],"id":"b1l1","t":"Short Recordings"},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Altı kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l2a","sc":[["M","How was the concert?"],["F","The band were great, but we were so far from the stage that we couldn't see anything."],["M","What a shame."]],"q":"What was the woman's problem at the concert?","o":["The band didn't play very well.","It was too crowded to hear anything.","They sat too far from the stage."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Sahneden çok uzaktaydılar, hiçbir şey göremediler."},{"id":"l2b","sc":[["F","I hear you're learning Japanese."],["M","Yes. Speaking isn't too bad, but I find the writing system really hard to remember."]],"q":"What does the man find difficult?","o":["writing the characters","speaking with people","understanding speech"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yazı sistemini hatırlamak zor."},{"id":"l2c","sc":[["M","Did you enjoy the hotel?"],["F","The room was lovely and the staff were friendly. I just wish it had been closer to the beach. We had to take a bus every day."]],"q":"What would the woman change about the hotel?","o":["the size of the room","the distance to the beach","the friendliness of the staff"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","wish-if-only"],"e":"\"I wish it had been closer to the beach\" – konum."},{"id":"l2d","sc":[["F","Are you going to the party tonight?"],["M","I'd like to, but I have an exam tomorrow morning. If I go out, I won't be able to concentrate."]],"q":"Why won't the man go to the party?","o":["He has to work early tomorrow.","He doesn't know anyone there.","He has a test in the morning."],"a":2,"tg":["inference","first-conditional"],"e":"Yarın sınavı var; dışarı çıkarsa odaklanamaz."},{"id":"l2e","sc":[["M","What do you think of the new sports centre?"],["F","It's modern and there's lots of equipment, but it costs twice as much as the old one. I don't think I'll renew my membership."]],"q":"What does the woman think about the sports centre?","o":["It has less equipment than the old one.","It costs much more than the old one.","It is too far from her house."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","comparatives-superlatives"],"e":"\"costs twice as much as the old one\" – çok pahalı."},{"id":"l2f","sc":[["F","You didn't answer my calls yesterday."],["M","Sorry. I left my phone at work and didn't realise until I got home. It must have been in my desk drawer."]],"q":"Why didn't the man answer his phone?","o":["He was talking on another call.","The battery had run out by then.","He had left it at his workplace."],"a":2,"tg":["detail","modals-deduction"],"e":"Telefonunu işte unutmuştu."}],"id":"b1l2","t":"Opinions"},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bir yaz kampı duyurusunu dinle. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","ty":"open","id":"b1l3","sc":[["M","Hello, and thanks for your interest in Green Valley Summer Camp. This year the camp runs from the fourteenth of July for two weeks. It is open to young people aged eleven to sixteen. Every morning there are outdoor activities, such as canoeing and climbing. In the afternoon, campers can choose between art, music and drama. The price is three hundred and fifty pounds, which includes all meals and equipment. Campers sleep in wooden cabins, with six people in each one. Please remember to bring a sleeping bag and a torch. You do not need to bring any sports equipment. To book a place, visit our website before the end of May. We look forward to seeing you!"]],"title":"Notlar","p":"Green Valley Summer Camp\nStart date: (1) ______ July\nAge: 11 to (2) ______\nAfternoon choices: art, music and (3) ______\nPrice: £(4) ______\nCampers sleep in wooden (5) ______\nRemember to bring a sleeping bag and a (6) ______","a":[["14","14th","fourteenth","fourteen"],["16","sixteen"],["drama"],["350","three hundred and fifty"],["cabins","cabin"],["torch"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"]],"e":["\"from the fourteenth of July\"","\"aged eleven to sixteen\"","\"art, music and drama\"","\"three hundred and fifty pounds\"","\"sleep in wooden cabins\"","\"bring a sleeping bag and a torch\""],"t":"Summer Camp"},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Bir gönüllüyle yapılan röportajı dinle ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","id":"b1l4","sc":[["F","My guest today is Tom Hughes, who spent a year volunteering on a farm in New Zealand. Tom, why did you decide to go?"],["M","I'd just finished university and I didn't know what job I wanted. A friend had done something similar and said it had changed her life, so I thought, why not?"],["F","What was a typical day like?"],["M","We started at six, which was hard at first because I'm not a morning person! We fed the animals, fixed fences and helped in the fields. The work was physical, but I got used to it after a few weeks."],["F","Was there anything you didn't enjoy?"],["M","Honestly, the work was fine. What I found difficult was being so far from my family. The farm was in the middle of nowhere, and the internet was terrible, so I could only call home once a week."],["F","What did you learn from the experience?"],["M","I learned that I really enjoy working outdoors. Before I went, I thought I wanted an office job, but now I'm training to be a park ranger."],["F","Would you recommend it to others?"],["M","Definitely, but I'd tell people to be prepared. It isn't a holiday. If you want to relax on the beach, it's not for you."]],"q":[{"q":"Why did Tom go to New Zealand?","o":["He needed to finish a university course.","A friend said it had changed her life.","He wanted to train as a farmer."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","reported-speech"],"e":"Arkadaşı benzer bir şey yapmış ve hayatını değiştirdiğini söylemişti."},{"q":"What was difficult for Tom at the beginning?","o":["living with strangers","working in the rain","starting work at six"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","used-to"],"e":"Sabah altıda başlamak ilk başta zordu; \"not a morning person\"."},{"q":"What did Tom find hardest during the year?","o":["being so far from home","the long working hours","the cold winter weather"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Ailesinden bu kadar uzak olmak."},{"q":"Why could Tom only call home once a week?","o":["Calls to New Zealand were too expensive.","He was often too tired in the evenings.","The farm's internet connection was poor."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the internet was terrible\""},{"q":"How has the experience changed Tom's plans?","o":["He wants to study farming at university.","He no longer wants to work indoors.","He plans to move to New Zealand for good."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Ofis işi isterken artık park bekçisi olmak için eğitim alıyor."},{"q":"What advice does Tom give?","o":["Be ready for hard work, not a holiday.","Bring a friend so you won't feel lonely.","Choose a place near the beach."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"be prepared. It isn't a holiday.\""}],"t":"Volunteering Abroad"},{"k":"L3","id":"b1l5","t":"A Cookery Course","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 3","ty":"open","title":"Notlar","ins":"Bir yemek kursu tanıtımını dinle. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","sc":[["M","Hello, and thanks for calling the Green Kitchen Cookery School. Here is some information about our summer course for young people. The course is for students aged fourteen to eighteen, and it runs for two weeks, starting on the third of July. Classes take place every weekday morning from ten until one. In the first week, students learn how to make different kinds of bread, and in the second week we focus on Italian dishes, especially pasta. The course costs one hundred and twenty pounds, and that includes all the food. Students only need to bring an apron. At the end of the course, there is a special lunch, and every student can invite two members of their family. To book a place, please visit our website or send us an email. We look forward to cooking with you!"]],"p":"Green Kitchen summer course\nAge: 14 to 18\nStarts on: (1) ______ July\nClasses every weekday: 10 a.m. to (2) ______ p.m.\nWeek 1: different kinds of (3) ______\nWeek 2: Italian food, especially (4) ______\nPrice: £(5) ______, including all the food\nStudents must bring: an (6) ______","a":[["3","3rd","third"],["1","one","1.00"],["bread"],["pasta"],["120","one hundred and twenty"],["apron"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"]],"e":["\"starting on the third of July\"","\"from ten until one\"","\"how to make different kinds of bread\"","\"Italian dishes, especially pasta\"","\"one hundred and twenty pounds\"","\"Students only need to bring an apron\""]},{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Yedi kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1ax","sc":[["F","Excuse me, how much is a return ticket to Brighton?"],["M","A single is twelve pounds, and a return is twenty. But if you have a student card, a return is only fifteen."],["F","Oh good, here it is."]],"q":"How much will the woman pay?","o":["£12","£15","£20"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","spelling"],"e":"Öğrenci kartı var: gidiş-dönüş 15 pound."},{"id":"l1bx","sc":[["M","Are we still playing tennis on Saturday?"],["F","The courts are closed for repairs this weekend. Shall we go swimming instead? Or there's badminton at the sports centre."],["M","I love badminton. Let's do that."]],"q":"What will they do on Saturday?","o":["play tennis","go swimming","play badminton"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Tenis kortları kapalı; badmintonda anlaştılar."},{"id":"l1cx","sc":[["F","Hello, this is a message for Mr Evans from the dentist's. Your appointment is on Thursday the fifth, not Tuesday the third as we said before. It's still at half past ten."]],"q":"When is Mr Evans's appointment?","o":["Thursday at 10.30","Tuesday at 10.30","Thursday at 3.00"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Salı değil perşembe, saat yine 10.30."},{"id":"l1dx","sc":[["M","What was the weather like on your holiday?"],["F","Well, we expected sunshine, but it was cloudy most of the time. It only rained on the last day, luckily."]],"q":"What was the weather like for most of the holiday?","o":["sunny","rainy","cloudy"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"it was cloudy most of the time\""},{"id":"l1ex","sc":[["F","Where shall I put these boxes?"],["M","Not in the kitchen, it's too small. Put them in the garage for now, and we'll move them up to the bedroom later."]],"q":"Where will the woman put the boxes now?","o":["in the garage","in the kitchen","in the bedroom"],"a":0,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"\"Put them in the garage for now\""},{"id":"l1fx","sc":[["M","I need a present for my sister. She loves reading, but she has so many books already."],["F","What about a lamp for reading in bed? Or a bookshop gift card?"],["M","The lamp is a great idea. She's always complaining her room is too dark."]],"q":"What will the man buy for his sister?","o":["a book","a lamp","a gift card"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Lambayı seçti çünkü kız kardeşinin odası karanlık."},{"id":"l1gx","sc":[["F","How are you getting to the airport tomorrow?"],["M","I was going to take the train, but there's a strike. My dad offered to drive me, but he's working, so I've booked a taxi."]],"q":"How will the man get to the airport?","o":["by train","in his father's car","by taxi"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Tren grevi var, babası çalışıyor: taksi."}],"id":"b1l1b","t":"Short Recordings (2)"},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Altı kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l2ax","sc":[["M","How's your new school?"],["F","The teachers are great and I've made some friends. The only thing is the journey – it takes almost an hour each way."]],"q":"What does the girl not like about her new school?","o":["the teachers","the other students","how long it takes to get there"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Yol gidiş-dönüş neredeyse birer saat."},{"id":"l2bx","sc":[["F","Did you like the film?"],["M","The story was quite predictable, to be honest. But the music was fantastic. I'm going to download the soundtrack."]],"q":"What did the man enjoy about the film?","o":["the story","the music","the ending"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Hikâye tahmin edilebilirdi ama müzik harikaydı."},{"id":"l2cx","sc":[["M","You look tired."],["F","I am. I've started going jogging before work. I feel healthier, but getting up at six isn't easy. I'm sure I'll get used to it."]],"q":"How does the woman feel about her new routine?","o":["She thinks it will get easier.","She wants to stop doing it.","She doesn't feel any healthier."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude","used-to"],"e":"\"I'm sure I'll get used to it\" – alışacağını düşünüyor."},{"id":"l2dx","sc":[["F","Are you enjoying your job at the café?"],["M","The customers are nice and the pay is OK. But I work every weekend, so I never see my friends."]],"q":"What is the man's problem with his job?","o":["He has no free time with friends.","The pay is low.","The customers are rude."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Her hafta sonu çalışıyor, arkadaşlarını göremiyor."},{"id":"l2ex","sc":[["M","I'm thinking of buying a second-hand car."],["F","Good idea, they're much cheaper. Just make sure a mechanic checks it before you pay. My brother bought one that broke down after a week."]],"q":"What does the woman advise the man to do?","o":["buy a new car instead","have the car checked first","ask her brother for help"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose","causative"],"e":"Ödemeden önce arabayı bir tamirciye kontrol ettirmesini öneriyor."},{"id":"l2fx","sc":[["F","How was the hotel?"],["M","The location was perfect, right by the beach. The room was a bit small, but we were hardly ever in it, so it didn't matter."]],"q":"What does the man say about the room?","o":["It was too small to enjoy.","Its size wasn't important to him.","It had a view of the beach."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","inference"],"e":"Oda küçüktü ama neredeyse hiç odada değillerdi, önemli değildi."}],"id":"b1l2b","t":"School, Work and Holidays"},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bir yerel müze rehberinin duyurusunu dinle. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","ty":"open","id":"b1l3b","sc":[["F","Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the Harbour Museum. Before you start, here is some useful information. The museum is open every day except Monday, from nine thirty until five. The tour of the old ship starts every hour from the main entrance and lasts forty minutes. On the first floor, you can see our new exhibition about fishing, which opened on the twelfth of March. Children can take part in a treasure hunt; just ask for a map at the information desk. Our café on the top floor serves hot meals until three o'clock, and from there you have a wonderful view of the harbour. Finally, please remember that you cannot use a camera with flash anywhere in the museum. Enjoy your visit!"]],"title":"Notlar","p":"Harbour Museum\nClosed on: (1) ______\nShip tour lasts: (2) ______ minutes\nNew exhibition about: (3) ______\nExhibition opened on: (4) ______ March\nChildren can get a map at the: (5) ______ desk\nHot meals in the café until: (6) ______","a":[["Monday","Mondays"],["40","forty"],["fishing"],["12","12th","twelfth"],["information"],["3","three","3 pm","3 p.m.","3.00"]],"tg":[["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"]],"e":["\"open every day except Monday\"","\"lasts forty minutes\"","\"exhibition about fishing\"","\"opened on the twelfth of March\"","\"ask for a map at the information desk\"","\"serves hot meals until three o'clock\""],"t":"The Harbour Museum"},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Bir genç şefle yapılan röportajı dinle ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","id":"b1l4b","sc":[["M","Today I'm talking to Selin Aydın, who opened her own restaurant at the age of twenty-four. Selin, when did you first become interested in cooking?"],["F","When I was about ten. My grandmother used to let me help her in the kitchen every Sunday. At first I only washed vegetables, but she slowly taught me her recipes."],["M","Did you study cooking at college?"],["F","No. Everyone expected me to, but I studied business instead. I thought it would help me if I ever had my own restaurant, and it did."],["M","What was the hardest part of opening the restaurant?"],["F","People think it's the long hours, and they are long. But for me the real problem was finding good staff. It took me six months to build a team I could trust."],["M","How would you describe your food?"],["F","Simple dishes made with fresh, local ingredients. I change the menu every season, so regular customers always find something new."],["M","What advice would you give young people who want to become chefs?"],["F","Get a part-time job in a real kitchen as soon as you can. You'll find out quickly if you love it – or if you hate it!"]],"q":[{"q":"How did Selin learn to cook?","o":["from her grandmother","at a cooking college","from television programmes"],"a":0,"tg":["detail","used-to"],"e":"Büyükannesi pazar günleri mutfakta yardım etmesine izin verirdi."},{"q":"Why did Selin study business?","o":["Her family wanted her to.","She thought it would be useful for running a restaurant.","She wasn't sure she wanted to cook."],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Kendi restoranı olursa işine yarayacağını düşündü."},{"q":"What was most difficult when she opened the restaurant?","o":["working long hours","paying for the building","finding staff she could trust"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the real problem was finding good staff\""},{"q":"How long did it take to build her team?","o":["six months","six weeks","a year"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"It took me six months\""},{"q":"Why does Selin change the menu every season?","o":["so regular customers can try new dishes","because ingredients become expensive","because her staff get bored"],"a":0,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Düzenli müşteriler her zaman yeni bir şey bulsun diye."},{"q":"What does Selin advise young people to do?","o":["study business first","work in a kitchen to see if they like it","open a small restaurant"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","gerund-infinitive"],"e":"Gerçek bir mutfakta yarı zamanlı çalışarak sevip sevmediklerini görmeleri."}],"t":"A Young Chef"},{"k":"L1","id":"b1l1c","t":"Short Recordings (3)","ty":"mc","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Yedi kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","q":[{"id":"c-a","sc":[["F","Excuse me, what time does the last train to Bath leave?"],["M","There's one at ten forty, but it only goes as far as Bristol. If you want Bath, you need the ten twenty."],["F","Then I'd better hurry!"]],"q":"Which train should the woman take?","o":["the 10.20","the 10.40","the 11.20"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Bath'a giden tren 10.20; 10.40 yalnızca Bristol'a gidiyor."},{"id":"c-b","sc":[["M","Have you decided what to buy Dad for his birthday?"],["F","I wanted to get him a book, but he already has hundreds. So I'm going to buy him a ticket for a football match instead."]],"q":"What is the girl going to buy for her father?","o":["a book","a football ticket","a shirt"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Kitap yerine futbol maçı bileti alacak."},{"id":"c-c","sc":[["F","Hello, this is a message for Daniel. It's Mrs Taylor from the library. The book you ordered has arrived. We'll keep it for you until Friday, so please collect it before then."]],"q":"What does the librarian want Daniel to do?","o":["pay for the book today","collect the book by Friday","order another book"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","purpose"],"e":"Kitap cumaya kadar saklanacak; ondan önce alınmalı."},{"id":"c-d","sc":[["M","How was the concert last night?"],["F","The band was fine, but I couldn't see anything because of the tall people in front of me. Next time I'll arrive earlier to get a better place."]],"q":"What was the girl's problem at the concert?","o":["She could not see the stage.","The music was too loud.","She arrived very late."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Önündeki uzun insanlar yüzünden hiçbir şey göremedi."},{"id":"c-e","sc":[["F","Do you want to come cycling on Sunday?"],["M","I'd love to, but my bike has a flat tyre, and the shop is closed until Monday. Could I borrow yours?"],["F","Sure. But you must bring it back before dinner."]],"q":"What does the man want to do?","o":["buy a new bike","borrow a bike","repair a tyre himself"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","can-cant"],"e":"Bisikletinin lastiği patlak; ödünç istiyor."},{"id":"c-f","sc":[["M","Attention, please. Passengers for flight BA 214 to Dublin, the flight is now boarding at gate 12. Please have your passports ready."]],"q":"Where should passengers for Dublin go?","o":["to gate 12","to the passport office","to the information desk"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Uçuş 12 numaralı kapıdan biniş yapıyor."},{"id":"c-g","sc":[["F","I'm thinking of joining the school choir. Do you sing?"],["M","Not really. I'm better at playing the piano. But I might come and listen to your first concert."]],"q":"What does the boy say about himself?","o":["He sings well.","He plays an instrument.","He wants to join the choir."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Şarkı söylemekte iyi değil, piyano çalmakta daha iyi."}]},{"k":"L2","id":"b1l2c","t":"Everyday Conversations","ty":"mc","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Altı kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","q":[{"id":"d-a","sc":[["F","Did you enjoy the football match yesterday?"],["M","The first half was boring, but after the break it got really exciting. We scored twice in five minutes!"]],"q":"What did the boy think of the match?","o":["It was boring from beginning to end.","It improved in the second half.","He did not watch it to the end."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"İkinci yarı çok heyecanlı oldu."},{"id":"d-b","sc":[["M","Why did you change your job?"],["F","The money was good, but I was sitting in front of a computer all day. Now I work outdoors with children, and I'm much happier."]],"q":"Why did the woman change her job?","o":["She wanted a higher salary.","She did not like working indoors all day.","She had problems with her colleagues."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","detail"],"e":"Bütün gün bilgisayar başında oturmaktan sıkılmıştı."},{"id":"d-c","sc":[["F","Is the new restaurant any good?"],["M","The food is excellent, though a bit expensive. The service was slow, but the waiters were so friendly that I didn't mind."]],"q":"What does the man say about the service?","o":["It was too fast.","It was slow but friendly.","It was unfriendly."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Hizmet yavaştı ama garsonlar çok sıcakkanlıydı."},{"id":"d-d","sc":[["M","Are you ready for the exam on Monday?"],["F","Not really. I've learnt the dates, but I always get confused by the grammar. I think I'll ask Mr Hill to explain it again."]],"q":"What is the girl going to do?","o":["study more history dates","ask her teacher for help","miss the exam"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Dilbilgisini Mr Hill'e tekrar sormayı düşünüyor."},{"id":"d-e","sc":[["F","How was your trip to the mountains?"],["M","Lovely! I thought I'd be scared of the cable car, but the view was so beautiful that I forgot to be afraid."]],"q":"How did the man feel on the cable car?","o":["frightened all the time","afraid at first, then calm","bored"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Manzara o kadar güzeldi ki korkmayı unuttu."},{"id":"d-f","sc":[["M","I'm not sure about this laptop. It's really cheap."],["F","Maybe too cheap. The battery only lasts three hours, and my brother had one like it that broke in a year. I'd save some more money."]],"q":"What does the woman advise the man to do?","o":["buy it immediately","wait and spend more money","ask her brother to repair it"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","inference"],"e":"Pil üç saat dayanıyor ve ucuz modeller bozuluyor; daha çok para biriktirmesini öneriyor."}]},{"k":"L3","id":"b1l3c","t":"A Cycle Tour","ty":"open","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bisiklet turu tanıtımını dinle. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","title":"Notlar","sc":[["F2","Hello, and thank you for calling Seaside Cycle Tours. I'd like to tell you about our weekend tour along the coast. The tour starts on the twelfth of June, and we meet at nine o'clock outside the old lighthouse. The ride is forty kilometres long, but don't worry, it's mostly flat, so it's suitable for beginners."],["F2","We stop for lunch in a small fishing village called Port Anna, where you can try fresh fish. The price is sixty-five pounds, and that includes the bike, a helmet and your lunch. If you would like to bring your own bike, the price is only forty pounds. Please remember to wear comfortable shoes, and bring a bottle of water, because it can be hot in the afternoon. To book, you can visit our website or call this number again after two o'clock."]],"p":"Seaside Cycle Tours\nDate: (1) ______ June\nMeet outside the: (2) ______\nDistance: (3) ______ km\nLunch in the village of: (4) ______\nPrice with bike: £(5) ______\nBring: a bottle of (6) ______","a":[["12","12th","twelfth","twelve"],["lighthouse"],["40","forty"],["port anna","anna"],["65","sixty-five","sixty five"],["water"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"]],"e":["\"the twelfth of June\"","\"outside the old lighthouse\"","\"forty kilometres long\"","\"a small fishing village called Port Anna\"","\"sixty-five pounds\"","\"bring a bottle of water\""]},{"k":"L4","id":"b1l4c","t":"The Village Doctor","ty":"mc","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Bir doktorla yapılan röportajı dinle ve soruları cevapla.","sc":[["M2","Today I'm talking to Dr Emma Walsh, who works as a doctor in a small village in the north of Scotland. Emma, why did you choose to work in the countryside?"],["F2","I grew up in a village myself, and I always knew I wanted to go back. In a big hospital, you often see a patient only once. Here I look after the same families for years, and that's very special."],["M2","What is a typical day like?"],["F2","There isn't really one! In the morning I see patients at the surgery. In the afternoon I visit people who are too ill to travel, sometimes driving for an hour along small roads. In winter the snow can make that quite difficult."],["M2","What do you find hardest about the job?"],["F2","Being available all the time. People know where I live, so they sometimes knock on my door at night. I don't mind when it's an emergency, but I have to say it can be tiring."],["M2","And what do you enjoy most?"],["F2","Seeing children I helped bring into the world start school. It makes me feel part of the community. Last year, the whole village organised a surprise party for my tenth anniversary here."],["M2","Would you recommend your job to young doctors?"],["F2","Definitely, but only if they love people more than they love money. The pay is lower than in the city, and the hours are long. Still, I wouldn't change anything."]],"q":[{"q":"Why did Emma choose to work in the countryside?","o":["She wanted to return to the kind of place she grew up in.","She did not like big hospitals.","She was offered more money."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"I grew up in a village myself, and I always knew I wanted to go back.\""},{"q":"What is difficult about her work in winter?","o":["Visiting patients when there is snow.","Finding enough medicine.","Working in the surgery in the dark."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Kar, hastaları evde ziyareti zorlaştırıyor."},{"q":"What does Emma find tiring?","o":["the long journey to the hospital","being asked for help at any time","writing reports for the council"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"İnsanlar geceleri kapısını çalıyor."},{"q":"What did the village do for Emma last year?","o":["They gave her a new car.","They organised a party for her.","They built a new surgery."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Onuncu yıl dönümü için sürpriz parti."},{"q":"What kind of young doctor would she recommend the job to?","o":["someone who wants a high salary","someone who loves working with people","someone who wants short working hours"],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"only if they love people more than they love money.\""},{"q":"How does Emma feel about her job overall?","o":["She is sorry she chose it.","She is satisfied with her decision.","She is thinking of leaving it."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I wouldn't change anything.\""}]},{"k":"L2","id":"b1l2d","t":"Plans and Opinions","ty":"mc","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Altı kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","q":[{"id":"g-a","sc":[["M","Are you coming to the gym tonight?"],["F","I can't, I have to finish my report. Maybe tomorrow, if I finish it in time."]],"q":"Why can't the woman go to the gym?","o":["She has some work to do.","She is ill.","She doesn't like the gym."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Raporunu bitirmesi gerekiyor."},{"id":"g-b","sc":[["F","Did you like the new teacher?"],["M","She's very friendly, but she gives a lot of homework. I think I'll have no free time this term."]],"q":"What does the boy think about the new teacher?","o":["She gives too much homework.","She is unfriendly.","She teaches too fast."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Arkadaş canlısı ama çok ödev veriyor."},{"id":"g-c","sc":[["M","The weather forecast for Saturday is terrible."],["F","Then let's have the barbecue on Sunday instead. At least the sun is going to shine then."]],"q":"What do they decide to do?","o":["have the barbecue on Sunday","cancel the barbecue","have the barbecue indoors on Saturday"],"a":0,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Barbekü pazar gününe kalıyor."},{"id":"g-d","sc":[["F","How was your first day at the new office?"],["M","Exhausting. I met so many people that I can't remember any of their names. But everyone was really kind."]],"q":"How does the man feel about his first day?","o":["tired but happy with the people","unhappy with his colleagues","excited about the work"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Yorucu ama herkes nazikti."},{"id":"g-e","sc":[["M","I'm thinking of selling my bike. I never use it."],["F","Why don't you put an advert in the shop window? That's how I sold mine last year."]],"q":"What does the woman suggest?","o":["advertising it in a shop window","giving it to a friend","keeping the bike"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Dükkân vitrinine ilan vermesini öneriyor."},{"id":"g-f","sc":[["F","I didn't enjoy the book. It was too long."],["M","Really? I loved it. The ending was a surprise, and I couldn't stop reading."]],"q":"What did the man think of the book?","o":["It was too long.","It had a surprising ending.","It was very boring."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Sonu sürprizdi, bırakamadı."}]},{"k":"L3","id":"b1l3d","t":"A Swimming Course","ty":"open","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bir yüzme kursu tanıtımını dinle. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","title":"Notlar","sc":[["M2","Welcome to Lakeside Swimming School. Our new course for adults starts on the fourteenth of September. Lessons take place on Tuesday and Thursday evenings, from seven until eight. There are only eight people in each class, so every student gets plenty of attention."],["M2","The course lasts ten weeks, and the cost is ninety pounds. This includes the use of the pool and a free towel on the first day. You should bring a swimming cap, because hair must be covered in the water. Our teacher, Mrs Hall, has taught swimming for more than twenty years. If you would like to join, please telephone the reception on weekdays before five o'clock."]],"p":"Lakeside Swimming School\nStarts on: (1) ______ September\nLessons on Tuesday and: (2) ______\nTime: 7 p.m. to (3) ______ p.m.\nNumber of students in each class: (4) ______\nPrice: £(5) ______\nBring a swimming: (6) ______\nTeacher: Mrs (7) ______","a":[["14","14th","fourteenth","fourteen"],["thursday"],["8","eight","8.00"],["8","eight"],["90","ninety"],["cap"],["hall"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["spelling"],["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"]],"e":["\"the fourteenth of September\"","\"Tuesday and Thursday evenings\"","\"from seven until eight\"","\"only eight people in each class\"","\"ninety pounds\"","\"bring a swimming cap\"","\"Mrs Hall\""]}],"W":[{"id":"b1w1","part":1,"t":"The New Sports Club","in":"Read this email from your English friend Alex and the notes you have made. Write your email to Alex using all the notes.","mail":"From: Alex\nSubject: Sports club\n\nHi!\nI've just joined a new sports club and I want you to come with me next Saturday. [Great!]\nWe can play tennis or go swimming – which would you prefer? [Tell Alex]\nThe club opens at 9 a.m. What time can you meet? [Suggest a time]\nAfter that, shall we have lunch together? [No, because ...]\nAlex","pts":[["Teşekkür et / sevindiğini söyle",["great","thanks","thank you","love to","sounds","fantastic","brilliant"]],["Tenis mi yüzme mi, seçimini söyle",["tennis","swim"]],["Bir buluşma saati öner",["o'clock","a.m","am ","p.m","half past","quarter","meet at","10","11","9.30","ten","eleven"]],["Öğle yemeğine gelemeyeceğini ve nedenini söyle",["because","can't","cannot","afraid","sorry","unfortunately"]]],"min":80,"max":130,"model":"Hi Alex,\nThanks for your email – it's great news that you've joined a sports club! I'd love to come with you on Saturday.\nI'd prefer to go swimming, because I haven't played tennis for years and I'm not very good at it. Swimming is also more relaxing.\nThe club opens at 9, but I have to take my little brother to his football match first. Shall we meet at half past ten outside the club?\nI'm afraid I can't have lunch with you after that, because my grandparents are visiting us and my mum wants me to be at home by one o'clock. Maybe we can have lunch next time?\nSee you on Saturday!\nDeniz","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Alex,\nGreat news about the sports club! Of course I'll come with you on Saturday – I've wanted to try something new for a while.\nI'd rather play tennis than go swimming. I swim every week at school, so tennis would be a nice change, and I've got my brother's old racket.\nCould we meet at 9.30 at the bus stop near your house? Then we can walk to the club together.\nI'm afraid I can't have lunch with you, because it's my grandmother's birthday and the whole family is eating together at one o'clock. Maybe next time!\nSee you on Saturday,\nCan"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Hi Alex,\nThanks for your message – I'm really pleased you've found a club you like, and I'd love to come along on Saturday.\nTo be honest, I'd prefer swimming. I hurt my wrist playing volleyball last month, and I don't think tennis would be a good idea until it's completely better.\nAs the club opens at nine, why don't we meet at quarter past outside the main entrance? That way we'll have plenty of time before it gets crowded.\nUnfortunately, I won't be able to stay for lunch, because I've promised to help my dad move some furniture in the afternoon. Shall we go for a pizza another day instead?\nSee you soon,\nNehir"]]},{"id":"b1w2","part":1,"t":"The Class Trip","in":"Read this email from your English teacher Mrs Brown and the notes you have made. Write your email to Mrs Brown using all the notes.","mail":"From: Mrs Brown\nSubject: Class trip\n\nDear students,\nNext month our class will go on a day trip. [Yes, I'd like to go]\nWe can visit either the science museum or the zoo. Which do you think the class would enjoy more? [Say which and why]\nWe also need to decide how to travel. [Suggest ...]\nIs there anything else you would like to do on the trip? [Tell Mrs Brown]\nBest wishes,\nMrs Brown","pts":[["Geziye katılmak istediğini söyle",["like to","love to","would like","excited","looking forward","great idea","join"]],["Müze mi hayvanat bahçesi mi, nedeniyle söyle",["museum","zoo"]],["Ulaşım önerisi yap",["bus","train","coach","car","walk","travel by","go by"]],["Gezide yapmak istediğin başka bir şeyi söyle",["also","picnic","photos","shop","lunch","park","visit","would like to"]]],"min":80,"max":130,"model":"Dear Mrs Brown,\nThank you for your email. I would really like to go on the class trip – it sounds like a great idea!\nI think the class would enjoy the science museum more than the zoo. Most of us went to the zoo last year, and the museum has a new space exhibition with lots of interactive activities.\nI suggest that we travel by train, because the station is near the school and it is cheaper than hiring a coach.\nAlso, it would be nice to have a picnic in the park next to the museum at lunchtime, if the weather is good.\nBest wishes,\nAyşe","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Dear Mrs Brown,\nThank you for telling us about the trip. Yes, I would definitely like to go!\nI think the class would enjoy the zoo more. We studied animals in science this term, and it would be great to see them in real life. Also, the zoo is outside, so we could have lunch in the park there.\nI suggest that we go by coach, because it's cheaper for a big group and we can all travel together.\nI would also like to visit the gift shop and buy some postcards for my little sister.\nBest wishes,\nEmre"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Dear Mrs Brown,\nThank you for your email. I'd be delighted to come on the class trip – it's a great way to end the term.\nIn my opinion, the science museum would be the better choice. Not only is it indoors, which is useful if it rains, but it also has a planetarium, which most of us have never been to.\nAs for travelling, I would suggest taking the train. The station is only five minutes from school, and it would be faster than a coach because there's always heavy traffic on the motorway.\nFinally, if there's time, it would be lovely to have a picnic in the gardens next to the museum.\nKind regards,\nIrmak"]]},{"id":"b1w3","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"Free Time","in":"You see this announcement on an English-language website for teenagers. Write your article in about 100 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED!\nMy favourite way to spend free time\nWhat do you enjoy doing in your free time? Why do you enjoy it? Is it better to spend free time alone or with other people?\nThe best articles will appear on our website.","pts":[["Ne yaptığını anlat",["enjoy","love","like","favourite","spend"]],["Neden sevdiğini açıkla",["because","so","reason","makes me","helps me"]],["Yalnız mı başkalarıyla mı, görüşünü söyle",["alone","with friends","with other","others","on my own","together"]],["Bir başlık kullan",["@title"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"Music: the best way to relax\nIn my free time, I love playing the guitar. I started when I was twelve, and now I practise almost every day after school.\nI enjoy it because it helps me forget about exams and homework. When I play a song well, I feel really proud of myself.\nSome people think hobbies are better with friends, but I think it depends on the hobby. I usually practise alone because I need to concentrate. However, at weekends I play with two friends in a small band, and that is the most fun of all. So, for me, the answer is both!","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Cooking: my secret hobby\nMost of my friends are surprised when I tell them my favourite free-time activity is cooking. Every Sunday, I choose a recipe from the internet and make lunch for my family.\nI enjoy it because it's creative and the result is always delicious – well, almost always! It also helps me relax after a busy week at school.\nI think some hobbies are better alone, but cooking is more fun with other people. When my cousins visit, we cook together, and the kitchen is full of noise and laughter. Food tastes better when you share it!"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Why I spend my weekends on two wheels\nIf you ask me how I prefer to spend my free time, the answer is simple: cycling. Every Saturday morning, I ride along the coast road with a small group from my neighbourhood.\nWhat I love most is the feeling of freedom. When I'm cycling, I forget about exams and problems, and I notice things I would never see from a car or bus, like tiny beaches and old fishing boats.\nAlthough some people prefer to exercise alone, I think it's much more motivating to go with others. On cold mornings, I would probably stay in bed if my friends weren't waiting for me! So my advice is: find a hobby you love, and find people to share it with."]]},{"id":"b1w4","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"Technology at School","in":"You see this announcement in an English-language magazine for students. Write your article in about 100 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED!\nShould students use phones in class?\nDo you use technology at school? How does it help you? Are there any problems with using phones in class?\nWrite an article answering these questions.","pts":[["Okulda teknoloji kullanımını anlat",["use","tablet","computer","phone","laptop","internet"]],["Faydasını açıkla",["help","useful","easier","quickly","find information"]],["Sorunlarını söyle",["problem","however","but","distract","cheat","games","social media"]],["Bir başlık kullan",["@title"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"Phones in class: friend or enemy?\nAt my school, we often use tablets in lessons, and sometimes our teachers let us use our phones to look things up.\nTechnology helps us in many ways. For example, we can find information quickly, watch short videos about science and practise English with apps.\nHowever, there are also problems. Some students play games or check social media instead of listening to the teacher. Phones can also make it easier to cheat in tests.\nIn my opinion, phones are useful if teachers have clear rules. Without rules, they cause more problems than they solve.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Technology: a useful tool, not a toy\nIn my class, we use laptops in most lessons, and sometimes we use our phones to take photos of experiments.\nTechnology helps me a lot. When I don't understand something, I can watch a short video about it, and online quizzes help me prepare for tests.\nHowever, phones can cause problems. Some students send messages during lessons or play games when the teacher isn't looking. I think phones should only be used when the teacher says so. Then they can be a great tool instead of a distraction."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Should phones be banned from classrooms?\nAt my school, we're allowed to use tablets in lessons, but phones must stay in our bags unless a teacher gives us permission.\nThere's no doubt that technology makes learning easier. We can look up information in seconds, practise vocabulary with apps and work on projects together online, even from home.\nOn the other hand, phones can be a serious distraction. It only takes one notification for a student to stop listening, and some people even use them to cheat in tests.\nIn my view, banning phones completely isn't the answer. Instead, schools should teach us how to use them responsibly – after all, we'll need those skills for the rest of our lives."]]},{"id":"b1w5","part":2,"kind":"story","t":"The Midnight Phone Call","in":"Your English teacher has asked you to write a story. Your story must begin with this sentence. Write your story in about 100 words.","mail":"Your story must begin with this sentence:\nWhen the phone rang at midnight, Sam knew something was wrong.","pts":[["Verilen cümleyle başla",["when the phone rang at midnight"]],["Geçmiş zamanla olayları anlat",["was","were","went","said","told","saw","had"]],["Bir sorun ve çözüm olsun",["but","suddenly","then","finally","in the end","luckily"]],["Hikâyeyi bitir (sonuç)",["in the end","finally","at last","never","happy","relieved","home"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"When the phone rang at midnight, Sam knew something was wrong. It was his sister, Lily, and she sounded worried.\n\"My car has broken down on the mountain road,\" she said. \"It's dark and my phone battery is almost dead.\"\nSam jumped out of bed, put on his coat and drove towards the mountains. It was raining heavily and he could hardly see the road. After forty minutes, he finally saw a small light by the side of the road. Lily was sitting in her car, cold but safe.\nThey drove home together, and in the end they laughed about the adventure over hot chocolate.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","When the phone rang at midnight, Sam knew something was wrong. It was his neighbour, Mrs Clark, and she sounded frightened.\n\"I can hear someone in my garden,\" she whispered.\nSam put on his shoes, took a torch and went next door. His heart was beating fast as he walked slowly around the house. Suddenly, he heard a noise behind the bins. He pointed his torch and saw two bright eyes looking at him.\nIt was a fox! It was eating the rubbish that the wind had knocked over. Sam laughed, and Mrs Clark made him a cup of hot chocolate to say thank you."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","When the phone rang at midnight, Sam knew something was wrong. Nobody ever called him that late. He picked it up and heard his best friend, Leo, who was clearly upset.\n\"I've missed the last train home,\" Leo explained. \"I'm at the station and my wallet has been stolen.\"\nWithout hesitating, Sam grabbed his car keys. The streets were empty and it was raining heavily, so the journey took longer than usual. When he finally arrived, he found Leo sitting alone on a bench, wet and shivering.\nOn the way home, Leo kept apologising, but Sam just smiled. \"That's what friends are for,\" he said. A week later, Leo surprised him with concert tickets to say thank you."]]},{"id":"b1w6","part":2,"kind":"story","t":"The Missing Key","in":"Your English teacher has asked you to write a story. Your story must begin with this sentence. Write your story in about 100 words.","mail":"Your story must begin with this sentence:\nAs soon as Elif opened her bag, she realised the key was missing.","pts":[["Verilen cümleyle başla",["as soon as elif opened her bag"]],["Geçmiş zamanla olayları anlat",["was","were","went","looked","found","had","decided"]],["Bir sorun ve çözüm olsun",["but","suddenly","then","luckily","so"]],["Hikâyeyi bitir (sonuç)",["in the end","finally","at last","happy","relieved","laughed"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"As soon as Elif opened her bag, she realised the key was missing. She was standing outside her flat, and her parents were on holiday for a week.\nShe tried to remember where she had been that day. She had gone to school, then to the library, and finally to a café with her friends.\nFirst, she called the café, but nobody had found a key. Then she went back to the library. Luckily, the librarian smiled and held up a small silver key. \"Is this yours?\" she asked.\nElif was so relieved that she bought the librarian a box of chocolates the next day.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","As soon as Elif opened her bag, she realised the key was missing. She had to open the school sports hall for the basketball team, and the match started in twenty minutes!\nShe ran back to the classroom and looked under her desk, but it wasn't there. Then she remembered that she had taken her bag to the canteen at lunchtime. She hurried there and asked the cook.\n\"Is this yours?\" the cook asked with a smile, holding up a small silver key.\nElif thanked her and ran to the sports hall. She opened the door just as the team arrived."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","As soon as Elif opened her bag, she realised the key was missing. It was the key to her grandfather's old wooden box, which he had given her before he moved abroad, and she had promised to keep it safe.\nShe tried to stay calm and thought about everything she had done that day. She had been to the library, then to the park with her friends. Feeling worried, she went back to the park and searched every bench, but there was no sign of it.\nAs she was walking home, disappointed, a little boy ran up to her. \"Did you drop this?\" he asked, holding out the key. He had seen it fall from her bag and followed her.\nElif was so relieved that she bought him an ice cream. That evening, she put the key on a chain and wore it around her neck."]]},{"id":"b1w7","part":1,"t":"The Birthday Surprise","in":"Read this email from your English friend Jo and the notes you have made. Write your email to Jo using all the notes.","mail":"From: Jo\nSubject: Mum's birthday\n\nHi!\nIt's my mum's 40th birthday next month and I want to give her a surprise party. [Great idea!]\nShould we have it at home or in a restaurant? [Say which and why]\nCould you help me prepare the food on the day? [Yes, but ...]\nWhat present do you think I should buy? [Suggest ...]\nJo","pts":[["Fikri beğendiğini söyle",["great","good idea","love","fantastic","brilliant","wonderful","sounds"]],["Ev mi restoran mı, nedenini söyle",["home","restaurant","because","cheaper","more"]],["Yemeğe yardım edeceğini ama bir koşulun olduğunu söyle",["help","but","unless","as long as","only","can't","until"]],["Hediye öner",["present","gift","suggest","buy","book","flowers","perfume","scarf"]]],"min":80,"max":130,"model":"Hi Jo,\nWhat a great idea! I'm sure your mum will love a surprise party for her fortieth birthday.\nI think it would be better to have it at home, because it's cheaper and we can decorate the rooms however we like. A restaurant would be quite noisy, and we would have to leave early.\nOf course I'll help you with the food, but I can only come after four o'clock because I have football training in the afternoon. I could make a cake and some sandwiches.\nAs for the present, why don't you buy her a nice scarf or a book by her favourite writer? Let me know what you decide!\nBest wishes,\nMerve","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Jo,\nThanks for telling me about the party – it's a really good idea! Your mum will be so surprised.\nI'd choose the restaurant. At home, your mum would see the preparations and guess the surprise. Also, nobody would have to wash up afterwards!\nI'd be happy to help with the food, but only if we decide to have the party at home. I can't cook in a restaurant, can I?\nFor a present, I suggest a framed photo of your family. It's cheap, and it's something she will keep for years.\nSpeak soon,\nMerve"]]},{"id":"b1w8","part":1,"t":"The Summer Job","in":"Read this email from your English friend Sam and the notes you have made. Write your email to Sam using all the notes.","mail":"From: Sam\nSubject: Summer work\n\nHello!\nI've found a summer job in a café by the sea. They need another waiter. [Tell Sam ...]\nThe work starts at 8 a.m. and finishes at 4 p.m. Do you think that's too long? [Say what you think]\nI'm going to save the money for a trip. Where would you go? [Suggest a place]\nWhen can we meet to talk about it? [Suggest a day]\nSam","pts":[["İşe başvurmak isteyip istemediğini söyle",["apply","interested","would like","want","love","work","job"]],["Saatler hakkındaki fikrini söyle",["too long","not too","long","hours","tired","early","fine"]],["Bir yer öner",["go to","visit","trip","travel","beach","city","country","mountain","italy","spain","london"]],["Bir gün öner",["monday","tuesday","wednesday","thursday","friday","saturday","sunday","weekend","tomorrow","next"]]],"min":80,"max":130,"model":"Hi Sam,\nThanks for your message. A job in a café by the sea sounds fantastic, and I'd love to apply for the waiter's job too.\nIn my opinion, eight hours isn't too long, as long as we have a lunch break. Getting up at six might be difficult, but I'm sure I'll get used to it.\nIf I earned enough money, I would go to Italy, because I have always wanted to see Rome and try real pizza. You could come with me!\nWhy don't we meet next Saturday afternoon at the library? Then we can write our applications together.\nSee you soon,\nAylin","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Sam,\nCongratulations on finding a job! I'm really interested in the waiter's job, so please send me the address of the café.\nI think eight hours is a bit long, especially in hot weather. It's fine if we can have two breaks, though.\nIf I were you, I'd save the money for a trip to Greece. The islands are beautiful, and the tickets are cheap in September.\nWhat about meeting on Sunday morning? We could go for a coffee and talk about everything.\nBest,\nAylin"]]},{"id":"b1w9","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"The Best Teacher","in":"You see this announcement in an English-language magazine for teenagers. Write your article in about 100 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED!\nA teacher I will always remember\nWho was your best teacher? What did he or she do that was special? How did this teacher help you?\nThe best articles will be published next month.","pts":[["Öğretmeni tanıt",["teacher","mr","mrs","ms","taught","lesson","subject"]],["Özel olarak ne yaptığını anlat",["always","used to","would","every","explained","helped","showed"]],["Sana nasıl yardım ettiğini söyle",["helped","because","thanks to","learnt","confidence","taught me","made me"]],["Bir başlık kullan",["@title"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"The teacher who changed my mind\nThe teacher I will always remember is Mrs Kaya, who taught me maths when I was thirteen. I hated maths before I met her, because I thought I was bad at it.\nMrs Kaya was different from other teachers. She used to explain every problem in three different ways, and she never got angry when we made mistakes. Every Friday, she gave us a puzzle to solve in teams, which made the lessons fun.\nThanks to her, I started to believe in myself, and my marks went up from 50 to 85. Now maths is my favourite subject, and I want to be an engineer one day.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","My football coach\nThe person who has taught me most is not a teacher at school, but my football coach, Mr Demir. He trained our team for three years.\nHe always said that losing was a part of learning. After every match, he sat with us and asked what we could do better. He never shouted, and that made us respect him more.\nBecause of him, I became the team captain at fifteen, and I also learnt how to speak in front of people. I will never forget his advice: \"Do your best, and the result will follow.\""]]},{"id":"b1w10","part":2,"kind":"story","t":"The Surprise Visitor","in":"Your English teacher has asked you to write a story. Your story must end with this sentence. Write your story in about 100 words.","mail":"Your story must end with this sentence:\nI have never been so happy to see my grandfather.","pts":[["Hikâyeyi verilen cümleyle bitir",["never been so happy to see my grandfather"]],["Geçmiş zamanla olayları anlat",["was","were","went","heard","opened","saw","decided","knocked"]],["Olaylar arasında bağlaç kullan",["when","then","after","suddenly","while","because","but","so"]],["Duyguları anlat",["felt","nervous","excited","afraid","surprised","worried","relieved","happy"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"It was a stormy night, and I was alone at home. My parents had gone to a wedding in another city, and the electricity had just gone off. I lit a candle and tried to read, but every noise made me jump.\nSuddenly, I heard someone knocking at the door. My heart was beating fast. I walked slowly into the hall and asked, \"Who is it?\"\n\"It's me!\" said a deep voice. I opened the door, and there stood my grandfather, wet from the rain, with a big torch in his hand. He had come to check that I was safe.\nI have never been so happy to see my grandfather.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","The night was cold and dark, and Leyla was waiting at the bus stop. Her phone had no battery, and the last bus had left an hour ago. She didn't know what to do.\nThen she saw the lights of a car in the distance. It stopped in front of her, and the window opened. Leyla was afraid at first, but then she recognised the driver. It was her grandfather! Her mother had called him when Leyla didn't come home.\n\"Get in, you'll catch a cold,\" he said with a smile.\nI have never been so happy to see my grandfather."]]},{"id":"b1w11","part":1,"t":"The School Trip Email","in":"Read this email from your English friend Chris and the notes you have made. Write your email to Chris using all the notes.","mail":"From: Chris\nSubject: Our school trip\n\nHi!\nOur class is going to a science museum next week. Do you want to join us? [Thank Chris]\nWe're meeting at the school gate at 8 a.m. Is that too early for you? [Say what you think]\nWe need to bring a packed lunch. What would you like to eat? [Suggest ...]\nShould I take photos for you? [Yes, because ...]\nChris","pts":[["Teşekkür et",["thank","thanks","kind","invite","great","lovely"]],["Saat hakkındaki görüşünü söyle",["early","late","fine","okay","ok","problem","don't mind","get up"]],["Yemek öner",["sandwich","salad","fruit","pizza","cheese","chicken","eat","lunch"]],["Fotoğraf isteğini ve nedenini söyle",["photo","picture","because","yes","please","remember"]]],"min":80,"max":130,"model":"Hi Chris,\nThanks a lot for inviting me to your school trip. I would love to join you! I've always wanted to visit the science museum.\nEight o'clock isn't too early for me. I usually get up at seven, so I'll have plenty of time to get ready. I'll be at the school gate five minutes before.\nFor lunch, I'd like to bring a cheese sandwich, an apple and a bottle of water. Maybe we could share some biscuits too.\nYes, please take some photos! I'd like to show them to my parents, because they are interested in science. See you next week!\nBest wishes,\nDilara","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Chris,\nThank you very much for the invitation. That sounds like a really interesting trip, and I'd love to come.\nTo be honest, eight is a bit early, because I usually take the bus at half past seven. But I'll manage if you can wait for me a few minutes.\nI'd like to take a chicken sandwich and some grapes. I don't eat chocolate before lunch!\nPlease do take photos, because I want to remember the day. I'll send you some money for the printing.\nSee you!\nDilara"]]},{"id":"b1w12","part":1,"t":"Moving to a New Town","in":"Read this email from your English friend Sue and the notes you have made. Write your email to Sue using all the notes.","mail":"From: Sue\nSubject: Big news!\n\nHello!\nMy family is moving to a new town next month. I'm very nervous about it. [Say something kind]\nI'll have to change schools. What advice would you give me? [Give advice]\nI want to join a club to make friends. Which one should I choose? [Suggest ...]\nCan you visit me in my new house? [Yes, when?]\nSue","pts":[["Nazik bir şey söyle",["sorry","understand","sure","don't worry","exciting","new start","you'll be fine"]],["Tavsiye ver",["should","try","advice","talk","smile","be yourself","ask"]],["Bir kulüp öner",["club","join","football","music","art","drama","chess","science"]],["Ziyaret için bir zaman öner",["visit","come","holiday","weekend","summer","next","july","august"]]],"min":80,"max":130,"model":"Hi Sue,\nWhat big news! I understand why you're nervous, but don't worry. Moving can be exciting, and I'm sure you'll be fine.\nMy advice is to talk to people in your new class on the first day and to smile a lot. You should also ask the teachers if you need help.\nAs for clubs, I think you should join the drama club. You like acting, and you'll meet people who enjoy the same things.\nOf course I'll visit you! Maybe I could come during the summer holidays. Send me your new address when you have it.\nLots of love,\nZeynep","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Sue,\nI'm sorry to hear that you're worried, but a new town can also bring good things. You'll make friends quickly, I'm sure.\nI think you should be yourself and not be afraid to ask questions. Joining a team is also a good idea.\nWhy don't you try the football club or the chess club? Both are great places to meet people.\nI'd love to see your new house. Could I come at the end of July? My parents will take me by train.\nBest wishes,\nZeynep"]]}],"S":[{"id":"b1s1","p":1,"t":"About You (1)","ins":"Examiner seni tanımak için kısa sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["What's your name? Where do you live?",20,["my name is","i live in","i'm from"],["My name's Deniz Kaya, and I live in Eskişehir, a student city in the middle of Turkey. I live with my parents and my younger sister in a flat near the river.","I'm Mert. I come from a small town near Bursa, but at the moment I live in Istanbul, because I'm studying there. I share a flat with two friends from university."]],["Do you work or are you a student?",20,["i'm a student","i study","i work","at the moment"],["I'm a student. I'm in my last year at high school, so I'm studying very hard for the university exam. My favourite subjects are biology and chemistry.","I work as a receptionist in a hotel. I've been there for two years. I like it because I meet people from different countries and I can practise my English."]],["What do you like doing in your free time?",20,["i like","i enjoy","in my free time","because"],["I really enjoy playing basketball. I play with my friends in the park two or three times a week. I also like watching series in the evenings to relax.","In my free time, I usually read novels or draw. I'm quite a quiet person, so I like hobbies I can do at home. At the weekend I sometimes go to art exhibitions."]],["Tell us about your best friend.",25,["my best friend","we","known","since"],["My best friend is called Ayşe. We met on our first day at secondary school, and we've been friends ever since. She's very funny and honest, and she always listens to my problems. We love going to concerts together.","My best friend's name is Tolga. He lives next door, so we see each other almost every day. He's really good at football and computers, and he's helped me a lot with my homework. We're planning a trip together next summer."]],["What did you do last weekend?",25,["last weekend","went","visited","watched"],["Last weekend I went to my cousin's wedding in Izmir. It was in a big garden by the sea, and there was lots of music and dancing. I didn't get home until two in the morning!","Last weekend was quite quiet. On Saturday I tidied my room and did my homework, and on Sunday I went for a long walk with my dog in the forest. In the evening I watched a football match."]],["What are you going to do next summer?",25,["going to","i'd like","i hope","plan"],["Next summer I'm going to work in a café near the beach, because I want to save money for a new laptop. If I have time, I'd also like to go camping with my friends.","I'm going to visit my aunt in Germany next summer. I've never been abroad before, so I'm really excited. I'm hoping to see Berlin and maybe practise a bit of German."]]]},{"id":"b1s2","p":1,"t":"About You (2)","ins":"Examiner seni tanımak için kısa sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["How do you usually travel to school or work?",20,["usually","by bus","on foot","takes"],["I usually go to school by bus. It takes about twenty-five minutes, so I listen to music or read on the way. When the weather's nice, I sometimes cycle instead.","I walk to work, because my office is only fifteen minutes from my flat. I think it's a good way to start the day, and it means I don't have to worry about traffic."]],["What kind of music do you like?",20,["i like","favourite","because","listen"],["I like lots of different music, but my favourite is rock. I love bands with loud guitars and good drummers. I also listen to Turkish pop when I'm with my friends.","I mostly listen to jazz and classical music, because it helps me relax and concentrate when I'm studying. My parents listened to it a lot when I was a child."]],["Tell us about your home.",25,["live in","flat","house","there is","there are"],["I live in a flat on the fourth floor of a building in the city centre. It has three bedrooms, a small kitchen and a balcony where we have breakfast in summer. The best thing about it is the view of the mountains.","My home is a small house with a garden in a quiet village. It's quite old, but it's very comfortable. My favourite room is the living room, because it has a big fireplace and we sit there together in winter."]],["What's your favourite food? Why?",20,["favourite","because","delicious","my mum"],["My favourite food is mantı, a kind of Turkish pasta with meat and yoghurt. My grandmother makes the best mantı in the world, and it always reminds me of family dinners.","I love sushi. I tried it for the first time two years ago and I loved it immediately. It's light and healthy, and there are so many different kinds to try."]],["Have you ever been to another country?",25,["have been","haven't been","never","last year","i'd like"],["Yes, I have. Two years ago I went to Greece with my family. We visited Athens and a small island. I loved the old buildings and the food, which was similar to Turkish food in many ways.","No, I haven't been abroad yet, but I'd love to go to Japan one day. I'm really interested in Japanese culture, and I've been watching lots of videos about Tokyo."]],["Is English important for you? Why?",25,["important","because","job","travel","future"],["Yes, it's very important for me, because I want to study at university abroad. English also helps me understand films, songs and websites, and I can talk to tourists in my city.","English is important for my job, because many of our customers are from other countries. Also, I like travelling, and English helps me communicate wherever I go."]]]},{"id":"b1s3","p":2,"t":"A Favourite Place","ins":"PET'te bu bölümde bir fotoğrafı anlatırsın. Burada fotoğraf yerine konu kartı var: 1 dakika boyunca anlat.","q":[["Describe a place in your town where people like to spend time. Talk about where it is, what people do there, what you can see and hear, and why people like it.",60,["there is","there are","people are","in the middle","on the left","i can see","because","looks"],["I'd like to talk about the seaside promenade in my town. It's a long path next to the sea, near the city centre. In the evenings, especially in summer, it's full of people. Families walk with their children, young people ride bikes or skateboards, and older people sit on benches and chat. You can see fishing boats, seagulls and, at sunset, the sky turns orange and pink. You can hear the waves, music from the cafés and people selling corn and tea. I think people like it because it's free, it's close to the water and it's a relaxing place to meet friends after a busy day.","One place that's very popular in my town is the central park. It's next to the old castle, so it's easy to find. At the weekend, families have picnics on the grass, children play in the playground and teenagers play football or volleyball. There's also a small lake with ducks, and people often feed them. You can hear children laughing, birds singing and sometimes a street musician playing the guitar. I think people love it because it's green and peaceful, and it's one of the few places where you can forget you're in a busy city."]]]},{"id":"b1s4","p":2,"t":"A Busy Moment","ins":"Fotoğraf yerine konu kartı: 1 dakika boyunca, sanki fotoğrafa bakıyormuş gibi anlat.","q":[["Imagine a photo of a busy train station. Describe the people, what they are doing, what they are wearing and how they might be feeling.",60,["in the picture","i can see","is wearing","are waiting","looks","might be","probably","in the background"],["In this photo, I can see a busy train station, probably in the morning. There are lots of people walking quickly in different directions. On the left, a man in a dark suit is looking at his watch – I think he's late for work. In the middle, a young couple with big suitcases are checking the departure board, so maybe they're going on holiday. They look excited. On the right, a woman is buying a coffee from a small café. Most people are wearing coats and scarves, so it's probably cold outside. In general, people seem a bit stressed, because everyone is in a hurry.","This photo shows the inside of a large station. It looks very modern, with a high glass roof. Near the front, a group of students with backpacks are sitting on the floor and laughing – maybe they're waiting for a school trip. Behind them, there's an old man holding a newspaper and looking at the trains. He looks relaxed. A mother is holding her little boy's hand, and he's pointing at a train. People are wearing summer clothes, like T-shirts and sunglasses, so I think it's a hot day. Most of them look quite happy and calm."]]]},{"id":"b1s5","p":3,"t":"Making New Friends","ins":"PET'te bu bölüm bir partnerle yapılır. Burada iki tarafı da sen düşün: seçenekleri karşılaştır ve bir karar ver. 1 dakika.","q":[["A friend is moving to a new city and doesn't know anyone. Talk about these ideas and decide which one is the best way to make new friends: joining a sports club, taking an evening class, volunteering, using a social app, talking to neighbours.",60,["what about","i think","because","it would be","on the other hand","i agree","the best","let's"],["Let's think about each idea. Joining a sports club is good because you meet people regularly and you have something to talk about. Taking an evening class is also useful, because people there are interested in the same subject. Volunteering is a great idea, because you help others and you work in a team. Using a social app is easy, but it can be a bit dangerous to meet strangers from the internet. Talking to neighbours is nice, but in big cities many people don't know their neighbours. In my opinion, the best way is joining a sports club, because you see the same people every week and it's easy to become friends.","I'll start with the social app. It's probably the quickest way to find people with similar interests, but I'm not sure the friendships last. Talking to neighbours is friendly, but you might not have much in common. A sports club is great if you like sport, but some people don't. An evening class is a good choice because it's relaxed and you can talk during breaks. I think volunteering is the best idea, though, because you meet kind people, you feel useful, and working together on something important brings people closer."]]]},{"id":"b1s6","p":4,"t":"Friendship","ins":"Examiner konuyla ilgili görüşünü soruyor. Her soruya fikrini ve nedenini söyle.","q":[["Do you prefer spending time with a big group of friends or with one or two close friends? Why?",40,["i prefer","because","for example","in my opinion","it depends"],["I prefer spending time with one or two close friends. In a big group it's difficult to have a real conversation, and some people don't get the chance to speak. With close friends, I can talk about anything, even my problems, and I feel more relaxed. But I think big groups are fun for parties or birthdays.","I prefer big groups, because there's always someone to talk to and there's more energy. For example, when we go to the beach with ten people, we can play volleyball and lots of games. With only one friend, it can get boring after a while."]],["Is it easier to make friends at school or online?",40,["i think","easier","because","however","on the other hand"],["I think it's easier at school, because you see the same people every day and you do things together, like projects and sports. Online, it's easy to start chatting, but you don't really know if people are honest about themselves. Real friendship needs time together.","In some ways, it's easier online. Shy people, like me, find it less stressful to write than to speak. Also, online you can find people who love the same things as you, even if they live far away. I met one of my best friends in an online game."]],["How can people keep in touch with friends who live far away?",40,["can","video call","message","visit","for example"],["Today it's quite easy. You can send messages every day, share photos and have video calls. I talk to my cousin in Germany every Sunday on video. But I think it's also important to visit each other when you can, because meeting face to face is different.","People can use social media to see what their friends are doing, and they can play online games together. Some people still send letters or postcards, which I think is very special. Planning a holiday together once a year is also a good way to stay close."]]]},{"id":"b1s7","p":1,"t":"Work and Education","ins":"Examiner okulun, işin ve becerilerin hakkında sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["What subject do you enjoy most at school, or did you enjoy most?",25,["i enjoy","my favourite","because","interesting"],"I enjoy history most, because I love learning how people lived in the past. Our teacher also tells really interesting stories in class."],["What job would you like to do in the future?",25,["i'd like to","i want to","because","i hope"],"I'd like to be an architect, because I'm good at drawing and I love interesting buildings. I hope to design schools one day."],["What skills are important for that job?",25,["you need to","be able to","good at","important"],"You need to be good at maths and drawing, and you have to be able to work in a team. It's also important to listen to what your clients want."],["Can you use a computer well? What do you use it for?",20,["i can","i use it","for","quite well"],"Yes, I can use a computer quite well. I use it for my homework, and sometimes I edit videos for my friends."],["Would you prefer to work in an office or outdoors? Why?",25,["i'd prefer","rather","because","outdoors"],"I'd prefer to work outdoors, because I don't like sitting still all day. I think I would get bored in an office."]]},{"id":"b1s8","p":1,"t":"Hobbies and Free Time","ins":"Examiner hobilerin ve tercihlerin hakkında sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["What do you enjoy doing in the evenings?",20,["i enjoy","i usually","in the evenings","because"],"In the evenings I usually read a book or watch a series with my sister. It helps me relax after a long day."],["Is there a hobby you'd like to try? Why?",25,["i'd like to try","because","looks","never"],"I'd like to try rock climbing. I've never done it, but it looks exciting and it's a good way to keep fit."],["What kind of things don't you like doing?",20,["i don't like","i hate","i can't stand","boring"],"I don't like shopping for clothes, because it takes a long time and I find it boring. I prefer to buy things online."],["Do you prefer spending your free time alone or with friends?",25,["i prefer","with friends","alone","but","depends"],"I usually prefer being with friends, because we have fun together. But sometimes I like to be alone, for example when I'm reading."],["How did you spend your last holiday?",25,["last","went","stayed","we","it was"],"Last summer I went to Bodrum with my family. We stayed in a small hotel and spent most of the time swimming and eating seafood."]]},{"id":"b1s9","p":2,"t":"At the Train Station","ins":"Fotoğrafı yaklaşık 1 dakika anlat: kimler var, ne yapıyorlar, nerede, neler görüyorsun. Kelimeyi bilmiyorsan başka sözcüklerle anlat (it's a kind of ..., it's something you use for ...).","img":[["🚉🧳👩‍🦰👨‍👦🕐📱","Kalabalık bir tren istasyonu: bir kadın valiziyle büyük saate bakıyor, bir baba oğluyla bilet makinesinin önünde, arkada tren bekliyor; biri telefonuna bakıyor."]],"q":[["Now, here is your photograph. It shows people at a train station. Please tell us what you can see in the photograph.",60,["in this photo","on the left","on the right","in the background","it looks like","maybe","it's a kind of"],"In this photo I can see a busy train station. On the left there's a woman with a big suitcase. She's looking at the clock, so maybe she's worried she'll miss her train. In the middle, a father and his son are standing in front of a ticket machine – I think they're buying tickets. In the background there's a train waiting at the platform. On the right, a young man is looking at his phone. It looks like early morning, because everyone seems to be in a hurry."]]},{"id":"b1s10","p":2,"t":"Moving House","ins":"Fotoğrafı yaklaşık 1 dakika anlat. İnsanların ne hissediyor olabileceğini de söyle (they seem ..., they might be ...).","img":[["📦🛋️👫🪴🖼️😊","Yeni bir eve taşınan genç bir çift: oturma odasında kutular var, biri duvara resim asıyor, diğeri bir bitkiyi pencerenin önüne koyuyor; ikisi de gülümsüyor."]],"q":[["Now, here is your photograph. It shows people moving into a new home. Please tell us what you can see in the photograph.",60,["in this photo","there are","they seem","they might be","next to","in front of","I think"],"This photo shows a young couple who are moving into a new flat. They are in the living room and there are lots of cardboard boxes on the floor. The man is putting a picture on the wall, and the woman is placing a plant in front of the window. There's a sofa next to the wall, but it still has plastic on it. They both seem happy and excited – I think it might be their first home together."]]},{"id":"b1s11","p":3,"t":"A Class Meal","ins":"Gerçek sınavda partnerle yapılır. İki tarafı da sen düşün: seçenekleri öner, öneriye cevap ver, sonra birini seç (How about ...?, Why don't we ...?, That's a good idea, but ...).","opts":["🍕 pizzacıda yemek","🧺 parkta piknik","🍲 herkes evden yemek getirsin","🍔 hamburgerci","🍰 okulda pasta partisi"],"q":[["A class wants to have a meal together at the end of the year. Here are some ideas. Talk together about the different ideas and say which would be most enjoyable for everyone.",90,["how about","why don't we","that's a good idea","but","I'm not sure","what do you think","it would be"],"Shall we start with the pizza restaurant? Most people like pizza, but it might be expensive for some students. How about a picnic in the park? That would be cheap and we could play games too, but if it rains it would be a disaster. Why don't we ask everyone to bring food from home? That's a nice idea because we could try different dishes, but some people might not have time to cook. The burger place is quick and easy, although not everyone eats meat. A cake party at school is fun, but it isn't really a meal."],["Now decide which idea would be best.",30,["I think","the best","because","let's","we agree"],"I think the picnic would be best, because it's cheap and everyone can relax and talk. If the weather is bad, we can move it to the school hall."]]},{"id":"b1s12","p":4,"t":"Shopping","ins":"Examiner alışveriş hakkında görüşünü soruyor. Fikrini söyle, nedenini açıkla, örnek ver.","q":[["Do you prefer shopping online or in shops? Why?",40,["I prefer","because","for example","on the other hand"],"I prefer shopping in shops for clothes, because I can try them on. For example, sizes are often different online. On the other hand, online shopping is much quicker for things like books."],["Do young people spend too much money on clothes?",40,["I think","some","because","it depends"],"I think some young people do, because they want to follow fashion and look like people on social media. But it depends – many of my friends buy second-hand clothes now."],["Is it better to buy something cheap or something expensive that lasts longer?",40,["in my opinion","because","in the end","worth"],"In my opinion it's often better to buy something more expensive if it lasts longer. My cheap trainers broke after two months, so in the end I spent more money."],["What would you change about the shops in your town?",40,["I'd like","there should be","because","more"],"I'd like more small independent shops. At the moment there are only big chains in the shopping centre, and they all sell the same things."]]},{"id":"b1s13","p":1,"t":"Daily Life","ins":"Examiner günlük hayatın hakkında sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["What time do you usually get up? What do you do in the morning?",15,["I usually","I get up","then","after that"],["I usually get up at seven o'clock. Then I have breakfast, brush my teeth and go to school by bus.","I get up at half past six during the week. I have a quick breakfast, then I catch the bus. At the weekend I sleep until ten."]],["What's your favourite meal of the day? Why?",10,["my favourite","because","I like","I enjoy"],["My favourite meal is dinner, because the whole family eats together. My mother always cooks something special.","My favourite meal is breakfast, because I love eggs and fresh bread. On Sundays we have a big breakfast together."]],["Tell me about the town or city where you live.",20,["I live in","there is","there are","it's a","you can"],["I live in a medium-sized city near the sea. There are lots of parks and cafés, and in summer you can swim at the beach. The only problem is the traffic in the centre.","I live in a small town in the mountains. It's very quiet and the air is clean, but there isn't much to do in the evenings, so young people go to the city."]],["How do you usually travel to school or work?",10,["I usually","by bus","on foot","it takes"],["I usually go by bus, and it takes about twenty minutes. Sometimes, when the weather is nice, I walk.","I go to school by bike. It takes me ten minutes, and it's a good way to wake up in the morning."]],["What are you going to do this evening?",10,["I'm going to","I'll probably","later","tonight"],["I'm going to do my homework first. After that, I'll probably watch a film with my brother.","I'm going to meet my friends at the cinema. If the film is good, we'll go for pizza afterwards."]]]},{"id":"b1s14","p":2,"t":"A Market Stall","ins":"Bu bölümde bir fotoğrafı yaklaşık 1 dakika anlatırsın: kimler var, ne yapıyorlar, nerede, neler görüyorsun.","q":[["Now, here is your photograph. It shows a market stall. Please tell us what you can see in the photograph.",60,["in this photo I can see","on the left","on the right","in the background","they are","it looks like","they seem"],["In this photo I can see a man who is selling fruit at a market stall. In the front, there are boxes of oranges, apples and strawberries. On the right, a woman is putting some fruit into a bag. She is smiling. In the background there are other stalls and a lot of people. It looks like a sunny day, and everybody seems relaxed. I think the fruit is very fresh. I would say it is a very typical scene in my country, because people there love shopping outdoors and chatting with the sellers. If I were there, I would buy some strawberries because they look delicious.","This photo shows a busy market. A young man is selling vegetables, and he is wearing a green apron. There are tomatoes, peppers and cucumbers on the table. On the left, an old lady is checking the price. In the background I can see some tents. The people look happy, and I think it's a good place to buy cheap, fresh food. I would say it is a very typical scene in my country, because people there love shopping outdoors and chatting with the sellers. If I were there, I would buy some strawberries because they look delicious."]]],"img":[["🍎🍊🍓🧺👩‍🌾🛒","Bir pazar tezgâhında satıcı meyve satıyor; önünde elma, portakal ve çilek kasaları var. Bir kadın poşetine meyve koyuyor, arkada başka tezgâhlar ve kalabalık var.","b1-market","A man is selling fruit at a market stall while a woman puts oranges into a bag. There are other stalls and people behind them."]]},{"id":"b1s15","p":3,"t":"A Farewell Party","ins":"Gerçek sınavda bu bölüm bir partnerle yapılır. Burada iki tarafı da sen düşün: seçenekleri tek tek değerlendir, sonra karar ver.","q":[["A teacher is leaving your school, and the students want to organise a farewell party. Here are some ideas they have. Talk about the different ideas and say which would be best for the teacher.",40,["what about","I think","because","it would be","on the other hand","that's a good idea","I'm not sure"],["Let's start with the picnic in the park. I think it would be relaxing, but the weather might be bad. A concert with songs by the students would be very personal, though some people might feel shy. A photo album is a nice present because the teacher can keep it for years.","I like the idea of a concert because it's lively and everybody can join in. A picnic is a nice idea too, but if it rains we'll have no plan. A cake in the classroom is simple and cheap, although it's perhaps a bit boring. I think a surprise video is original, because every student can say a few words to the teacher."]],["Now decide which idea would be best.",15,["I think the best","because","I'd choose","in my opinion"],["In my opinion, the best idea is the photo album, because it doesn't depend on the weather and the teacher will remember us every time she looks at it.","In my opinion, the surprise video is the best idea, because it is personal and the teacher can watch it again whenever she misses us."]]],"opts":["🧺 park pikniği","🎵 öğrenci konseri","📷 fotoğraf albümü","🎂 sınıfta pasta","🎁 sürpriz video"]},{"id":"b1s16","p":4,"t":"Teachers and Gifts","ins":"Sınav görevlisi 3. bölümün konusuyla ilgili görüşünü soruyor. Her soruya fikrini ve nedenini söyle.","q":[["Do you think teachers need to be given presents? Why or why not?",15,["I think","because","for example","it depends"],["I don't think presents are necessary, because teachers are only doing their job. However, a small gift at the end of the year is a nice way to say thank you.","I think a small present is a good idea, especially something handmade. It shows that you care, and it doesn't need to be expensive."]],["What makes a good teacher, in your opinion?",15,["I think","a good teacher","patient","explains","because"],["I think a good teacher is patient and explains things clearly. It's also important that he or she makes lessons interesting, for example by using games or videos.","For me, a good teacher listens to students and gives them a chance to ask questions. Teachers who only shout are not usually good at explaining."]],["Would you like to be a teacher one day? Why or why not?",15,["I would","I wouldn't","because","one day"],["I would like to be a teacher one day, because I enjoy helping other people learn. On the other hand, I know the job is tiring, so I'm not completely sure.","I don't think I would like to be a teacher, because I wouldn't have enough patience. I'd prefer a job where I can work with computers."]]]},{"id":"b1s17","p":1,"t":"Food and Health","ins":"Examiner yeme-içme ve sağlık hakkında sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["What did you eat for breakfast today?",10,["I had","I ate","this morning","and"],["This morning I had bread with cheese and olives and a glass of tea. I was in a hurry, so I didn't eat much.","I only had a cup of coffee, because I got up late. I'm hungry now, so I'll have a big lunch."]],["Do you like cooking? What can you make?",10,["I like","I can","I'm good at","not very"],["I like cooking, but I'm not very good at it yet. I can make pasta and omelettes, and I'm learning to bake cakes.","I don't cook much, but I enjoy making salads and soup. My grandmother is teaching me how to make dolma."]],["What do you do to stay healthy?",15,["I try to","I usually","I never","it's important"],["I try to walk to school every day, and I never skip breakfast. I also drink a lot of water, because it's important for the body.","I do sport three times a week, and I try to eat fruit every day. Sleeping enough is also very important."]],["Is there any food you don't like? Why?",10,["I don't like","I hate","because","the taste"],["I don't like spinach, because it has a strange taste. My mother says I should eat it, but I can't.","I really don't like fish, because of the smell. When my family cooks it, I usually eat rice instead."]],["What would you like to eat tonight?",10,["I'd like","I'd love","I feel like","maybe"],["I'd love to eat grilled chicken with rice. Maybe we can order it from my favourite restaurant.","I feel like eating something sweet, so maybe I'll have a piece of chocolate cake with ice cream."]]]},{"id":"b1s18","p":1,"t":"Family and Home","ins":"Examiner ailen ve evin hakkında sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["Tell me about your family.",20,["there are","my mother","my father","we"],["There are four of us in my family: my parents, my younger sister and me. My mother is a teacher, and my father works in a bank. We get on well and often have dinner together.","We're a family of five: my parents, my two brothers and me. My father is an engineer, and my mother looks after the house. On Sundays, we usually visit my grandparents."]],["What do you like about your home?",10,["I like","it's","there is","because"],["I like my room because it has a big window and a lot of light. I can study there quietly.","What I like most is the garden. In summer, we have breakfast there, and I can read under the tree."]],["Who do you spend most time with?",15,["I spend","with my","most of","because"],["I spend most of my time with my best friend, because we sit next to each other at school and live in the same street.","Probably with my brother, because we share a room and have the same hobbies. We play video games and talk a lot."]],["What do you usually do at home in the evening?",15,["I usually","after dinner","I like","then"],["I usually do my homework first. After dinner, I watch a series or talk to my friends online. Then I go to bed at about eleven.","I often help my mother in the kitchen, and then I watch some TV. At weekends, I stay up later and play games with my cousins."]],["Would you like to live in a different place in the future?",15,["I'd like to","I would love to","because","maybe"],["I'd like to live in a big city one day, because there are more jobs and things to do. But I would also like to have a small house by the sea for the holidays.","I'd love to move abroad, maybe to England, because I want to improve my English. However, I would miss my family very much."]]]},{"id":"b1s19","p":2,"t":"A Rainy Day","ins":"Bu bölümde bir fotoğrafı yaklaşık 1 dakika anlatırsın: kimler var, ne yapıyorlar, nerede, neler görüyorsun.","q":[["Now, here is your photograph. It shows people in the rain. Please tell us what you can see in the photograph.",60,["in this photo I can see","on the left","on the right","in the background","they are","it looks like","they seem"],["In this photo I can see two girls who are walking along a street in the rain. They are holding colourful umbrellas, and one of them is laughing. On the left, there is a shop with its lights on. In the background, a bus is passing, and the road is wet and shiny. It looks like autumn, because the trees have yellow leaves. They seem to be happy, even though the weather is bad. I think they are going to school together. I would not like to be outside in this weather, but the photo looks very cheerful.","This picture shows a rainy street in a city. There is a boy with a yellow raincoat who is jumping into a puddle. His mother is standing behind him with a big black umbrella, and she looks surprised. On the right there is a café, and people are sitting inside with hot drinks. It looks like a cold day, but the boy seems very happy. I think children often enjoy rain more than adults. Personally, I prefer sunny days, but rain also has a nice atmosphere in the city."]]],"img":[["☔👧👧🚌🍂🌧️","Yağmurlu bir sokakta iki kız renkli şemsiyelerle yürüyor, biri gülüyor. Solda ışıkları yanık bir dükkân, arkada geçen bir otobüs ve sarı yapraklı ağaçlar var.","b1-rain","Two girls are walking in the rain with colourful umbrellas. A bus is passing behind them and the trees have yellow leaves."]]}]},"B2":{"R":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","id":"b2r1","t":"Why Volunteer?","ty":"cloze","title":"Why volunteer?","p":"Volunteering is often seen as something people do purely to help others, but research shows that volunteers (1) ______ a great deal themselves. Regular volunteers tend to report higher (2) ______ of happiness and lower stress. Volunteering also gives people the chance to (3) ______ new skills, which can be useful when looking for a job. Many employers (4) ______ great importance on this kind of experience. Of course, not everyone has much spare time. (5) ______, even a few hours a month can make a difference. The important thing is to choose an activity that (6) ______ your interests, so that you are more likely to stick (7) ______ it. Some organisations are happy to (8) ______ volunteers for a single day.","o":[[["win","gain","earn","reach"],1,["collocation"]],[["levels","grades","marks","amounts"],0,["collocation"]],[["catch","acquire","reach","achieve"],1,["vocab"]],[["put","give","place","set"],2,["collocation"]],[["Therefore","Moreover","Otherwise","However"],3,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],[["joins","equals","matches","agrees"],2,["vocab"]],[["on","with","at","to"],1,["collocation"]],[["take on","take up","take over","take in"],0,["vocab"]]],"e":["gain from: kazanç sağlamak (faydalanmak).","levels of happiness: mutluluk düzeyi.","acquire skills: beceri edinmek.","place importance on: önem vermek.","However: zıtlık; boş zamanı yok → yine de.","match your interests: ilgi alanlarına uymak.","stick with it: devam etmek, bırakmamak.","take on volunteers: gönüllü almak (işe almak anlamında)."]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 2","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","id":"b2r2","t":"Teenagers and Sleep","ty":"open","one":true,"title":"Teenagers and sleep","p":"Parents often complain that their teenage children stay up late and are impossible to wake (1) ______ in the morning. But according to scientists, this is not simply a matter (2) ______ laziness. During adolescence, the body clock shifts, (3) ______ means that teenagers naturally feel sleepy later at night. If they have (4) ______ get up early for school, they may get far (5) ______ sleep than they need. Some schools have responded by starting lessons later, and the results have been impressive. Students' grades have improved, and (6) ______ have their attendance and mood. (7) ______ some people argue that later start times cause problems for families, many experts believe the benefits are too important (8) ______ ignore.","a":[["up"],["of"],["which"],["to"],["less"],["so"],["although","though","while","whilst"],["to"]],"tg":[["vocab"],["vocab"],["relative-clauses"],["must-have-to-should"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["vocab"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["gerund-infinitive"]],"e":["wake up: uyanmak / uyandırmak.","a matter of: meselesi.","Tüm cümleye gönderme yapan which.","have to: zorunda olmak.","far less ... than: çok daha az.","\"and so have ...\" : -ları da (öyle).","Although: zıtlık bağlacı + cümle.","too ... to + fiil."]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Kelime türetme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Metni oku. Her boşluk için verilen kök kelimeyi uygun biçime getir (isim, sıfat, zarf, olumsuz vb.).","id":"b2r3","t":"Working from Home","ty":"open","title":"Working from home","st":["INCREASE","FLEXIBLE","EMPLOY","PRODUCE","COMMUNICATE","LONELY","EXPECT","HAPPY"],"p":"An (1) ______ number of people now work from home at least part of the week. For many, the (2) ______ to organise their own time is the main attraction. (3) ______ have also discovered benefits: some studies suggest that remote workers are more (4) ______ than their office-based colleagues. However, working from home is not without its problems. Poor (5) ______ between team members can lead to misunderstandings, and some people suffer from (6) ______ because they miss the social side of office life. It is also (7) ______ difficult for some people to separate work from home life. Those who fail to do so often report (8) ______ with their work-life balance.","a":[["increasing"],["flexibility"],["employers"],["productive"],["communication"],["loneliness"],["unexpectedly"],["unhappiness"]],"tg":[["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"]],"e":["an + sıfat + number: increasing (artan).","the + isim: flexibility (esneklik).","Fiil \"have discovered\" öznesi; anlam: işverenler (çoğul).","more + sıfat: productive.","Poor + isim: communication.","suffer from + isim: loneliness (yalnızlık).","Sıfatı niteleyen zarf, anlam \"beklenmedik şekilde\": unexpectedly.","report + isim; olumsuz anlam: unhappiness."]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 2–5 kelime yaz.","id":"b2r4","t":"Key Word Transformations","ty":"kwt","q":[{"s1":"I haven't been to the cinema for months.","key":"SINCE","s2":"It's been months ______ to the cinema.","a":["since I last went","since I went","since I have been","since I've been"],"tg":["present-perfect"],"e":"It's been months since + geçmiş zaman."},{"s1":"\"Don't forget to lock the door,\" Mum told me.","key":"REMINDED","s2":"Mum ______ the door.","a":["reminded me to lock"],"tg":["reporting-verbs"],"e":"remind someone to do something."},{"s1":"The concert was cancelled because of the storm.","key":"CALLED","s2":"They ______ because of the storm.","a":["called off the concert","called the concert off"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"call off = cancel."},{"s1":"It was such a boring film that I fell asleep.","key":"SO","s2":"The film ______ I fell asleep.","a":["was so boring that","was so boring"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"so + sıfat + that."},{"s1":"I regret not taking that job.","key":"WISH","s2":"I ______ that job.","a":["wish I had taken","wish I'd taken"],"tg":["wish-if-only"],"e":"Geçmişe pişmanlık: wish + had + V3."},{"s1":"Someone stole my bike last night.","key":"HAD","s2":"I ______ last night.","a":["had my bike stolen"],"tg":["causative"],"e":"have something done (başına gelen olay): had my bike stolen."}]},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 6","ins":"Metinden altı cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–G) seç. Bir cümle fazladır.","id":"b2r6","t":"A Year in Antarctica","ty":"gap","title":"A year in Antarctica","p":"When I accepted a job as a research assistant at a base in Antarctica, my friends thought I was mad. (1) ______ I had never even seen snow until I was twelve.\nThe first thing that struck me was the silence. (2) ______ After a few weeks, though, I started to find it peaceful rather than frightening.\nLife at the base was highly organised. (3) ______ This was necessary because in such an extreme environment, a single careless mistake could be dangerous.\nThe hardest period was the winter, when the sun did not rise for months. (4) ______ To stay positive, we organised film nights, cooking competitions and even a small music festival.\nI had expected to feel isolated from the rest of the world. (5) ______ We had internet access for a few hours a day, and I spoke to my family every week.\nWhen I finally returned home, I found ordinary life strangely overwhelming. (6) ______ It took me months to get used to it again.","opts":[["A","In fact, I felt more connected than I had imagined."],["B","Everyone had a list of duties, from cleaning to checking equipment."],["C","There was no traffic, no birds, not even the sound of wind some days."],["D","I could understand why: I had grown up in a hot country."],["E","The noise and crowds in the city seemed unbearable."],["F","The temperature rarely rose above minus twenty, even in the summer months."],["G","Many of us found it hard to sleep and felt low."],["H","Most of my colleagues had worked in similar places for several years."]],"a":["D","C","B","G","A","E"],"tg":[["reference"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["reference"]],"e":["Arkadaşları deli sandı → \"I could understand why\" + 12 yaşına kadar kar görmemiş: D.","\"silence\" → trafik yok, kuş yok: C.","\"highly organised\" → herkesin görev listesi: B.","Güneş doğmadı → uyuyamadık, moral bozuk; sonra \"To stay positive\": G.","\"expected to feel isolated\" → \"In fact, I felt more connected\": A.","\"ordinary life overwhelming\" → şehirdeki gürültü ve kalabalık: E. F fazladır."]},{"k":"R4","id":"b2r4b","t":"Key Word Transformations 2","ty":"kwt","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 2–5 kelime yaz.","title":"Key word transformations 2","q":[{"s1":"I haven't seen her since 2019.","key":"LAST","s2":"The ______ her was in 2019.","a":["last time i saw"],"tg":["present-perfect"],"e":"The last time + özne + V2: en son ... -tiğim zaman.","mx":5},{"s1":"\"Don't forget to lock the door,\" Mum said to me.","key":"REMINDED","s2":"Mum ______ the door.","a":["reminded me to lock"],"tg":["reporting-verbs"],"e":"remind + kişi + to + yalın fiil.","mx":5},{"s1":"Unless you hurry, you'll miss the bus.","key":"IF","s2":"You'll miss the bus ______ hurry.","a":["if you don't"],"tg":["first-conditional"],"e":"unless = if ... not.","mx":5},{"s1":"It isn't necessary for you to wait for me.","key":"NEED","s2":"You ______ for me.","a":["don't need to wait","needn't wait","do not need to wait"],"tg":["can-cant"],"e":"don't need to / needn't + yalın fiil: gerek yok.","mx":5},{"s1":"They are repairing our street at the moment.","key":"BEING","s2":"Our street ______ repaired at the moment.","a":["is being"],"tg":["passive-advanced"],"e":"Present Continuous pasif: is being + V3.","mx":5},{"s1":"I'd prefer to stay at home tonight than go out.","key":"RATHER","s2":"I ______ at home tonight than go out.","a":["would rather stay","'d rather stay"],"tg":["wish-if-only"],"e":"would rather + yalın fiil + than + yalın fiil.","mx":5},{"s1":"She is too young to drive a car.","key":"ENOUGH","s2":"She isn't ______ drive a car.","a":["old enough to"],"tg":["so-such-too-enough"],"e":"enough sıfattan sonra gelir: old enough to.","mx":5},{"s1":"Although the film was long, we enjoyed it.","key":"DESPITE","s2":"We enjoyed the film ______ so long.","a":["despite it being","despite its being","despite the fact it was","despite the fact that it was"],"tg":["linkers-contrast-purpose"],"e":"despite + isim/-ing ya da despite the fact that + cümle.","mx":6}]},{"k":"R4","id":"b2r4c","t":"Key Word Transformations 3","ty":"kwt","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 2–5 kelime yaz.","title":"Key word transformations 3","q":[{"s1":"I started learning Spanish three years ago.","key":"BEEN","s2":"I ______ Spanish for three years.","a":["have been learning"],"tg":["present-perfect-continuous"],"e":"for three years: süren eylem, have been + -ing.","mx":5},{"s1":"People say he is a millionaire.","key":"SAID","s2":"He ______ a millionaire.","a":["is said to be"],"tg":["passive-advanced"],"e":"is said to + fiil.","mx":5},{"s1":"Someone broke into our house last night.","key":"BROKEN","s2":"Our house ______ into last night.","a":["was broken"],"tg":["passive-voice"],"e":"Geçmiş pasif: was broken into.","mx":5},{"s1":"I regret not studying harder.","key":"WISH","s2":"I ______ harder.","a":["wish i had studied","wish i'd studied"],"tg":["wish-if-only"],"e":"Geçmiş pişmanlık: wish + had + V3.","mx":5},{"s1":"You should have asked me first.","key":"OUGHT","s2":"You ______ me first.","a":["ought to have asked"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"ought to have + V3: geçmiş eleştiri.","mx":5},{"s1":"Lena doesn't earn as much as her sister does.","key":"MORE","s2":"Lena's sister ______ Lena.","a":["earns more than"],"tg":["comparatives-superlatives"],"e":"not as much as = daha fazla kazanır: earns more than.","mx":5},{"s1":"\"I'll call you tomorrow,\" she said.","key":"PROMISED","s2":"She ______ me the next day.","a":["promised to call","promised to phone","promised to ring"],"tg":["reporting-verbs"],"e":"promise + to + yalın fiil.","mx":5},{"s1":"I find it hard to get up early.","key":"USED","s2":"I'm not ______ up early.","a":["used to getting"],"tg":["used-to"],"e":"be used to + -ing: alışkın olmak.","mx":5}]},{"k":"R1","id":"b2r1b","t":"The Umbrella","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B ya da C) seç.","title":"The umbrella","p":"Umbrellas are such a (1) ______ sight today that it is hard to imagine a time without them. Yet for centuries they were used mainly to (2) ______ people from the sun, not the rain. In ancient China, rain umbrellas were made by (3) ______ paper with wax. In Europe, men refused to carry them for a long time, (4) ______ they were considered a feminine accessory. The situation changed in the eighteenth century, when a traveller named Jonas Hanway began to use one in London, and people (5) ______ fun of him. Eventually the idea (6) ______ on. Modern designs can (7) ______ up to strong winds, although most of us have seen one turn inside out. In (8) ______ of its simple design, the umbrella remains a remarkable invention.","o":[[["common","frequent","regular"],0,["collocation"]],[["protect","defend","save"],0,["collocation"]],[["treating","dressing","washing"],0,["vocab"]],[["although","since","unless"],1,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],[["made","had","took"],0,["collocation"]],[["caught","held","kept"],0,["vocab"]],[["stand","stay","run"],0,["vocab"]],[["spite","view","case"],0,["collocation"]]],"e":["such a common sight: çok yaygın bir görüntü.","protect someone from: -den korumak.","treat paper with wax: kâğıdı mumla işlemek.","since = çünkü (neden). although zıtlık, unless koşul bildirir.","make fun of: alay etmek.","catch on: tutmak, yaygınlaşmak.","stand up to: -e dayanmak.","in spite of: -e rağmen."]},{"k":"R2","id":"b2r2b","t":"Libraries of Things","ty":"open","nm":"Okuma 2 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 2","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","title":"Libraries of things","one":true,"p":"A growing number of towns are opening \"libraries of things\", where members can borrow tools, kitchen equipment and even musical instruments (1) ______ of buying them. The idea is simple: most of us use a drill only a few minutes a year, so there is little point in everyone owning (2) ______. Members pay a small fee, and (3) ______ they join, they can borrow as many items as they like. Organisers say the scheme has been (4) ______ success, not only because it saves money but also because it brings neighbours together. One volunteer told me she had never spoken to anyone on her street until she (5) ______ a ladder from the library. (6) ______ the benefits, some people worry that borrowed tools may not be as safe as new ones. To deal with this, every item is checked carefully (7) ______ it is lent out. The scheme's founder hopes that, in the future, (8) ______ town will have its own library of things.","a":[["instead"],["one"],["once","when","after"],["a"],["borrowed"],["despite"],["before"],["every","each"]],"tg":[["gerund-infinitive"],["vocab"],["vocab"],["articles"],["past-simple"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["vocab"],["vocab"]],"e":["instead of + -ing: -mek yerine.","owning one: birer tane sahip olmak (one = a drill).","once they join: üye olur olmaz.","a success: başarı (sayılabilir isim, a).","borrowed: ödünç aldı (past simple).","despite the benefits: yararlarına rağmen.","before it is lent out: ödünç verilmeden önce.","every/each town: her kasaba."]},{"k":"R4","id":"b2r4d","t":"Key Word Transformations 4","ty":"kwt","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 2–5 kelime yaz.","title":"Key word transformations 4","q":[{"s1":"It was a mistake to ignore his advice.","key":"SHOULD","s2":"You ______ his advice.","a":["should not have ignored","shouldn't have ignored"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"Geçmişte yanlış yapılan şey: should not have + V3.","mx":5},{"s1":"We have very little time left.","key":"HARDLY","s2":"We have ______ time left.","a":["hardly any"],"tg":["quantifiers"],"e":"hardly any: neredeyse hiç.","mx":5},{"s1":"Someone has stolen my bike.","key":"HAD","s2":"I ______ stolen.","a":["had my bike"],"tg":["causative"],"e":"have + nesne + V3: başıma gelen olay.","mx":5},{"s1":"She started working here two years ago.","key":"BEEN","s2":"She ______ here for two years.","a":["has been working"],"tg":["present-perfect"],"e":"for two years: has been working.","mx":5},{"s1":"It is possible that he missed the train.","key":"MIGHT","s2":"He ______ the train.","a":["might have missed"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"might have + V3: geçmiş olasılık.","mx":5},{"s1":"I wish I had more free time.","key":"ONLY","s2":"If ______ more free time.","a":["only i had"],"tg":["wish-if-only"],"e":"If only + Past Simple: keşke.","mx":5}]},{"k":"R4","id":"b2r4e","t":"Key Word Transformations 5","ty":"kwt","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 2–5 kelime yaz.","title":"Key word transformations 5","q":[{"s1":"Learning a language needs patience.","key":"TAKES","s2":"It ______ to learn a language.","a":["takes patience"],"tg":["it-structures"],"e":"It takes + isim + to-inf.","mx":5},{"s1":"Crying will not help you.","key":"USE","s2":"It ______ crying.","a":["is no use","'s no use"],"tg":["it-structures"],"e":"It's no use + -ing.","mx":5},{"s1":"Visiting the old town is a good idea.","key":"WORTH","s2":"The old town ______ visiting.","a":["is worth"],"tg":["it-structures"],"e":"be worth + -ing.","mx":5},{"s1":"You were kind to lend me your car.","key":"KIND","s2":"It was ______ to lend me your car.","a":["kind of you"],"tg":["it-structures"],"e":"Kişi niteliği: kind of you.","mx":5},{"s1":"Is it easy for you to get up early?","key":"FIND","s2":"Do ______ to get up early?","a":["you find it easy"],"tg":["it-structures"],"e":"find it + adj + to-inf.","mx":5},{"s1":"He seems to be tired.","key":"SEEMS","s2":"It ______ he is tired.","a":["seems that"],"tg":["it-structures"],"e":"It seems that + cümle.","mx":5}]},{"k":"R5","id":"b2r5a","t":"Learning a Language Later in Life","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Learning a language later in life","p":["When I turned forty, I decided to learn Portuguese. My partner's family comes from Lisbon, and after years of nodding politely at dinner parties, I felt it was time to understand what was actually being said. I assumed that, as an adult with a good education, I would make quick progress. I could not have been more wrong.","The first few months were humbling. Children, it seems, absorb a language almost without effort, whereas I had to struggle with every verb ending. My teacher, a patient woman called Inês, explained that adults learn differently: we are better at understanding rules, but worse at copying sounds. \"Stop translating in your head,\" she kept telling me. \"Just listen.\"","That advice changed everything. I started listening to radio programmes while I cooked, and I stuck notes on everything in the flat. At first, the words slid past me like a river, but gradually I began to catch individual phrases. The turning point came when I understood a joke told by my partner's grandfather, and the whole table laughed with me instead of at me.","I am still far from fluent, and I doubt that I ever will be. But I no longer see mistakes as failures. They are, in fact, the proof that I am trying. If there is one lesson I would pass on to other late learners, it is this: do not wait until you feel ready. You will never feel ready, and the language is waiting for you anyway."],"q":[{"q":"What does the writer say about his expectations at the beginning?","o":["He thought learning would be easy for an educated adult.","He was afraid that his partner's family would laugh at him.","He expected to need many years before speaking.","He had learnt other languages quickly before."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I assumed ... I would make quick progress. I could not have been more wrong.\""},{"q":"According to Inês, why do adults find language learning difficult?","o":["They are not interested in grammar.","They are less able to imitate sounds.","They have too little time to practise.","They are afraid of making mistakes."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yetişkinler kuralları daha iyi anlar ama sesleri taklitte daha zayıftır."},{"q":"What does \"the words slid past me like a river\" suggest?","o":["He found the vocabulary beautiful.","He could not separate the words he heard.","He was listening to the radio too loudly.","He felt calm while listening."],"a":1,"tg":["vocab","inference"],"e":"Kelimeler akıp gidiyordu; tek tek ayırt edemiyordu."},{"q":"What was the turning point for the writer?","o":["Passing a language exam","Understanding a joke at the family table","Moving to Lisbon","Reading a book in Portuguese"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Büyükbabanın şakasını anlayıp masayla birlikte güldü."},{"q":"What is the writer's attitude to mistakes now?","o":["He tries to avoid them at all costs.","He thinks they show how little he knows.","He accepts them as a sign of effort.","He believes teachers should correct them immediately."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"They are the proof that I am trying.\""}]},{"k":"R5","id":"b2r5b","t":"The Future of the High Street","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The future of the high street","p":["Walk through almost any town centre today and you will see the same pattern: empty shops with \"to let\" signs in the windows, next to a handful of coffee chains and phone stores. Many commentators blame online shopping, and there is no doubt that sales on the internet have increased dramatically. However, the real picture is more complicated than the headlines suggest.","In some places, the high street is flourishing. In a market town in the north of England, the council gave cheap rents to small independent businesses on the condition that they offered something local. Within three years, a bakery, a bookshop and a repair café had opened, and the number of visitors on Saturdays had doubled. \"People don't come to the high street just to buy things any more,\" says the town's manager, \"they come for the experience.\"","Yet not every initiative has succeeded. Another town spent a large sum on new paving and flower baskets, hoping that a prettier centre would attract shoppers. It did not. The car park was still expensive, the buses were infrequent, and the rents remained too high for small traders. As one shopkeeper put it, \"You can't eat flowers.\"","The lesson seems to be that high streets survive when they offer what the internet cannot: personal service, a sense of community and places to meet. Rather than competing with online giants, successful towns are learning to complement them."],"q":[{"q":"What is the writer's main point in the first paragraph?","o":["Online shopping is the only cause of the problem.","The situation is not as simple as many people say.","Coffee chains are better than independent shops.","Town centres will soon disappear completely."],"a":1,"tg":["gist"],"e":"\"the real picture is more complicated than the headlines suggest\""},{"q":"What condition did the northern council set for cheap rents?","o":["The shops had to open on Sundays.","The businesses had to be run by local people.","The businesses had to offer something local.","The owners had to repair old buildings."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"on the condition that they offered something local\""},{"q":"Why did the second town's plan fail?","o":["The flowers died quickly.","Basic problems such as parking and rents remained.","Too many shops closed in the first year.","Shoppers preferred the old paving."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","inference"],"e":"Otopark pahalı, otobüsler seyrek, kiralar yüksek kaldı."},{"q":"What does the shopkeeper mean by \"You can't eat flowers\"?","o":["Decoration does not solve real economic needs.","Flower shops are too expensive.","Food is more important than shopping.","Customers do not like flowers."],"a":0,"tg":["vocab","inference"],"e":"Süs, esnafın gerçek sorunlarını çözmez."},{"q":"What does the writer conclude?","o":["High streets should copy online shops.","Successful towns offer things the internet cannot.","Councils should stop spending money.","Personal service is no longer important."],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Son paragraf: internetin veremediğini sunan caddeler hayatta kalıyor."}]},{"k":"R6","id":"b2r6a","t":"A Revolution in the School Canteen","ty":"gap","nm":"Okuma 6 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 6","ins":"Metinden altı cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–G) seç. Bir cümle fazladır.","title":"A revolution in the school canteen","p":"When Chef Marta Silva took over the kitchen at a large secondary school, she faced a difficult challenge. Most students were choosing chips and sugary drinks, and a lot of the healthy food was ending up in the bin. (1) ______\nHer first step was to talk to the students. She asked them what they liked and what they found boring. (2) ______ She used their ideas to redesign the menu, with names such as \"Super Power Bowl\" instead of \"vegetable salad\".\nThe second change was practical. Queues had always been long, so many students gave up and bought snacks from the shop. (3) ______ Now they can order on their phones and collect their meals in seconds.\nThe results surprised everyone. Within a year, the amount of waste had fallen by half. (4) ______ Several teachers even reported that afternoon lessons had become calmer.\nNot everything has been easy. (5) ______ Marta, however, remains hopeful: \"Change takes time, but taste is something you can teach.\" Other schools are now asking her for advice. (6) ______","opts":[["A","Some parents complained that the new meals were more expensive."],["B","She has already agreed to visit three of them next month."],["C","To solve this, she introduced a simple ordering system."],["D","Many of them said that the food looked unattractive."],["E","This was not just a problem of taste, but of habits."],["F","Students also seemed more energetic in the afternoons."],["G","The canteen had been built in the 1960s."]],"a":["E","D","C","F","A","B"],"tg":[["coherence"],["reference","coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"]],"e":["\"This was not just a problem of taste\" önceki sorunları özetler (E).","\"Many of them\" = students; yiyecekler çekici görünmüyor (D).","Uzun kuyruklar sorun; \"To solve this\" ile çözüm (C).","Atık azaldı; öğrenciler öğleden sonra daha enerjik (F).","\"Not everything has been easy\": pahalı olduğuna dair şikâyet (A).","Diğer okullar tavsiye istiyor; \"them\" = okullar (B)."]},{"k":"R1","id":"b2r1c","t":"Silent Discos","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","title":"Silent discos","p":"Silent discos, where people dance to music through wireless headphones, have become (1) ______ popular at festivals and student parties. The idea is simple: instead of loudspeakers, every guest (2) ______ a pair of headphones with two or three channels, and each channel plays a different style of music. The dance floor is quiet to anyone who isn't wearing a pair, which makes it (3) ______ funny to watch.\nOne major advantage is that the event does not (4) ______ the neighbours. In cities with strict noise rules, organisers can therefore hold parties in parks and squares late at night. Another benefit is that people can (5) ______ the volume to suit themselves, so those who prefer a quieter evening can still join in.\nCritics, (6) ______, say that wearing headphones separates people from one another. They argue that dancing is a shared experience, and that you lose the atmosphere if everybody listens to something different. Nevertheless, (7) ______ the number of silent discos has grown steadily, it seems that most people (8) ______ the idea.","o":[[["increasingly","extremely","equally","highly"],0,["collocation"]],[["is given","gives","has given","is giving"],0,["passive-voice"]],[["particularly","entirely","absolutely","simply"],0,["collocation"]],[["disturb","interrupt","prevent","damage"],0,["collocation"]],[["adjust","adapt","arrange","repair"],0,["collocation"]],[["however","therefore","meanwhile","otherwise"],0,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],[["since","unless","whereas","although"],0,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],[["welcome","accept","enjoy","approve"],2,["collocation"]]],"e":["become increasingly popular: gitgide yaygınlaşmak.","every guest is given headphones: pasif.","particularly funny: özellikle komik.","disturb the neighbours: komşuları rahatsız etmek.","adjust the volume: sesi ayarlamak.","Critics, however, say...: ama eleştirmenler ...","since the number has grown: ... -den beri artıyor.","welcome the idea: fikri memnuniyetle karşılamak (enjoy the idea doğal bir kalıp değil)."]},{"k":"R3","id":"b2r3a","t":"The Power of Taking Breaks","ty":"open","nm":"Okuma 3 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","title":"The power of taking breaks","one":true,"p":"Many people believe that working without a break is the best way to get things done. In fact, research suggests the opposite. When we concentrate (1) ______ a long time, our attention starts to fall, and we make more mistakes. A short break every hour can help us (2) ______ fresh and productive.\nOne study followed office workers for a month. Those (3) ______ took regular breaks were not only happier but also finished their tasks faster. It seems that even a five-minute walk can make a big difference, as long (4) ______ you leave your phone behind.\nUnfortunately, many employees feel guilty when they stop working. They are afraid that colleagues will think they (5) ______ lazy. Yet companies (6) ______ encourage breaks often discover that their staff become more creative. If you have never tried it, (7) ______ not start tomorrow? You may be surprised (8) ______ how much better you feel.","a":[["for"],["stay","remain","keep","feel","be"],["who","that"],["as"],["are","were"],["that","which","who"],["why"],["at","by"]],"tg":[["prepositions-in-on-at"],["gerund-infinitive"],["relative-clauses"],["first-conditional"],["present-simple"],["relative-clauses"],["question-words"],["prepositions-in-on-at"]],"e":["concentrate for a long time: uzun süre.","help us stay fresh: bize ... kalmada yardım eder.","Those who took breaks: ... alanlar.","as long as: -dığı sürece.","think they are lazy: tembel olduklarını düşünürler.","companies that encourage breaks: ... teşvik eden şirketler.","why not start tomorrow?: neden yarın başlamıyorsun?","surprised at/by: şaşırmak."]},{"k":"R5","id":"b2r5c","t":"Is the Four-Day Week the Future?","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Is the four-day week the future?","p":["For most of the last century, the working week has followed the same pattern: five days at the office, two days off. Recently, however, a growing number of companies have begun to question whether this arrangement still makes sense. In several trials across Europe, employees were asked to work four days a week for the same pay, and the results were surprising.","The most common worry was that productivity would suffer. In practice, the opposite often happened. Workers who knew they had a long weekend ahead became more focused, held shorter meetings and avoided wasting time online. One manager at a software firm admitted that she had expected chaos. \"Instead,\" she said, \"people simply stopped doing the things that were never useful in the first place.\"","Employees also reported that they felt healthier and less stressed. Many used the extra day to see family, exercise or study, and fewer of them called in sick. Companies noticed another benefit: it became much easier to recruit talented staff, because a shorter week was seen as a valuable perk.","Even so, the idea is not suitable for every business. Hospitals, shops and restaurants cannot simply close for an extra day, and some workers ended up squeezing five days of work into four, which left them exhausted. Experts therefore advise that each organisation should design its own version rather than copy a successful example from elsewhere."],"q":[{"q":"What does the writer say about trials of the four-day week?","o":["They proved that the idea does not work.","They produced unexpected results.","They were limited to one country.","They were criticised by employees."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the results were surprising\"."},{"q":"Why did productivity often improve?","o":["Workers were paid more.","Meetings were abolished.","People concentrated better and wasted less time.","Managers watched the staff more closely."],"a":2,"tg":["detail","inference"],"e":"Daha odaklı oldular, toplantılar kısaldı, zaman kaybı azaldı."},{"q":"What did the software manager mean by \"people simply stopped doing the things that were never useful\"?","o":["Staff refused to follow instructions.","Pointless activities disappeared.","The company lost important clients.","Workers became less friendly."],"a":1,"tg":["vocab","inference"],"e":"Işe yaramayan işler bırakıldı."},{"q":"What extra advantage did companies notice?","o":["Lower electricity bills","Easier recruitment of good employees","More customers on Fridays","Better office equipment"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"easier to recruit talented staff\"."},{"q":"What is the writer's conclusion?","o":["All businesses should adopt the four-day week immediately.","The idea works only in the technology sector.","Organisations should adapt the idea to their own situation.","The five-day week is still the best system."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Her kurum kendi versiyonunu tasarlamalı."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"b2r5d","t":"The Sounds of the City","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The sounds of the city","p":["When I moved from a quiet village to a big city, the first thing I noticed was the noise. Traffic roared day and night, neighbours argued through thin walls, and a building site next door started work at seven every morning. For weeks I slept with earplugs and dreamed of the silence I had left behind.","Then something unexpected happened. A friend, a sound artist, invited me on what she called a \"listening walk\". We spent an hour moving slowly through the streets without speaking, paying attention only to what we could hear. At first, it was just a blur. After a while, though, I began to separate individual sounds: the rhythm of footsteps, a street musician practising scales, the soft hum of a distant train.","My friend explained that most of us have learnt to \"switch off\" from everyday sounds in order to concentrate, and that this habit, while useful, makes us miss a great deal. Research suggests that people who regularly listen carefully to their surroundings feel calmer, because they no longer treat noise as an enemy.","I would not pretend that I now love the building site. But the city no longer feels hostile. On my morning walk, I find myself listening for the cheerful whistle of the baker, and I have even stopped using my earplugs."],"q":[{"q":"How did the writer feel about the city at the beginning?","o":["He found it exciting.","He was disturbed by the noise.","He quickly got used to it.","He enjoyed the sound of the building site."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Kulak tıkacıyla uyudu; sessizliği özledi."},{"q":"What was the purpose of the \"listening walk\"?","o":["To find a quieter street","To record sounds for a project","To pay attention to the sounds around them","To practise a musical instrument"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yalnızca duyduklarına dikkat ettiler."},{"q":"What does \"switch off\" mean in paragraph 3?","o":["turn off electrical equipment","stop noticing something","go to sleep early","leave a place quickly"],"a":1,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Günlük seslere kulağı kapatmak."},{"q":"According to research, why do careful listeners feel calmer?","o":["They avoid noisy places.","They treat noise as an enemy.","They do not see noise as a threat.","They listen to music more often."],"a":2,"tg":["detail","inference"],"e":"Gürültüyü artık düşman görmüyorlar."},{"q":"How does the writer feel at the end?","o":["He has changed his attitude, but not completely.","He now loves every noise in the city.","He plans to return to the village.","He wants to become a sound artist."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I would not pretend that I now love the building site.\""}]},{"k":"R6","id":"b2r6b","t":"A Forest in the City","ty":"gap","nm":"Okuma 6 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 6","ins":"Metinden altı cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–G) seç. Bir cümle fazladır.","title":"A forest in the city","p":"In the middle of a busy industrial district, a small forest has appeared on a piece of land that was once a car park. (1) ______ It was planted by volunteers using a method that creates dense woodland in a very small space.\nThe idea came from a Japanese botanist, who noticed that natural forests grow quickly when many local species are planted close together. (2) ______ Within three years, the trees were taller than the volunteers who had planted them.\nThe benefits are already visible. The temperature on the site is several degrees lower than in the surrounding streets. (3) ______ Birds, insects and even a family of hedgehogs have moved in.\nResidents have welcomed the project. Children from the local school visit every week to measure the trees and to record the animals they find. (4) ______ \"I used to walk past this place with my eyes on my phone,\" says one neighbour. \"Now I slow down and listen.\"\nOf course, there are limits to what a small wood can achieve. (5) ______ Even so, the organisers hope that other cities will follow their example. (6) ______","opts":[["A","It cannot replace the large forests that have been lost."],["B","They have already received requests for advice from five countries."],["C","The site is no larger than a tennis court."],["D","Wildlife has also benefited."],["E","The trees compete for light, so they grow upwards very fast."],["F","Many of the volunteers had never planted a tree before."],["G","Several shops in the district have closed."]],"a":["C","E","D","F","A","B"],"tg":[["coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["reference","coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"]],"e":["Orman çok küçük: tenis kortundan büyük değil (C).","Ağaçlar ışık için yarışır, bu yüzden hızlı büyür; ardından \"Within three years\" (E).","Sıcaklık düştü; vahşi yaşam da yararlandı (D).","Çocuklar ve gönüllüler; \"many of the volunteers\" bağlamı (F).","\"Of course, there are limits\": küçük orman büyük ormanların yerini tutmaz (A).","Diğer şehirlerin örnek alması; beş ülkeden tavsiye talebi (B)."]},{"k":"R1","id":"b2r1d","t":"Beekeeping on City Roofs","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","title":"Beekeeping on city roofs","p":"Keeping bees on the roof of an office block may sound (1) ______, but urban beekeeping is becoming more and more common. In fact, city bees often (2) ______ better than their country cousins, because gardens, parks and balconies provide a wide variety of flowers throughout the year.\nThe first (3) ______ to remember is that bees are not aggressive if they are treated with respect. Beekeepers wear protective clothing and use smoke to keep the insects calm when they open the hive. Most companies that (4) ______ hives report that their employees quickly become fond of them.\nHoney from a city roof has its own special taste, (5) ______ on the plants that grow nearby. Some producers even sell it under the name of the district it comes from. However, experts (6) ______ out that bees alone cannot save the environment. Cities also need to protect wild insects, (7) ______ they too are essential for pollination. Nevertheless, as long as people are willing to learn, the number of rooftop hives is (8) ______ to keep growing.","o":[[["unusual","rare","single","particular"],0,["collocation"]],[["do","make","work","get"],0,["collocation"]],[["thing","matter","point","item"],0,["collocation"]],[["install","establish","instal","settle"],0,["collocation"]],[["depending","relying","based","resting"],0,["prepositions-in-on-at"]],[["point","tell","show","explain"],0,["collocation"]],[["since","whereas","unless","although"],0,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],[["likely","sure","possible","probable"],0,["collocation"]]],"e":["sound unusual: alışılmadık gelmek.","do well: iyi gelişmek (bees do better).","The first thing to remember: akılda tutulacak ilk şey.","install hives: kovan kurmak.","depending on: -e bağlı olarak (taste depending on the plants).","point out: belirtmek, işaret etmek.","since: çünkü (yabani böcekler de gerekli).","be likely to: -mesi muhtemel."]},{"k":"R3","id":"b2r3b","t":"Why Do We Put Things Off?","ty":"open","nm":"Okuma 3 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","title":"Why do we put things off?","one":true,"p":"Almost everybody has, at some time, left an important task until the last moment. Psychologists call this habit procrastination, and it is far (1) ______ common than most people admit. Contrary to popular belief, it is not simply a sign of laziness. In fact, many people who put things off are hard-working individuals who worry (2) ______ doing the job badly.\nResearchers have found that we tend to prefer a pleasant activity now (3) ______ a better reward later. Checking your phone feels good immediately, (4) ______ finishing a report does not. The result is that unpleasant tasks keep moving to tomorrow, and then to the day after.\nSo how can we break the habit? One simple trick is to divide a big task into small steps. If you tell yourself that you will only work for ten minutes, you are (5) ______ likely to start. Another useful tip is to remove distractions, such as turning (6) ______ notifications. Finally, it helps to forgive yourself. People who criticise themselves harshly (7) ______ end up delaying even more, (8) ______ feeling guilty makes the task seem more frightening.","a":[["more"],["about"],["than","to","over"],["but","while","whereas","yet"],["more"],["off"],["often","usually","tend","sometimes"],["because","since","as"]],"tg":[["comparison-advanced"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["comparison-advanced"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["comparison-advanced"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["present-simple"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],"e":["far more common: çok daha yaygın.","worry about: endişelenmek.","prefer ... to ...: ... yerine ...yi tercih etmek.","but / whereas: karşıtlık.","more likely: daha olası.","turn off: kapatmak.","often end up: çoğu zaman sonunda ... olur.","because: çünkü."]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_remote2","t":"Remote Work: Freedom or Isolation?","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Remote Work: Freedom or Isolation?","p":["Since the pandemic, working from home has become normal for millions of employees around the world. Supporters argue that it saves commuting time, reduces costs and allows people to organise their day more flexibly. A parent can take a child to school and then start work, and an employee who lives far from an expensive city centre can find a good job without moving. Many companies have also noticed that some staff are more productive when they are not constantly interrupted by colleagues in a busy open-plan office.","The environmental argument is also often mentioned. Fewer people travelling to work means less traffic, less pollution and lower fuel consumption. Some firms have even reduced the size of their offices and saved large amounts of rent, which they can invest in training or higher salaries.","However, remote work is not suitable for everyone. Some employees complain that the boundary between work and private life disappears: they answer emails late at night, eat lunch in front of the computer and find it difficult to switch off. Young employees, who have less experience, often miss the chance to learn by watching more senior colleagues, and they sometimes feel invisible when promotions are discussed. Others feel lonely because they miss the casual conversations that happen in the corridor or at the coffee machine. Psychologists warn that long-term isolation can increase anxiety and reduce creativity, as good ideas often appear during informal exchanges.","Managers face challenges too. It is much harder to build a team spirit through a screen, and some bosses do not trust their staff when they cannot see them. This sometimes leads to excessive monitoring, with software that records every click of the mouse, which employees find stressful and insulting. Experts believe that managers should judge people by the results of their work, not by the number of hours they spend online.","As a result, a growing number of organisations are choosing a hybrid model, in which staff spend part of the week in the office and the rest at home. In this system, the office becomes a place for meetings, teamwork and social contact, while quiet, concentrated tasks are done at home. Whether this compromise will last remains to be seen, but it suggests that most people want both independence and human contact."],"q":[{"q":"Which benefit of remote work is mentioned for people who live far from the city centre?","o":["They can negotiate higher salaries.","They can choose to work fewer hours.","They can find a good job without moving.","They can get help with housing costs."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"an employee who lives far from an expensive city centre can find a good job without moving.\""},{"q":"What problem do some employees experience?","o":["Their emails are checked by managers.","Work and private life become mixed.","They are paid less than office workers.","They are invited to too many meetings."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the boundary between work and private life disappears.\""},{"q":"Why can remote work be a problem for young employees?","o":["They find it hard to use new technology.","They miss the chance to learn from seniors.","They are usually paid less than others.","They tend to work far too many hours."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"miss the chance to learn by watching more senior colleagues.\""},{"q":"What do experts say managers should do?","o":["Judge people by the results of their work.","Check what every employee does each hour.","End remote work for most departments.","Hire more staff to share the workload."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"judge people by the results of their work, not by the number of hours.\""},{"q":"What is a \"hybrid model\"?","o":["Working partly in the office and partly at home","Working at home for the whole of the month","Working in the office with fully flexible hours","Working in a different city office each week"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Metinde açıklanmış: \"part of the week in the office and the rest at home.\""},{"q":"What does the writer suggest at the end?","o":["Remote work will probably disappear within years.","Companies should close their city offices.","Employees dislike any change in routine.","Most people want both independence and contact."],"a":3,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Son cümle: \"most people want both independence and human contact.\""},{"q":"The word \"casual\" in \"casual conversations\" is closest in meaning to…","o":["formal","private","frequent","informal"],"a":3,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"casual = gündelik, resmî olmayan."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_electric2","t":"The Rise of Electric Cars","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Rise of Electric Cars","p":["Electric cars were once seen as expensive toys for people who cared about the environment. Twenty years ago, they were slow, they could travel only a short distance, and there was almost nowhere to charge them. Today they are becoming a mainstream choice. Prices have fallen, the distance a car can travel on one charge has more than doubled, and more charging stations are appearing in cities, at shopping centres and along motorways.","Drivers who have changed to an electric car often mention practical advantages. The running costs are lower, since electricity is cheaper than petrol in most countries and the engine has far fewer moving parts that can break. The cars are very quiet, and they accelerate quickly because the motor gives full power from the very first moment. In many cities, owners also pay lower taxes or can use special parking places.","The main environmental argument is that electric cars produce no exhaust fumes, so the air in busy cities becomes cleaner and people suffer fewer breathing problems. Critics, however, point out that the electricity itself must come from somewhere. If it is produced by burning coal, the overall benefit is much smaller, because the pollution simply moves from the street to the power station. Producing the batteries also requires large amounts of energy and raw materials such as lithium and cobalt, whose mining can damage landscapes and create difficult working conditions.","Another concern is infrastructure. If millions of people charge their cars in the evening at the same time, the electricity network may not be able to cope. Engineers are therefore developing \"smart charging\", which automatically starts the charge when the demand is low, for example in the middle of the night. Some experiments even use the batteries of parked cars as a storage system that gives energy back to the grid when it is needed.","Despite these concerns, most experts agree that the long-term trend is clear. As more electricity comes from wind and solar power, the advantages of electric vehicles will grow. The remaining challenges are to make charging faster, to reduce the cost of batteries even further and to recycle old batteries safely. If these problems are solved, the petrol car may soon look as old-fashioned as the horse and carriage."],"q":[{"q":"Why are electric cars becoming more popular?","o":["Petrol is now much harder to buy in cities.","Prices have fallen and they can travel further.","They are faster than all other types of car.","Governments have banned petrol cars everywhere."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Prices have fallen, the distance... has more than doubled.\""},{"q":"Which practical advantage is mentioned?","o":["Their running costs are lower.","They almost never need servicing.","They are allowed to use bus lanes.","They are cheaper to buy than petrol cars."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"The running costs are lower.\""},{"q":"What do critics say about electric cars?","o":["The electricity may come from polluting sources.","Their silent engines are dangerous for walkers.","Their batteries wear out after a few years.","Cleaner city air is not worth the cost."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"If it is produced by burning coal, the overall benefit is much smaller.\""},{"q":"What is \"smart charging\"?","o":["Charging only with solar panels at home","Charging at special stations for free","Charging for a short time during the day","Charging automatically when demand is low"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"automatically starts the charge when the demand is low.\""},{"q":"What is true about producing batteries, according to the text?","o":["It is cheap because the parts are simple.","It is done mostly in countries with cheap coal.","It is harmless once the factory is built.","It needs large amounts of energy and materials."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"requires large amounts of energy and raw materials.\""},{"q":"What does the writer think about the future?","o":["Electric cars will probably disappear again.","Nobody can say what will happen next.","The long-term trend is clearly positive.","Petrol cars will soon cost much less."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"the long-term trend is clear.\""}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_procrastinate2","t":"Why Do We Procrastinate?","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Why Do We Procrastinate?","p":["We all know the feeling: an important task is waiting, but instead we tidy our desk, check our phone for the tenth time or suddenly become very interested in cleaning the kitchen. This behaviour is called procrastination, and almost everybody does it. A famous survey suggests that about one in five adults is a chronic procrastinator, and among university students the number is even higher. Many people think it is simply laziness, but psychologists say it has very little to do with that.","Research suggests that people often delay a task not because they are idle, but because the task makes them feel uncomfortable. They may be anxious about doing it badly, bored by its detail or confused about where to begin. Avoiding the task provides a short moment of relief, and this is the problem: the brain learns that avoiding the task makes bad feelings go away, so it repeats the behaviour next time. In other words, procrastination is not a problem of time management, but of managing emotions.","The costs can be serious. People who postpone tasks often work under pressure at the last moment, make more mistakes and sleep badly. They may also feel guilty, which makes them even more reluctant to start the next time. Some studies link chronic procrastination with higher stress, weaker health and lower income, because opportunities are lost when we wait too long.","Fortunately, there are practical ways to break the cycle. Dividing a big project into very small steps makes the starting point less frightening: instead of \"write the report\", the first step can be \"write the title and one sentence\". Another popular method is to work for only twenty-five minutes and then take a five-minute break, which makes the task feel short and manageable. It also helps to remove distractions by putting the phone in another room and by telling a friend what you intend to finish today.","Most importantly, being kind to yourself after a delay works better than harsh self-criticism. In one experiment, students who forgave themselves for delaying their first exam preparation procrastinated less before the next exam than those who blamed themselves. Self-criticism tends to increase stress and lead to further delay, while self-compassion gives us the energy to try again."],"q":[{"q":"According to psychologists, procrastination is mostly connected to…","o":["a lack of basic time-management skills","uncomfortable feelings about the task","having too much free time at home","poor intelligence or a weak memory"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"it has very little to do with laziness\" ve devamı."},{"q":"Why does avoiding a task become a habit?","o":["It gives short-term relief the brain remembers.","It makes the task much easier to do later on.","It improves the way we plan our daily time.","It makes other people do the whole job for us."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"a short moment of relief... the brain learns... repeats the behaviour.\""},{"q":"What is one cost of procrastination mentioned in the text?","o":["Working under pressure and making mistakes","Losing the ability to concentrate for long","Having less time to relax at weekends","Earning less money than colleagues do"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"work under pressure at the last moment, make more mistakes and sleep badly.\""},{"q":"What does the method of \"twenty-five minutes of work and a five-minute break\" try to do?","o":["It helps people to sleep much better at night.","It replaces the need for long holidays.","It removes the need to plan the work in advance.","It makes the task feel short and manageable."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"makes the task feel short and manageable.\""},{"q":"What happened in the experiment with students?","o":["Students who criticised themselves delayed less.","No students changed how they studied.","Most students failed the second exam.","Students who forgave themselves delayed less."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"students who forgave themselves... procrastinated less before the next exam.\""},{"q":"Which method is NOT suggested?","o":["Dividing a project into small steps","Removing distractions from your desk","Waiting until the last day","Being kind to yourself after a delay"],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Son tarihe kadar beklemek önerilmiyor."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_attention3","t":"The Price of Constant Attention","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Price of Constant Attention","p":["Few people today can remember the last time they spent an entire hour on a single task without checking a phone, answering a message, or opening a new browser tab. This is not entirely surprising. Modern devices and applications are designed by teams of engineers whose success is measured by how much of our time they capture. Every sound, every red badge, and every vibration is carefully tested to make us look. What is less well understood is how much this constant switching costs us.","Researchers who study attention have found that a brief interruption does far more damage than its length suggests. In one well-known experiment, office workers who were interrupted by a short message needed, on average, more than twenty minutes to return fully to their original task. Even when the interruption lasted only seconds, the mind had to rebuild its mental picture of the problem, remember where it had stopped, and recover its earlier momentum. People who were interrupted more frequently completed their work faster, but they made more mistakes and reported higher levels of stress.","The problem is not just that we are distracted; it is that we are distracting ourselves. Many interruptions come not from colleagues or family but from our own impulse to check something. Psychologists compare this behaviour to a slot machine: because a new message or a piece of news might be waiting, the habit of checking is rewarded unpredictably, which is exactly the pattern that makes habits difficult to break. After a while, the urge becomes automatic, and people may pick up their phones without even noticing.","The consequences extend beyond productivity. Deep, sustained thinking, the kind needed to write a complicated report, learn a difficult skill, or solve a technical problem, seems to depend on long periods of uninterrupted concentration. If we rarely experience such periods, our ability to produce them may weaken. Some teachers have observed that students find it increasingly hard to read long texts, not because they lack intelligence but because their minds are accustomed to jumping from one short piece of content to another. Critics call this 'shallow' thinking, although the term may be unfair to those who handle large amounts of information with great skill.","Not everyone agrees that the situation is as serious as these warnings suggest. Some scholars point out that every new communication technology, from the printing press to television, was once accused of damaging human intelligence. Moreover, the ability to switch quickly between tasks is valuable in many modern jobs, from emergency medicine to journalism. These sceptics argue that what looks like lost concentration may in fact be an adaptation to a faster environment. The evidence for long-term damage to the brain, they note, is still limited and often based on small studies.","Even so, most researchers accept that individuals can benefit from managing their attention more deliberately. A number of strategies have proved useful. The simplest is to turn off non-essential notifications, so that the device no longer decides when we look at it. Another is to work in blocks of focused time, perhaps forty-five minutes, with the phone out of sight, followed by a short break that is genuinely restful rather than filled with more screens. Some people schedule fixed times for email and social media, treating them as tasks like any other.","Employers have a role to play as well. Companies that expect an immediate reply to every message create an atmosphere in which no one can concentrate. A few organisations have experimented with 'quiet hours', during which meetings and chat messages are not allowed, and they report improvements in both the quality of work and the satisfaction of employees. The key, according to the managers involved, is that the policy comes from the top: if senior staff continue to send messages at all hours, ordinary employees will feel obliged to respond.","Ultimately, attention is a limited resource, and every society decides, usually without realising it, how to spend it. For the first time in history, a handful of private companies have a major influence on where billions of people direct their minds each day. Whether that is a healthy arrangement is a question that individuals, schools, employers, and governments will need to consider seriously. Until then, perhaps the most practical step is also the most modest: to notice, the next time the phone lights up, that we are being asked to give something valuable, and to choose whether to give it."],"q":[{"q":"Why are devices designed to capture our attention, according to the first paragraph?","o":["Users demand products that are hard to put down.","Engineers are judged by how much time they capture.","Governments require companies to keep users online.","Studies show that screen time improves health."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Teams of engineers whose success is measured by how much of our time they capture."},{"q":"What did the experiment about interrupted office workers show?","o":["Workers needed over twenty minutes to refocus.","Short interruptions did not affect their work.","Workers made fewer mistakes after a break.","Workers said they preferred being interrupted."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"They needed on average more than twenty minutes to return fully to their task."},{"q":"Why do psychologists compare checking a phone to a slot machine?","o":["The reward is unpredictable, so the habit grows.","Both can lead to large financial losses.","Both are designed to look bright and colourful.","Both are forbidden for children under sixteen."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"The habit of checking is rewarded unpredictably, which makes habits hard to break."},{"q":"What do the sceptics argue?","o":["Technology is clearly lowering intelligence.","People should give up phones altogether.","Real concentration is no longer possible.","Fast task-switching may be a useful adaptation."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"What looks like lost concentration may be an adaptation; evidence is limited."},{"q":"Which strategy is NOT mentioned?","o":["Turning off non-essential notifications","Working in focused blocks of time","Setting fixed times for checking email","Moving to a different country"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The text lists notifications, focused blocks and fixed email times only."},{"q":"Why must a \"quiet hours\" policy come from the top?","o":["Senior staff are better at managing their time.","It costs less than organising many team meetings.","Staff feel obliged to reply if seniors keep sending.","It is against the law to hold evening meetings."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"If senior staff continue sending messages at all hours, employees will feel obliged to respond."},{"q":"What is the author's final suggestion?","o":["Ban all phones from schools and workplaces.","Accept the problem because it cannot change.","Notice when attention is requested and choose.","Replace old phones with simpler models."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Notice that we are being asked to give something valuable, and choose whether to give it."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_dialect3","t":"Why Languages Disappear","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Why Languages Disappear","p":["Linguists estimate that there are roughly seven thousand languages spoken in the world today, but nearly half of them are endangered. On average, one language falls silent every few weeks, usually when its last fluent speaker dies. Because many of these languages have never been written down, they vanish without leaving a trace. The loss is easy to overlook, since it happens quietly and far from major cities, yet scholars regard it as one of the largest cultural losses of our time.","Languages rarely die because of a single dramatic event. More often, the process is gradual and takes place over three or four generations. In the first generation, adults speak the local language at home but also learn a dominant language in order to work, attend school, or deal with officials. In the second, parents begin to speak the dominant language to their children, believing it will give them better opportunities. By the third generation, the children understand their grandparents but answer in the majority language. The chain of transmission is broken, and within a few decades the language is spoken only by the elderly.","Economic and social pressures play a decisive role. When one language is associated with jobs, education, and modern life, while another is linked to poverty or a rural past, families often make a practical choice. Parents do not necessarily love their own language less; they simply do not want their children to be disadvantaged. In some places, the decision has not been entirely voluntary. Governments have at times banned minority languages in schools or punished children for using them, which created a lasting sense that speaking the old language was something to be ashamed of.","Why does it matter if a language disappears? Linguists give several answers. First, each language contains a unique way of describing the world, including detailed knowledge of local plants, animals, and landscapes that may not exist in any other form. When the speakers of a language of the rainforest are lost, for example, so may be their knowledge of medicinal plants. Second, languages are the basis of identity and heritage, carrying stories, songs, and traditions that give a community its sense of itself. Third, studying the world's diversity helps scientists understand how the human mind organises grammar and meaning.","There are, however, voices that question whether efforts to save languages are always realistic. Some argue that communities have the right to choose a language that serves them, and that outsiders who romanticise small languages may ignore the real needs of their speakers. Others point out that revival projects can be costly and that their results are uncertain. These critics do not deny the value of diversity, but they insist that preservation should never be forced on people who do not want it.","Despite these debates, some revival efforts have been remarkably successful. In New Zealand, Māori communities created 'language nests', pre-schools in which very young children were taught entirely in Māori by elders. A generation later, many of those children had become parents who spoke the language to their own families. In Wales, laws that require road signs, public services, and school lessons to include Welsh have helped to stabilise a language that once seemed likely to decline. In both cases, the key factor was not only government support but also the enthusiasm of the speakers themselves.","Technology now offers new tools. Researchers record elderly speakers on smartphones and build online dictionaries. Young people create videos, songs, and social media posts in their heritage languages, giving them a modern and attractive image. Some companies have begun to add smaller languages to their translation services, although the quality is still uneven. Such efforts cannot replace a living community of speakers, but they can slow the decline and provide material for future generations to learn from.","The central lesson of the research seems to be that a language survives only when it is used in daily life, particularly by children. Dictionaries and archives are valuable, but a language that exists only in books is more like a museum object than a means of communication. Preventing the disappearance of the world's languages will therefore require more than recording words; it will require societies that give people a real reason, and a real freedom, to keep speaking them."],"q":[{"q":"According to the passage, when does a language usually disappear?","o":["When the government stops funding it","When its last fluent speaker dies","When it is no longer taught in schools","When its grammar becomes too complicated"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Usually when its last fluent speaker dies."},{"q":"What happens in the third generation of language decline?","o":["Children understand elders but reply in another language.","Children speak only the old language at home and school.","Parents begin teaching the old language again at home.","The old language becomes an official language of the state."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Children understand their grandparents but answer in the majority language."},{"q":"Why do parents often choose the dominant language for their children?","o":["They do not want the children to be disadvantaged.","They feel ashamed of speaking their own language.","Tradition says that their children must do so.","The dominant language is much simpler to spell."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"They do not want their children to be disadvantaged."},{"q":"Which is NOT given as a reason why language loss matters?","o":["Unique knowledge about the natural world","A link to identity and cultural heritage","Insights into how the mind handles grammar","Lower translation costs for companies"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The reasons given are knowledge, identity, and scientific understanding."},{"q":"What was the key factor in successful revival efforts like those in New Zealand and Wales?","o":["Large sums of money from foreign organisations","Laws forbidding the use of other languages","New technology used without community help","Government support plus speakers' enthusiasm"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Not only government support but also the enthusiasm of the speakers."},{"q":"What does the author mean by \"more like a museum object\"?","o":["Books about languages are very valuable.","Museums are the best places to teach languages.","A language kept only in books is not really used.","Language archives cost too much to maintain."],"a":2,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"A language that exists only in books is not a means of communication."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_tourism3","t":"When Visitors Outnumber Residents","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"When Visitors Outnumber Residents","p":["On a summer afternoon, the narrow streets of an old European port town can be almost impossible to walk through. Groups of visitors follow guides holding umbrellas, cruise passengers fill the small squares, and the local café owner serves more customers in one hour than he once did in a day. For the tourists, the town is a charming escape. For the people who live there, it is increasingly difficult to call it home. This tension has given rise to a new word: overtourism.","Tourism is one of the world's largest industries, and for many regions it is the main source of income. It creates jobs for guides, hotel workers, drivers, and shopkeepers, and it brings foreign money that can pay for roads, museums, and the restoration of historic buildings. Before the pandemic, international arrivals had been growing steadily for decades, helped by cheap flights, online booking platforms, and a growing middle class in many countries. Some destinations saw their number of visitors multiply many times within a generation.","The costs, however, are not shared equally. Residents of popular towns report that rents have risen sharply because landlords prefer to offer short holiday lets rather than long-term homes. Teachers, nurses, and young families are driven out of the centre, and ordinary shops that sold bread and tools are replaced by souvenir stores. In some historic districts, the permanent population has fallen by half, leaving neighbourhoods that look lively during the day and empty at night. 'We are living in a theatre set,' one resident remarked.","Environmental damage is another concern. Crowds wear down ancient stones, litter collects on beaches, and boats leave pollution in fragile waters. Popular natural sites, once peaceful, now suffer from erosion because of the number of hikers. A single large cruise ship can release as much air pollution in a day as thousands of cars, and travel by plane contributes to climate change, which in turn threatens many of the places visitors come to enjoy.","Authorities have tried a variety of solutions. Some cities charge an entry fee to day visitors, hoping both to raise revenue and to discourage the most casual crowds. Others limit the number of tickets for the most famous monuments or require advance booking, so that arrivals are spread throughout the day. A few have restricted the number of holiday apartments or banned the construction of new hotels in the centre. These policies are controversial. Business owners fear that visitors will go elsewhere, while some critics argue that entry fees turn historic places into attractions that only wealthy people can afford.","Many experts believe that the answer lies not in stopping tourism but in changing its pattern. Encouraging travel outside the peak summer months would reduce pressure and provide year-round work for local people. Promoting lesser-known towns and regions could spread income more widely, instead of concentrating it in a handful of famous spots. Visitors themselves can make a difference by staying longer in fewer places, choosing locally owned accommodation, and respecting the rules and customs of the community.","There is also an argument that residents should have a stronger voice in decisions. In several places, local councils have organised public meetings and surveys to ask what kind of tourism people actually want. The results are often more moderate than expected: most residents do not wish to eliminate visitors, but they want to see more of the profits stay in their own neighbourhoods and a guarantee that housing remains affordable for those who work there.","The debate over overtourism is ultimately a question of balance. A destination that depends completely on visitors is vulnerable, as the pandemic showed when whole towns lost their income overnight. Yet a place that ignores its residents risks losing the very character that made it attractive. The most sustainable tourism, many say, is the kind that leaves a community richer, not poorer, than it found it, and in which the people who live somewhere remain welcome in their own streets."],"q":[{"q":"What does the word \"overtourism\" describe?","o":["A lack of tourists during the winter","A new type of cheap holiday ticket","The tension caused by too many visitors","Visitors who stay for only a day"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The tension between visitors and residents gave rise to the word."},{"q":"Why have rents risen in popular towns?","o":["Local wages have fallen over the years.","Landlords prefer short holiday lets.","Property taxes have been abolished.","Hotels have become too cheap to use."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Landlords prefer short holiday lets rather than long-term homes."},{"q":"What does the resident mean by \"a theatre set\"?","o":["It has many theatres and cultural events all year.","It looks lively, but few people really live there.","It is a very old town with historic buildings.","It is home to a lot of actors and other artists."],"a":1,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Neighbourhoods look lively during the day and empty at night."},{"q":"Which policy is mentioned as controversial?","o":["Entry fees for day visitors","Planting trees along the old streets","Building new schools for local children","Lowering the price of train tickets"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Entry fees may raise revenue but critics say they exclude poorer visitors."},{"q":"What do experts suggest about the pattern of tourism?","o":["Spread visitors over seasons and quieter places.","Stop all tourism in the most popular towns.","Allow more cruise ships to visit each year.","Raise only the price of hotel rooms for tourists."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Encourage travel outside peak months and promote lesser-known towns."},{"q":"What lesson does the pandemic provide?","o":["Tourism is a safe and stable source of income.","Local residents prefer to have fewer jobs in the town.","Flights have become too cheap for travellers.","A place that depends only on visitors is vulnerable."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Whole towns lost their income overnight."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_robot3","t":"Working Alongside Machines","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Working Alongside Machines","p":["Every few decades, a new wave of technology provokes the same fear: that machines will take away human work. In the early nineteenth century, textile workers destroyed looms that threatened their jobs. In the twentieth century, factory automation and computers raised similar worries. Today, advances in artificial intelligence and robotics have revived the debate, and this time the discussion includes not only factory workers but also accountants, translators, and even doctors.","History offers some reassurance, but not a guarantee. In the past, technology did destroy particular jobs, yet it also created new ones, often in areas no one had imagined. Few people in 1900 predicted that millions would one day work as software developers, flight attendants, or social media managers. Economists note that automation tends to make goods cheaper and companies more productive, which increases demand and eventually creates additional employment elsewhere. The difficulty is that this process is neither immediate nor painless. A worker in his fifties who loses a factory job cannot easily become a programmer.","What seems different about the current wave is the range of tasks that machines can learn. Earlier automation mainly replaced physical, repetitive work. Modern systems can recognise images, process language, and make predictions, abilities once considered uniquely human. As a result, some researchers estimate that a large share of existing occupations include tasks that could be partly automated. It is important to notice the word 'partly': most jobs consist of many different activities, and only some of them are routine enough to hand over to a machine.","This suggests that the more realistic scenario is not the complete replacement of workers but a change in how work is organised. A radiologist who uses software to scan thousands of images may spend less time on routine checking and more on discussing results with patients. A lawyer might use a program to search documents in minutes rather than days, leaving more time for strategy and negotiation. In these cases, technology acts as a tool that increases productivity rather than as a rival. Nevertheless, the benefits may go mainly to those who already have skills and capital, which could widen inequality.","The question for societies, then, is how to prepare. Education is the most frequently mentioned answer. If machines handle routine tasks, people will need skills that are harder to automate, such as creativity, critical thinking, and the ability to work with others. But many experts warn against simple slogans. Schools cannot predict exactly which skills will be needed in twenty years, so they argue that the most useful lesson may be how to keep learning throughout life. Governments and employers will need to fund retraining programmes, and workers will need the time and support to use them.","Others focus on protection. Some propose stronger unemployment benefits or wage subsidies for people whose jobs disappear, while a few advocate a universal basic income, a regular payment to every citizen. Supporters say that such a payment would give people security in an uncertain labour market, but opponents worry about the cost and fear that it might reduce the motivation to work. Experiments in several countries have produced mixed results, and the debate remains open.","There is also a question about who decides how technology is used. Companies often adopt automation to reduce costs, but they could equally use it to improve the quality of products or to reduce dangerous and exhausting work. Workers and unions increasingly ask to take part in these decisions, especially when systems are used to monitor performance. Without clear rules, they argue, machines may be used to make jobs more stressful rather than better.","None of this means that the future must be gloomy. Technology has already removed some of the heaviest and most dangerous tasks from human life, and it may continue to do so. But the outcome depends less on the machines themselves than on the choices societies make about education, fairness, and the sharing of benefits. As in earlier centuries, the question is not whether technology will change work, but who will be asked to adapt, and who will profit when they do."],"q":[{"q":"What does the passage say about earlier technological change?","o":["It created more jobs than it destroyed.","It destroyed some jobs but created new ones.","It mainly helped the owners of factories.","It had little effect on working life."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Technology destroyed particular jobs, yet also created new ones."},{"q":"Why is the adjustment \"not painless\"?","o":["Older workers cannot easily switch occupations.","New jobs appear before old ones disappear.","Machines are too costly for most companies.","Retraining courses are unavailable everywhere."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"A worker in his fifties cannot easily become a programmer."},{"q":"What is new about modern automation?","o":["Machines can recognise images and process language.","Machines can now do all kinds of physical work.","Machines have become much cheaper to produce.","Machines are used only in modern large factories."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Modern systems can recognise images, process language and make predictions."},{"q":"In the radiologist example, how does technology help?","o":["It replaces the doctor in making all decisions.","It makes more mistakes than the radiologist does.","It reduces the number of patients to see.","It cuts routine checking, freeing time for patients."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Less time on routine checking, more on discussing results with patients."},{"q":"What is a concern about universal basic income?","o":["It would give people no financial security.","It has been tried in every large country.","It would raise unemployment benefits.","It may be costly and weaken the wish to work."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Opponents worry about cost and reduced motivation."},{"q":"According to the conclusion, what does the outcome depend on?","o":["The speed at which machines are improved","The price of the new technology for firms","Societal choices about education and fairness","How quickly older workers retire from work"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Less on the machines themselves than on the choices societies make."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_dream6","t":"Why Do We Dream?","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Why Do We Dream?","p":["Every night, whether we remember it or not, we dream. A typical person spends about two hours dreaming, which adds up to roughly six years over a lifetime. Yet scientists are still debating a basic question: why? Dreams have fascinated philosophers for thousands of years, but it is only in the last century that researchers have been able to study sleeping brains in detail.","The most vivid dreams occur during a stage called REM sleep, when the eyes move rapidly and the brain is almost as active as when we are awake. One influential theory suggests that this is when the brain sorts through the day's experiences, deciding which memories to keep and which to discard. In experiments, students who slept after learning a list of words remembered more of them the next day than students who stayed awake, which supports the idea that sleep strengthens memory.","A second theory focuses on emotions. Dreams, according to this view, work like a kind of overnight therapy. Because the chemicals associated with stress are reduced during REM sleep, the brain can revisit painful experiences without feeling their full force. People who have lost a loved one or survived a frightening event often dream about it repeatedly, and these dreams usually become less distressing over time.","Some researchers are more sceptical. They argue that dreams may simply be a by-product of the brain's activity, a kind of random noise that the mind turns into stories. According to this \"activation-synthesis\" hypothesis, the content of a dream has no special meaning. Supporters of this view point out that dreams are often illogical: we accept impossible events without surprise and rarely notice that we are dreaming.","Whatever their purpose, dreams can influence waking life. Artists and scientists have reported solving problems or finding ideas in their sleep, and some therapists use dreams as a starting point for discussion. Perhaps the most sensible conclusion is that dreaming serves more than one function. As technology improves, researchers hope to track brain activity so precisely that the mystery will finally be solved."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the text?","o":["To teach readers to remember their dreams","To show that dreams can predict the future","To describe each stage of sleep in detail","To compare different explanations for dreaming"],"a":3,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"The text presents memory, emotion and random-activity theories."},{"q":"What does the experiment with students suggest?","o":["Learning is easier in the evening than morning.","Students who sleep less remember more words.","Sleep helps the brain store new information.","Dreaming improves problem-solving skills."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Students who slept remembered more words."},{"q":"According to the second theory, why do disturbing dreams become less upsetting over time?","o":["The brain deletes painful memories completely at night.","People wake up before the dream turns frightening.","Stress-related chemicals are lower in REM sleep.","Dreams become shorter as people grow much older."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"The brain can revisit painful memories with less stress."},{"q":"In paragraph 4, \"sceptical\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["unwilling to listen to other people","doubtful about whether something is true","excited by a surprising new idea","certain that an explanation is correct"],"a":1,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Sceptical researchers question the earlier theories."},{"q":"Why do supporters of the \"activation-synthesis\" idea mention illogical dreams?","o":["They believe illogical dreams are more creative.","They think it shows dreams have no hidden meaning.","They want to show that dreams are rather rare.","They think the dreamer can control the story."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Illogical events suggest random brain noise."},{"q":"What does the writer conclude?","o":["Dreaming probably serves several different purposes.","Dreams are just meaningless activity in the brain.","The mystery of dreams has now been solved.","Dreams matter only for the storing of memories."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"Dreaming serves more than one function.\""},{"q":"Which of these is NOT mentioned as a possible influence of dreams on waking life?","o":["Dreams can improve a person's physical health.","Dreams can give artists new ideas.","Dreams can help scientists solve problems.","Dreams can give therapists topics to discuss."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Health benefits are not mentioned in paragraph 5."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_street6","t":"Vandalism or Culture?","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Vandalism or Culture?","p":["Not long ago, painting on a public wall without permission was seen as little more than vandalism. Today, the work of street artists hangs in galleries, sells for millions at auction and attracts tourists from around the world. This change has led to a lively debate: should street art remain a rebellious act, or can it become a respectable part of the art world?","Defenders of street art argue that it brings colour and meaning to neglected places. In many cities, councils now invite artists to decorate blank walls, abandoned factories and tunnels. In one industrial area of Bristol, a series of murals reduced the amount of graffiti tagging by almost half, as local people felt more proud of their neighbourhood and less willing to damage it.","Critics, however, point out that legal commissions take away what made street art special. Part of its appeal came from its risk and its unexpected appearance; once it is organised by officials, they say, it loses its voice. Others worry about a phenomenon known as \"artwashing\", in which property developers pay for attractive murals to make an area fashionable, only for rents to rise and long-term residents to be forced out.","Then there is the question of ownership. When a famous artist paints on a wall, who does the picture belong to: the artist, the building owner or the public? In several cases, owners have sold walls with the artwork still on them, while artists have complained that they received nothing. Because the paintings are exposed to weather and are sometimes covered over by other artists, they are also temporary by nature.","Many artists accept this impermanence as part of the point. \"A wall is not a canvas,\" says one painter, \"it is a conversation. Someone will answer me by painting over my work, and that's fine.\" Whether it is vandalism or culture, street art has changed how many of us look at the city around us, and it encourages us to see public space as something that anyone can shape."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main topic of the text?","o":["How to become a successful professional street artist","The shifting status of street art and its debates","The history of graffiti in old industrial cities","The best cities in the world for large murals"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The text weighs both sides of a debate."},{"q":"What happened in the industrial area of Bristol?","o":["Murals reduced damage because people felt proud.","Tourists stopped coming as the area grew crowded.","The council banned all painting on walls.","Property prices fell after artists moved in."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Graffiti tagging dropped by almost half."},{"q":"What worry do critics express about legal commissions?","o":["Officially organised art may lose its independent voice.","Artists are paid far too little for their murals.","Murals are often painted in a poor and careless style.","Councils usually pick only the most famous artists."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Critics say organised art \"loses its voice\"."},{"q":"What does \"artwashing\" refer to?","o":["Washing graffiti off public walls with chemicals","Selling street art to private collectors","Copying famous paintings onto buildings","Using art to make an area trendy and raise rents"],"a":3,"tg":["reference"],"e":"Developers pay for murals; rents then go up."},{"q":"Why is ownership a problem?","o":["Artists are not allowed to sign works in public places.","Building owners must pay to keep the picture on the wall.","The public is not allowed to take photographs of murals.","It is unclear who owns a picture on another's wall."],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Owners have sold walls and artists got nothing."},{"q":"What attitude does the painter in the last paragraph show?","o":["He gets angry when people cover his pictures.","He prefers to paint on canvas instead of walls.","He accepts that others may paint over his work.","He thinks only famous artists should paint walls."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"Someone will answer me by painting over my work, and that's fine.\""},{"q":"Which statement best describes the writer's approach?","o":["It strongly supports the idea of legal murals.","It suggests that street art wastes public money.","It gives space to both defenders and critics.","It argues that street art should be banned."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Both defenders and critics are given space."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_gig6","t":"The Gig Economy","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Gig Economy","p":["Ten years ago, few people had heard of \"gig work\". Today, millions earn money by delivering food on bicycles, driving passengers or completing small online tasks, with each job arranged through a smartphone app. This \"gig economy\" has changed how people think about employment, and opinion is divided on whether the change is good.","For many workers, the main attraction is flexibility. Gig workers decide when and how long to work, which suits students, parents with young children and anyone who needs extra income. Mara, a 24-year-old university student, delivers meals at weekends. \"I can switch off the app when I have an exam,\" she says. \"In a regular job, my manager would probably not be so understanding.\"","Yet this freedom comes at a price. Most gig workers are classed as self-employed, so they receive no paid holidays, sick pay or pension contributions from the companies whose apps they use. If they fall ill or have an accident, they simply stop earning. Their income can also be unpredictable: demand rises and falls, and the payment for each job may change without warning. Some workers find that after paying for fuel, insurance and repairs, their real hourly pay is below the minimum wage.","Governments are struggling to respond. In some countries, courts have ruled that certain gig workers should be treated as employees, which would entitle them to basic rights. Companies argue that such rules would force them to cut jobs and raise prices, and that workers themselves value independence. Surveys show that opinions differ: many people appreciate the freedom, but a majority also say they would like more protection.","Economists suggest that the answer may lie in a middle path, such as a flexible contract that still provides basic security. Whatever solution is found, the gig economy is unlikely to disappear. As more of our lives move online, the question is not whether this type of work will exist, but how fairly it will be organised."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main idea of the text?","o":["Gig companies pay better than normal employers.","Gig work offers flexibility but also insecurity.","Smartphone apps have made every job easier.","Governments have banned most forms of gig work now."],"a":1,"tg":["gist"],"e":"The text balances freedom against lack of protection."},{"q":"Why does Mara like gig work?","o":["She can switch off the app during her exams.","She earns more than regular employees do.","Her manager helps her to prepare for exams.","She works full time with paid holidays."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"She can switch off the app during exams."},{"q":"What does \"self-employed\" mean for gig workers here?","o":["They are responsible for their own income.","They receive a fixed salary each month.","They are guaranteed a minimum number of jobs.","They work only for firms in their own country."],"a":0,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"No paid holidays, sick pay or pension contributions."},{"q":"Why might some workers' real pay be below the minimum wage?","o":["Companies lower payments at the end of the month.","They spend hours waiting for the app to load.","Customers rarely leave tips for delivery workers.","They have to pay for fuel and insurance."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Fuel, insurance and repairs reduce real pay."},{"q":"In paragraph 4, \"entitle\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["force someone to do something unpleasant","give a title or a name to someone","allow someone to avoid a responsibility","give someone the right to something"],"a":3,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Employee status would give them basic rights."},{"q":"What do the surveys show?","o":["Most people want gig work to be banned altogether.","Most workers are happy with the money they earn.","Many like the freedom but want more protection.","Few people know what the gig economy actually is."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Many appreciate freedom; a majority want more protection."},{"q":"What do economists suggest?","o":["A complete return to traditional permanent jobs","Letting the companies make all their own working rules","A flexible arrangement that still gives basic security","An end to the use of apps for organising work"],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"They propose a middle path."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_bees6","t":"Saving the Bees","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Saving the Bees","p":["If you enjoyed an apple, a cup of coffee or a handful of almonds today, you can thank a bee. About one third of the food we eat depends on pollination, the process by which insects carry pollen from flower to flower so that plants can produce fruit and seeds. Honeybees get most of the attention, but thousands of wild bee species do the same job, often more efficiently.","Unfortunately, bees are in trouble. In many parts of the world, their numbers have fallen sharply over the last few decades. Scientists rarely blame a single cause. Instead, they point to a combination of pressures: the loss of wild flowers as farmland and cities expand, diseases carried by small creatures called mites, climate change that disturbs the timing of flowering, and the widespread use of certain pesticides.","The pesticide issue has been especially controversial. A group of chemicals known as neonicotinoids was found to damage bees' ability to find their way home. After years of research and campaigning, the European Union banned their use on outdoor crops in 2018. Farming organisations objected, arguing that the alternatives were more expensive and less effective, but many scientists welcomed the decision as a sign that evidence can influence policy.","Ordinary people can help, too. Planting flowers on balconies, leaving part of a garden uncut and avoiding chemical sprays all give bees food and shelter. Some cities have turned roofs into meadows, and a few have even created \"bee corridors\" of flowering plants along roads. Beekeeping has become popular as well, though experts warn that keeping hives in a city without enough flowers can actually harm wild species by creating competition for food.","Protecting bees is not only about saving an insect. It is about protecting the systems that feed us. Without pollinators, farmers would have to move pollen by hand, as already happens in parts of China, at enormous cost. As one ecologist puts it, \"Looking after bees is the cheapest insurance policy humanity has.\""],"q":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the text?","o":["To persuade readers to start keeping bees at home","To explain why bees matter and what threatens them","To describe step by step how honey is made by bees","To criticise the farmers who still use strong chemicals"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"The text covers importance, threats and ways to help."},{"q":"According to paragraph 2, why are bee numbers falling?","o":["Several pressures combine, such as pesticides.","One disease has spread throughout the world.","Bees are unable to survive in large cities.","Farmers have deliberately stopped planting flowers."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Scientists rarely blame a single cause."},{"q":"What did neonicotinoids do to bees?","o":["They damaged the bees' ability to find home.","They destroyed the flowers that bees eat.","They made the bees far more aggressive.","They cut the amount of honey produced."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The chemicals affected navigation."},{"q":"How did farming organisations react to the ban?","o":["They welcomed the ban as a great victory for science.","They agreed to use fewer machines on their farms.","They asked governments to pay for their beekeepers.","They said the alternatives cost more and work worse."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"They objected to the ban."},{"q":"Why do experts warn about beekeeping in cities?","o":["Honeybees may attack people in crowded places.","Flowers in cities contain too many chemicals.","Hives are not allowed on city rooftops.","Too many hives can leave wild bees short of food."],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Hives can create competition for food."},{"q":"In paragraph 3, \"controversial\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["completely new and unusual","extremely dangerous to bees","causing a lot of disagreement","widely forgotten by people"],"a":2,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Farmers and scientists disagreed about the ban."},{"q":"What point does the example from China make?","o":["Chinese farmers have found a method better than bees.","Bees are not really needed in modern farming.","Replacing natural pollination by hand would be costly.","Hand pollination produces better quality fruit."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"It is done \"at enormous cost\"."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_acad_bees","t":"The Quiet Return of Urban Bees","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Quiet Return of Urban Bees","p":["Cities are not usually thought of as good places for wildlife, yet bees seem to be thriving in many of them. In London, rooftop hives have multiplied over the past decade, and some beekeepers report that their urban colonies produce more honey than those in the countryside.","The reason is partly a matter of food. Intensive farming has reduced the variety of flowers in rural areas, while city gardens, parks and balconies offer plants that bloom at different times of the year. Warmer temperatures in cities also allow bees to forage for longer.","Not everyone is delighted, however. Some ecologists worry that large numbers of honeybees may compete with wild species for limited food. They suggest that planting more flowers would help wild bees more than adding hives. The debate shows that good intentions do not always lead to good results."],"q":[{"q":"Why do some urban colonies produce more honey?","o":["City bees are larger","Beekeepers in cities are more experienced","There are fewer flowers in the countryside to compete with","Cities offer a wider variety of flowers and a warmer climate"],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Food variety and warmth are given as reasons."},{"q":"What worry do some ecologists have?","o":["Hives are too noisy","Honey from cities is unsafe","Honeybees may take food from wild bees","Bees may leave the cities"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"They fear competition with wild species."},{"q":"What do these ecologists suggest?","o":["Banning rooftop hives","Moving hives to farms","Planting more flowers","Feeding bees with sugar"],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"More flowers would help wild bees."},{"q":"What is the writer's final point?","o":["Bees are dangerous in cities","Good intentions can have unexpected results","Farming is better for bees","Beekeeping should be stopped"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Good intentions do not always lead to good results."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_acad_screen","t":"Learning on a Screen","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Learning on a Screen","p":["Online learning grew quickly in recent years, and many students now attend lessons from their bedrooms. Supporters say that it saves travel time, lets people study at their own pace and gives access to teachers from around the world.","Yet research on how much students actually learn is mixed. Some studies find that learners retain less information from a screen than from a printed page, partly because notifications and other distractions interrupt their concentration. Others show that well-designed online courses with regular feedback can be just as effective as classroom teaching.","Many universities are therefore moving towards blended models that combine online material with face-to-face seminars. The idea is to use technology where it works best, such as for lectures and practice quizzes, and to keep discussion and hands-on work in the classroom."],"q":[{"q":"What do supporters of online learning say?","o":["It saves time and allows flexible study","It is cheaper for teachers","It needs no feedback","It is better for group work"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Travel time, own pace and access to teachers."},{"q":"Why might students remember less from a screen?","o":["The text is too small","Lessons are shorter","Teachers speak too fast","Distractions interrupt their concentration"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Notifications and other distractions."},{"q":"What can make online courses as effective as classes?","o":["Longer lectures","Fewer quizzes","No discussion","Well-designed content with regular feedback"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Design and feedback are the key."},{"q":"What is a blended model?","o":["Only online lectures","Only classroom teaching","A mix of online and face-to-face teaching","A course without tests"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"It combines online material with seminars."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_fashion7","t":"The True Cost of Fast Fashion","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The True Cost of Fast Fashion","p":["A T-shirt for the price of a sandwich sounds like a bargain, but few shoppers stop to ask how it can possibly be so cheap. Fast fashion, the business model that produces trendy clothes at very low prices, depends on speed and volume: some brands now release thousands of new designs every week.","The consequences are considerable. Producing a single cotton T-shirt can require around two and a half thousand litres of water, and synthetic fabrics release tiny plastic fibres every time they are washed. Meanwhile, the average garment is worn only a handful of times before it is thrown away, and much of it ends up in landfill.","There are also human costs. In order to keep prices down, many companies buy from factories where workers earn very little and conditions can be unsafe. Because supply chains are so long and complicated, even well-meaning shoppers find it difficult to know who made their clothes.","Encouragingly, attitudes are beginning to change. Second-hand shops and online resale platforms are growing rapidly, and some young people proudly describe themselves as \"thrifters\". Experts suggest that the most sustainable garment is the one already in your wardrobe, so buying fewer, better items and wearing them for longer may be the simplest solution of all."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main characteristic of fast fashion?","o":["It sells only high-quality clothes.","It is produced only in small workshops.","It depends on customers who keep clothes for years.","It makes trendy clothes cheaply and in large numbers."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Hız ve hacim; çok sayıda ucuz tasarım."},{"q":"What problem is mentioned about synthetic fabrics?","o":["They need more water than cotton.","They are always unsafe to wear.","They release plastic fibres when washed.","They cost too much to make."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yıkamada mikro plastik lifler salıyorlar."},{"q":"Why is it hard for shoppers to know who made their clothes?","o":["Companies refuse to sell online.","Factories only work at night.","The supply chains are long and complicated.","Labels are written in foreign languages."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Tedarik zinciri uzun ve karmaşık."},{"q":"What do experts suggest in the last paragraph?","o":["Giving up second-hand shops","Buying fewer clothes and wearing them longer","Choosing the cheapest brands","Washing clothes more often"],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Daha az, daha iyi ve uzun süre giymek."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_garden7","t":"Gardens in the Sky","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Gardens in the Sky","p":["In cities where space is expensive, it is surprising to see lettuces growing between the chimneys. Yet rooftop gardens are becoming increasingly common, from restaurants that grow their own herbs to apartment blocks with vegetable beds on the roof.","The benefits go beyond fresh produce. Plants absorb heat, so a green roof can keep a building cooler in summer and warmer in winter, reducing the cost of air conditioning and heating. They also soak up rainwater, which helps cities cope with heavy storms, and they provide a home for insects such as bees.","There are practical difficulties, however. A garden of soil and water is heavy, so the building must be strong enough to support it. Winds can be fierce at that height, and a high yield requires careful watering. For these reasons, many projects begin with lightweight containers rather than deep beds.","Perhaps the greatest value is social. In one neighbourhood, residents who had never spoken to each other started to meet on the roof every weekend. They now share the harvest, swap recipes and organise summer parties. As one gardener told a reporter, \"We came for the tomatoes, but we stayed for the people.\""],"q":[{"q":"How can a green roof help a building?","o":["It can lower heating and cooling costs.","It makes the building taller.","It stops all rain from entering.","It replaces the need for windows."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Bina yazın serin, kışın sıcak kalır."},{"q":"Why do many projects start with lightweight containers?","o":["Containers are more beautiful.","Plants grow faster in them.","The council requires them.","Deep beds of soil are too heavy for many buildings."],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Toprak ve su ağırdır."},{"q":"What happened in one neighbourhood?","o":["Neighbours argued about the harvest.","The roof had to be closed.","The garden was sold to a restaurant.","Residents who did not know each other became friends."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Birbirini tanımayan sakinler dost oldu."},{"q":"What does the quotation at the end suggest?","o":["The tomatoes were of poor quality.","The gardener wanted to leave.","People value the social side as much as the food.","The reporter disliked the project."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"We came for the tomatoes, but we stayed for the people.\""}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_detox7","t":"Switching Off","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Switching Off","p":["Last month, I put my phone in a drawer for a whole weekend. I had read an article about \"digital detox\", and I was curious to see whether I could survive without my devices for forty-eight hours. The answer, it turned out, was complicated.","The first morning was strange. I kept reaching for my pocket, and I felt a kind of restlessness I could not explain. Without notifications, the day seemed much longer. But by the afternoon, I had started a book I had been ignoring for months, and I noticed that I was able to read for an hour without stopping.","Not everything was easy. I missed several messages from friends, and my sister, who could not reach me, became quite worried. Without a map, I also got lost on the way to a café, and I had to ask a stranger for directions, something I had not done in years.","Would I do it again? Probably, but not completely. Instead of cutting myself off, I have decided to set boundaries: no phone at meals, no notifications after nine in the evening and one phone-free day a month. Technology is useful, but it should serve us, not the other way round."],"q":[{"q":"How did the writer feel on the first morning?","o":["Calm and relaxed","Angry with his sister","Proud of himself","Restless without his phone"],"a":3,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Sürekli cebine uzanıyor, huzursuzdu."},{"q":"What positive result did he notice by the afternoon?","o":["He finished his work earlier.","He made new friends.","He could read for an hour without stopping.","He found the café at once."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Bir saat kesintisiz kitap okudu."},{"q":"Why did his sister become worried?","o":["He had lost his keys.","He did not visit her.","She could not contact him.","He had changed his number."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Ona ulaşamadı."},{"q":"What has the writer decided to do?","o":["Never use a phone again","Set limits on his phone use","Buy a new phone","Delete all his messages"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Yemekte telefon yok, 9'dan sonra bildirim yok, ayda bir telefonsuz gün."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_fail7","t":"Learning from Failure","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Learning from Failure","p":["In many cultures, failure is considered something to hide. Students are rewarded for correct answers, and a bad mark can feel like a personal disaster. Yet researchers who study achievement increasingly argue that the fear of failure may be doing more harm than failure itself.","One university has started an unusual experiment: a \"failure CV\", on which students list the projects that did not work out, the jobs they were refused and the exams they did not pass. Alongside each entry, they write what they learnt. The idea is not to feel sad about the past, but to show that setbacks are a normal part of any career.","Psychologists describe a quality called resilience, the ability to recover after a difficulty. According to their research, resilient people do not avoid mistakes; they treat them as information. When something goes wrong, they ask, \"What can I change?\" rather than \"What is wrong with me?\"","This does not mean that we should look for failure. But perfectionism, the belief that everything must be done without error, can stop people from trying new things at all. As the saying goes, a ship is safe in the harbour, but that is not what ships are for."],"q":[{"q":"What do researchers increasingly argue about fear of failure?","o":["It helps students work harder.","It is disappearing in schools.","It only affects young children.","It can be more harmful than failure itself."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Başarısızlık korkusu bazen başarısızlıktan daha zararlı."},{"q":"What is the purpose of a \"failure CV\"?","o":["To help students find jobs faster","To punish students who fail","To show that setbacks are normal and teach lessons","To list the best projects only"],"a":2,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Aksilikler normal ve her birinden ders çıkar."},{"q":"How do resilient people react to mistakes?","o":["They avoid taking any risks.","They blame themselves.","They treat them as information.","They ignore them."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Hataları bilgi olarak görürler."},{"q":"What does the writer say about perfectionism?","o":["It leads to better exam results.","It can prevent people from trying new things.","It is the key to resilience.","It only exists in schools."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Yeni şeyler denemekten alıkoyabilir."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_homesick7","t":"Coping with Homesickness","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Coping with Homesickness","p":["Moving to another country is exciting, but nearly everyone who does so feels homesick at some point. For Ali, who came to Germany to study engineering, it started in the third week. The novelty had worn off, the weather was grey, and he missed his mother's cooking more than he had expected.","Psychologists explain that homesickness is a normal part of what is called culture shock. At first, everything seems new and interesting. Later, small differences, such as different meal times, unfamiliar customs or the language itself, can feel tiring, and people long for the familiar.","Ali tried several things. He called his family every Sunday, but he found that talking to them too often only made him sadder. What really helped was building a routine in his new city: a morning walk, a weekly football match and cooking Turkish dishes with two classmates who loved them.","Experts agree that it takes time, usually a few months, to adapt. They advise newcomers to stay in touch with home without living there in their minds, to look for people who share their interests and to be patient with themselves. As Ali says now, \"It never disappears completely, but it becomes a small, warm feeling instead of a heavy one.\""],"q":[{"q":"When did Ali begin to feel homesick?","o":["On his first day","When he failed an exam","After the novelty had worn off","When his mother visited"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Üçüncü haftada, yenilik geçince."},{"q":"Why did calling his family often not help?","o":["It was too expensive.","It made him feel sadder.","His family did not answer.","He had no time."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Çok sık konuşmak onu daha üzgün yaptı."},{"q":"What really helped Ali?","o":["Returning home for a month","Building a routine and meeting people","Changing universities","Avoiding Turkish food"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Rutin, spor ve yemek yapma."},{"q":"What do experts say about adapting?","o":["It usually takes a few months.","It happens within a week.","It is impossible for most people.","It depends on the weather."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Genellikle birkaç ay sürer."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_climate7","t":"A Warmer World","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"A Warmer World","p":["Scientists agree that the Earth is getting warmer. Over the last hundred years, the average temperature has risen by about one degree, and most of the increase has happened in recent decades. This may not sound like much, but even small changes can have large effects on weather and nature.","The consequences are already visible. Glaciers are shrinking, sea levels are rising, and in many regions summers are becoming hotter and drier. Farmers in some countries face longer droughts, while others suffer from heavier rain and flooding.","The main cause is the gas produced when we burn coal, oil and petrol. Governments are therefore trying to reduce emissions by building wind and solar power stations and by encouraging people to use public transport. Progress, however, is uneven, and critics say that promises are not always kept.","Individuals can also contribute. Saving energy at home, eating less meat and choosing to walk or cycle for short journeys all make a difference when millions of people do them together. As one scientist put it, \"We cannot do everything, but everyone can do something.\""],"q":[{"q":"How much has the average temperature risen in a hundred years?","o":["About ten degrees","Nearly nothing","About one degree","About five degrees"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yaklaşık bir derece."},{"q":"Which problem do some farmers face?","o":["Too much sunshine","Longer droughts","Shorter summers","Colder winters"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Daha uzun kuraklıklar."},{"q":"What is the main cause of warming mentioned?","o":["Volcanoes","Burning coal, oil and petrol","Plastic bags","Farming only"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Kömür, petrol ve benzin yakmak."},{"q":"What is the scientist's message?","o":["Everyone can do something.","Only governments can help.","Nothing can be done.","Individuals do not matter."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Herkes bir şey yapabilir."}]}],"L":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Sekiz farklı durumda kısa kayıtlar dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","id":"b2l1","t":"Short Extracts (1)","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"b2l1a","sc":[["M","I wasn't sure about joining the drama club – I'm quite shy. But it's actually helped a lot. I'm still nervous before we perform, but I've learned to speak up in class, which I never used to do."]],"q":"You hear a student talking about a drama club. What does he say about it?","o":["It has helped him speak up outside the club.","It has cured his fear of performing.","It has helped him make a lot of new friends."],"a":0,"tg":["inference","used-to"],"e":"Sahnede hâlâ gergin ama sınıfta konuşmayı öğrendi."},{"id":"b2l1b","sc":[["F","We were going to drive, but with the price of petrol and the traffic in summer, the train made more sense. It's a bit slower, but at least we can relax and read."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking about a journey. Why did she choose the train?","o":["It was quicker than driving in summer.","It was a more sensible way to travel.","Her car wasn't available that week."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Benzin ve trafik yüzünden tren daha mantıklı."},{"id":"b2l1c","sc":[["M","Honestly, the new manager has some good ideas, but she doesn't listen. She makes decisions without asking anyone, and then wonders why people aren't enthusiastic."],["F","Maybe give her time. It's only been a month."]],"q":"You hear two colleagues talking. What does the man criticise about the manager?","o":["her failure to consult colleagues","her lack of interest in new ideas","her unclear instructions to staff"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Kimseye sormadan karar veriyor."},{"id":"b2l1d","sc":[["F","If you're going to the market, could you get some tomatoes? Not the big ones, they're tasteless. The small cherry ones. And if they haven't got any, don't bother – we'll manage without."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking to a family member. What does she want him to do?","o":["buy the largest tomatoes he can find","buy cherry tomatoes or nothing at all","check the price of tomatoes first"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Sadece küçük kiraz domatesler; yoksa hiç alma."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Cümle tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Cümleleri bir kelime ya da kısa bir ifadeyle tamamla.","id":"b2l2","t":"Beekeeping","ty":"open","title":"Cümleler","sc":[["M","Good evening. My name's David, and I've been keeping bees for about fifteen years. I started after my doctor suggested I find a hobby that would get me outdoors. I bought my first hive from a retired teacher in my village. Many people think beekeeping is mainly about honey, but for me the most rewarding part is simply watching the bees work. A healthy hive in summer can contain around fifty thousand bees. The biggest threat to bees today, in my opinion, isn't disease but the loss of wild flowers. If you want to help bees, the easiest thing you can do is plant lavender in your garden. Most new beekeepers worry about being stung, but it rarely happens if you move slowly and stay calm. Last year my bees produced about thirty kilos of honey, which I share with my neighbours."]],"p":"David started keeping bees on the advice of his (1) ______.\nHe bought his first hive from a former (2) ______.\nFor David, the best part of beekeeping is (3) ______ the bees.\nIn summer, a hive may have about (4) ______ bees.\nDavid thinks the main danger to bees is the loss of (5) ______.\nHe recommends planting (6) ______ to help bees.\nLast year his bees made about (7) ______ kilos of honey.","a":[["doctor"],["teacher"],["watching"],["50,000","50000","fifty thousand"],["wild flowers","wildflowers","flowers"],["lavender"],["30","thirty"]],"tg":[["detail"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["spelling"]],"e":["\"my doctor suggested\".","\"a retired teacher\" – former = eski, emekli.","\"simply watching the bees work\".","\"around fifty thousand bees\".","\"the loss of wild flowers\".","\"plant lavender\".","\"about thirty kilos of honey\"."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Beş kişinin konuşmasını dinleyeceksin. Her konuşmacı için doğru seçeneği (A–H) seç. Üç seçenek fazladır.","id":"b2l3","t":"Moving to a New City","ty":"match","sc":[["F","Speaker one. When I moved to London for work, I expected it to be exciting, and it was. What I hadn't expected was how expensive everything would be. I spent half my salary on rent for a tiny room."],["M","#1"],["M","Speaker two. I moved to a small town in the north, and people were so welcoming. Within a week my neighbours had invited me for dinner. I'd heard northerners were friendly, but this was something else."],["F","#1"],["F2","Speaker three. Moving to Berlin was hard because I didn't speak German. I took evening classes, and after six months I could manage. Now I dream in German sometimes!"],["M","#1"],["M2","Speaker four. I'd always lived in big cities, so moving to the countryside was a shock. At first the quiet kept me awake at night. Now I can't imagine going back to the noise."],["F","#1"],["F","Speaker five. My company moved me to Madrid. The work was the same, but the lifestyle was completely different. People eat dinner at ten at night! It took me months to adjust my routine."]],"opts":[["A","I was surprised by the cost of living."],["B","I had to learn a new language."],["C","I found it hard to make friends."],["D","People were even kinder than I had heard."],["E","I had to change my daily habits."],["F","I regret the move."],["G","I found the silence difficult at first."],["H","I missed my family."]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"A","tg":["gist"],"e":"Her şeyin ne kadar pahalı olduğunu beklemiyordu."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"D","tg":["gist"],"e":"Duyduğundan bile daha misafirperver."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"B","tg":["gist"],"e":"Almanca öğrenmek zorunda kaldı."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"G","tg":["gist"],"e":"Sessizlik başta uyutmadı."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"E","tg":["gist"],"e":"Günlük rutinini değiştirmesi aylar sürdü."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Bir röportaj dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","id":"b2l4","t":"A Wildlife Photographer","ty":"mc","sc":[["F","With me today is Marco Ruiz, a wildlife photographer. Marco, what got you interested in photographing animals?"],["M","My grandmother, actually. She was a keen birdwatcher, and she gave me her old camera when I was ten. I spent hours in her garden trying to photograph birds. Most of the pictures were terrible, but I was hooked."],["F","What's the most difficult part of your job?"],["M","People assume it's the danger, but I've rarely felt unsafe. It's the waiting. I once spent eleven days in a hide to photograph a single bird. You need enormous patience."],["F","Has technology changed wildlife photography?"],["M","Hugely. Cameras are so good now that almost anyone can take a sharp picture. That means the competition is tougher. What makes a photo stand out today isn't technical quality – it's the story it tells."],["F","Do you think photography can help protect animals?"],["M","I'd like to think so. A single powerful image can make people care about a species they'd never heard of. But photos alone aren't enough. They have to be followed by action."]],"q":[{"q":"Who first encouraged Marco's interest in photography?","o":["a relative who loved watching birds","a teacher who taught him photography","a wildlife photographer he once met"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Büyükannesi ona eski fotoğraf makinesini verdi."},{"q":"What does Marco find hardest about his work?","o":["working in dangerous conditions","the long periods of waiting","carrying heavy camera equipment"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"It's the waiting\"."},{"q":"According to Marco, what makes a wildlife photo special today?","o":["its technical quality","the rarity of the animal","the story it tells"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"it's the story it tells\"."},{"q":"What is Marco's view on photography and conservation?","o":["Photographs alone can save endangered species.","Photos can make people care, but more is needed.","Photography has little effect on public opinion."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"photos alone aren't enough. They have to be followed by action\"."}]},{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Sekiz farklı durumda kısa kayıtlar dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","id":"b2l5","t":"Short Extracts (2)","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"b2l5a","sc":[["F","I've been using the new gym for a month. The equipment's excellent, and it's never crowded. My only problem is that it closes at eight, which is too early for people who work late like me."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking about a gym. What does she dislike about it?","o":["the choice of equipment","the crowds at busy times","its early closing time"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Saat sekizde kapanıyor; geç çalışanlar için erken."},{"id":"b2l5b","sc":[["M","I'm calling about the advert for the flat in Green Street. Is it still available? And could you tell me whether pets are allowed? I've got a small dog."]],"q":"You hear a man leaving a message. What does he want to know?","o":["whether his dog can live there","how big the garden of the flat is","how quickly he could move in"],"a":0,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Evcil hayvana izin olup olmadığını soruyor."},{"id":"b2l5c","sc":[["F","Have you tried that new podcast about history?"],["M","I listened to one episode. The presenters clearly know their stuff, but they kept interrupting each other. I found it really hard to follow."]],"q":"What does the man think of the podcast?","o":["The topics are uninteresting.","The presenters aren't well informed.","The conversation is hard to follow."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Sunucular birbirinin sözünü kesiyor; takip etmesi zor."},{"id":"b2l5d","sc":[["M","We'd planned to go camping, but the forecast was awful, so we stayed in a cottage instead. In the end it was sunny all weekend! Still, the cottage was lovely, so I'm not complaining."]],"q":"How does the man feel about the weekend?","o":["annoyed that the forecast was wrong","pleased with how the weekend turned out","unhappy about the cottage they stayed in"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I'm not complaining\" – memnun."}]},{"k":"L1","id":"b2l6","t":"Short Extracts (3)","ty":"mc","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa parçalar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Altı kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","q":[{"id":"f-a","sc":[["F","I've decided to give up my gym membership. I only went twice last month, and it costs more than I expected. I think I'll just go running in the park instead – it's free, and I can do it whenever I like."]],"q":"Why has the woman decided to stop going to the gym?","o":["It is too far from her home.","She is not using it enough to justify the cost.","She has found a cheaper gym."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","purpose"],"e":"Ayda yalnızca iki kez gitti ve beklediğinden pahalı."},{"id":"f-b","sc":[["M","So what did you think of the lecture?"],["F","Interesting, but he spoke so quickly that I couldn't take notes. I'll ask him if he can send us the slides."]],"q":"What is the woman going to do?","o":["ask for the lecture slides","record the next lecture","speak to her classmates"],"a":0,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Çok hızlı konuştuğu için not alamadı; slaytları isteyecek."},{"id":"f-c","sc":[["M","Honestly, I was expecting the exhibition to be crowded, but we were almost alone. That was great for seeing the paintings, though I did wonder why nobody else was there. Maybe it should have been advertised better."]],"q":"What is the man's opinion of the exhibition?","o":["It was too busy.","It deserved more visitors.","The paintings were disappointing."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","inference"],"e":"Daha iyi duyurulması gerektiğini düşünüyor."},{"id":"f-d","sc":[["F","Our flight was cancelled, so the airline put us up in a hotel. It was nicer than I expected, but the best part was the free breakfast. We ate so much that we nearly missed the replacement flight!"]],"q":"What does the woman say about the hotel?","o":["Breakfast was the highlight.","It was not as good as she hoped.","It was far from the airport."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the best part was the free breakfast\""},{"id":"f-e","sc":[["M","Could you tell me how long the film is? My little girl has to be in bed by nine."],["F","It starts at six thirty and finishes at about eight forty-five. If you leave before the credits, you'll make it."]],"q":"What does the woman suggest?","o":["choosing a shorter film","leaving the cinema a little early","coming to a later show"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","inference"],"e":"Jenerik bitmeden çıkarlarsa yetişirler."},{"id":"f-f","sc":[["F","I used to hate cooking, to be honest, but then I started watching videos online. Now I make dinner every night, and it's become the best way for me to relax after work."]],"q":"How does the woman feel about cooking now?","o":["It is a chore.","It helps her unwind.","It is too time-consuming."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","used-to"],"e":"Akşam yemeği hazırlamak artık dinlenme yolu."}]},{"k":"L2","id":"b2l7","t":"Coastal Wildlife Project","ty":"open","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Cümle tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir gönüllü programı tanıtımını dinle. Her boşluğa bir ya da iki kelime yaz.","title":"Notlar","sc":[["F2","Welcome to the Coastal Wildlife Project. This summer, we are looking for volunteers to help protect the sea turtles that nest on our beaches. The programme runs for six weeks from the first of July, and volunteers must be at least eighteen years old."],["F2","Most of the work takes place at night, because that is when the turtles come ashore. Volunteers walk along the beach with a torch and record the number of nests they find. During the day, you may help in the visitor centre or clean the beach. Accommodation is provided in shared cabins, and all meals are included, but you must pay for your own travel. The fee for the whole programme is three hundred and fifty pounds, which goes directly to the protection of the nests. Please bring a waterproof jacket, because it can rain without warning, even in summer. To apply, send us your name and a short letter explaining why you want to take part."]],"p":"Coastal Wildlife Project\nLength of programme: (1) ______ weeks\nMinimum age: (2) ______\nMost work is done at: (3) ______\nVolunteers count the: (4) ______\nAccommodation: shared (5) ______\nFee: £(6) ______\nBring a: waterproof (7) ______\nApplication: send a short (8) ______","a":[["6","six"],["18","eighteen"],["night","nighttime","night-time"],["nests"],["cabins"],["350","three hundred and fifty"],["jacket"],["letter"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"]],"e":["\"runs for six weeks\"","\"at least eighteen years old\"","\"most of the work takes place at night\"","\"record the number of nests\"","\"shared cabins\"","\"three hundred and fifty pounds\"","\"a waterproof jacket\"","\"a short letter\""]},{"k":"L1","id":"b2l8","t":"Short Extracts (4)","ty":"mc","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa parçalar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Altı kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","q":[{"id":"g-a","sc":[["F","I was going to buy a new coat today, but the one I liked had sold out. The assistant said they would get more in next week, though. I think I'll wait, because I'd rather not pay the price in the other shop."]],"q":"What does the woman decide to do about the coat?","o":["buy it in a different shop","wait until more are delivered","choose a different style"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yeni gelmesini bekleyecek; diğer dükkânın fiyatı yüksek."},{"id":"g-b","sc":[["M","Did you read the email about next week? Apparently the meeting has moved from Tuesday to Thursday because the director is travelling. It doesn't matter to me, but you might need to rearrange your dentist appointment."]],"q":"Why has the meeting been moved?","o":["The room is not free.","The director will be away.","Some staff are ill."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Müdür seyahatte."},{"id":"g-c","sc":[["F","The lecture was brilliant, but the exam is going to be tough. The professor said we need to read at least three of the articles, and I've only finished one. I'm going to spend the whole weekend in the library."]],"q":"What is the woman worried about?","o":["finding a place to study","completing the reading in time","understanding the lecture"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Üç makaleden yalnızca birini bitirdi."},{"id":"g-d","sc":[["M","I've been using this app for a month, and honestly, I'm disappointed. It's supposed to help you learn languages, but it only repeats the same exercises. I'll probably cancel my subscription before it renews."]],"q":"What does the man say about the app?","o":["It is too expensive.","It lacks variety.","It is difficult to use."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Aynı alıştırmaları tekrarlıyor."},{"id":"g-e","sc":[["F","Excuse me, is this the right platform for the train to Bath?"],["M","No, I'm afraid it's the one over there, platform nine. But hurry – it leaves in four minutes, and you have to go through the underpass."]],"q":"What does the man advise the woman to do?","o":["buy a ticket first","go to another platform quickly","wait for the next train"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Dokuzuncu perona acele etmeli."},{"id":"g-f","sc":[["F","I thought the film would be boring, because it's three hours long, but the time just flew by. The story kept surprising me, and the acting was superb. I'd happily watch it again."]],"q":"What does the woman think of the film?","o":["It was too long.","It was better than she expected.","The ending was disappointing."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Zaman su gibi aktı."}]},{"k":"L2","id":"b2l9","t":"Young Inventors Workshop","ty":"open","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Cümle tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir okul atölyesi tanıtımını dinle. Her boşluğa bir ya da iki kelime yaz.","title":"Notlar","sc":[["M2","Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the Young Inventors Workshop at the City Science Museum. This is a free programme for students between twelve and sixteen, and it takes place every Saturday for eight weeks, starting on the fifth of October."],["M2","In each session, you will work in a small team to design and build a simple machine. The first project is a bridge made of paper, followed by a model wind turbine and, finally, a robot that can follow a line. A qualified engineer will help each group, and all the materials are provided. The workshops start at ten and finish at half past twelve, and there is a short break for a snack halfway through. On the last day, parents are invited to see the work and vote for the best invention. The winning team will receive a trip to the national science festival in Birmingham. If you would like to take part, you must register on our website before the end of September, as there are only thirty places available."]],"p":"Young Inventors Workshop\nFor students aged: twelve to (1) ______\nDay: every (2) ______\nFirst project: a (3) ______ made of paper\nLast project: a (4) ______ that follows a line\nHelp from a: qualified (5) ______\nFinishes at: (6) ______ thirty\nWinners get a trip to: a science (7) ______\nPlaces available: (8) ______","a":[["sixteen","16"],["saturday"],["bridge"],["robot"],["engineer"],["twelve","12"],["festival"],["thirty","30"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"]],"e":["\"between twelve and sixteen\"","\"every Saturday\"","\"a bridge made of paper\"","\"a robot that can follow a line\"","\"A qualified engineer\"","\"finish at half past twelve\"","\"national science festival\"","\"only thirty places available\""]},{"k":"L3","id":"b2l10","t":"First Job Experiences","ty":"match","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Beş kişinin konuşmasını dinleyeceksin. Her konuşmacı için doğru seçeneği (A–H) seç. Üç seçenek fazladır.","sc":[["F","Speaker one. My first job was in a café, and I was terrified on the first day. I dropped a tray of cups in front of everybody. But my boss just laughed and helped me clean up, and after that I felt part of the team."],["M","#1"],["M","Speaker two. I worked in my uncle's shop during the holidays. I assumed it would be easy, but the days were long and I was on my feet constantly. What I enjoyed was dealing with customers; it made me a lot more confident."],["F","#1"],["F2","Speaker three. I spent a summer delivering newspapers. I had to get up at five in the morning, which I hated at first. However, I saved enough money to buy my first laptop, and I've never been so proud."],["M","#1"],["M2","Speaker four. I was an assistant at a veterinary clinic. I'd always loved animals, so I thought it would be perfect, but I had no idea how much cleaning was involved. I decided that it wasn't the career for me."],["F","#1"],["F","Speaker five. I did voluntary work in a library. It was unpaid, but the staff taught me a lot about organising information. I'm now studying to become a librarian, and it all began with that experience."]],"opts":[["A","I gained self-confidence from meeting people."],["B","I realised the job was not suitable for me."],["C","I was pleased to be able to afford something I wanted."],["D","I was made to feel welcome after a mistake."],["E","It led directly to my present studies."],["F","The salary was disappointingly low."],["G","I had to work with people I disliked."],["H","I was too tired to enjoy my free time."]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"D","tg":["gist"],"e":"Patronu güldü ve temizlemesine yardım etti."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"A","tg":["gist"],"e":"Müşterilerle ilişki özgüvenini artırdı."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"C","tg":["gist"],"e":"İlk dizüstü bilgisayarını alabildi."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"B","tg":["gist"],"e":"Bunun kendisine göre bir meslek olmadığına karar verdi."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"E","tg":["gist"],"e":"Şimdi kütüphaneci olmak için okuyor."}]},{"k":"L4","id":"b2l11","t":"A Street Food Chef","ty":"mc","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Bir şefle yapılan röportajı dinle ve soruları cevapla.","sc":[["F2","My guest today is Tariq Hassan, who runs one of the city's most popular street food stalls. Tariq, how did it all begin?"],["M2","Quite by accident. I was working as an accountant and cooking for my friends at the weekends. One of them said I should sell my food at the Sunday market, so I borrowed a small stall, and the first lot of sandwiches sold out in an hour."],["F2","And was it easy to leave your job?"],["M2","Not at all. My family thought I had lost my mind. I kept my office job for another two years, getting up at four to prepare food before work. It was exhausting, but I wanted to be sure the business could survive."],["F2","What is your secret?"],["M2","Honestly, it's simple ingredients and fresh ones. I buy vegetables every morning from local farmers, and I never use frozen meat. People can taste the difference."],["F2","What are your plans for the future?"],["M2","I'd like to open a small restaurant, but I'm in no hurry. The stall is where I meet my customers, and I wouldn't want to lose that. If I do open a restaurant, the stall will stay."]],"q":[{"q":"How did Tariq start selling food?","o":["He answered a job advertisement.","A friend suggested it.","He took over his family's stall."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Bir arkadaşı pazarda satmasını önerdi."},{"q":"Why did he keep his office job for two years?","o":["He did not have enough money to buy a stall.","He wanted to be certain the business would last.","His family insisted that he stay."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","attitude"],"e":"İşin ayakta kalacağından emin olmak istedi."},{"q":"What does Tariq say makes his food special?","o":["unusual recipes","fresh, simple ingredients","low prices"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Taze malzeme, dondurulmuş et yok."},{"q":"What does he say about opening a restaurant?","o":["He plans to do it very soon.","He would close the stall if he did.","He is not rushing and would keep the stall."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude","inference"],"e":"Acelesi yok; tezgâh kalacak."}]}],"W":[{"id":"b2w1","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Social Media and Young People","in":"In your English class you have been talking about technology. Now your English teacher has asked you to write an essay. Write your essay using all the notes and giving reasons for your point of view. Write 140–190 words.","mail":"Has social media done more harm than good for young people?\n\nNotes – write about:\n1. staying in touch with friends\n2. mental health\n3. ............ (your own idea)","pts":[["Arkadaşlarla iletişimi tartış",["in touch","friends","contact","communicate","connect"]],["Ruh sağlığını tartış",["mental health","anxiety","stress","self-esteem","depressed","compare"]],["Kendi fikrini ekle",["also","another","in addition","moreover","furthermore"]],["Sonuç / görüş",["in conclusion","to sum up","overall","on balance","in my opinion"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Social media is now a central part of most teenagers' lives, and opinions are divided about whether this is a positive development.\n\nOn the one hand, social media makes it easy to stay in touch with friends, even those who live far away. Young people can share experiences instantly and find others with similar interests, which can be especially valuable for those who feel isolated.\n\nOn the other hand, there is growing evidence that heavy use can damage mental health. Teenagers constantly compare themselves with the perfect images they see online, and this can lead to anxiety and low self-esteem.\n\nAnother important issue is the spread of false information. Many young people get their news from social media without checking whether it is reliable.\n\nOn balance, I believe social media is not harmful in itself; the problem is how it is used. If schools teach young people to use it critically and to limit their screen time, its benefits can outweigh its disadvantages.","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","Social media has changed the way young people communicate, learn and spend their free time. In my view, it has done more harm than good.\n\nIt is true that social media makes it easier to stay in touch with friends. However, many teenagers now spend hours scrolling through messages instead of meeting face to face, and these online conversations are often shallow.\n\nThe effect on mental health is even more worrying. Young people are constantly exposed to edited photos and seemingly perfect lives, which can make them feel that they are not good enough. Studies have linked heavy use to anxiety, poor sleep and low self-esteem.\n\nA further problem is that social media is designed to be addictive. Endless feeds and notifications make it extremely difficult to concentrate on homework or reading, and many students admit that they check their phones dozens of times an hour.\n\nIn conclusion, although social media has some benefits, I believe its negative effects on young people's well-being and concentration are far more significant. Parents and schools need to do much more to help teenagers use it in moderation."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Few inventions have transformed teenage life as dramatically as social media, and its impact remains fiercely debated.\n\nAdmittedly, it has made staying connected almost effortless. Friendships no longer end when someone moves to another city, and young people who feel isolated – perhaps because of their interests or identity – can find communities where they are accepted.\n\nNevertheless, the cost to mental health cannot be ignored. Platforms reward appearance and popularity, encouraging constant comparison. Many teenagers measure their worth in likes, and when the likes do not come, the consequences can be painful.\n\nWhat is perhaps less often discussed is the impact on attention. Short videos and endless feeds train the brain to expect constant stimulation, making it harder to focus on longer tasks such as reading a novel or revising for an exam.\n\nOn balance, I would argue that social media itself is neither good nor bad; the harm comes from unlimited, uncritical use. If young people were taught from an early age how these platforms work and how to set limits, they could enjoy the advantages while avoiding the worst of the risks."]]},{"id":"b2w2","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Tourism","in":"In your English class you have been talking about travel. Now your English teacher has asked you to write an essay. Write your essay using all the notes and giving reasons for your point of view. Write 140–190 words.","mail":"Is tourism good for the places that tourists visit?\n\nNotes – write about:\n1. jobs and money\n2. the environment\n3. ............ (your own idea)","pts":[["İş ve gelirden bahset",["jobs","money","economy","income","employment"]],["Çevreden bahset",["environment","pollution","rubbish","nature","damage"]],["Kendi fikrini ekle",["also","another","in addition","moreover","culture","local people"]],["Sonuç / görüş",["in conclusion","to sum up","overall","on balance","in my opinion"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Tourism is one of the largest industries in the world, but its effects on popular destinations are not always positive.\n\nThe most obvious advantage is economic. Tourism creates jobs in hotels, restaurants and shops, and the money visitors spend can be used to improve roads, hospitals and schools. For many small towns, it is the main source of income.\n\nHowever, mass tourism can seriously damage the environment. Beaches become polluted, wildlife is disturbed and huge amounts of water and energy are used in areas where they are already scarce.\n\nIn addition, local people sometimes suffer. When too many apartments are rented to tourists, housing becomes too expensive for residents, and traditional neighbourhoods lose their character.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe tourism can be good for a place, but only if it is carefully managed. Limiting visitor numbers and investing tourist money in protecting nature would allow destinations to enjoy the benefits without destroying what makes them special.","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","Every summer, millions of tourists visit popular destinations, and local people are increasingly asking whether this is really good for them.\n\nOf course, tourism brings money and jobs. Hotels, restaurants and tour companies employ thousands of people, and many small businesses depend entirely on visitors. Without tourism, some coastal towns would have very little income.\n\nHowever, this money comes at a price. Popular beaches are often covered in rubbish, and new hotels are built on land that was once home to wildlife. In some places, there is not even enough water for residents during the busy season.\n\nAnother serious problem is the effect on everyday life. As more flats are rented to tourists, rents rise and young local people can no longer afford to live in their own neighbourhoods. Traditional shops are replaced by souvenir stalls.\n\nIn my opinion, tourism in its current form does more harm than good to many places. Governments should limit the number of visitors to the most crowded destinations and make sure that tourist money is used to protect the environment and support local communities."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Tourism is often presented as a straightforward success story: visitors arrive, spend money and leave happy. The reality, however, is considerably more complicated.\n\nThere is no denying the economic benefits. Tourism generates employment, supports local crafts and can fund the restoration of historic buildings that might otherwise fall into ruin. For developing regions in particular, it can be a lifeline.\n\nYet the environmental consequences are often severe. Fragile ecosystems such as coral reefs and mountain trails are damaged by overuse, while the flights that bring tourists contribute significantly to climate change.\n\nPerhaps the most overlooked issue is cultural. When a town reshapes itself entirely around visitors' expectations, it risks losing the very authenticity that attracted them in the first place. Festivals become performances, and residents begin to feel like extras in their own home.\n\nUltimately, I believe tourism can benefit the places people visit, but only if it is managed with the long term in mind. Limiting numbers, encouraging travel outside peak season and ensuring that profits stay in the local community would go a long way towards achieving this balance."]]},{"id":"b2w3","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"My Perfect Weekend","in":"You see this announcement in an English-language magazine. Write your article in 140–190 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED\nMy perfect weekend\nDescribe your idea of a perfect weekend. Where would you be? Who would you spend it with? What would you do?\nThe best articles will be published in our next issue.","pts":[["Nerede olacağını anlat",["would be","in a","by the","at a","somewhere"]],["Kiminle olacağını söyle",["with my","friends","family","alone","on my own"]],["Ne yapacağını anlat",["would","start","spend","then","after that"]],["Bir başlık kullan",["@title"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Mountains, books and no phone signal\n\nAsk most people about their perfect weekend and they will talk about parties or shopping. Mine would be very different.\n\nI would spend it in a small wooden cabin in the mountains, ideally somewhere without a phone signal. The only people I would invite are my two closest friends, who share my love of walking and long conversations.\n\nOn Saturday, we would get up early and climb to the top of the nearest peak, taking a picnic with us. After lunch, we would lie in the grass and watch the clouds – something I never have time for during the week. In the evening, we would cook a simple meal on a fire and talk until midnight.\n\nSunday would be a lazy day. I would sleep late, read a good novel by the window and maybe go for a swim in a cold mountain lake.\n\nIt may not sound exciting, but for me, a weekend like this would be the best way to recharge before another busy week.","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","A weekend in the city that never sleeps\n\nWhile some people dream of peaceful beaches, my perfect weekend would be the opposite: two days in a big, busy city like Istanbul with my three closest friends.\n\nWe would arrive on Friday evening and go straight to a rooftop restaurant with a view of the Bosphorus. After dinner, we would wander through the narrow streets, stopping for tea and listening to street musicians.\n\nOn Saturday, we would get up early – well, not too early! – and visit the Grand Bazaar, where we could practise our bargaining skills. In the afternoon, we would take a ferry to the Asian side, eat fish sandwiches by the water and watch the sunset.\n\nSunday would be for museums and, of course, more food. I would make sure we tried at least five different desserts.\n\nIt might sound exhausting, but for me there is nothing more exciting than exploring a new place with people who make me laugh. By Sunday night, I would be tired but completely happy."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","The art of doing absolutely nothing\n\nWe are constantly told to make the most of our free time: learn a language, run a marathon, see the world. My idea of a perfect weekend, however, is gloriously unproductive.\n\nI would spend it at my grandparents' house in a small village in the Black Sea mountains, where the phone signal is unreliable and nobody seems to be in a hurry. My only company would be my grandparents, their elderly cat and perhaps my younger brother, provided he left his games console at home.\n\nI would wake up without an alarm to the smell of fresh bread and spend the morning reading in the garden, surrounded by tea plants and mist. In the afternoon, I might help my grandfather in his vegetable garden, although I suspect I would mostly be listening to his stories about the village fifty years ago.\n\nEvenings would be spent around the stove, playing cards and eating far too much.\n\nIt may not sound exciting, but in a world that never stops, I think the greatest luxury of all is having time to slow down."]]},{"id":"b2w4","part":2,"kind":"review","t":"A TV Series","in":"You see this announcement on an English-language website. Write your review in 140–190 words.","mail":"REVIEWS WANTED\nA TV series worth watching\nHave you watched a TV series recently that you think others would enjoy? Write a review telling us what it is about, what you liked or disliked, and who you would recommend it to.","pts":[["Konusunu anlat",["about","story","follows","plot","set in"]],["Beğendiklerini / beğenmediklerini söyle",["liked","loved","enjoyed","however","disappointing","weak"]],["Kime tavsiye ettiğini söyle",["recommend","anyone who","fans of","if you"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"A gripping mystery: The Lighthouse\n\nIf you are looking for something to watch on a long winter evening, The Lighthouse is an excellent choice.\n\nThe series is set on a small island off the coast of Scotland, where a young detective is sent to investigate the disappearance of a lighthouse keeper. As she talks to the island's few residents, she discovers that everyone seems to be hiding something.\n\nWhat I enjoyed most was the atmosphere. The wild landscapes and constant storms create a sense of isolation that keeps you on the edge of your seat. The acting is also superb, particularly the performance of the main character, who is both clever and vulnerable.\n\nMy only criticism is that the middle episodes are a little slow, and one or two storylines are never fully explained.\n\nOverall, I would highly recommend The Lighthouse to anyone who enjoys mysteries with complex characters. Just do not watch it alone at night!","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","Breaking news, broken hearts: Newsroom\n\nNewsroom, a drama about a group of young journalists working for a struggling newspaper, is one of the most gripping series I have watched this year.\n\nEach episode follows a different news story, from a political scandal to a missing child, but the real focus is on the characters and the difficult choices they face. Should they publish a story that is true but could ruin someone's life? How far should they go to get the truth?\n\nThe writing is sharp and often surprisingly funny, and the cast is excellent. I particularly enjoyed the relationship between the experienced editor and the ambitious new reporter, which is both touching and realistic.\n\nOn the negative side, the final season feels rushed, and some storylines are resolved too quickly. A few of the newsroom scenes are also rather unrealistic – I doubt real journalists have time for so many dramatic arguments!\n\nDespite these weaknesses, I would strongly recommend Newsroom to anyone who enjoys intelligent dramas with strong characters. It will also make you think twice about the news you read every day."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Small town, big secrets: a review of Hollow Creek\n\nAt first glance, Hollow Creek appears to be yet another crime series set in a sleepy village. Within the first ten minutes, however, it becomes clear that this is something rather more ambitious.\n\nThe story begins when a respected local teacher disappears on the night of the annual summer festival. Rather than following the investigation from the police's point of view, each episode is told through the eyes of a different villager, gradually revealing how their lives – and lies – are connected.\n\nWhat makes the series stand out is its patience. The director is not afraid of long, silent scenes, and the stunning landscape becomes almost a character in itself. The performances are uniformly strong, although the young actor playing the teacher's son deserves particular praise for a remarkably subtle performance.\n\nThat said, the slow pace will not suit everyone, and the final twist, while clever, stretches belief somewhat.\n\nAll in all, Hollow Creek is a thoughtful, beautifully made series that rewards viewers who are willing to pay attention. Fans of fast-moving action may be disappointed, but anyone who enjoys complex characters will be hooked."]]},{"id":"b2w5","part":2,"kind":"email","t":"Advice on a Summer Job","in":"You have received this email from your English-speaking friend Chris. Write your email in 140–190 words.","mail":"From: Chris\nI'm thinking of getting a summer job this year, but I'm not sure what kind of job would suit me. You had a summer job last year, didn't you? What did you do, and what did you learn from it? What advice would you give me?\nThanks,\nChris","pts":[["Ne iş yaptığını anlat",["worked","job","last summer","as a"]],["Ne öğrendiğini söyle",["learned","learnt","taught me","realised"]],["Tavsiye ver",["should","advice","recommend","if i were you","why don't you","make sure"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Hi Chris,\nGreat to hear from you! Getting a summer job is a brilliant idea – I'm sure you won't regret it.\n\nLast summer I worked as a waiter in a busy seaside restaurant. To be honest, the first week was a nightmare. I kept forgetting orders and once I dropped a whole tray of drinks! But I soon got the hang of it.\n\nThe job taught me a lot. I learned how to stay calm under pressure and how to deal with difficult customers politely. I also realised how important teamwork is – we only survived the busy evenings because we helped each other.\n\nAs for advice, I think you should choose a job where you meet lots of people, because you're so sociable. If I were you, I'd start applying now, as the best jobs go quickly. And make sure you ask about the working hours before you accept anything – some places expect you to work very late.\n\nGood luck, and let me know what you decide!\nBest wishes,\nDeniz","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","Hi Chris,\nThanks for your email. It's great that you're thinking about getting a summer job – I learned so much from mine.\n\nLast summer I worked at a children's summer camp near the lake. I helped organise games, took the kids swimming and made sure everyone was safe. It was exhausting, especially when it rained and thirty children were stuck indoors!\n\nThe most important thing I learned was patience. Some children were homesick or didn't want to join in, and I had to find ways to help them feel comfortable. I also became much more confident about speaking in front of groups.\n\nIf I were you, I'd think about what you enjoy before choosing. You love being outdoors, so maybe a job at a campsite or a sports centre would suit you. Also, try to get some experience that will look good on your CV – employers really value it.\n\nOne last piece of advice: don't forget to have some fun too. Make sure you have at least a couple of weeks free at the end of summer.\n\nGood luck!\nSelin"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Hi Chris,\nWhat a great idea! I'm sure a summer job will be a brilliant experience, even if it's hard work at times.\n\nAs you know, last summer I worked as an assistant in a small bookshop in the old town. I mainly served customers and arranged displays, but I also helped organise a couple of events with local authors, which was the highlight for me.\n\nThe job taught me far more than I expected. I discovered that I'm better at dealing with people than I thought, even the occasional rude customer! I also learned how a small business actually works – how much planning goes into ordering stock, for example – which has made me consider studying business at university.\n\nAs for advice, my main suggestion would be to choose something that might help you in the future, rather than simply the job that pays the most. Since you're interested in design, why not ask local studios or print shops whether they need help? Even if they don't advertise, they might be happy to take on someone enthusiastic.\n\nWhatever you choose, I'm sure you'll do brilliantly. Let me know how it goes!\nBest wishes,\nDeniz"]]},{"id":"b2w6","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Cars in City Centres","in":"In your English class you have been talking about transport. Now your English teacher has asked you to write an essay. Write your essay using all the notes and giving reasons for your point of view. Write 140–190 words.","mail":"Should private cars be banned from city centres?\n\nNotes – write about:\n1. pollution and noise\n2. public transport\n3. ............ (your own idea)","pts":[["Kirlilik ve gürültüden bahset",["pollution","noise","air","traffic","fumes","health"]],["Toplu taşımayı tartış",["public transport","bus","metro","train","tram","cheap","frequent"]],["Kendi fikrini ekle",["also","another","in addition","moreover","shops","business","disabled","elderly"]],["Sonuç / görüş",["in conclusion","to sum up","overall","on balance","in my opinion"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Many city centres are busy, noisy and polluted, so it is not surprising that some people want to ban private cars altogether. In my opinion, such a ban would be a good idea, provided that certain conditions are met.\n\nFirstly, cars are a major source of air pollution, which damages people's health, and the constant noise of traffic makes city life stressful. Without cars, streets would be cleaner and much quieter.\n\nHowever, a ban only works if public transport is reliable. If buses and trams are slow, infrequent or expensive, people will simply refuse to give up their cars. Cities therefore need to invest in cheap and frequent services first.\n\nAnother point to consider is the effect on local businesses. Some shop owners fear that customers will stay away, but experience in other cities suggests that pedestrian streets actually attract more visitors.\n\nOn balance, I believe that banning cars from the centre would improve city life, as long as alternatives are available.","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","Should private cars be banned from city centres?\n\nIt is easy to dream of car-free streets full of cafés and cyclists, but I believe that a complete ban would cause more problems than it solves.\n\nOf course, pollution and noise are serious issues. Traffic fumes harm our lungs, and the constant roar of engines makes city life tiring. However, modern electric cars produce no fumes and very little noise, so the problem is gradually becoming smaller without any need for a ban.\n\nA second point is that not everybody can use public transport. Elderly people, disabled passengers and parents with small children often depend on a car, and buses and trams are not always suitable for them. Shop owners also worry that customers who cannot park nearby will simply go to out-of-town shopping centres.\n\nA better solution, in my opinion, would be to limit cars rather than forbid them: higher parking charges, special zones and cheap, frequent buses would persuade most people to leave their cars at home. In conclusion, a ban is too extreme."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Should private cars be banned from city centres?\n\nFew policies divide urban opinion as sharply as the proposal to keep private cars out of city centres. Having considered both sides, I am convinced that a ban, introduced gradually, would benefit almost everybody.\n\nThe case for it is overwhelming. Exhaust fumes are linked to asthma and heart disease, while the incessant noise of traffic undermines the quality of life of residents. Streets that were once dominated by vehicles could be handed back to pedestrians, markets and children.\n\nNaturally, such a transformation depends on reliable public transport. Unless trams and buses are fast, frequent and affordable, motorists will be understandably reluctant to give up their cars. Exceptions should also be made for people with reduced mobility, as well as for deliveries.\n\nCritics claim that shops would suffer, yet evidence from cities such as Oslo and Ghent suggests that footfall actually rises once traffic disappears. On balance, therefore, the advantages clearly outweigh the drawbacks."]]},{"id":"b2w7","part":2,"kind":"review","t":"A Film Worth Watching","in":"You see this announcement on an English-language website. Write your review in 140–190 words.","mail":"REVIEWS WANTED\nA film you enjoyed\nHave you seen a film recently that you would like to tell other people about? Write a review saying what the film is about, what you liked or disliked, and whether you would recommend it.\nThe best reviews will be published on our website.","pts":[["Filmin konusunu kısaca anlat",["about","story","plot","tells","follows","set in"]],["Beğendiğin ya da beğenmediğin yönleri söyle",["acting","actor","music","special effects","script","ending","enjoyed","disappointed"]],["Tavsiye et ya da etme",["recommend","worth","would suggest","avoid","must-see"]],["Ilgi çekici bir başlık",["@title"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"A story that stays with you\n\nI went to see \"The Last Lighthouse\" without knowing much about it, and I came out of the cinema feeling that I had watched something special.\n\nThe film follows a retired sailor who agrees to look after a remote lighthouse for one winter. At first, he enjoys the peace, but when a storm cuts off the island, he discovers that he is not alone. I will not say more, because the plot has several surprises.\n\nWhat impressed me most was the acting. The main actor hardly speaks, yet his face tells the whole story. The photography is also stunning: every scene looks like a painting. On the negative side, the middle section is rather slow, and a few viewers might lose patience.\n\nOverall, I would strongly recommend this film to anyone who prefers atmosphere to action. Take a friend, and be prepared to talk about the ending afterwards.","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","A series I could not stop watching\n\nI am not usually a fan of science fiction, but \"The Quiet Planet\" completely changed my mind.\n\nThe series is set on a distant planet where a small group of scientists must survive after their spaceship breaks down. It follows their struggle to find water, food and, more importantly, a way to trust one another. What I liked most was the characters: they are ordinary, flawed people rather than heroes, which makes their decisions feel real.\n\nThe special effects are impressive but never take over the story, and the music, played mostly on a single piano, creates a wonderfully lonely atmosphere. On the other hand, the final episode left several questions unanswered, which annoyed me a little.\n\nAll the same, I would warmly recommend it to anyone who enjoys thoughtful stories. Watch it in the evening, with the lights off, and you will not regret it."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Comedy with a conscience: \"The Last Laugh\"\n\nWhen a friend suggested I watch \"The Last Laugh\", I expected a light-hearted comedy to take my mind off exams. What I actually got was one of the most thought-provoking films I have seen all year.\n\nThe story centres on an ageing comedian who discovers that his jokes have, over the decades, hurt more people than he ever realised. Rather than defending himself, he sets out to apologise to each of them, with hilarious and moving consequences.\n\nThe lead actor is magnificent: he switches from slapstick to heartbreak within a single scene. The script is equally sharp, although a handful of subplots feel unnecessary and slow the film down in the second half.\n\nUltimately, it succeeds because it makes you laugh and think at the same time. I would unreservedly recommend it, particularly to anyone who thinks comedy cannot be serious."]]},{"id":"b2w8","part":2,"kind":"email","t":"A Complaint About a Hotel","in":"You recently stayed in a hotel and were not satisfied. Write an email to the manager. Write 140–190 words.","mail":"Your email should:\n– explain what the problem was\n– say how it affected your stay\n– ask the manager to do something about it\n\nBegin your email \"Dear Mr Roberts,\"","pts":[["Sorunu açıkla",["problem","broken","dirty","noisy","did not work","was not","unfortunately"]],["Konaklamanı nasıl etkilediğini anlat",["as a result","because","ruined","spoil","unable","disappointed","affected"]],["Yöneticiden bir şey iste",["would like","request","refund","compensation","expect","please","could you"]],["Resmî bir üslup kullan",["i am writing","dear","yours sincerely","faithfully","i look forward"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Dear Mr Roberts,\n\nI am writing to complain about my recent stay at the Riverside Hotel from 3rd to 6th May.\n\nWhen we arrived, we discovered that the air conditioning in our room was not working. Although I reported the problem to reception on the first evening, nobody came to repair it until the day we left. As a result, the room was extremely hot at night, and we hardly slept.\n\nThe problem seriously affected our trip. We had planned to spend the days sightseeing, but we were too tired to leave the hotel on the second and third days. We were also disappointed that the staff did not seem to take our complaint seriously.\n\nI would therefore be grateful if you could refund at least part of the cost of the room. I would also appreciate an explanation of why the problem was not solved sooner.\n\nI look forward to hearing from you.\n\nYours sincerely,\nCan Aydın","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","Dear Mr Roberts,\n\nI am writing to express my disappointment with the service I received during my stay at your hotel from 14th to 17th August.\n\nWe booked a double room with a sea view, as shown on your website. On arrival, however, we were given a room overlooking the car park, and the receptionist told us that no other rooms were available. Moreover, the bathroom was dirty and the shower did not work properly.\n\nThis was extremely frustrating, as we had saved for months for this holiday. Instead of relaxing, we spent the first evening complaining at reception, and the problem with the shower was never fixed.\n\nI would therefore like to ask for a partial refund, since we did not receive the room we paid for. I would also be grateful if you could look into the matter so that other guests do not have the same experience.\n\nI hope to hear from you soon.\n\nYours sincerely,\nElif Demir"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Dear Mr Roberts,\n\nI am writing to bring to your attention several unsatisfactory aspects of my recent stay at your hotel, which I had expected to be a highlight of my trip.\n\nUpon arrival, I discovered that the room I had booked, at a considerable premium, had been given to somebody else. Although the receptionist apologised, the alternative she offered was noisy, as it faced the hotel's nightclub. Consequently, I was unable to sleep on either of the two nights I was there, which left me exhausted during an important business meeting.\n\nI should add that the staff, while polite, seemed unwilling to take responsibility for the error.\n\nIn view of this, I would appreciate a full refund for the first night, together with a written explanation of how such a mistake occurred. Unless the matter is resolved satisfactorily, I shall have no choice but to seek advice from a consumer organisation.\n\nI look forward to your prompt reply.\n\nYours sincerely,\nCan Aydın"]]}],"S":[{"id":"b2s1","p":1,"t":"Habits and Routines","ins":"Her soruya birkaç cümleyle, nedenler ve örnekler vererek cevap ver.","q":[["How do you usually spend your evenings?",30,["usually","tend to","unless"],["On weekdays, I tend to spend most evenings at home studying, although I always try to take a break to have dinner with my family. If I've finished my homework early, I might go for a walk or meet a friend for a coffee. On Fridays, we usually watch a film together, which is a nice way to start the weekend.","My evenings are quite busy, actually. Three times a week I go to football training, which finishes at about eight. After that, I'm usually too tired to do much, so I just eat, chat with my friends online and maybe watch an episode of a series before bed."]],["Do you prefer reading books or watching films? Why?",30,["I prefer","whereas","because"],["I'd say I prefer reading, mainly because I can use my imagination. When I read, I picture the characters and places in my own way, whereas films sometimes disappoint me if they're different from what I expected. Having said that, films are better when I'm tired and just want to relax.","To be honest, I prefer films. I spend a lot of time reading for school, so in my free time I want something more relaxing. I also love the music and the visual effects in films – a good soundtrack can make a story so much more powerful."]],["What's something new you'd like to learn in the future?",30,["I'd love to","I've always wanted","one day"],["I've always wanted to learn to sail. My uncle has a small boat on the coast, and every summer he promises to teach me, but we never seem to find the time. Being out on the open sea, completely in control of the boat, must be an amazing feeling.","I'd really like to learn Japanese. I'm a big fan of Japanese films and food, and I'm hoping to visit Japan after university. I know it's a difficult language, especially the writing, but I think the challenge would make it even more rewarding."]],["How important is it for you to have a routine?",30,["quite important","otherwise","I find that"],["Quite important, actually. I find that if I don't have a routine, I waste a lot of time deciding what to do and end up stressed before exams. Having fixed times for studying, exercise and sleep makes me more productive, and paradoxically gives me more free time.","Not very, to be honest. I get bored if every day is the same, so I prefer to plan my week loosely and decide things as I go. Of course, I need some routine for school, but at the weekends I like to be spontaneous."]]]},{"id":"b2s2","p":2,"t":"Ways of Learning","ins":"İki fotoğrafı 1 dakika karşılaştır ve soruyu cevapla.","img":[["👩‍🏫📚🧑‍🎓","Fotoğraf 1: Bir sınıfta öğretmen tahtada ders anlatıyor, öğrenciler not alıyor.","b2-classroom","A teacher is writing on the board in a classroom while three students sit at their desks and take notes."],["🧑‍💻🎧🏠","Fotoğraf 2: Bir genç evde kulaklıkla bilgisayardan çevrim içi ders izliyor.","b2-online","A young man with headphones is following an online lesson on his laptop at home."]],"q":[["Your photographs show people learning in different ways. Compare the photographs, and say what the advantages of learning in each way might be.",60,["both photos","whereas","in contrast","might","I suppose","seems to"],["Both photos show people learning, but in quite different settings. In the first one, there's a traditional classroom, with a teacher explaining something on the board and students taking notes, whereas in the second, a young man seems to be following an online lesson at home, wearing headphones. One advantage of the classroom is that students can ask questions immediately and learn from each other through discussion. The teacher can also see when someone is confused. In contrast, learning online is much more flexible. The young man can probably study whenever he wants and watch the lesson again if he doesn't understand something. I suppose he also saves time and money on travelling. However, he might find it harder to stay motivated without other people around him.","These two photographs both show ways of learning, but the atmosphere is very different. The classroom looks lively and quite formal – the students are sitting in rows and paying attention to the teacher. By contrast, the young man at home looks relaxed, sitting at his desk with a cup of coffee. I think the main advantage of learning in a classroom is the social side: you make friends, you can work in groups and you feel part of a community. Learning online, on the other hand, allows you to work at your own speed and choose exactly what you want to study. It's probably ideal for people who work or live far from a school."]],["Which way of learning do you prefer? Why?",30,["I'd go for","I prefer","because"],["I'd go for the classroom, mainly because I find it hard to concentrate on my own – I get distracted by my phone far too easily. I also enjoy discussing ideas with my classmates, which helps me remember things better.","Personally, I prefer learning online. I'm quite an independent person, and I like being able to pause the lesson, take notes and go back over the difficult parts as many times as I need. It also means I can fit my studies around other things, like sport or a part-time job."]]]},{"id":"b2s3","p":2,"t":"Celebrations","ins":"İki fotoğrafı 1 dakika karşılaştır ve soruyu cevapla.","img":[["🎉🎂👨‍👩‍👧‍👦","Fotoğraf 1: Evde, kalabalık bir ailenin doğum günü kutlaması.","b2-birthday","A family is celebrating a birthday at home. There is a cake with candles on the table and balloons in the room."],["🎆🎶🏙️","Fotoğraf 2: Şehir meydanında havai fişekli büyük bir yılbaşı kutlaması.","b2-fireworks","A big crowd is watching fireworks over a city at night."]],"q":[["Your photographs show people celebrating in different ways. Compare the photographs, and say why the people might be celebrating like this.",60,["both","while","perhaps","might be","I imagine"],["Both photographs show people celebrating, but the scale and atmosphere are completely different. The first one shows a family birthday party at home, with a big cake, balloons and lots of relatives around a table, while the second shows a huge crowd watching fireworks in a city square, probably on New Year's Eve. The family might be celebrating at home because it feels more personal – they can talk, share food and spend quality time together. It also seems quite relaxed and informal. The people in the city, on the other hand, perhaps want to feel part of something bigger. There's something exciting about counting down with thousands of strangers, and the fireworks and music create an atmosphere you can't get at home.","In the first picture, a family is celebrating a birthday in a living room, whereas the second picture shows a big public celebration with fireworks over a city. I imagine the family chose to celebrate at home because they want to make the person whose birthday it is feel special, and perhaps because there are young children and elderly relatives who might not enjoy a crowd. The people in the city are probably celebrating a national holiday or New Year, and they've come out to enjoy the spectacle together. Big events like that make people feel connected to their community, even if they don't know anyone around them."]],["Which kind of celebration would you prefer? Why?",30,["I'd rather","I prefer","because"],["I'd rather celebrate at home with my family. Big crowds make me a bit nervous, and I prefer having real conversations with people I care about. Also, the food is always better at home! And honestly, I think the best memories come from small, personal moments rather than big public events.","I'd probably choose the city celebration. I love the energy of big events, and watching fireworks with thousands of people is an experience you remember for years. Home celebrations are lovely, but they can feel quite similar from year to year."]]]},{"id":"b2s4","p":3,"t":"A School Trip","ins":"Önce seçenekleri tartış (2 dakika), sonra karar ver (1 dakika).","opts":["🏛️ a history museum","🏭 a factory","🌲 a national park","🎭 a theatre","🔬 a science centre"],"q":[["Imagine your school is planning a day trip for students. Here are some places they could visit. Talk about what students could learn from visiting these places.",120,["let's start with","what about","I think","that's true, but","students would learn"],["Let's start with the history museum. Students could learn about the past in a much more memorable way than from a textbook, especially if there are interactive exhibitions. A factory visit might sound boring, but it would show students how everyday things are actually made and what kinds of jobs exist, which could help them think about their future careers. The national park would be great for learning about nature and the environment, and students would get some fresh air and exercise too. A theatre trip could help students understand literature better – seeing a play performed is very different from reading it in class. Finally, a science centre is usually very hands-on, so students learn by doing experiments themselves, which is probably the most effective way to learn. I think each place has something to offer, but it depends on the age of the students and what they're studying at the moment.","I'll begin with the theatre. It's a great way to make literature come alive, and many students have never been to a real theatre, so it would be a new experience for them. The science centre is fun and educational, though some can be a bit childish for older students. The history museum is a classic choice; it's informative, but students might get bored if they just look at objects behind glass. A factory is interesting because it connects school subjects like chemistry or engineering to the real world. And the national park would teach students about ecosystems and environmental protection, and it would also be good for team building, since students would have to work together on activities like orienteering. So there are clear benefits to all of them. The question is really which one would offer the most to the greatest number of students, and whether the cost and travel time would be reasonable. A trip that's too far away might mean students spend more time on the bus than actually learning."]],["Now decide which place would be the best for a school trip.",60,["I think we should","the best option","because","although"],["I think the best option would be the science centre, because it's both educational and entertaining, and students of all ages would enjoy it. Students learn best when they're actively involved, and a science centre allows them to experiment and ask questions. The national park would be my second choice, although it depends a lot on the weather. Another advantage of the science centre is that it works well for mixed groups: students who aren't very academic often enjoy practical activities more than lessons, so everyone would get something out of it.","For me, the national park is the clear winner. Students spend most of their time indoors looking at screens, so a day outside would be really valuable. They'd learn about nature, get some exercise and probably talk to classmates they don't normally spend time with. It would also be relatively cheap, since entrance to most national parks is free, and teachers could organise activities linked to biology and geography lessons so that the day still has a clear educational purpose."]]]},{"id":"b2s5","p":4,"t":"Education","ins":"Görüşünü nedenler ve örneklerle açıkla.","q":[["Do you think students learn more outside the classroom than inside it?",45,["in some ways","however","for instance","I'd argue"],["In some ways, yes. For instance, you learn teamwork from playing sport, responsibility from part-time jobs and confidence from travelling or meeting new people. These are things that are difficult to teach in a classroom. However, I'd argue that the classroom is still essential for building basic knowledge, like maths, science and languages. Ideally, schools should combine both.","I don't think so. Outside the classroom, learning is often random – you might learn useful things, but you might also learn nothing at all. In a classroom, teachers plan lessons carefully so that knowledge builds step by step. Without that structure, most students would have big gaps in their education. Of course, experiences outside school are valuable, but they work best when they build on what students have already learned in class."]],["Should school days be shorter so that students have more free time?",45,["I'm not convinced","on the one hand","on the other hand"],["I'm not convinced. On the one hand, students would be less tired and have more time for hobbies and sport, which is good for their mental health. On the other hand, many of them would probably just spend the extra time on their phones. Also, if the school day were shorter, teachers would have to cover the same material in less time, which could increase stress.","Yes, I think they should. Many students are at school until late afternoon and then have hours of homework, so they have almost no free time. Shorter days would give them time to rest, exercise and pursue interests like music or art, which are just as important for their development. Some countries with shorter school days, like Finland, still achieve excellent results, so it's clearly possible."]],["How might education change in the next twenty years?",45,["I imagine","is likely to","will probably","might"],["I imagine technology is likely to play a much bigger role. Lessons will probably be more personalised, with programs adapting to each student's level and speed. Students might also learn more through virtual reality, for example visiting ancient Rome in a history lesson. But I think teachers will still be needed to motivate students and help them develop social skills.","I think there'll be a bigger focus on practical skills, like problem solving, communication and managing money, rather than memorising facts, since everyone can look up information instantly. Exams might also change, with more projects and less pressure on a single test at the end of the year. I think that would be fairer, because some students perform badly under exam pressure even when they understand the subject well."]]]},{"id":"b2s6","p":1,"t":"Technology and Habits","ins":"Sınav görevlisi günlük hayat ve teknoloji hakkında sorular soruyor. Nedenler ve örneklerle cevap ver.","q":[["How often do you use your phone during the day, and what do you use it for?",20,["I use it","mainly","whenever","for example"],["Mostly for communication and entertainment, to be honest. I message my friends, watch short videos and listen to podcasts on the bus. I also use it for school, for instance to translate words or check my timetable, so it's with me almost all day.","Quite a lot, I think. Between messages, music, maps and photos, I'd say it's in my hand for four or five hours a day. I know that sounds a lot, but most of it is useful rather than just scrolling."]],["Do you think people spend too much time online? Why?",25,["I think","on the one hand","however","for example"],["In many cases, yes. People check their phones during meals and even while talking to someone, which I find a bit sad. That said, being online also lets us learn and keep in touch, so I think the problem isn't the technology itself but how we use it.","I'd say it depends on the person. Some spend hours on social media without noticing, whereas others use the internet for study or work. Personally, I try to limit myself, because I feel tired when I've spent too long on a screen."]],["What technology would you find it hardest to live without?",20,["I would find it","without","mainly because","I rely on"],["Probably my phone, since it combines so many things: a camera, a map, a bank and a diary. If I lost it, I'd have to find new ways to do all of those at once, which would be very inconvenient.","I'd miss my laptop most, because I use it for homework and for my hobbies, such as editing videos. I could manage without my phone for a day or two, but without my laptop I wouldn't be able to study properly."]],["Tell us about a time when technology caused you a problem.",25,["I remember","it was","what happened","in the end"],["Yes, there was one occasion last year. I was writing an essay and the software closed without saving it, so I lost nearly two hours of work. I was furious at the time, but it taught me to back up everything. Since then I save my documents automatically online.","I remember buying train tickets online for a trip, and the website charged me twice. It took a week of emails to get the money back. What I learnt is to check payments carefully, and to keep every confirmation message."]]]},{"id":"b2s7","p":3,"t":"A Community Project","ins":"Gerçek sınavda bu bölüm bir partnerle yapılır. Burada iki tarafı da sen düşün: seçenekleri tek tek değerlendir, sonra karar ver.","q":[["A town wants to improve life for young people. Here are some things the council is thinking about. Talk about how each idea could help young people, and then decide which one is the most important.",40,["one advantage","it could help","on the other hand","that's true, but","I'd argue that","what about"],["I think a study room is a very good idea, because many students don't have a quiet place at home. It could also bring young people together. A music studio would be fantastic for a few, but it might not benefit everyone. The job fair is useful for older teenagers, though it would only happen once a year, whereas a sports centre could be used every day.","Let me start with the free bus. It would help teenagers who live far from the centre, and it would make it easier to go to training or classes. However, it doesn't create any new activities. A sports centre does, but it's expensive. As for the study room, it's cheap and useful, so it could be a realistic option."]],["Now decide which idea would be the most important for the town.",20,["I'd say","the most important","because","in the long run"],["Taking everything into account, I'd choose the study room, because it's inexpensive, helps with school results and is open to everyone. In the long term, better education improves young people's opportunities more than anything else.","In my opinion, the free bus service is the most important, because without transport young people can't reach any of the other facilities. It solves a basic problem first, and in the long run the other ideas could be added."]]],"opts":["🏟️ spor merkezi","🚌 ücretsiz otobüs","🎧 müzik stüdyosu","💼 iş fuarı","📚 ders çalışma odası"]}]},"C1":{"R":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","id":"c1r1","t":"Digital Minimalism","ty":"cloze","title":"Digital minimalism","p":"A growing number of people are choosing to (1) ______ back on their use of technology. So-called digital minimalists do not reject technology outright; rather, they are (2) ______ about which tools they allow into their lives. The movement has (3) ______ particular popularity among young professionals who feel that constant notifications are taking a (4) ______ on their concentration. Advocates argue that we have drifted into habits without ever consciously (5) ______ whether they serve us. Critics, (6) ______, contend that the movement is something of a luxury, available mainly to those whose jobs do not (7) ______ them to be constantly reachable. Whatever its limitations, digital minimalism has undoubtedly (8) ______ a nerve.","o":[[["cut","set","hold","take"],0,["collocation"]],[["selective","elective","select","selected"],0,["vocab"]],[["got","gained","reached","made"],1,["collocation"]],[["cost","price","toll","charge"],2,["collocation"]],[["questioning","arguing","disputing","objecting"],0,["vocab"]],[["meanwhile","therefore","likewise","furthermore"],0,["advanced-linkers"]],[["demand","insist","require","press"],2,["vocab"]],[["struck","beaten","reached","met"],0,["collocation"]]],"e":["cut back on: azaltmak.","selective about: seçici.","gain popularity: popülerlik kazanmak.","take a toll on: olumsuz etkilemek, yıpratmak.","question whether: -ip -mediğini sorgulamak.","meanwhile: öte yandan (karşı görüşe geçiş).","require someone to do: -mesini gerektirmek.","strike a nerve: hassas bir noktaya dokunmak."]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Kelime türetme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Her boşluk için verilen kök kelimeyi uygun biçime getir.","id":"c1r3","t":"Bilingualism and the Brain","ty":"open","title":"Bilingualism and the brain","st":["ABLE","ADVANTAGE","STAND","FLEXIBLE","CONVINCE","APPEAR","COMPARE","DENY"],"p":"For much of the twentieth century, raising children with two languages was thought to hinder their (1) ______ to learn. Today, the picture looks very different, and many researchers argue that bilingualism confers clear cognitive (2) ______. Bilingual people, it is claimed, have an (3) ______ ability to switch between tasks, a sign of greater mental (4) ______. However, recent studies have failed to replicate some of the more (5) ______ early findings. In some cases, the (6) ______ benefits seem to disappear when researchers take into account factors such as income and education. Still, even sceptics agree that, (7) ______ speaking, learning another language does no harm, and the cultural benefits are (8) ______.","a":[["ability"],["advantages"],["outstanding"],["flexibility"],["convincing"],["apparent"],["comparatively","relatively"],["undeniable"]],"tg":[["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"]],"e":["their + isim: ability.","confer advantages: avantaj sağlamak (çoğul).","an + sıfat: outstanding (üstün).","greater + isim: flexibility.","more + sıfat: convincing (ikna edici).","the + sıfat + benefits: apparent (görünürdeki).","comparatively speaking: göreceli olarak.","are + sıfat, olumlu anlam: undeniable (inkâr edilemez)."]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 3–6 kelime yaz.","id":"c1r4","t":"Key Word Transformations","ty":"kwt","q":[{"s1":"I had never seen such a beautiful sunset before.","key":"HAD","s2":"Never before ______ such a beautiful sunset.","a":["had i seen"],"tg":["inversion"],"e":"Never before + devrik: had I seen.","mx":6},{"s1":"It's possible that the train has been delayed.","key":"MAY","s2":"The train ______ delayed.","a":["may have been"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"may have been + V3.","mx":6},{"s1":"He was extremely tired, but he kept working.","key":"DESPITE","s2":"He kept working ______ extremely tired.","a":["despite being","despite the fact that he was"],"tg":["linkers-contrast-purpose"],"e":"despite + -ing / despite the fact that.","mx":6},{"s1":"\"You should really try the fish,\" the waiter said to us.","key":"RECOMMENDED","s2":"The waiter ______ the fish.","a":["recommended that we try","recommended trying","recommended that we should try","recommended us to try"],"tg":["reporting-verbs"],"e":"recommend + -ing / recommend that + özne + (should) fiil.","mx":6},{"s1":"As soon as she arrived, the meeting started.","key":"SOONER","s2":"No ______ than the meeting started.","a":["sooner had she arrived"],"tg":["inversion"],"e":"No sooner had + özne + V3 + than.","mx":6},{"s1":"It doesn't matter how hard you try, you won't convince him.","key":"HOWEVER","s2":"You won't convince him, ______ try.","a":["however hard you","however hard you may"],"tg":["advanced-linkers"],"e":"however + sıfat/zarf: ne kadar ... olursa olsun.","mx":6}]},{"k":"R7","nm":"Okuma 7 · Paragraf yerleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 7","ins":"Metinden altı paragraf çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun paragrafı (A–G) seç. Bir paragraf fazladır.","id":"c1r7","t":"Walking the Length of a Country","ty":"gap","title":"Walking the length of a country","p":"Last spring I set out to walk the entire length of Britain, from Land's End to John o' Groats, a distance of roughly 1,200 miles. I had no particular reason, other than a growing sense that I had spent too much of my life looking at screens.\n(1) ______\nThe first week was brutal. My boots, which I had foolishly bought just before setting off, gave me blisters so painful that I considered giving up on the third day.\n(2) ______\nBy the second week, however, something had shifted. My body had adjusted, and I began to notice things I would normally have rushed past.\n(3) ______\nThe people I met along the way were another revelation. Farmers invited me in for tea; a retired couple let me sleep in their spare room when a storm made camping impossible.\n(4) ______\nThere were low moments, of course. Somewhere in the north of England, after five consecutive days of rain, I sat in a bus shelter and wondered what on earth I was doing.\n(5) ______\nWhen I finally reached the northern tip of Scotland, I felt less triumph than a strange sadness that it was over.\n(6) ______","opts":[["A","A kind man in a nearby pharmacy, seeing me limp in, spent half an hour showing me how to tape my feet properly. I have never been so grateful to a stranger."],["B","I had imagined that the walk would be a solitary experience, but it turned out to be anything but."],["C","The way the landscape changed gradually from one region to the next, the different accents, even the shapes of the hedges – all of it became fascinating."],["D","It was a text from my sister, telling me she was proud of me, that persuaded me to keep going."],["E","Friends were sceptical. Most assumed I would last a week before catching a train home."],["F","Since returning, I have tried to hold on to the slower pace of those months, with mixed success."],["G","I had trained for months beforehand, walking twenty miles every weekend and studying the whole route in detail."]],"a":["E","A","C","B","D","F"],"tg":[["coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence"]],"e":["Yola çıkış nedeni → arkadaşlar şüpheliydi: E.","Su toplamış ayaklar → eczacı ayaklarını bantlamayı gösterdi: A.","\"notice things I would normally have rushed past\" → manzara, aksanlar: C.","Yardımsever insanlar paragrafından sonra: \"Yalnız bir deneyim olacağını sanmıştım ama hiç de öyle olmadı\": B.","Durakta ne yaptığını sorguladı → kız kardeşinin mesajı devam ettirdi: D.","Bitiş → dönünce yavaş tempoyu korumaya çalıştı: F. G (aylarca antrenman) aceleyle alınmış botlarla çelişir; fazladır."]},{"k":"R8","nm":"Okuma 8 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 8","ins":"Metin bölümlerini (A–E) oku. Her soru için doğru bölümü seç. Bölümler birden fazla kez seçilebilir.","id":"c1r8","t":"Four Cities","ty":"match","opts":[["A","Lisbon","I moved to Lisbon for the light and stayed for the people. What I hadn't expected was how difficult it would be to find affordable housing – prices have risen sharply as more foreigners like me have arrived, which makes me feel slightly uncomfortable."],["B","Seoul","Seoul is extraordinarily efficient; everything from the metro to online banking works seamlessly. The pace, though, can be exhausting, and it took me a long time to build friendships, partly because colleagues tend to socialise only within work."],["C","Vancouver","Living in Vancouver means having mountains and ocean on your doorstep. I spend almost every weekend hiking. The downside is the rain – months of it – which affects my mood far more than I anticipated."],["D","Cape Town","Cape Town is breathtakingly beautiful, and I have never felt so creative. But the inequality is impossible to ignore, and I have had to think hard about my own position as a relatively privileged newcomer."]],"q":[{"q":"Who feels some guilt about their effect on the local area?","a":"A","tg":["inference"],"e":"Yabancılar geldikçe fiyatlar arttı; kendini rahatsız hissediyor."},{"q":"Who was surprised by how the climate affected them?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Yağmur moralini beklediğinden çok etkiliyor."},{"q":"Who found it hard to form social connections?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Arkadaşlık kurmak uzun sürdü."},{"q":"Who mentions a positive effect on their work or creativity?","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"\"I have never felt so creative\"."},{"q":"Who praises the city's practical systems?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Metro ve bankacılık sorunsuz."},{"q":"Who has reflected on social differences in the city?","a":"D","tg":["inference"],"e":"Eşitsizlik ve kendi ayrıcalıklı konumu."}]},{"k":"R4","id":"c1r4b","t":"Key Word Transformations 2","ty":"kwt","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 3–6 kelime yaz.","title":"Key word transformations 2","q":[{"s1":"People think that the company has lost millions.","key":"THOUGHT","s2":"The company ______ lost millions.","a":["is thought to have"],"tg":["passive-advanced"],"e":"is thought to have + V3: geçmişe dönük yaygın kanı.","mx":6},{"s1":"I only found out about the problem when you told me.","key":"DID","s2":"Not until you told me ______ about the problem.","a":["did i find out","did i learn","did i know","did i hear"],"tg":["inversion"],"e":"Not until ... + devrik yapı: did I + yalın fiil.","mx":6},{"s1":"She regrets not accepting the job offer.","key":"WISHES","s2":"She ______ the job offer.","a":["wishes she had accepted","wishes she'd accepted"],"tg":["wish-if-only"],"e":"Geçmiş pişmanlık: wish + had + V3.","mx":6},{"s1":"The manager made us work late.","key":"MADE","s2":"We ______ work late by the manager.","a":["were made to"],"tg":["passive-voice"],"e":"make aktifte to almaz, pasifte to alır: were made to work.","mx":6},{"s1":"He took no notice of the warning.","key":"ATTENTION","s2":"He ______ to the warning.","a":["paid no attention","paid little attention","paid no attention"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"pay attention to: dikkat etmek.","mx":6},{"s1":"Despite his injury, he finished the race.","key":"ALTHOUGH","s2":"He finished the race ______ injured.","a":["although he was","although he had been","although he were"],"tg":["linkers-contrast-purpose"],"e":"although + özne + fiil: despite + isim yerine.","mx":6}]},{"k":"R4","id":"c1r4c","t":"Key Word Transformations 3","ty":"kwt","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 3–6 kelime yaz.","title":"Key word transformations 3","q":[{"s1":"I last visited the museum five years ago.","key":"BEEN","s2":"I ______ the museum for five years.","a":["haven't been to","have not been to"],"tg":["present-perfect"],"e":"for five years: have not been to.","mx":6},{"s1":"You're not allowed to park here.","key":"MUST","s2":"You ______ here.","a":["must not park","mustn't park"],"tg":["may-might"],"e":"mustn't: yasak.","mx":6},{"s1":"The film was so boring that we left early.","key":"SUCH","s2":"It was ______ we left early.","a":["such a boring film that"],"tg":["so-such-too-enough"],"e":"such a + sıfat + isim + that.","mx":6},{"s1":"Someone is going to cut my hair tomorrow.","key":"HAVING","s2":"I ______ cut tomorrow.","a":["am having my hair"],"tg":["causative"],"e":"have + nesne + V3: başkasına yaptırma.","mx":6},{"s1":"He was so nervous that he couldn't speak.","key":"TOO","s2":"He ______ nervous to speak.","a":["was too"],"tg":["so-such-too-enough"],"e":"too + sıfat + to: -emeyecek kadar.","mx":6},{"s1":"If you hadn't helped me, I would have failed.","key":"BUT","s2":"I would have failed ______ your help.","a":["but for"],"tg":["third-conditional"],"e":"but for + isim: -in olmaması durumunda.","mx":6}]},{"k":"R1","id":"c1r1b","t":"Urban Beekeeping","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","title":"Urban beekeeping","p":"Beekeeping has long been associated with the countryside, but in recent years it has (1) ______ a surprising foothold in cities. Rooftop hives now (2) ______ the skylines of London, Paris and New York. Supporters point (3) ______ that urban bees are healthier than their rural cousins, which often face pesticides and a lack of variety in their diet. City gardens, (4) ______, offer flowers that bloom throughout the year. Nevertheless, some ecologists (5) ______ a note of caution, arguing that too many hives may (6) ______ with wild pollinators for scarce resources. They suggest that planting more flowers would be far more (7) ______ in supporting insects than adding hives. The debate is likely to (8) ______ as the trend grows.","o":[[["gained","earned","obtained","taken"],0,["collocation"]],[["dominate","dot","dye","dig"],1,["vocab"]],[["out","up","at","to"],0,["vocab"]],[["however","therefore","moreover","otherwise"],0,["advanced-linkers"]],[["sound","ring","give","play"],0,["collocation"]],[["compete","combat","rival","oppose"],0,["vocab"]],[["effective","productive","capable","powerful"],0,["collocation"]],[["intensify","intend","intervene","intrude"],0,["vocab"]]],"e":["gain a foothold: ayak basmak, yerleşmek.","dot the skylines: ufuk çizgisine serpiştirilmiş olmak.","point out that: -diğine dikkat çekmek.","however: ama. therefore/moreover bu karşılaştırmaya uymaz.","sound a note of caution: temkinli olunması gerektiğini söylemek.","compete with ... for: -le ... için rekabet etmek.","effective in + -ing: -mede etkili.","intensify: şiddetlenmek, yoğunlaşmak."]},{"k":"R3","id":"c1r3b","t":"The Slow Food Movement","ty":"open","nm":"Okuma 3 · Kelime türetme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Her boşluk için verilen kök kelimeyi uygun biçime getir.","title":"The slow food movement","st":["EXPAND","EMPHASISE","CARE","REAL","ENVIRONMENT","ACHIEVE","SURPRISE","SOFT"],"p":"The (1) ______ of fast-food chains in the late twentieth century transformed the way many people eat. In response, a group of Italian campaigners launched a movement that (2) ______ the value of traditional cooking. Its members argue that meals should be (3) ______ prepared from local ingredients and enjoyed without hurry. Critics dismiss the idea as (4) ______ for those who cannot afford fresh produce, yet supporters point to the (5) ______ benefits of eating seasonally, from better flavour to reduced waste. Whatever one's view, the movement's greatest (6) ______ lies in having made people think about where their food comes from. Its (7) ______ rapid spread to over a hundred countries has even (8) ______ some of its harshest critics.","a":[["expansion"],["emphasises","emphasizes"],["carefully"],["unrealistic"],["environmental"],["achievement"],["surprisingly"],["softened"]],"tg":[["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"]],"e":["The + isim: expansion (yayılma).","that + fiil: emphasises (3. tekil, geniş zaman).","fiili niteleyen zarf: carefully.","olumsuz sıfat: unrealistic (gerçekçi olmayan).","benefits niteleyen sıfat: environmental.","greatest + isim: achievement (başarı).","sıfatı niteleyen zarf: surprisingly rapid.","has + V3: softened (yumuşattı)."]},{"k":"R1","id":"c1r1c","t":"Describing a Chart","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","title":"Describing a chart","p":"The chart (1) ______ the average number of hours that students spent studying each week between 2000 and 2020. Overall, study time (2) ______ steadily until 2010, after which it levelled (3) ______. Female students consistently studied longer than male students, although the gap (4) ______ narrower over the period. The most striking feature is the sharp (5) ______ in 2015, which can be (6) ______ to a change in exam rules. In (7) ______, the figures for part-time students remained relatively (8) ______ throughout.","o":[[["illustrates","illuminates","imitates","inspects"],0,["vocab"]],[["rose","raised","arose","rised"],0,["past-simple"]],[["off","away","in","by"],0,["vocab"]],[["became","widened","rose","expanded"],0,["vocab"]],[["decline","decrease down","lessening","lower"],0,["collocation"]],[["attributed","contributed","distributed","submitted"],0,["collocation"]],[["contrast","contradiction","conclusion","comparison"],0,["collocation"]],[["stable","steady up","static up","stability"],0,["vocab"]]],"e":["illustrate: (grafik) göstermek.","rose: arttı (rise düzensiz, nesnesiz fiil).","level off: sabitlenmek, düzleşmek.","became narrower: daha dar hale geldi (grow narrower de olur ama bu şıkta became doğru).","a sharp decline: keskin düşüş.","be attributed to: -e bağlanmak, -in sonucu sayılmak.","in contrast: buna karşılık.","remain relatively stable: nispeten sabit kalmak."]},{"k":"R4","id":"c1r4d","t":"Key Word Transformations 4","ty":"kwt","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 3–6 kelime yaz.","title":"Key word transformations 4","q":[{"s1":"The rise in prices was caused by bad harvests.","key":"RESULT","s2":"The rise in prices ______ bad harvests.","a":["was the result of","was a result of"],"tg":["advanced-linkers"],"e":"be the result of: -in sonucudur.","mx":6},{"s1":"Many experts believe that the policy will fail.","key":"WIDELY","s2":"It ______ that the policy will fail.","a":["is widely believed","is widely thought","is widely accepted"],"tg":["passive-advanced"],"e":"It is widely believed that ...: akademik edilgen yapı.","mx":6},{"s1":"Sales were about the same in the two years.","key":"LITTLE","s2":"There ______ in sales between the two years.","a":["was little difference","was little change"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"little difference: çok az fark.","mx":6},{"s1":"Unemployment fell sharply in 2015.","key":"DROP","s2":"There ______ unemployment in 2015.","a":["was a sharp drop in"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"a sharp drop in: ... -de keskin düşüş.","mx":6},{"s1":"The survey included 500 people.","key":"UP","s2":"The survey sample ______ 500 people.","a":["was made up of"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"be made up of: -den oluşmak.","mx":6},{"s1":"It is possible that the results are wrong.","key":"MAY","s2":"The results ______ wrong.","a":["may be","may well be"],"tg":["may-might"],"e":"may be: olabilir.","mx":6}]},{"k":"R1","id":"c1r1d","t":"Green Spaces in Cities","ty":"cloze","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","title":"Green spaces in cities","p":"Urban planners increasingly (1) ______ that green spaces are essential to the health of city dwellers. Parks offer a refuge from the noise and pollution that (2) ______ big cities, and studies suggest that even a short walk among trees can (3) ______ stress. However, land in city centres is scarce and (4) ______ expensive, so developers often argue that every square metre should be used for housing. Campaigners point out that the long-term benefits (5) ______ the short-term costs. Some cities have found a (6) ______ by turning rooftops and abandoned railway lines into gardens. Such projects show that it is possible to (7) ______ the needs of residents with those of the economy, and they have (8) ______ interest from planners around the world.","o":[[["recognise","realise","remark","regard"],0,["vocab"]],[["characterise","describe","illustrate","indicate"],0,["vocab"]],[["reduce","relate","rotate","reject"],0,["collocation"]],[["extremely","extreme","extremity","more extreme"],0,["vocab"]],[["outweigh","overcome","overtake","overlook"],0,["vocab"]],[["compromise","conclusion","comparison","combination"],0,["collocation"]],[["reconcile","resemble","reflect","reserve"],0,["collocation"]],[["attracted","attached","attributed","attempted"],0,["collocation"]]],"e":["recognise that: -diğini kabul etmek, fark etmek.","characterise: nitelemek, belirgin özellik olmak.","reduce stress: stresi azaltmak.","extremely expensive: son derece pahalı (zarf + sıfat).","outweigh: ağır basmak.","find a compromise: orta yol bulmak.","reconcile ... with ...: ... ile ... uzlaştırmak.","attract interest: ilgi çekmek."]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_choice2","t":"The Paradox of Choice","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Paradox of Choice","p":["Conventional wisdom holds that the more options we have, the happier we are. Freedom of choice is widely regarded as one of the great achievements of modern societies: supermarkets offer dozens of kinds of bread, streaming services provide thousands of films, and online shops allow us to compare hundreds of nearly identical products at any hour of the day. Yet a growing body of research suggests that an abundance of choice can be paralysing rather than liberating.","In one well-known experiment, shoppers in a food store were offered either a small selection of jams or a very large one. The larger display attracted more attention, and more people stopped to taste the jams. But customers who saw the smaller selection were considerably more likely to buy something. Similar results have been found in studies of retirement plans, where employees confronted with many investment funds were less likely to enrol in any of them at all.","One explanation is that every additional option raises our expectations and increases the cost of a wrong decision. When we eventually choose, we are haunted by the thought that a better alternative was available, a feeling that psychologists call regret. Moreover, when something disappoints us, we have nobody to blame but ourselves; in a world with fewer options, we could attribute disappointment to circumstances. Psychologist Barry Schwartz has argued that this is one reason why levels of depression have risen in wealthy societies, even though people have more freedom than ever before.","Researchers also distinguish between two types of decision makers. \"Maximisers\" try to find the absolute best option and will compare every alternative before they commit. \"Satisficers\", on the other hand, settle for the first option that meets their needs. Surprisingly, maximisers often achieve objectively better results, such as higher-paying jobs, but they tend to be less satisfied with their decisions than satisficers, because they constantly wonder whether an even better option exists.","This does not mean that choice is inherently harmful. Rather, it implies that the quality of a decision depends on how we make it. Setting clear criteria in advance, limiting the number of alternatives we consider, and accepting that no choice is perfect can turn an overwhelming process into a manageable one. Some people even deliberately reduce their options, for example by wearing similar clothes every day, in order to save mental energy for the decisions that really matter."],"q":[{"q":"What did the jam experiment show?","o":["Bigger displays drew more attention and more sales.","Bigger displays drew more attention but fewer sales.","Smaller displays drew more attention but fewer sales.","Shoppers lost interest in the jam altogether."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Büyük sergi dikkat çekti ama küçük sergide satın alma olasılığı daha yüksekti."},{"q":"What happened in the studies of retirement plans?","o":["Staff offered many funds were less likely to enrol.","Staff offered many funds chose the costliest one.","Staff offered many funds all picked the same plan.","Staff offered many funds could not understand them."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"were less likely to enrol in any of them at all.\""},{"q":"According to the text, why can more options reduce satisfaction?","o":["They raise expectations and the cost of a wrong choice.","They take longer to produce and so they cost more.","They make it much harder to compare the real prices.","They are usually of lower quality than few options."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"every additional option raises our expectations and increases the cost of a wrong decision.\""},{"q":"What is true of \"maximisers\"?","o":["They feel happier with the choices they make.","They rarely regret a decision once it is made.","They try to avoid important decisions.","They often do better but feel less satisfied."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"often achieve objectively better results... but they tend to be less satisfied.\""},{"q":"What does Barry Schwartz argue?","o":["Wealth always makes people far more depressed.","Choice improves happiness in almost every case.","Supermarkets ought to offer far fewer products.","Depression has risen partly through self-blame."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Daha çok seçenek = hayal kırıklığı için sadece kendimizi suçlamak."},{"q":"What is the writer's overall attitude to choice?","o":["It is harmful in nearly every kind of situation.","It is harmless as long as prices are low.","Its effect depends on how decisions are made.","It should be kept well away from all consumers."],"a":2,"tg":["gist"],"e":"\"the quality of a decision depends on how we make it.\""},{"q":"The phrase \"settle for\" means…","o":["move somewhere to live more permanently","argue about a small difference","accept something less than the best","search carefully for a better solution"],"a":2,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"settle for = (en iyisi olmasa da) yetinmek."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_green2","t":"Green Spaces in Crowded Cities","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Green Spaces in Crowded Cities","p":["As urban populations continue to swell, city planners face a dilemma: land is scarce and expensive, yet residents increasingly demand parks and gardens. According to the United Nations, more than two thirds of the world's people will live in cities by the middle of this century, and the pressure on every square metre will grow accordingly. For a long time, green areas were regarded as a luxury that could be sacrificed whenever developers needed room for housing or offices. That view is now being challenged.","Studies indicate that access to green space is associated with lower stress levels, better physical health and stronger community ties. Patients in hospital rooms with a view of trees have been found to recover faster than patients who look at a brick wall. Parks also give children a safe place to play and elderly people a reason to leave their flats, which helps to prevent loneliness. Trees cool the surrounding air by several degrees, which is becoming vital as heatwaves grow more frequent; in some neighbourhoods, the temperature on a tree-lined street can be ten degrees lower than on a nearby asphalt road. Moreover, parks absorb rainwater, reducing the risk of flooding after heavy storms.","Critics counter that such benefits are unevenly distributed. Wealthier districts tend to enjoy leafy avenues and well-kept parks, while poorer neighbourhoods are left with concrete, noise and traffic. Researchers describe this as a form of environmental inequality, since the people who most need the cooling and cleaning effects of vegetation are often the ones who have the least access to it. Some cities have therefore adopted policies requiring that every resident live within a short walk of a park.","Creative solutions have emerged where space is limited. Planners have turned abandoned railway lines into elevated gardens, covered roofs with grass and flowers, and planted vertical forests on the sides of tall apartment blocks. In several cities, residents are even encouraged to transform unused corners of land into small community gardens, which brings neighbours together and provides fresh vegetables.","Yet a paradox remains. When a neighbourhood becomes greener and more attractive, property prices tend to rise, and this process, often called \"green gentrification\", can push out the very people the improvements were meant to help. Whether ambitious green targets can be met without raising housing costs, and who should pay for them, remains hotly debated."],"q":[{"q":"What was the traditional view of green spaces?","o":["They were essential for every city planner.","They were cheaper to build than housing.","They were seen as a danger to public health.","They were a luxury that could be sacrificed."],"a":3,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"regarded as a luxury that could be sacrificed.\""},{"q":"Which benefit of green space is NOT mentioned?","o":["Cooling the air in hot weather","Absorbing rainwater during storms","Reducing the price of land","Helping patients recover faster"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Arazi fiyatlarının düşmesi metinde yok."},{"q":"What is the main criticism of the distribution of green space?","o":["The costs are hidden from taxpayers.","The parks are poorly looked after.","The benefits are not shared equally.","The trees there cause local flooding."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"such benefits are unevenly distributed.\""},{"q":"Which creative solution is mentioned?","o":["Moving parks underground below roads","Turning old railway lines into gardens","Closing every road in the city centre","Replacing parks with large car parks"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"turned abandoned railway lines into elevated gardens.\""},{"q":"What is \"green gentrification\"?","o":["A tax that governments charge on very large parks","Prices rising in a greener area, forcing out the poor","Planting trees for free along all the busy roads","Painting old buildings green to save a lot of energy"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Son paragrafta açıklanıyor: yeşillenen mahallede fiyatlar artar, yoksullar taşınmak zorunda kalır."},{"q":"What is the \"hotly debated\" issue at the end?","o":["Whether green space can grow without higher housing costs","Whether parks are an attractive feature of modern cities","Whether trees really do cool the air in hot summers","Whether heatwaves will become more common in future"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Son cümle: hedefler konut maliyetlerini artırmadan karşılanabilir mi ve bedeli kim ödemeli?"}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_memory4","t":"The Unreliable Witness Within","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Unreliable Witness Within","p":["The common metaphor for memory is a recording device: we imagine that experiences are stored somewhere in the brain like footage on a hard drive, to be played back when needed. Decades of psychological research have shown that this picture is deeply misleading. Remembering is not retrieval but reconstruction. Each time we recall an event, the brain assembles it afresh from fragments, filling the gaps with expectations, general knowledge, and information acquired later. The result feels vivid and authentic, yet it may differ in significant ways from what actually occurred.","The pioneering experiments of Elizabeth Loftus in the 1970s demonstrated how easily this reconstruction can be influenced. Participants who watched footage of a car accident were asked how fast the vehicles were going when they 'smashed into' each other, or alternatively when they 'hit' each other. Those who heard the more forceful verb gave higher speed estimates and, a week later, were more likely to report having seen broken glass, although none had appeared in the film. A single word, introduced after the event, had altered what people believed they had witnessed.","Subsequent studies pushed the findings further. Researchers managed to persuade a considerable proportion of volunteers that they had been lost in a shopping mall as children, an event that never happened, by presenting it alongside genuine memories supplied by relatives. Some participants not only accepted the story but elaborated it with convincing detail. The implication is unsettling: confidence and vividness, the qualities by which we usually judge our own recollections, are poor indicators of accuracy.","Nowhere are these findings more consequential than in the courtroom. Eyewitness testimony carries enormous persuasive weight, since jurors tend to trust a confident witness who describes a crime in detail. Yet analyses of cases in which convictions were later overturned by DNA evidence reveal that mistaken identification was a contributing factor in a large majority. Witnesses had, in good faith, selected the wrong person from a line-up, often because the procedure itself subtly guided them. A detective who knows the suspect's identity may inadvertently signal it through tone or expression, and a witness who is told that the culprit is 'definitely in the group' feels pressure to choose someone.","In response, many jurisdictions have reformed their procedures. Line-ups are now frequently administered by officers unaware of who the suspect is, witnesses are told that the offender may not be present, and their level of certainty is recorded immediately, before feedback can inflate it. Some researchers also argue that the first, spontaneous identification is more reliable than later ones, since repeated questioning gives the mind opportunities to blend genuine memory with suggestion.","It would be mistaken, however, to conclude that memory is simply worthless. In most everyday circumstances, the gist of what we remember is accurate, and the reconstructive nature of memory serves a purpose. It allows us to generalise from past experience, to imagine future situations, and to avoid being burdened by every trivial detail. Indeed, some scientists argue that the same system that produces false memories is the one that makes planning and creativity possible. The flaws, in other words, may be the price of a remarkably flexible faculty.","The practical lesson is a measured scepticism. When an account is crucial, whether in a legal case, a historical inquiry, or a family dispute, it should be corroborated by independent evidence such as documents, recordings, or other witnesses. And when our own memory insists on a version of events that others contest, humility is warranted. Feeling certain is not the same as being right, and recognising that distinction may be among the most useful habits a thoughtful person can cultivate."],"q":[{"q":"What is the author's main criticism of the \"recording device\" metaphor?","o":["Recording devices are too technical to compare.","Memory is rebuilt each time rather than retrieved.","Hard drives break down more often than memory.","Memory leaves out the emotions of an event."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Remembering is not retrieval but reconstruction."},{"q":"What did Loftus's car accident experiment show?","o":["A verb added after the event changed beliefs.","People recalled the car speeds quite accurately.","Film proved more reliable than eyewitnesses.","Broken glass was remembered by every witness."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"A single word introduced after the event altered beliefs, including false glass."},{"q":"Why is the \"lost in a shopping mall\" study unsettling?","o":["People believed a false event, so confidence misleads.","The children in the study were left in real danger.","Parents refused to help with the experiment at all.","The study was banned in several countries later."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Confidence and vividness are poor indicators of accuracy."},{"q":"How can a line-up procedure mislead a witness?","o":["Suspects often speak too quietly to be heard clearly.","Most witnesses in a line-up are far too young.","The lights in the room make faces hard to see.","An officer may signal the suspect or pressure them."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"A detective may inadvertently signal the suspect; a witness feels pressure to choose."},{"q":"What reform is mentioned?","o":["A ban on using eyewitnesses in serious trials","Longer trials with more time for each witness","Payments made to witnesses for their time and effort","Line-ups run by officers unaware of the suspect"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Line-ups administered by officers unaware of who the suspect is."},{"q":"What positive aspect of reconstructive memory is suggested?","o":["It helps police to solve serious crimes more quickly.","It protects people from forgetting details.","It supports generalising, planning and creativity.","It removes painful emotions from old memories."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"The same system makes planning and creativity possible."},{"q":"What does the author recommend?","o":["Complete distrust of everything people remember","Trust in witnesses who sound very confident","Measured scepticism and proof from other sources","Keeping eyewitnesses out of the courtroom"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Crucial accounts should be corroborated by independent evidence."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_cities4","t":"The Quiet Revolution in Urban Planning","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Quiet Revolution in Urban Planning","p":["For much of the twentieth century, the dominant image of the modern city was shaped by the automobile. Planners separated homes, offices, and shops into distinct zones, connected by wide roads, on the assumption that residents would drive between them. The outcome in many countries was a landscape of sprawling suburbs, where even a loaf of bread required a car journey. Only in recent decades has this model been seriously questioned, and a quiet revolution in urban planning is now under way.","At the centre of the new thinking is the idea of the compact, mixed-use neighbourhood, sometimes summarised as the 'fifteen-minute city', in which most daily needs, such as schools, shops, parks, and health services, can be reached on foot or by bicycle within a quarter of an hour. Proponents argue that this arrangement reduces emissions, saves time, and strengthens local communities, since people who walk past one another every day are more likely to form relationships. It also benefits those who cannot drive, including children, the elderly, and people on low incomes.","Achieving this vision is considerably harder than describing it. Existing cities cannot be rebuilt from scratch, and planners must work within a dense mixture of historic buildings, private property, and entrenched habits. Much of the effort therefore consists of modest adjustments: relaxing zoning rules to allow small shops in residential streets, converting unused offices into flats, and reclaiming parking spaces for trees and cafés. Individually, these measures appear unremarkable; collectively, they can alter the character of a district.","Resistance is common, and not always irrational. Shopkeepers worry that reducing parking will drive customers away, although studies in a number of cities suggest that pedestrians and cyclists spend more in aggregate than drivers who visit less often. Residents of established neighbourhoods fear that increased density will bring noise, congestion, and a change in the appearance of their streets. There is also a legitimate concern about gentrification: as an area becomes more attractive, rents rise, and the long-standing residents whom the improvements were meant to serve may be displaced.","For this reason, many urbanists insist that environmental goals cannot be separated from questions of fairness. A neighbourhood with excellent amenities but no affordable housing merely relocates the problem. Some municipalities have attempted to address it by requiring developers to reserve a share of new apartments at controlled prices, by supporting community land trusts, or by directing investment first toward the districts that have been neglected the longest. The results have been uneven, which illustrates how difficult it is to improve a place without altering who can afford to live there.","Critics from another direction suggest that the fifteen-minute concept oversimplifies how people actually live. Individuals do not necessarily work, shop, and socialise close to home; they choose jobs and friends across an entire metropolitan region, and a city's vitality depends in part on the ease with which they can move beyond their own neighbourhood. A balanced approach, these critics maintain, would combine strong local centres with fast, reliable public transport, rather than treating proximity as the sole virtue.","What is striking about the present moment is the degree of convergence across very different cities. Paris, Melbourne, and Bogotá, each with its own history and politics, have all experimented with restricting traffic, widening pavements, and creating new public spaces. Their success has varied, but they share a recognition that streets are not merely corridors for vehicles; they are among the most valuable shared spaces a community possesses.","If the revolution has been quiet, it is because it proceeds through small decisions: a bench here, a bicycle lane there, a changed regulation in an obscure planning document. Yet the cumulative effect may prove as profound as the arrival of the motorcar itself. Whether it ultimately produces cities that are healthier and more inclusive, or merely more expensive, will depend on whether planners treat the improvement of the physical environment and the protection of the people who live in it as a single task."],"q":[{"q":"What did twentieth-century planning assume?","o":["Residents would walk to most destinations.","Residents would drive between separate zones.","Cities would be built partly underground.","Shops would gradually leave the centre."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Planners separated zones connected by wide roads on the assumption residents would drive."},{"q":"What is the main idea of the \"fifteen-minute city\"?","o":["Daily needs should be fifteen minutes away.","Everyone should work from home most days.","Roads in city centres should be made wider.","Cities should keep their populations small."],"a":0,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Daily needs reachable within a quarter of an hour on foot or bicycle."},{"q":"Why are small measures such as relaxing zoning rules significant?","o":["Together they can change a district's character.","Each one is cheap and changes nothing at all.","Each one is demanded by the national government.","Together they remove every car from the area."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Individually unremarkable; collectively they can alter the character of a district."},{"q":"What concern is expressed about gentrification?","o":["Neighbourhoods become less safe at night.","Green parks gradually disappear from view.","Local shops start closing much earlier each year.","Rents rise and long-standing residents may leave."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Rents rise and long-standing residents may be displaced."},{"q":"What is the criticism of the fifteen-minute concept?","o":["It would cost far too much to put into practice.","It forgets the needs of people who cycle to work.","It would ban cars from the whole of the city.","It oversimplifies how people use the wider region."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"People choose jobs and friends across the region; transport matters too."},{"q":"Why is the revolution called \"quiet\"?","o":["It takes place mostly during the night.","It is kept secret from most local people.","It moves forward through small daily decisions.","It has made city centres much quieter at night."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"It proceeds through small decisions: a bench, a lane, a changed regulation."},{"q":"What is the author's concluding condition for success?","o":["Building more roads to ease the pressure","Allowing density to rise without any limits","Treating improvement and protection as one task","Reducing public transport to cut spending"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Treat improving the environment and protecting residents as one task."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_ethics5","t":"Moral Progress and Its Discontents","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Moral Progress and Its Discontents","p":["Few ideas are as intuitively appealing, or as philosophically fraught, as that of moral progress. We tend to assume that our ancestors' acceptance of slavery, the subjugation of women, and the public torture of criminals represents a stage of ignorance that we have since transcended. To speak in this way, however, is to presuppose a standard by which moral views can be ranked, and it is precisely the existence of such a standard that sceptics have contested. If morality is merely the expression of a society's conventions, then the change from one set of values to another is mere change, not improvement, and our sense of superiority is nothing more than the parochial vanity of every age.","Defenders of progress typically respond by pointing to features of moral change that are difficult to attribute to fashion alone. The abolition of slavery, they note, did not simply replace one preference with another; it was driven in large part by the recognition that the interests of the enslaved were of the same kind as those of their masters, and that the arguments used to justify their treatment could not withstand consistent scrutiny. On this account, moral advance resembles intellectual advance: it consists in the discovery of inconsistencies, the extension of principles to cases previously overlooked, and the correction of factual errors, such as false beliefs about the capacities of particular groups.","This analogy, though illuminating, invites complications. Scientific progress is evaluated against a physical world that resists our theories, whereas it is far from obvious what plays the corresponding role in ethics. Moral realists maintain that there are objective truths about right and wrong, independent of what anyone believes; yet they struggle to explain how such truths could be known, or why human beings, products of a blind evolutionary process, should be equipped to detect them. Anti-realists, by contrast, can account for the origin of our moral sense but have difficulty justifying the confidence with which we condemn practices in other cultures and periods.","A subtler challenge arises from the observation that moral progress has been neither uniform nor irreversible. The twentieth century, which produced the most elaborate declarations of human rights in history, also witnessed atrocities on an industrial scale, committed in some cases by societies that were by any measure highly educated. The lesson drawn by many is that moral advance is contingent: it depends on institutions, on the diffusion of particular habits of thought, and on conditions of security and prosperity that can disappear. Any teleological story in which history moves inevitably toward justice is therefore as unfounded as it is comforting.","Moreover, the very notion of progress can foster a corrosive complacency. If we are persuaded that we stand at the summit of moral understanding, we are unlikely to ask what future generations will condemn in us. Historians have observed that those who defended slavery or the exclusion of women from public life rarely thought of themselves as monsters; they were, for the most part, respectable people confident in their rectitude. It would be remarkable if we alone had escaped such blindness. A more defensible attitude, perhaps, is one that retains the conviction that some moral views are better than others while holding that conviction with a measure of humility.","Candidates for our own blind spots are not hard to find. Some point to the treatment of animals in industrial agriculture, others to the neglect of distant strangers whose suffering we could relieve at modest cost, and others still to the obligations we owe to people not yet born. What these examples share is a structure: in each, a group whose interests are real but whose voice is absent bears the consequences of practices from which others benefit. Whether or not one accepts any particular example, the pattern is instructive, since it suggests where moral scrutiny should be directed.","The upshot is a position that is neither triumphalist nor despairing. One may hold that moral progress is real, in the sense that certain changes reflect better reasoning and greater impartiality, without supposing that it is guaranteed or complete. Such a view imposes demanding obligations. It requires that we defend hard-won achievements against erosion, that we remain open to arguments that unsettle our assumptions, and that we recognise that the question of what we owe to one another is never finally closed. Progress, on this understanding, is not a destination that history delivers but a task that each generation must undertake anew."],"q":[{"q":"What do sceptics claim about the idea of moral progress?","o":["It is obviously true to anyone who studies history.","Without an objective standard, change is not progress.","It makes sense only when applied to science.","It was invented by historians to flatter the present."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"If morality is conventions, change is not improvement; superiority is parochial vanity."},{"q":"How do defenders explain the abolition of slavery?","o":["As the discovery of inconsistencies in accepted principles","As a change in fashion among powerful groups of people","As an accident caused by economic pressure alone","As a command that came only from religion"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Recognition of equal interests and arguments failing under consistent scrutiny."},{"q":"Why is the analogy with scientific progress problematic?","o":["It is unclear what in ethics resists our theories.","Science has not made progress in recent centuries.","Ethical questions are easier to settle than scientific ones.","Scientists often disagree about moral questions."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"It is far from obvious what plays the corresponding role in ethics."},{"q":"What does the twentieth-century example demonstrate?","o":["Education alone is enough to guarantee good morals.","Declarations of rights have no value whatsoever.","Serious atrocities have become extremely rare.","Moral advance can depend on conditions and be undone."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Atrocities by educated societies show advance depends on conditions that can disappear."},{"q":"How can the idea of progress become \"corrosive\"?","o":["It makes us ask too many awkward questions of ourselves.","It encourages people to turn to violence when they lose.","It slows down the progress of scientific research.","It breeds complacency about our own blind spots."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"We are unlikely to ask what future generations will condemn in us."},{"q":"What do the suggested blind spots have in common?","o":["They mostly concern very wealthy individuals and families.","They are all matters that are against the law.","A voiceless group with real interests bears the cost.","They all involve the use of new forms of technology."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"A group whose interests are real but whose voice is absent bears the consequences."},{"q":"What is the author's final position?","o":["Progress is guaranteed so long as people keep on learning.","Progress is only a comforting illusion of each generation.","Progress is real but not guaranteed, and must be renewed.","Progress came to an end in the twentieth century."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Progress is not a destination history delivers but a task each generation undertakes anew."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_language5","t":"Does Language Shape Thought?","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Does Language Shape Thought?","p":["The suggestion that the language we speak shapes the way we think has fascinated scholars and the public alike for nearly a century. Associated with the linguists Edward Sapir and Benjamin Lee Whorf, the hypothesis of linguistic relativity holds, in its strongest version, that speakers of different languages inhabit different conceptual worlds, since the categories their language provides set the boundaries of what they can think. Few researchers now defend this extreme position, but the more moderate question of whether language influences cognition in measurable ways has proved remarkably productive, and its answer remains disputed.","The strong version encounters immediate difficulties. If thought were determined by language, translation would be impossible, and children who have not yet acquired language, along with the deaf individuals who grow up without any, would be incapable of reasoning. Neither is the case. Infants demonstrate sophisticated expectations about objects and quantities months before they speak, and bilingual speakers move between conceptual systems with little apparent difficulty. Moreover, the popular claim that the Inuit possess dozens of words for snow, so often repeated as evidence, turns out to rest on a misunderstanding of how their languages form words, and is in any case inadequate to establish a difference in thinking.","More persuasive evidence comes from carefully controlled experiments. Russian distinguishes obligatorily between lighter and darker blues, using two separate basic terms, and Russian speakers have been found to discriminate between shades straddling that boundary slightly faster than English speakers, whose language lacks the distinction. Speakers of a language of Australia that uses absolute compass directions instead of 'left' and 'right' maintain an extraordinary sense of orientation, and arrange sequences of events, such as a series of photographs, along an east-to-west axis irrespective of how they are seated. Such results indicate that habitual linguistic distinctions can sharpen attention to corresponding features of the world.","The interpretation of these findings is nevertheless contentious. Critics observe that the effects are typically small, appear only under particular conditions, and often vanish when participants are occupied with a second task, which suggests that language modulates the speed of a judgement without altering the underlying perception. Others argue that the correlation can run in the opposite direction: a community that frequently needs to describe compass directions, because of its environment, may develop the vocabulary for them, rather than the vocabulary producing the skill. Disentangling culture, environment, and language, which are inevitably intertwined, remains a formidable methodological problem.","Perhaps the most defensible formulation is that languages differ less in what they allow their speakers to think than in what they oblige them to notice. A speaker of a language that marks grammatical gender on every noun must repeatedly attend to a property that speakers of other languages may ignore; one whose grammar requires the source of information to be indicated, whether witnessed or merely inferred, cannot make an assertion without implicitly addressing the evidence for it. Because such obligations apply constantly, even slight tendencies may accumulate, affecting what becomes salient in memory, description, and perhaps reasoning.","There are practical dimensions to the question. Translators and diplomats know that apparently equivalent expressions carry different connotations, and misunderstandings sometimes arise from differences that are subtle rather than obvious. In education, there is evidence that children whose language expresses numbers in a regular, transparent way learn to count earlier than those who must master irregular forms. None of this implies that one language is superior; it suggests only that the tools of expression are not entirely neutral.","What emerges, then, is a nuanced picture that neither the enthusiasts nor the dismissive sceptics anticipated. Language does not imprison thought, and the dream of a hidden mental world accessible only to speakers of a particular tongue is a romantic exaggeration. Yet to treat language as a transparent window onto a universal mind is equally simplistic. The more plausible conclusion is that linguistic habits exert a gentle, pervasive pressure on attention and memory, one whose strength depends on the task and the context, and whose precise extent will occupy researchers for many years to come."],"q":[{"q":"What does the strong version of linguistic relativity claim?","o":["Language makes translation between cultures simple.","Language sets the limits of what speakers can think.","Children invent new languages by themselves.","All languages share exactly the same structure."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Categories provided by language set the boundaries of thought."},{"q":"Why does the strong version face difficulties?","o":["Babies and people without language can still reason.","Russian has two different words that both mean blue.","Linguists cannot agree on how to spell words.","Very few people in the world speak a second language."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Infants show sophisticated expectations before speaking; translation is possible."},{"q":"What does the Russian blue experiment suggest?","o":["Language distinctions can slightly speed up judgement.","Russian speakers are unable to see certain colours.","English is poorer than Russian in colour words.","Colour words are exactly the same in every language."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Russian speakers discriminate faster between shades straddling the boundary."},{"q":"How do critics interpret the small effects?","o":["The effects prove that the strong version is right.","The effects are too small for scientists to measure.","The effects are caused mainly by the speaker's age.","Language may change speed of judgement, not perception."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Effects vanish under a second task, suggesting language modulates speed only."},{"q":"What does \"oblige them to notice\" mean in the fifth paragraph?","o":["Speakers are always corrected when they make mistakes.","Languages do not allow some ideas to be expressed at all.","Grammar rules can be ignored by most speakers today.","Grammar makes speakers attend to certain details."],"a":3,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Grammar like gender or evidentiality requires attention to certain properties each time."},{"q":"Which practical example is given?","o":["Typists working faster in English than in other languages","Better memory for names among speakers of German today","Children counting earlier in languages with regular numbers","Easier travel for diplomats who know several languages"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Children learn to count earlier where numbers are expressed regularly."},{"q":"What is the final conclusion?","o":["Language traps thought and makes change impossible for us.","Language is a clear window onto a universal human mind.","Language exerts a gentle pressure that depends on context.","Language has no real effect on how people think at all."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Linguistic habits exert a gentle pervasive pressure whose strength depends on task and context."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_fashion6","t":"The True Price of Fast Fashion","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The True Price of Fast Fashion","p":["A T-shirt that costs less than a sandwich has become an ordinary sight on the high street. Fast fashion, the business model of producing inexpensive clothes in response to rapidly changing trends, has transformed the industry. Where designers once showed two collections a year, some brands now introduce thousands of new styles every week. For shoppers, the result is a seemingly endless supply of affordable novelty; for the planet and for the people who make the clothes, the picture is considerably less attractive.","The environmental toll is substantial. The fashion industry is estimated to account for around ten per cent of global carbon emissions, more than international flights and shipping combined. Producing a single cotton shirt can require thousands of litres of water, and the dyes used in textile factories frequently pollute rivers in the countries where manufacturing is concentrated. Synthetic fabrics such as polyester add a further problem: each wash releases microscopic plastic fibres that eventually find their way into the oceans.","The human cost is equally troubling. To keep prices low, brands often outsource production to countries where wages are minimal and regulations are weakly enforced. The 2013 collapse of the Rana Plaza factory in Bangladesh, which killed more than a thousand garment workers, exposed how unsafe conditions can be. Although the disaster prompted some reforms, campaigners maintain that many workers still earn too little to live decently, and that brands are reluctant to accept responsibility for their suppliers.","Consumers are not blameless, but placing the burden solely on them is misleading. Surveys indicate that a large share of shoppers claim to care about sustainability, yet few change their habits, partly because ethical alternatives are expensive and information is often vague. Terms such as \"eco-friendly\" or \"conscious\" are rarely regulated, which allows companies to engage in what critics call \"greenwashing\": promoting a modest environmental initiative to distract from damaging practices elsewhere.","Proposed solutions range from the individual to the systemic. Buying fewer, better-made items, repairing garments and choosing second-hand clothing can reduce personal impact, and the resale market is growing rapidly. More ambitious ideas include legislation that obliges companies to disclose their supply chains, taxes on non-recyclable textiles, and \"extended producer responsibility\" schemes that make brands pay for the disposal of what they sell. Whether such measures can succeed against an industry that thrives on volume remains an open question, but it is increasingly difficult to argue that the true price of a cheap shirt is the one printed on the label."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the text?","o":["To advise readers on how to find the best bargains.","To trace the history of the fashion industry.","To examine the hidden costs of cheap clothing","To argue that clothing prices should be regulated."],"a":2,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"The text describes environmental and human costs and possible remedies."},{"q":"What does the comparison with flights and shipping show?","o":["Clothes are shipped further than any other product is.","The industry's emissions exceed both sectors combined.","Aeroplanes carry most garments to the shops each year.","Transport is the largest source of fashion's emissions."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The text says fashion emits more than flights and shipping combined."},{"q":"What does the writer say about polyester?","o":["Growing it uses a lot more water than cotton does.","Washing it releases tiny plastic fibres into the sea.","It is the cheapest kind of fabric to recycle properly.","Dyeing it always pollutes the rivers near the factories."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Microscopic fibres end up in the sea."},{"q":"What did the Rana Plaza collapse show?","o":["How dangerous garment factory conditions could be.","That brands had taken responsibility for suppliers.","That wages in Bangladesh were rising quickly.","That most factories were carefully inspected."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"It exposed how unsafe conditions can be."},{"q":"What does \"placing the burden solely on them is misleading\" mean?","o":["Blaming only shoppers gives an incomplete picture.","Shoppers should not be given any information.","Consumers have no influence on companies at all.","Shoppers are too busy to change their habits."],"a":0,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Companies and systems share responsibility."},{"q":"What is \"greenwashing\"?","o":["Cleaning clothes with environmentally friendly products","Cutting production by using more recycled fibres","Inventing new ecological labels for consumers","Promoting a small green effort to hide harmful practice"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"A modest initiative distracts from damaging practices."},{"q":"What does the final sentence suggest?","o":["Fashion brands ought to print higher prices.","Cheap clothes are always very badly made indeed.","Shoppers should stop buying clothes entirely.","The label price hides the real costs of production."],"a":3,"tg":["inference"],"e":"The \"true price\" is not the one on the label."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_placebo6","t":"The Power of the Placebo","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Power of the Placebo","p":["In 1955, an American anaesthetist named Henry Beecher published a paper that would reshape medical thinking. Reviewing fifteen studies, he noticed that around a third of patients improved after receiving a treatment with no active ingredient, such as a sugar pill or an injection of salt water. This phenomenon, called the placebo effect, has since been demonstrated in conditions ranging from pain and depression to Parkinson's disease, and it continues to puzzle researchers.","The mechanism appears to involve expectation. When a person believes that a treatment will work, the brain can release natural painkillers called endorphins, and brain scans show measurable changes in activity. Importantly, the effect is not simply imagination: patients report real relief, and in some cases physical symptoms improve. The strength of a placebo varies with its presentation. Large pills work better than small ones, injections outperform tablets, and expensive brands seem more effective than cheaper but identical versions.","The ritual of care matters as well. Studies show that patients treated by a warm, attentive doctor respond better than those seen by a distant one, even when the treatment is identical. This suggests that part of what we call healing is relational: being listened to and reassured changes how we experience illness. Some researchers argue that a significant portion of the benefit attributed to certain therapies may stem from context rather than from the therapy itself.","The placebo effect presents doctors with an ethical dilemma. Prescribing a treatment that is known to be inert seems to involve deception, and deception undermines the trust on which medicine depends. Remarkably, a handful of trials have found that placebos can work even when patients are told openly that the pills contain no medicine. In one study of people with irritable bowel syndrome, those who knowingly took placebos reported greater improvement than those who received no treatment, although the sample was small and the results require replication.","Critics caution against exaggerating the phenomenon. Much of the apparent improvement in early studies, they note, may reflect natural recovery or the tendency of symptoms to fluctuate, rather than the pill's influence. Placebos cannot shrink tumours or cure infections, and their effects on subjective experiences such as pain are easier to detect than on measurable markers of disease. The most defensible conclusion is that placebo responses are genuine but limited, and that they offer a reminder that the mind and body are not separate systems. For medicine, the challenge lies in harnessing context, communication and expectation without compromising honesty."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main focus of the text?","o":["The nature, causes and limits of the placebo effect.","A history of ethical rules in medical research.","The reasons why doctors should prescribe sugar pills.","The way that painkillers work inside the brain."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The text describes the effect, explanations and criticism."},{"q":"What did Beecher notice in 1955?","o":["Most patients preferred having injections to taking pills.","Salt water cured several of the most serious illnesses.","Doctors gave out sugar pills far too often to patients.","About a third of patients improved on inactive treatments."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Around a third improved without any active ingredient."},{"q":"How does paragraph 2 describe the placebo effect?","o":["As imagined relief with no physical effect.","As the result of stronger chemicals in the pills.","As something seen only with costly medicines.","As a real change shaped by what a patient expects."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Expectation can trigger real, measurable changes."},{"q":"What does paragraph 3 suggest about a doctor's manner?","o":["Distant doctors tend to prescribe stronger drugs.","Warm doctors can make diagnoses more quickly.","Feeling listened to can improve treatment responses.","Patients prefer to see doctors they know well."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Warm, attentive doctors get better responses."},{"q":"In paragraph 4, \"inert\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["extremely harmful to health","difficult to detect in tests","having no active effect","recently developed in labs"],"a":2,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"An inert treatment contains no active medicine."},{"q":"What qualification does the writer add about the open-label study?","o":["The patients had not understood their instructions.","The sample was small and results need confirming.","The placebos cost more than ordinary pills.","The control group improved more than the others."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"The sample was small and the results require replication.\""},{"q":"What point do critics make about early studies?","o":["Doctors failed to record the results properly.","Improvement may have come from natural recovery.","Patients were not given enough time to improve.","The treatments were too costly to compare."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Symptoms fluctuate and people recover naturally."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_night6","t":"Reclaiming the Night Sky","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Reclaiming the Night Sky","p":["Few people alive today have seen the sky as their ancestors did. For most of human history, a clear night revealed thousands of stars and the pale band of the Milky Way; now, in many cities, only a few of the brightest stars are visible through a permanent orange haze. This phenomenon, known as light pollution, is the unintended consequence of a world that has become brighter each year, and scientists increasingly warn that its effects extend well beyond the disappointment of amateur astronomers.","The most obvious victims are wildlife. Migrating birds, which often navigate by the stars, are drawn towards illuminated skyscrapers and collide with them in vast numbers. Newly hatched sea turtles crawl towards streetlights rather than the moonlit sea, and moths circle lamps until they are exhausted or eaten. Even small amounts of artificial light can disrupt the breeding patterns of frogs and the pollination activity of nocturnal insects, with consequences that spread through entire ecosystems.","Humans are not immune. Our bodies depend on a daily rhythm regulated by light and darkness, and exposure to bright light in the evening suppresses melatonin, the hormone that prepares us for sleep. Epidemiological studies have linked night-shift work and bright bedrooms with sleep disorders, depression and, more tentatively, certain cancers. While causation is difficult to prove, the correlation has been sufficiently consistent for some health agencies to classify night-shift work as probably carcinogenic.","What makes light pollution unusual among environmental problems is how easily it can be solved. Unlike greenhouse gases, light leaves no residue; switch off a lamp, and the effect disappears instantly. Much of the illumination is simply wasted, directed upwards or sideways instead of onto the ground where it is needed. Simple measures such as shielding fixtures, using warmer-coloured bulbs, installing motion sensors and dimming lights after midnight can cut glare dramatically while saving energy and money.","Some places have already acted. Designated \"dark sky reserves\" in countries including New Zealand, Namibia and the United Kingdom restrict outdoor lighting to protect both the night sky and local wildlife, and they have become popular with tourists seeking stargazing holidays. Critics sometimes object that darker streets compromise safety, yet evidence for this is surprisingly weak; well-designed lighting that illuminates paths without blinding drivers appears to be at least as effective as indiscriminate brightness. The question, then, is less about choosing between light and dark than about using light with intention."],"q":[{"q":"What is the main idea of the text?","o":["Too much artificial light harms nature and people.","Stargazing should take the place of other hobbies at night.","Cities should switch off all of their outdoor lights at night.","Astronomers need much better equipment to see the stars."],"a":0,"tg":["gist"],"e":"The text describes harm and simple remedies."},{"q":"Why are sea turtles mentioned?","o":["They breed more successfully in busy city areas now.","They use the stars to cross whole oceans every year.","They are poisoned by the chemicals in streetlights.","Hatchlings are misled by artificial light near beaches."],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Hatchlings crawl towards streetlights instead of the sea."},{"q":"In paragraph 3, \"suppresses\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["gradually increases","carefully measures","accidentally releases","reduces or prevents"],"a":3,"tg":["vocab"],"e":"Evening light lowers melatonin levels."},{"q":"What does the writer say about the link between light and cancer?","o":["It has been proved by many long and careful experiments.","It applies only to people who work at night in hospitals.","It is a consistent link that is hard to prove as cause.","It has been rejected by most of the health agencies."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Causation is hard to prove, but the correlation is consistent."},{"q":"Why is light pollution described as \"unusual\" among environmental problems?","o":["It only affects places with very large populations.","It cannot be measured with any real accuracy.","It ends as soon as the light source is switched off.","It costs more to solve than other problems do."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Light leaves no residue."},{"q":"How does the writer respond to the safety objection?","o":["By saying crime is lower in brightly lit areas","By saying that the evidence for it is weak","By saying drivers prefer streets without lighting","By saying that safety matters less than nature"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"Evidence for this is surprisingly weak.\""},{"q":"What does the final sentence imply?","o":["Light and darkness can never exist in cities together.","Light should be used with purpose, not in excess.","Governments should ban all artificial lighting.","People generally prefer darkness to brightness."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"Using light with intention.\""}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_acad_sleep","t":"Do Students Learn Better After Sleep?","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Do Students Learn Better After Sleep?","p":["A growing body of research suggests that sleep plays a central role in learning. In one widely cited experiment, two groups of students studied the same list of words. One group slept normally that night, while the other stayed awake. When tested two days later, the rested group recalled roughly a fifth more words, even though both groups had slept equally well on the second night.","Researchers attribute this difference to a process known as memory consolidation, during which the brain strengthens newly formed connections. However, the authors are careful not to overstate their findings. The sample consisted of only forty undergraduates, all of whom were recruited from a single university, and the task itself was highly artificial. It remains unclear whether the effect would be as pronounced for complex skills such as writing an essay or solving mathematical problems.","Despite these limitations, the results have already influenced policy debates. Several schools have experimented with later start times, and early reports indicate modest improvements in attendance. Yet critics point out that such changes may owe as much to other factors, including teacher enthusiasm, as to extra sleep. Most specialists therefore regard the conclusions as tentative until larger studies have replicated the original results."],"q":[{"q":"What did the experiment compare?","o":["Students who studied alone and students who studied in groups","Students who slept normally and students who stayed awake after studying","Morning and evening exam results","Students at different universities"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"One group slept, the other stayed awake the first night."},{"q":"Why do the authors avoid overstating their findings?","o":["The sample was small and the task was artificial","The results contradicted earlier studies","The rested group performed worse","The experiment lasted only one day"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Forty students from one university, and a highly artificial task."},{"q":"What do critics say about later school start times?","o":["Improvements may be caused by other factors as well as sleep","They have made attendance worse","They are too expensive for most schools","They prove that sleep has no effect"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Critics mention other factors such as teacher enthusiasm."},{"q":"What is the writer's overall attitude towards the findings?","o":["Completely convinced","Openly hostile","Totally indifferent","Cautiously interested"],"a":3,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Results are promising but described as tentative."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_acad_cities","t":"The Hidden Cost of Cheap Delivery","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Hidden Cost of Cheap Delivery","p":["Few consumers stop to consider what happens between clicking a button and finding a parcel on their doorstep. Yet the rapid growth of next-day delivery has created what economists call an externality: a cost that falls on people who had no part in the transaction. Delivery vans double-park in narrow streets, adding to congestion, while the emissions they produce affect the air that everyone breathes.","Retailers argue that customers demand speed and that companies are simply responding to the market. This explanation is only partly convincing. Fast delivery is frequently offered free of charge precisely because firms compete for customers, which means that buyers never see the true price of their choice. Had they done so, many might well opt for slower, cheaper options.","Some city councils are therefore considering a small levy on rapid deliveries, with the revenue going towards cycle lanes and electric vehicle charging points. Supporters believe that such a measure would give shoppers an incentive to plan ahead. Opponents warn that the cost would be passed on to lower-income households, who depend most on home delivery. Whether a charge could curb congestion without penalising vulnerable groups remains an open question."],"q":[{"q":"What is an \"externality\" in this text?","o":["A fee paid directly by the customer","A cost that affects people who did not take part in the transaction","A tax on retailers","A discount for fast delivery"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The text defines it as a cost falling on people outside the transaction."},{"q":"Why does the writer find the retailers' argument only partly convincing?","o":["Free fast delivery hides the real price from buyers","Customers actually prefer slow delivery","Retailers make no profit from deliveries","Delivery vans cause no pollution"],"a":0,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Buyers never see the true price of their choice."},{"q":"What is the main objection of opponents to the levy?","o":["Lower-income households might end up paying more","It would increase traffic","It would reduce the number of cycle lanes","Retailers would stop selling online"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"The cost would be passed on to lower-income households."},{"q":"How does the text end?","o":["With a firm recommendation","With a warning to retailers","With a summary of the figures","With an unresolved question"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"It remains an open question."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_acad_language","t":"Why Languages Change","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"Why Languages Change","p":["It is tempting to think of a language as a fixed system that is gradually spoiled by careless speakers. Linguists, however, take a very different view. Change is not a sign of decay but an inevitable feature of any language that is actually used, and it has been documented in every community that has ever been studied.","Some changes begin with a small group. A new pronunciation may spread because it is associated with a group that has prestige, for instance young city dwellers or well-known broadcasters. Others result from contact between languages: English has borrowed so extensively from French, Norse and Latin that a modern speaker can express the same idea in several different registers.","Attempts to halt change have rarely succeeded. Academies founded to protect national languages have issued dictionaries and rules, yet ordinary speakers have gone on innovating. This does not mean that standards are pointless; clear conventions help writers communicate across regions. But a standard is best seen as a useful agreement at one moment in time rather than a permanent law."],"q":[{"q":"How do linguists view language change?","o":["As a natural and unavoidable feature of living languages","As a sign that standards have declined","As something that only affects dialects","As a recent phenomenon"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Change is inevitable, not decay."},{"q":"According to the text, why might a new pronunciation spread?","o":["It is taught in schools","It is easier to write","It is forced by dictionaries","It is linked to a group with prestige"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Prestige groups spread new forms."},{"q":"What does the example of English show?","o":["English has never borrowed words","French has replaced English","Latin is spoken in most countries","Contact between languages can add vocabulary"],"a":3,"tg":["detail"],"e":"English borrowed from French, Norse and Latin."},{"q":"What is the writer's view of standards?","o":["They are completely pointless","They should be strictly enforced","They are useful but not permanent","They are older than any language"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"A standard is a useful agreement at one moment in time."}]},{"k":"R5","id":"okr_acad_memory","t":"The Problem With Perfect Memory","ty":"mc","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","title":"The Problem With Perfect Memory","p":["It is easy to envy people with extraordinary memories. Yet the few documented cases of individuals who forget almost nothing suggest that perfect recall comes at a price. Such people often report that trivial details intrude on their thoughts constantly, making it hard to concentrate or to see the bigger picture.","Psychologists argue that forgetting is not simply a failure of the brain but an adaptive feature. By letting go of information that is unlikely to be useful, the mind can prioritise what matters and avoid being overwhelmed. Memories that are rarely retrieved fade, while those that are linked to emotion or repeated experience remain strong.","This view has practical consequences. Students who try to memorise everything often perform worse than those who review selectively, and workers who record every conversation may spend more time searching than thinking. In an age of unlimited digital storage, the real skill may lie not in remembering more, but in deciding what deserves to be remembered."],"q":[{"q":"What problem do people with perfect recall report?","o":["They cannot learn new languages","Trivial details constantly intrude on their thoughts","They forget names","They sleep too little"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Details intrude and make concentration hard."},{"q":"According to psychologists, why is forgetting useful?","o":["It helps the mind focus on what matters","It makes memories stronger","It prevents emotions","It speeds up reading"],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Forgetting lets the mind prioritise."},{"q":"Which memories tend to stay strong?","o":["Those linked to emotion or repetition","Those from childhood","Those that are trivial","Those written down"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Emotion and repetition keep them strong."},{"q":"What does the writer suggest in the last paragraph?","o":["Digital storage should be limited","Students should record everything","Memory training is useless","Selecting what to remember may matter more than remembering more"],"a":3,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"The real skill is deciding what to remember."}]}],"L":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Üç farklı kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin; her biri için iki soru var. En uygun cevabı seç.","id":"c1l1","t":"A Podcast","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"c1l1a","sc":[["F","When we started the podcast, we assumed listeners wanted expert interviews. It turned out they much preferred episodes where we just talked through our own confusion about a topic. It made the whole thing feel more like a conversation between friends."],["M","Which, honestly, made it a lot less work to prepare."]],"q":"What did the speakers discover about their listeners?","o":["They found the expert interviews too technical to follow.","They wanted the hosts to prepare more thoroughly.","They liked hearing the hosts work through their confusion."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Uzman röportajı yerine kendi kafa karışıklıklarını konuştukları bölümleri tercih ettiler."},{"id":"c1l1b","sc":[["F","When we started the podcast, we assumed listeners wanted expert interviews. It turned out they much preferred episodes where we just talked through our own confusion about a topic. It made the whole thing feel more like a conversation between friends."],["M","Which, honestly, made it a lot less work to prepare."]],"q":"What does the man suggest about this discovery?","o":["It made their episodes less professional.","It saved them effort behind the scenes.","It was a surprise that worried them."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Hazırlığı çok kolaylaştırdı."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Cümle tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Cümleleri bir kelime ya da kısa bir ifadeyle tamamla.","id":"c1l2","t":"An Ancient Shipwreck","ty":"open","title":"Cümleler","sc":[["M","The wreck we discovered lies at a depth of about forty metres off the coast of Sicily. It's a Roman merchant ship, probably dating from the first century. What makes it exceptional is the state of the cargo: hundreds of clay jars, known as amphorae, most of them still sealed. Initially, we assumed they would contain wine, which was the most commonly traded goods. In fact, analysis showed they were filled with fish sauce, a kind of Roman ketchup that was hugely popular. The ship was probably heading for Spain when it sank, possibly in a sudden storm. Excavation is extremely slow – each dive lasts only twenty minutes because of the depth. The biggest threat to the site isn't the sea, surprisingly, but illegal divers who steal objects to sell to collectors. To prevent this, the site is now monitored by underwater cameras."]],"p":"The ship was found at a depth of around (1) ______ metres.\nThe clay jars on board are called (2) ______.\nThe team first thought the jars contained (3) ______.\nThe jars actually contained a kind of (4) ______.\nThe ship may have sunk during a (5) ______.\nEach dive lasts only (6) ______ minutes.\nThe main threat to the site comes from (7) ______.\nThe site is now protected by underwater (8) ______.","a":[["40","forty"],["amphorae"],["wine"],["fish sauce","sauce"],["storm","sudden storm"],["20","twenty"],["illegal divers","divers"],["cameras"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"]],"e":["\"a depth of about forty metres\".","\"known as amphorae\".","\"we assumed they would contain wine\".","\"filled with fish sauce\".","\"possibly in a sudden storm\".","\"each dive lasts only twenty minutes\".","\"illegal divers who steal objects\".","\"monitored by underwater cameras\"."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bir röportaj dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","id":"c1l3","t":"Procrastination","ty":"mc","sc":[["F","Today I'm talking to psychologist Dr Sam Okafor about procrastination. Sam, why do we put things off?"],["M","The common assumption is that procrastinators are lazy, but that's rarely the case. Procrastination is mainly about managing emotions. We avoid tasks that make us feel anxious, bored or inadequate, and we get a short-term sense of relief."],["F","So it's not a time-management problem?"],["M","Not primarily. You can give someone the best planner in the world, and if the task still makes them feel bad, they'll find a way to avoid it. That's why so many productivity apps fail."],["F","What actually helps?"],["M","Self-compassion, surprisingly. Studies show that students who forgave themselves for procrastinating on one exam procrastinated less on the next. Being harsh on yourself just adds to the negative feelings that caused the problem."],["F","Is there any upside to procrastination?"],["M","Some people argue that a bit of delay allows ideas to develop, and there's something to that. But I'd be wary of using it as an excuse. Delaying because you're thinking is very different from delaying because you're avoiding."]],"q":[{"q":"According to Sam, procrastination is mainly caused by","o":["an inability to estimate how long tasks take","the wish to escape uncomfortable emotions","a lack of self-discipline and motivation","pressure from too many competing demands"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Procrastination is mainly about managing emotions\"."},{"q":"What does Sam say about productivity apps?","o":["They help most when deadlines are strict.","They tend to increase people's anxiety.","They ignore the emotional cause of delay.","They are useful but badly designed."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Görev kötü hissettirdiği sürece en iyi planlayıcı da işe yaramaz."},{"q":"What does Sam recommend?","o":["setting firmer deadlines for tasks","breaking tasks into smaller steps","forgiving oneself for past delays","rewarding progress with small treats"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Self-compassion\"."},{"q":"What is Sam's attitude to the idea that procrastination can be useful?","o":["He regards it as a myth without any basis.","He accepts it only partly and urges caution.","He is convinced it helps creative work most.","He welcomes it as a justification for delay."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"there's something to that. But I'd be wary\"."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Eşleştirme (görev 1)","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Beş kişinin konuşmasını dinleyeceksin. Görev 1: Her konuşmacının bu işi seçme nedenini (A–H) bul.","id":"c1l4","t":"A New Hobby","ty":"match","sc":[["F","Speaker one. I took up pottery after a stressful year at work. A colleague mentioned a class and I thought, why not? What I love is that you can't think about anything else while you're working the clay."],["M","#1"],["M","Speaker two. I started rock climbing because my son wanted to try it and needed an adult with him. Now I go more than he does! It's the problem-solving I enjoy – every route is like a puzzle."],["F","#1"],["F2","Speaker three. Learning the violin at fifty was something I'd promised myself for years. I'm not very good, but I love the challenge of slowly getting better."],["M","#1"],["M2","Speaker four. I joined a choir after moving to a new town, mainly to meet people. And it worked – most of my friends here are from the choir."],["F","#1"],["F","Speaker five. I began sea swimming after reading about its health benefits. It's freezing, but the feeling afterwards is incredible – I feel more alive than at any other time."]],"opts":[["A","to keep a promise to themselves"],["B","to make friends"],["C","to improve their health"],["D","to accompany a family member"],["E","to relieve stress"],["F","to impress others"],["G","to start a new career"],["H","to spend less money"]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"E","tg":["gist"],"e":"Stresli bir yıldan sonra başladı."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"D","tg":["gist"],"e":"Oğlunun yanında bir yetişkin olması gerekiyordu."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"A","tg":["gist"],"e":"Yıllardır kendine verdiği söz."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"B","tg":["gist"],"e":"Yeni bir kasabada insanlarla tanışmak için."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"C","tg":["gist"],"e":"Sağlık faydalarını okuduktan sonra."}]},{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Üç farklı kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin; her biri için iki soru var. En uygun cevabı seç.","id":"c1l5","t":"Two Colleagues","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"c1l5a","sc":[["M","I read your report on the new software. I agree with your conclusions, but I think you were a bit too diplomatic about the problems."],["F","Maybe. I didn't want to upset the IT team – they've worked so hard on it."],["M","I understand, but if the board doesn't realise how serious the issues are, they won't fund the fixes."]],"q":"What does the man think about the woman's report?","o":["It was unfair to the IT team's efforts.","It played down how serious the problems were.","It reached conclusions he doesn't accept."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"a bit too diplomatic about the problems\"."},{"id":"c1l5b","sc":[["M","I read your report on the new software. I agree with your conclusions, but I think you were a bit too diplomatic about the problems."],["F","Maybe. I didn't want to upset the IT team – they've worked so hard on it."],["M","I understand, but if the board doesn't realise how serious the issues are, they won't fund the fixes."]],"q":"Why is the man concerned?","o":["The board might not pay for the fixes.","The IT team might feel upset by it.","The project may be cancelled outright."],"a":0,"tg":["inference","first-conditional"],"e":"Yönetim sorunların ciddiyetini anlamazsa düzeltmeleri finanse etmeyecek."}]}],"W":[{"id":"c1w1","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Public Support for the Arts","in":"Your class has watched a discussion about how governments should support the arts. Write an essay discussing TWO of the methods in your notes, explaining which you think is more important and giving reasons. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"Ways governments can support the arts:\n• funding museums and galleries\n• providing arts education in schools\n• giving grants to individual artists\n\nOpinions expressed:\n\"Museums should be free so that everyone can enjoy them.\"\n\"Children who never learn an instrument miss out on so much.\"\n\"Artists shouldn't depend on government money.\"","pts":[["İlk yöntemi tartış",["museum","gallery","education","school","grant","artist"]],["İkinci yöntemi tartış",["whereas","on the other hand","by contrast","turning to","second"]],["Hangisinin daha önemli olduğunu gerekçelendir",["more important","in my view","i would argue","on balance","ultimately"]],["Akademik bağlaçlar",["however","furthermore","moreover","nevertheless","consequently"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Public money for the arts is often among the first things to be cut when budgets are tight. Yet there are strong arguments for continued support, particularly through funding museums and providing arts education in schools.\n\nFunding museums and galleries ensures that a country's cultural heritage is preserved and accessible. When entry is free, as it is in many British museums, visitor numbers rise dramatically and include people who would never otherwise attend. However, free museums mainly benefit those who live nearby and already have an interest in culture; they do little to create new audiences.\n\nArts education in schools, by contrast, reaches every child, regardless of background. Learning an instrument, acting in a play or studying painting develops creativity, confidence and the ability to collaborate – skills that are valuable far beyond the arts. Moreover, children who discover a passion for the arts early are likely to become the museum visitors, concert-goers and even artists of the future.\n\nOn balance, I would argue that arts education is the more important of the two. While museums preserve the culture of the past, education creates the culture of the future. Without it, the arts risk becoming the preserve of a privileged minority who can afford private lessons. That is not to say museums should be neglected, but if governments must prioritise, investing in children's creative development is likely to have the greater long-term impact on society as a whole.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","When public budgets are under pressure, funding for the arts is often regarded as an easy target. However, there are compelling reasons for governments to continue supporting culture, notably through grants to individual artists and arts education in schools.\n\nGrants to individual artists allow creative work to be produced that the market alone would not support. Experimental theatre, contemporary music and poetry rarely generate enough income to sustain their creators, yet they often shape the culture of the future. Critics argue that artists should not depend on public money, and there is some truth in this: grants can encourage work that appeals to funding committees rather than audiences. Nevertheless, without some form of support, only those with private wealth could afford to pursue an artistic career.\n\nArts education, by contrast, benefits society as a whole. Every child, regardless of background, gains the opportunity to discover a talent or simply develop the confidence that comes from performing or creating. These skills – creativity, collaboration, self-expression – are increasingly valued in the workplace.\n\nOf the two, I believe education is the more important priority. Grants support a relatively small number of professionals, whereas arts education reaches millions of young people and creates the audiences, and indeed the artists, of tomorrow. If governments must choose, investing in schools is likely to produce the broader and more lasting benefit, although ideally a modest grant system should be maintained alongside it to ensure that talent from all backgrounds can flourish."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","It has become fashionable in some quarters to dismiss public spending on the arts as a luxury that hard-pressed governments can ill afford. Such a view, however, underestimates the extent to which a nation's cultural life depends on deliberate investment. Two forms of support – funding museums and galleries, and subsidising individual artists – illustrate the dilemma particularly well.\n\nPublicly funded museums are, in many respects, the cornerstone of cultural democracy. Free admission, where it exists, has been shown to transform visitor numbers, opening collections to schoolchildren, pensioners and families who might never otherwise cross the threshold. The objection that free museums chiefly benefit the educated middle classes is not without merit, yet it argues for better outreach rather than for withdrawing funding altogether.\n\nGrants to individual artists raise thornier questions. Supporters maintain that they protect creative risk-taking from the tyranny of the market; detractors counter that they foster dependency and reward those adept at filling in application forms rather than those with genuine talent.\n\nWeighing the two, I am persuaded that museums represent the more valuable use of limited funds. They preserve a shared heritage, serve as places of learning and reflection, and – crucially – remain accessible to everyone, rather than benefiting a select few. That said, the most enlightened cultural policies recognise that heritage and innovation are not rivals: today's experimental work, if it is allowed to emerge at all, may well become tomorrow's museum piece."]]},{"id":"c1w2","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"An Ageing Population","in":"Your class has attended a lecture on the challenges of an ageing population. Write an essay discussing TWO of the issues in your notes, explaining which you think governments should address first, giving reasons. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"Challenges of an ageing population:\n• healthcare costs\n• pensions\n• loneliness among older people\n\nOpinions expressed:\n\"We can't afford to keep paying for longer retirements.\"\n\"Hospitals are already under pressure.\"\n\"Isolation can be as harmful as smoking.\"","pts":[["İlk konuyu tartış",["healthcare","hospital","pension","retire","lonely","loneliness","isolation"]],["İkinci konuyu tartış",["whereas","on the other hand","by contrast","turning to"]],["Öncelikli olanı gerekçelendir",["first","priority","more urgent","in my view","i would argue"]],["Akademik bağlaçlar",["however","furthermore","moreover","nevertheless","consequently"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"As life expectancy rises and birth rates fall, many countries face the challenge of supporting a growing number of older citizens. Two of the most pressing issues are the rising cost of healthcare and the problem of loneliness.\n\nHealthcare costs are perhaps the most visible concern. Older people are more likely to suffer from long-term conditions, and hospitals in many countries are already struggling to cope. Without reform, governments may have to choose between raising taxes and reducing the quality of care, neither of which is politically attractive.\n\nLoneliness, by contrast, receives far less attention, yet its effects are serious. Research suggests that chronic isolation increases the risk of heart disease and dementia, and some studies have compared its impact to that of smoking. As families become more scattered, many older people spend days without speaking to anyone.\n\nI would argue that governments should address loneliness first, not because it is more important than healthcare, but because the two are closely connected. Older people who remain socially active tend to stay healthier for longer and need less medical treatment. Consequently, relatively inexpensive measures such as community centres, volunteer visiting schemes and intergenerational projects could reduce pressure on hospitals. Furthermore, these measures can be introduced quickly, whereas reforming healthcare systems takes years. Tackling isolation is therefore not only the humane choice but also a practical investment in the sustainability of public services.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","As populations age across much of the world, governments are being forced to rethink how they support older citizens. Of the many challenges this creates, the sustainability of pensions and the rising cost of healthcare are arguably the most urgent.\n\nPension systems in many countries were designed when people lived only a few years beyond retirement. Today, retirement can last two or three decades, and a shrinking working population must fund an ever-growing number of pensioners. Raising the retirement age is the most obvious solution, but it is deeply unpopular and particularly unfair to those in physically demanding jobs.\n\nHealthcare costs present a similar problem. Older people are more likely to live with chronic conditions requiring long-term treatment, and hospitals are already under severe strain. Without reform, waiting times will lengthen and the quality of care is likely to decline.\n\nIn my view, healthcare should be addressed first. While pension reform is undoubtedly necessary, its effects are gradual and can be planned over many years. A failing healthcare system, by contrast, has immediate and sometimes fatal consequences. Moreover, investment in preventive care – encouraging exercise, improving diet and detecting illness early – would allow people to remain healthy and active for longer, reducing costs and enabling many to work later in life if they wish. In this sense, addressing healthcare effectively would also ease the pressure on pensions, making it the more strategic priority."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","The demographic shift towards an older population is frequently framed as a looming crisis. While this may be an exaggeration, it undeniably presents governments with difficult choices, of which the funding of pensions and the problem of loneliness are among the most pressing.\n\nThe pensions dilemma is essentially one of arithmetic. As the ratio of workers to retirees declines, maintaining current levels of support will require higher taxes, a later retirement age, or both. Each option carries political risk, which perhaps explains why so many governments have postponed meaningful reform for as long as possible.\n\nLoneliness, though less quantifiable, is no less serious. A growing body of research links chronic isolation to depression, cognitive decline and heart disease, with some studies suggesting its effects are comparable to smoking fifteen cigarettes a day. As families become more geographically dispersed, an increasing number of older people may go days without meaningful human contact.\n\nIt is tempting to prioritise pensions simply because the financial stakes are so visible. I would contend, however, that tackling loneliness should come first, precisely because it is so often neglected and because the solutions are relatively inexpensive. Community centres, befriending schemes and intergenerational housing projects require modest investment yet can transform lives – and, by keeping older people healthier, reduce demands on public services. Pension reform cannot be avoided indefinitely, but a society that ignores the isolation of its older members has, in a very real sense, already failed them."]]},{"id":"c1w3","part":2,"kind":"report","t":"Student Clubs","in":"The director of your college wants to know how the student clubs could be improved. Write a report evaluating the current clubs, identifying any problems and making recommendations. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"REPORT REQUEST\nStudent clubs\nPlease evaluate the current range of student clubs, explain any problems that limit participation, and recommend changes that would encourage more students to take part.","pts":[["Başlıklar kullan (report biçimi)",["introduction","findings","recommendations","conclusion","aim"]],["Mevcut durumu değerlendir",["currently","at present","range","popular","participation"]],["Sorunları belirt",["problem","however","limited","lack","clash"]],["Öneriler sun",["recommend","suggest","should","it would be advisable"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Report on student clubs\n\nIntroduction\nThe aim of this report is to evaluate the college's current student clubs and to suggest ways of increasing participation. It is based on a questionnaire completed by 85 students and interviews with four club leaders.\n\nCurrent situation\nThe college offers twelve clubs, ranging from debating to photography. The sports clubs and the drama society are well attended and highly rated. However, overall participation is low: only around a third of students belong to a club, and several clubs have fewer than five regular members.\n\nMain problems\nThe most frequently mentioned problem was timing. Most clubs meet immediately after classes, which clashes with part-time jobs and long journeys home. A second issue is awareness; many students, particularly first-years, were unaware that some clubs existed. Finally, a number of respondents felt that the range of clubs did not reflect current interests, with no options related to technology, the environment or mental well-being.\n\nRecommendations\nFirstly, it would be advisable to hold some club meetings at lunchtime, when most students are on site. Secondly, a club fair at the start of each term, together with a regularly updated online page, would raise awareness. Thirdly, students should be invited to propose new clubs, with a small budget available for those that attract sufficient interest.\n\nConclusion\nIf these changes are implemented, I am confident that participation would increase significantly, enriching students' college experience.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","Report on student clubs\n\nPurpose\nThis report assesses the effectiveness of the college's student clubs and proposes measures to increase participation. The findings are drawn from an online survey of 120 students and conversations with club organisers.\n\nOverview of current clubs\nThere are currently fourteen clubs, covering sport, music, drama, debating and languages. The football and music clubs are thriving, with waiting lists for new members. By contrast, the language and chess clubs struggle to attract more than a handful of students each week.\n\nKey problems\nThree main obstacles emerged. Firstly, many students consider the clubs too formal; several respondents described them as \"an extra lesson\" rather than a social activity. Secondly, there is little variety for students who are not interested in sport or performing arts. Thirdly, club information is spread across noticeboards, emails and social media, so students often miss announcements.\n\nRecommendations\nIn order to make clubs more appealing, organisers should be encouraged to include informal, social elements, such as games, trips or shared meals. The college could also introduce a simple process allowing students to start new clubs, perhaps requiring a minimum of eight interested members. Finally, it is recommended that all club information be gathered in a single online calendar.\n\nConclusion\nThe college clearly has a strong foundation of clubs, but several are failing to reach their potential. Implementing the measures above would require minimal expense and, in my view, would lead to a noticeable increase in student involvement."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","Student clubs: evaluation and recommendations\n\nIntroduction\nAt the director's request, this report evaluates the college's extracurricular clubs, identifies factors limiting participation and puts forward a number of recommendations. It draws on a questionnaire completed by 96 students, attendance records for the past term and informal interviews with club leaders.\n\nStrengths\nThe college offers a respectable range of clubs, and several – most notably the debating society and the orchestra – have achieved considerable success in regional competitions. Members of these clubs spoke enthusiastically about the friendships and skills they had developed.\n\nAreas of concern\nDespite this, participation is uneven. Attendance records indicate that almost half of all club members belong to just three clubs, while others have dwindled to only a few regular attendees. Survey responses point to two underlying causes: a perception that clubs cater primarily to students who are already highly skilled, and a lack of clubs reflecting newer interests such as coding, sustainability or mental well-being.\n\nRecommendations\nIt would be advisable for established clubs to introduce beginner sessions, making it clear that no prior experience is required. In addition, the college should invite proposals for new clubs at the start of each academic year, supporting promising ideas with a small start-up budget. Finally, a brief \"taster week\", during which students could attend any club without commitment, would lower the barrier to joining.\n\nConclusion\nWhile the existing clubs have much to be proud of, they currently serve a relatively narrow group of students. If adopted, the measures outlined above should broaden participation significantly at minimal cost."]]},{"id":"c1w4","part":2,"kind":"proposal","t":"A Town Festival","in":"Your town council is planning to hold an annual festival and has asked residents for proposals. Write a proposal suggesting what the festival should include, explaining how it would benefit the town and addressing any potential problems. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"PROPOSALS WANTED\nA new town festival\nWhat kind of festival would bring the community together and attract visitors? Explain your ideas, how they would benefit the town, and how any problems could be dealt with.","pts":[["Başlıklar kullan (proposal biçimi)",["introduction","proposal","benefits","potential problems","conclusion"]],["Festival içeriği öner",["include","feature","stalls","music","food","workshop"]],["Faydaları açıkla",["benefit","local businesses","community","attract","boost"]],["Olası sorunlara çözüm",["problem","concern","could be addressed","to avoid","solution"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Proposal for an annual town festival\n\nIntroduction\nThis proposal outlines a festival celebrating the town's food and craft traditions, which I believe would bring residents together and attract visitors from the surrounding area.\n\nWhat the festival should include\nI propose a two-day weekend event held in the main square and along the riverside. The centrepiece would be a market featuring local food producers, bakers and craftspeople. Alongside this, there could be cooking demonstrations by local restaurants, workshops where children learn traditional crafts such as pottery and weaving, and live music in the evenings performed by local bands and school groups.\n\nBenefits for the town\nThe festival would give local businesses valuable publicity and additional income. It would also strengthen the community by involving schools, clubs and residents of all ages. In the longer term, a successful festival could put the town on the map as a destination, encouraging tourism throughout the year.\n\nPotential problems\nResidents in the centre may be concerned about noise and parking. These concerns could be addressed by ending live music at 10 p.m. and providing a free shuttle bus from car parks on the edge of town. Another risk is bad weather; a covered area near the town hall should therefore be reserved for key events.\n\nConclusion\nWith careful planning, a food and craft festival would be an affordable and inclusive way to celebrate the town's identity. I strongly recommend that the council consider this proposal.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","Proposal: a riverside music and arts festival\n\nAim\nThe purpose of this proposal is to suggest a festival that would celebrate local talent, bring residents of all ages together and attract visitors to the town.\n\nFestival content\nI propose an annual weekend festival along the riverbank each June. The main attraction would be two outdoor stages featuring local bands, choirs and school orchestras, with a well-known headline act on the Saturday evening. Alongside the music, an arts trail could display work by local painters and photographers in shop windows throughout the town centre. For families, a children's area with face painting, storytelling and craft workshops would ensure the event appealed to all ages.\n\nBenefits to the town\nSuch a festival would provide a valuable platform for local performers, many of whom have few opportunities to perform in public. Shops, cafés and hotels would benefit from increased trade, and the arts trail in particular would encourage visitors to explore the centre rather than remaining by the river.\n\nPossible difficulties\nThe most significant concerns are likely to be noise and litter. To address these, music could finish at 10.30 p.m., and volunteers from local schools and clubs could be recruited to keep the site clean in exchange for a donation to their organisation. Funding could be secured through sponsorship from local businesses and a modest charge for the headline concert only.\n\nConclusion\nA riverside festival of this kind would be relatively inexpensive to organise and would give the town an event to be genuinely proud of."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","Proposal for a \"Festival of Lights\" in the old town\n\nIntroduction\nIn response to the council's invitation, this proposal sets out plans for an annual winter festival which, I believe, would enliven the town during its quietest months and strengthen community ties.\n\nThe concept\nTaking place over the first weekend of December, the festival would transform the historic streets of the old town with light installations created by local artists, students and community groups. Each evening, a lantern procession – led by children from local schools – would wind its way to the main square, where a programme of acoustic music and storytelling would follow. Stalls selling hot food and handmade crafts would line the route, with priority given to local producers.\n\nAnticipated benefits\nUnlike a summer festival, which would compete with countless similar events, a winter festival would fill a genuine gap, attracting visitors at a time when local businesses most need custom. Furthermore, the collaborative nature of the installations would involve hundreds of residents in the months leading up to the event, fostering a strong sense of ownership.\n\nAddressing potential concerns\nSafety on narrow, crowded streets would naturally be a priority; a one-way pedestrian route and clearly marked exits would help to manage crowds. Concerns about cost could be mitigated by using energy-efficient lighting and by seeking sponsorship from local firms, who would gain considerable visibility.\n\nConclusion\nThe Festival of Lights would offer a distinctive, inclusive celebration of the town's heritage and creativity. I would urge the council to give it serious consideration."]]},{"id":"c1w5","part":2,"kind":"letter","t":"Saving the Library","in":"You have read an article in a local newspaper arguing that the town library should be closed to save money. Write a letter to the editor responding to the article and explaining why the library should remain open. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"From the article:\n\"In an age when information is available online, libraries are an expensive luxury. The council should close the town library and use the money for more urgent needs.\"","pts":[["Uygun hitap ve kapanış",["dear","editor","yours faithfully","yours sincerely"]],["Makaleye yanıt ver",["article","claim","argue","suggests","i was"]],["Kütüphanenin faydalarını açıkla",["library","free","community","access","children","elderly"]],["Güçlü bir sonuç",["urge","strongly","therefore","in conclusion","i hope"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Dear Editor,\n\nI am writing in response to last week's article suggesting that the town library should be closed to save money. While I understand the pressure on the council's budget, I believe the article seriously underestimates the role the library plays in our community.\n\nThe writer claims that libraries are unnecessary because information is available online. This overlooks the fact that many residents do not have reliable internet access at home. For them, the library's free computers are essential for applying for jobs, accessing government services and helping children with homework.\n\nFurthermore, the library is far more than a collection of books. It hosts reading groups for children, language classes for newcomers and a weekly meeting for elderly residents, many of whom live alone. For these people, the library is one of the few warm, safe and free public spaces left in the town.\n\nThe article also describes the library as an \"expensive luxury\". Yet its annual budget is a tiny fraction of the council's total spending, and the cost of closing it – in terms of increased isolation, reduced literacy and fewer opportunities – would be far greater in the long run.\n\nRather than closing the library, the council could explore ways of making it more financially sustainable, such as renting rooms to local groups or seeking support from businesses.\n\nI therefore urge the council to reject this proposal and to recognise the library as the valuable public service it is.\n\nYours faithfully,\nZeynep Arslan","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","Dear Editor,\n\nI was dismayed to read last week's article calling for the closure of our town library. As someone who has used the library regularly for over twenty years, I would like to offer a rather different perspective.\n\nThe article suggests that, because information is now available online, libraries have become obsolete. This argument fundamentally misunderstands what a library offers. Online information is abundant, but it is also unreliable and often hidden behind paywalls. Librarians help people of all ages find trustworthy sources – a skill that has arguably never been more important.\n\nMoreover, the library serves as a lifeline for groups who are easily overlooked. Job seekers without home computers use it to apply for work; parents bring toddlers to free rhyme-time sessions; and students who lack a quiet place at home rely on it to study for their exams. Closing it would hit precisely those residents who can least afford alternatives.\n\nThe writer also claims that the money could be spent on \"more urgent needs\". Yet the library's running costs amount to a very small proportion of the council's budget, and the savings would be modest compared with the long-term costs to education and community life.\n\nIf the council is genuinely concerned about value for money, it should consider ways of making better use of the building, for instance by sharing it with other public services, rather than closing it altogether.\n\nI hope the council will listen to the many residents who value this vital service.\n\nYours faithfully,\nMehmet Yıldız"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","Dear Editor,\n\nYour recent article describing the town library as \"an expensive luxury\" has prompted considerable debate in our household, and I feel compelled to respond.\n\nThe author's central claim – that the internet has rendered libraries redundant – rests on a misconception. A library is not merely a warehouse for books, any more than a school is merely a building containing teachers. It is a public space in the truest sense: free, open to all and requiring no justification for one's presence. In an age when almost every other public place expects us to spend money, its value can hardly be overstated.\n\nConsider who would bear the cost of closure. Not those of us with fast broadband and shelves of books at home, but elderly residents who rely on the library for company and assistance with online forms, newcomers attending its language classes, and teenagers for whom it offers the only quiet place to study. To describe such provision as a luxury is, frankly, to misunderstand who it serves.\n\nI do not dispute that the council faces difficult financial choices. However, closing a library is a decision that is easy to make and almost impossible to reverse. Once the building is sold and the collection dispersed, it will not return when finances improve.\n\nI would therefore urge the council to explore alternatives – partnerships with local businesses, shared use of the building, volunteer support – before taking such an irreversible step.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAyşe Demir"]]}],"S":[{"id":"c1s1","p":1,"t":"Opinions and Experiences","ins":"Doğal ve gelişmiş cevaplar ver.","q":[["What kind of things do you find yourself spending too much time on?",30,["I have to admit","I find myself","far more than"],["I have to admit I find myself scrolling through news websites far more than I should. I tell myself I'm staying informed, but more often than not I'm just reading the same story from slightly different angles. It's become a sort of reflex – I'll pick up my phone to check the time and twenty minutes later I'm still reading.","Probably planning things that never actually happen. I'll spend hours researching a holiday, comparing flights and reading reviews of hotels, and then end up not going because of work. My friends joke that I enjoy the planning more than the trip itself, and they might have a point."]],["How has the area where you live changed in recent years?",30,["it's changed","considerably","for better or worse","these days"],["It's changed considerably, especially over the last five years or so. A lot of the old family-run shops have closed, and there are far more cafés and chain stores now. On the plus side, the area feels livelier and safer at night, but for better or worse, it's lost some of its character and feels much more like any other city.","Surprisingly little, actually. I live in quite a traditional neighbourhood, and many of the same families have been there for generations. The main difference is the traffic – there are far more cars now, and the streets where we used to play football as children are permanently lined with parked vehicles."]],["What's the best piece of advice you've ever been given?",30,["the best advice","stuck with me","it taught me"],["The best advice I've ever been given was from my grandmother, who told me never to make an important decision when I'm angry. It sounds obvious, but it's stuck with me, and it's saved me from a few regrettable emails and arguments. Now, whenever I feel annoyed, I make myself wait until the next morning before responding.","A teacher once told me that it's fine not to know the answer, as long as you know how to find it. At the time I was terrified of making mistakes in class, and that completely changed my attitude. I became much more willing to ask questions, and I think it's made me a better learner ever since."]]]},{"id":"c1s2","p":2,"t":"Learning from Others","ins":"Üç fotoğraftan ikisini seç ve 1 dakika karşılaştır.","img":[["👵📱👦","Fotoğraf 1: Bir genç, büyükannesine akıllı telefon kullanmayı öğretiyor.","c1-phone","A teenage boy is showing his grandmother how to use a smartphone on the sofa."],["🧑‍🔧🚗👨","Fotoğraf 2: Bir usta, çırağına araba tamiri gösteriyor.","c1-mechanic","An experienced mechanic is showing a young apprentice how to repair a car in a garage."],["👩‍🏫🌍👧","Fotoğraf 3: Bir öğretmen öğrencilere küre üzerinde ülkeleri gösteriyor.","c1-globe","A teacher is showing a globe to a group of young children in a classroom."]],"q":[["These pictures show people learning from others. Compare two of the pictures and say what the people might be learning, and how they might be feeling.",60,["whereas","I'd imagine","both","it seems","presumably"],["I'll compare the grandmother and the apprentice. In both pictures, someone is passing on practical knowledge, but the roles are interestingly reversed. In the first, a teenager is presumably teaching his grandmother how to use a smartphone, whereas in the second, an experienced mechanic is showing a younger man how to repair a car. The grandson is probably learning patience, since explaining technology to someone unfamiliar with it can be surprisingly challenging, while his grandmother is learning a skill that could help her stay in touch with family. I'd imagine she might be feeling slightly frustrated, or perhaps a little embarrassed at having to learn from her grandson, although she also seems amused. The apprentice, on the other hand, is probably concentrating hard, aware that he'll soon have to do the job on his own, and perhaps feeling a mixture of curiosity and pressure.","Let me compare the classroom and the car workshop. Both show people learning from someone more experienced, but the type of learning is quite different. In the classroom, the teacher is showing children countries on a globe, so they're presumably acquiring general knowledge about the world, whereas the apprentice is learning a very specific, hands-on skill that will directly affect his career. The children seem fascinated – geography can be quite exciting when it's presented visually – though one or two may simply be waiting for the bell. The apprentice probably feels a greater sense of responsibility, since a mistake on a real car could have serious consequences. I'd also guess the mechanic feels quite proud, passing on skills he's spent years developing."]],["Which situation do you think would be the most rewarding for the person teaching?",30,["I'd say","probably","because"],["I'd say the grandson, probably, because helping a family member become more independent is particularly satisfying. Seeing his grandmother send her first video message would be a lovely moment, and it might bring them closer too. It also reverses the usual dynamic, which I imagine both of them would find rather touching.","I'd argue it's the mechanic. Training an apprentice means seeing someone gradually become competent in a skill you've mastered, and there's a real sense of legacy in that – knowing your knowledge will be carried forward. In a trade like that, skills are often learned by watching rather than reading, so the relationship really matters."]]]},{"id":"c1s3","p":3,"t":"Choosing a Job","ins":"Önce seçenekleri tartış (2 dakika), sonra karar ver (1 dakika).","opts":["💰 salary","⏰ flexible hours","🌱 company values","📈 career progression","🤝 colleagues"],"q":[["Here are some factors people consider when choosing a job. Talk about how important these factors are for young people today.",120,["I'd argue","by contrast","that's a fair point","it depends on","increasingly"],["I'd argue that salary is still important, especially given how expensive housing has become for young people – it's very difficult to feel motivated if you can barely pay the rent. That said, it's no longer the only consideration. Flexible hours have become increasingly important since the pandemic; many young people now see the ability to work from home at least part of the week as essential rather than a perk. Company values are an interesting one. Surveys suggest young people care a lot about working for an ethical company, although I suspect that matters less when jobs are scarce and people simply need an income. Career progression depends very much on the individual – some people are highly ambitious and want to climb quickly, while others prioritise balance and stability. And colleagues are often underestimated. A supportive team can make even a fairly dull job enjoyable, whereas a toxic atmosphere can ruin a job that looks perfect on paper. So I'd say all of these factors matter, but their importance varies hugely from person to person.","Let's start with career progression, since I think that's often what young people focus on first. Many graduates want to know they won't be stuck in the same role for years, so clear opportunities for promotion and training are a major attraction. Salary is obviously important too, but interestingly, studies show that above a certain level it doesn't make people much happier. Company values have become far more prominent – young people are increasingly unwilling to work for organisations that damage the environment or treat workers badly, and they're very aware of a company's reputation online. Flexible hours are, in my view, almost expected nowadays. As for colleagues, I think people only realise how much they matter once they've started a job, so they're rarely the deciding factor when choosing between offers, even though they probably should be. If I had to rank them, I'd probably put flexibility and progression at the top for most people, with salary close behind, but I'd say the balance shifts depending on whether someone has family responsibilities or student debts to pay off."]],["Now decide which factor is the most important for young people when choosing a job.",60,["on balance","if we had to pick","I'd go for","although"],["On balance, I'd go for flexible hours. Although salary is clearly important, flexibility affects every aspect of life, from physical and mental health to relationships and hobbies. Young people seem increasingly unwilling to sacrifice their personal lives for their careers, and a job that allows them to organise their own time is often worth more to them than a higher salary. It also tends to benefit employers, since people who feel trusted are generally more loyal and productive, so it's arguably the factor that makes the biggest difference on both sides.","If I had to pick one, I'd choose colleagues. You spend more time with them than with almost anyone else, and they have an enormous impact on whether you enjoy going to work. A good team will support you, teach you and make difficult periods bearable, which in turn often leads to better progression and opportunities. Salary and flexibility can be negotiated, but you can't negotiate a good atmosphere – either it's there or it isn't – and that's why I think it deserves more attention than it usually gets when people are comparing job offers."]]]},{"id":"c1s4","p":4,"t":"Technology and Society","ins":"Görüşünü gerekçeler ve farklı bakış açılarıyla açıkla.","q":[["Do you think artificial intelligence will create more jobs than it destroys?",45,["it's hard to say","historically","on the other hand","I suspect"],["It's hard to say. Historically, new technologies have created more jobs than they've destroyed – the internet, for example, wiped out some jobs but created entire industries. However, AI is different in that it can perform cognitive tasks, not just physical ones, so it could affect lawyers, translators and programmers as well as factory workers. I suspect the real challenge won't be the total number of jobs but retraining people quickly enough.","I'm rather pessimistic, to be honest. Even if new jobs are created, they may require very different skills and be concentrated in particular cities or sectors. A fifty-year-old administrative worker can't easily become a data scientist. So while the overall number of jobs might recover eventually, many individuals could find themselves left behind in the meantime. Governments would need to invest heavily in lifelong education to prevent a sharp rise in inequality."]],["Is it the responsibility of governments to regulate technology companies?",45,["to a large extent","after all","the risk is","I'd argue"],["To a large extent, yes. After all, these companies now have enormous influence on public life, from what news we read to how our personal data is used, and they're accountable mainly to their shareholders. Governments have a duty to protect citizens. The risk, of course, is that regulation is always one step behind the technology, and poorly designed rules could stifle innovation.","I think regulation is necessary, but it needs to be international to be effective. These companies operate globally, so if one country introduces strict rules, they can simply move their operations elsewhere. That's why agreements between countries, like the European Union's data protection laws, seem to me the most promising approach. Of course, reaching international agreement is notoriously slow, but the alternative is a patchwork of rules that companies can easily avoid."]],["How might our relationship with technology change in the next decade?",45,["I imagine","is likely to","might well","I'd hope"],["I imagine we'll become more selective about the technology we use. There's already a backlash against constant connectivity – some schools have banned phones, and more people are deliberately buying simpler devices. I'd hope that over the next decade technology becomes something we use deliberately rather than compulsively, although the companies behind it obviously have strong incentives to keep us hooked.","I suspect technology will become even more invisible – built into our homes, cars and even clothes – so we'll interact with it constantly without noticing. That could make life more convenient, but it also raises serious questions about privacy, since we'll be generating data about almost everything we do. Who owns that data, and how it's used, is likely to become one of the defining political questions of the next decade."]]]},{"id":"c1s5","p":4,"t":"The Environment","ins":"Görüşünü gerekçeler ve örneklerle açıkla.","q":[["Should individuals or governments take the main responsibility for protecting the environment?",45,["both","ultimately","without","I'd argue"],["I'd argue both have a role, but ultimately governments have far more power. Individuals can recycle, cycle to work and eat less meat, and those choices do add up. But without regulation and investment in things like renewable energy and public transport, large-scale progress simply isn't possible. In fact, I think placing too much emphasis on individual responsibility can distract from the decisions that really matter.","I think individuals have to take the lead, because governments generally follow public opinion. If enough people change their behaviour and demand action, politicians will respond. We've seen this with plastic bags – consumer pressure led to bans in many countries. So individual action isn't just about its direct impact; it also sends a powerful political signal."]],["Do you think people are willing to change their lifestyles to help the environment?",45,["to some extent","as long as","in practice","I suspect"],["To some extent, as long as the changes are convenient and affordable. In practice, I suspect most people support green policies in principle but become far more reluctant when it affects their own habits, like flying on holiday or driving to work. That's why making the sustainable option the easiest and cheapest one is probably more effective than relying on people's goodwill.","More than they used to be, certainly. Younger generations in particular seem genuinely willing to make sacrifices – many of my friends have stopped eating meat or refuse to fly short distances. Whether that commitment will last as they get older and have families is another question, but the shift in attitudes is real. It's also visible in what people buy – second-hand clothes, for instance, have gone from being slightly embarrassing to genuinely fashionable."]],["Is economic growth compatible with protecting the environment?",45,["it's a complex","in theory","arguably","green technology"],["It's a complex question. In theory, green technology can create growth while reducing emissions, and arguably that's already happening in renewable energy, which now employs millions of people worldwide. But it requires a fundamental shift in how we measure success – for instance, accounting for environmental damage rather than simply counting economic output. If pollution and the loss of natural resources were included in calculations of growth, many apparently successful economies would look rather different.","I'm doubtful, if we mean unlimited growth. The planet's resources are finite, and so far, no country has managed to reduce its environmental impact as fast as its economy has grown. Some economists argue we need to focus on well-being rather than growth, and I find that argument increasingly persuasive, even if it's politically very difficult. No government wants to tell voters that their standard of living may not keep rising."]]]}]}}};
